window.PROSE=window.PROSE||{};Object.assign(window.PROSE,{"britten-string-quartet-no-2":["Three movements, about thirty minutes, with the weight thrown onto the last. The first states three themes all beginning with the same wide upward leap of a tenth, and closes with all four instruments spinning the interval in unison. A ghostly, muted scherzo follows, fast and almost entirely quiet. Then a Chacony of twenty-one variations over a repeating theme, punctuated by unaccompanied cadenzas for cello, viola and violin, and ending on some twenty bars of hammered C major.","Written in London in October 1945 for the two hundred and fiftieth anniversary of Purcell's death, and premiered by the Zorian Quartet at the Wigmore Hall on 21 November, Purcell's anniversary. Britten had returned to England in 1942 as a pacifist and registered conscientious objector; that summer he had played piano for a recital tour of the newly liberated Belsen camp with Yehudi Menuhin, and had just finished Peter Grimes. Purcell's music was central to his sense of an English tradition worth reclaiming.","The finale's title is spelled Chacony, Purcell's own English form of chaconne, and the ground bass is deliberately Purcellian in shape. Listen for the three solo cadenzas that divide the twenty-one variations into groups: each is a single instrument left alone, and each changes what follows. The rising interval of a tenth that opens the first movement is not decoration — it is the germ of all three main themes, and it returns at the very end.","The Second Quartet was better received than most of Britten's instrumental music of the period, though critics who preferred his operas treated it as a sideline. Its reputation has risen steadily; the Chacony is now often cited as the finest English variation movement since Purcell himself. Performers debate the final pages, where the insistence on C major can sound either like hard-won affirmation or like something being asserted rather too loudly.","Go straight to the Chacony and listen for the repeating bass line, then notice the three moments when a single player is left alone. Afterwards return to the first movement for the rising leap. Compare with Purcell's own chaconnes and with Bartók's Sixth Quartet from 1939. British ensembles have owned this repertoire; look for a recording that keeps the muted scherzo genuinely soft."],"shostakovich-string-quartet-no-3":["Five movements, about thirty-three minutes, and the largest of his quartets. It begins deceptively, with a jaunty tune and a dry academic fugue, then darkens sharply: a lurching waltz in five, a savage march in three whose accents fall in the wrong places, a passacaglia of grinding slowness over a repeated bass, and a finale that gathers everything and ends with the cello holding a single note while the music dissolves into a long, unresolved quiet.","Written in 1946, between the end of the war and the Zhdanov decree of 1948 that would denounce Shostakovich again. He was living in Moscow, decorated and outwardly successful; the Ninth Symphony, deliberately light where a victory symphony was expected, had already annoyed the authorities the year before. The quartet is dedicated to the Beethoven Quartet, who premiered it in Moscow on 16 December 1946 and played every subsequent quartet he wrote.","Shostakovich supplied the movements with programmatic titles for an early performance — describing calm unawareness of coming disaster, rumblings of unrest, the forces of war unleashed, homage to the dead, and a final question of why and for what — then withdrew them and never allowed them to be printed. Listen to the fourth movement's repeating bass line under a lament: it is a passacaglia, the form he used elsewhere for grief. The finale's last minutes simply refuse to answer the question the titles pose.","The Third was played, then quietly dropped after 1948 and only returned to programmes in the 1950s. The withdrawn titles have driven the argument ever since: those who read Shostakovich as a coded dissident treat them as the key, while others note that he supplied and withdrew such glosses freely and that the music's shape does not depend on them. Most performers now let the passacaglia speak without commentary.","Start with the fourth movement, the passacaglia, and follow the repeating bass. Then the third, the march, and try to feel where its accents land. Compare with the Eighth Quartet, more famous and more compressed, and with Britten's Second Quartet from the previous year. The Borodin Quartet's recordings, made with the composer's involvement, remain the reference."],"ligeti-string-quartet-no-2":["Five movements, about twenty-one minutes, each exploring a single kind of behaviour rather than a theme. The first alternates frozen stillness with sudden violent flurries. The second is extremely slow and almost inaudibly quiet throughout. The third is a machine movement, played entirely plucked, in which the four players tick at slightly different speeds. The fourth is furious and jagged; the fifth dissolves into a haze of trills, harmonics and dust that fades to nothing.","Ligeti had escaped Hungary in 1956 and by 1968 was living between Vienna and Germany, having made his name with the dense orchestral clouds of Atmosphères and Lux aeterna, which Stanley Kubrick used without permission in 2001: A Space Odyssey the same year. The quartet was written for the LaSalle Quartet, specialists in the Second Viennese School, and premiered in Baden-Baden in 1969. It was his first quartet since leaving Hungary, seventeen years after his first.","The third movement is the seed of Ligeti's lifelong obsession with malfunctioning clockwork: four plucked pulses set going at speeds that almost but never quite align, so the ensemble drifts in and out of phase. He had already written a piece for a hundred metronomes. Listen in the first movement for the transitions — the shift from stasis to frenzy happens without a preparation of any kind, as if a switch were thrown.","Ligeti described the quartet as five aspects of the same idea seen from different sides, and pointed to Bartók as the ancestor even though no folk material survives. Early reception focused on the technical demands, which are severe; the second movement asks for a dynamic level barely above silence for minutes at a time. It is now firmly in the repertoire and generally considered the most important quartet written between Bartók's Sixth and the end of the century.","Begin with the third movement, the plucked machine, which is short and immediately gripping. Then attempt the second in a genuinely silent room with the volume up — the piece depends on your hearing almost nothing. Compare with Bartók's Fourth and with Ligeti's own Six Bagatelles for wind quintet. The Arditti Quartet's recordings are the standard, with the Hagen and Parker quartets offering warmer alternatives."],"dutilleux-ainsi-la-nuit":["Eighteen minutes in seven short movements played almost without a break, separated by brief linking passages the composer calls parentheses. The sound world is nocturnal and jewelled: harmonics, tremolos, sudden pizzicato flashes, chords spaced so that the four instruments seem to shimmer rather than blend. Movements carry titles such as Nocturne, Mirror of Space, Litanies and Constellations. The last, Suspended Time, ends with the music evaporating upward rather than concluding.","The Koussevitzky Foundation commissioned it for the Juilliard Quartet, and Dutilleux, characteristically slow, spent three years on eighteen minutes. He was in his late fifties, living in Paris, and had written no chamber music of consequence before; to prepare, he composed a set of private studies for quartet, Nuits, that he never published in his lifetime. The Parrenin Quartet gave the first performance in Paris in January 1977.","The parentheses between movements are the structural secret: each anticipates material from a movement still to come or recalls one already heard, so the piece grows forward and backward at once. Dutilleux called this progressive growth, and the opening chord of the whole work is not fully explained until the end. Listen also for the constant use of harmonics and of a chord that Dutilleux keeps respacing, so the same harmony appears in different lights, like an object turned in the hand.","Dutilleux belonged to no faction, which for years cost him attention: the post-war avant-garde considered him insufficiently radical and conservatives found him too free. Ainsi la nuit changed that. It is now widely regarded as the most important French string quartet since Ravel's, and its influence on later French composers, and on writers about musical memory, is considerable. Performers argue mainly about pacing — whether the parentheses should sound like silence or like music.","Listen to the whole thing in one sitting; at eighteen minutes it works as a single arc, and dipping in destroys the point. Then hear it again attending only to the short passages between movements. Compare with Debussy's and Ravel's quartets and with Ligeti's Second. Recordings by the Juilliard, Arditti and Belcea quartets differ sharply in atmosphere; look for one with real quiet."],"ades-arcadiana":["Seven short movements, about nineteen minutes, each a picture of a vanished or vanishing idyll. A Venetian gondola song wobbles as if the water were rising; a Mozart tune from The Magic Flute is heard through distortion; Schubert's song about singing on the water becomes a rippling accompaniment with the melody missing. A tango arrives, marked mortale. The sixth, O Albion, is a slow, plain, aching hymn to an England that never existed. The last, Lethe, forgets itself and disappears.","Adès was twenty-three, fresh from Cambridge and already the most talked-about young composer in Britain, when he wrote Arcadiana for the Endellion Quartet in 1994. The same year brought his chamber opera Powder Her Face. The title points to Arcadia, the pastoral paradise of poetry and painting, and to the Latin tag et in Arcadia ego — even in Arcadia, I, death, am present — which supplies the fourth movement's title and its tone.","Each movement hides a source. The second quotes Papageno's music, the third is built over the rhythm of Schubert's Auf dem Wasser zu singen with the tune withheld, the fifth evokes Watteau's painting of an embarkation for Cythera, and O Albion is written in the manner of Elgar without quoting him. Lethe, the river of forgetting, empties the material out: the players are asked to lose definition until the sound is barely identifiable as a quartet.","Arcadiana was received as proof that a composer of Adès's generation could write music that was learned, allusive and immediately affecting at the same time, and it won the Paris Rostrum prize in 1996. O Albion has escaped into an independent life, arranged for string orchestra and used widely, which some critics regard as a misunderstanding of a movement that depends on its bleak surroundings. The debate is really about whether nostalgia in Adès is sincere or quoted.","Hear O Albion on its own once — it is under three minutes and will pull you in — then listen to the whole set so that it lands in context. Watch for the tango. Compare with Britten's Second Quartet for another English reckoning with the past, and with Dutilleux's Ainsi la nuit for the seven-panel form. The Endellion and Calder quartets have both recorded it convincingly."],"berlioz-harold-en-italie":["A symphony in four movements with a solo viola threaded through it, never quite a concerto. The viola sings one long melody — Berlioz calls it Harold — that returns in every movement without ever developing into display. Harold walks in the mountains, watches a pilgrims' procession pass with tolling bells, hears a mountaineer serenade his mistress over a drone, then is swallowed by an orgy of brigands. Roughly forty minutes; the viola falls silent long before the end.","Paganini, newly the owner of a Stradivari viola, asked Berlioz for a piece to show it off. Berlioz produced something else entirely, and Paganini refused it: too few notes for the soloist. He never played it. Berlioz drew instead on his own memories of tramping through the Abruzzi hills in 1831 as a Prix de Rome laureate, and on Byron's Childe Harold, then the fashionable model for the wandering, disaffected young man.","Listen closer: the Pilgrims' March plants two notes — a bell-like B and C — that keep sounding whether or not they fit the harmony, so the procession seems to move past a fixed point rather than the orchestra move with it. And the finale opens by quoting each earlier movement in turn and shouting it down, a device Berlioz took straight from the opening of Beethoven's Ninth.","The 1834 premiere went well enough, though Berlioz complained the orchestra mangled it. Paganini finally heard the work in 1838, knelt before Berlioz in public and sent him twenty thousand francs — an episode contemporaries doubted and modern scholarship largely accepts. Violists adopted it as their one great Romantic vehicle while grumbling that the part is short on fireworks, which was of course the point.","Start with the Pilgrims' March and simply follow the two chiming notes; once you hear them you cannot unhear them. Then go back to the opening and wait for the viola's first entry over quiet strings. Compare it with Symphonie fantastique, written four years earlier, to hear how much cooler and stranger Berlioz's orchestra has become."],"berlioz-romeo-et-juliette":["Berlioz called it a dramatic symphony: seven large sections lasting well over an hour, for chorus, three soloists and a very big orchestra. The lovers themselves never sing. Their scenes — the balcony love scene, Romeo alone at the Capulet ball, Juliet's funeral procession, the tomb — are given entirely to the orchestra, while the singers handle prologue, commentary and the friar's closing sermon. In between sits the Queen Mab scherzo, a shimmering four-minute blur of muted strings, harps and high woodwind.","Paganini's gift of twenty thousand francs cleared Berlioz's debts and bought him a year to write whatever he liked. He chose the play that had knocked him sideways when Charles Kemble's English company brought it to Paris in 1827 — the same season in which he fell in love with the Irish actress Harriet Smithson, by 1839 his unhappy wife. He used Émile Deschamps's French text, and conducted the premiere himself at the Paris Conservatoire.","Listen closer: Berlioz explains in a preface exactly why the lovers are silent — sung duets were common property, and the orchestra could say what words had worn out. The Capulet ball music is heard twice, once as a distant party behind Romeo's solitude and once at full blast, so the same tune measures how far outside it he stands.","Parisian critics found the shape baffling and still do; the finale, with its choral reconciliation, is the section most often called a miscalculation. But the young Wagner sat in the hall and never forgot the love scene, whose long-breathed orchestral yearning surfaces again in Tristan. Conductors frequently extract the three orchestral movements, a practice Berlioz himself sanctioned.","Go straight to the Queen Mab scherzo — four minutes that sound a century ahead of 1839 — then the love scene, which repays sitting still for its full quarter hour. Look for a recording with a French chorus and a conductor willing to let the quiet passages stay quiet; the piece dies under a heavy hand."],"schumann-symphony-no-4":["Four movements played without a break, about half an hour of continuous music. A slow introduction sinks a quiet turning figure into the orchestra; that figure then generates almost everything that follows. The Romanze offers an oboe and cello melody over plucked strings, with a solo violin decorating the middle. The scherzo inverts the Romanze's violin line. The finale grows out of the first movement's main theme, brightening from D minor to D major only in the closing pages.","Schumann wrote it in 1841, the year after his marriage to Clara and the year he suddenly turned from piano music to the orchestra. He first called it a Symphonistic Fantasy and gave it to Clara for her birthday. The Leipzig premiere that December went badly — it shared a crowded programme — and he shelved it. In Düsseldorf ten years later he rescored it, thickening the wind writing and welding the movements together.","Listen closer: the four-note figure from the slow introduction never leaves. It becomes the Romanze's oboe tune, the scherzo's trio, and the transition into the finale, where a long crescendo over a held brass chord unlocks the door to D major. Schumann marked the original score with only rehearsal letters and no movement breaks, a hint to conductors that stopping for applause was never the plan.","Brahms preferred the leaner 1841 scoring and published it in 1891 over Clara's furious objection, opening an argument that is still live. Critics from Weingartner onwards have called the 1851 orchestration muddy; period-instrument bands have shown that much of the supposed mud is really modern string weight. Most orchestras still play the revision, and increasingly play it fast.","Listen for the moment the third movement dissolves and the brass chord swells into the finale — one of the great hinges in Romantic music. Then hear the 1841 version, ideally on gut strings under Gardiner or Dausgaard, and decide for yourself which Schumann you prefer."],"saint-saens-symphony-no-3-organ":["Two large movements, each split in half, so it plays like a four-movement symphony run together. A restless C minor allegro gives way to a hushed Adagio in which the organ enters for the first time on a single sustained chord under divided strings. Then a scherzo with glittering piano duet, and finally the organ's full-throated C major chord that everyone remembers, with the main theme turned into a broad chorale. Around thirty-six minutes.","The Royal Philharmonic Society commissioned it and Saint-Saëns conducted the London premiere in 1886, at the height of his reputation as France's most fluent composer. He was also, in the years after the Franco-Prussian War, campaigning for a French symphonic tradition to rival Germany's. Liszt died two months after the premiere and Saint-Saëns dedicated the score to his memory; Liszt had championed his opera Samson et Dalila when no French theatre would.","Listen closer: one theme runs through the whole symphony, changing character each time — anxious and scurrying at the start, consoling in the Adagio, triumphal in the finale. Its opening shape leans on the Dies irae plainchant. And the organ is used with real restraint: it plays in only two of the four sections, which is why the final entry lands like a door thrown open.","The London audience cheered; French critics noted, not always kindly, how German the argument was. Saint-Saëns said he had given everything he was able to give, and never wrote another symphony. The piece slipped into the category of showpiece — helped by a 1970s film borrowing the finale — and conductors have since had to argue that the first three-quarters deserve as much attention as the last five minutes.","Resist skipping to the organ blast. Start at the Adagio and notice how low and soft the organ's first entry is; the finale only works because of that restraint. Seek a recording made in a hall with a real pipe organ rather than a spliced-in instrument — the room's echo is part of the sound."],"bizet-symphony-c":["Four movements, half an hour, scored for a modest classical orchestra with no trombones. The outer movements run on bright, clean-cut tunes and a great deal of nervous energy; the finale is a perpetual-motion romp for the violins. At the centre is an Adagio in which a solo oboe unwinds a long, curling melody over pizzicato strings, an idea that returns later in fugal imitation. Nothing is padded and nothing outstays its welcome.","Bizet was seventeen, a Paris Conservatoire student writing an exercise under Gounod's eye, and he never showed it to anyone. The manuscript passed to his widow, then to the singer and composer Reynaldo Hahn, who gave it to the Conservatoire library, where it sat unread until the scholar Jean Chantavoine came across it in 1933. Felix Weingartner conducted the first performance in Basel in 1935, eighty years after it was written.","Listen closer: the oboe melody of the slow movement reappears almost note for note in Bizet's opera Don Procida — or rather, he lifted it from himself, since he had recently written it into an unperformed stage work. The whole symphony also shadows Gounod's own Symphony in D of 1855, which Bizet had just arranged for piano; the debts are audible in the phrasing and the scoring of the scherzo's trio.","The rediscovery was greeted as a small miracle, and Balanchine's 1947 ballet Le Palais de cristal fixed it in the repertoire. Some critics complain that it is more charming than characteristic — no trace of the composer of Carmen — while others hear the clarity of orchestration that would later make the Habanera sound so unforced. It is now standard training-orchestra fare and no worse for it.","Begin with the Adagio and count how long the oboe holds the floor before anything interrupts. Then take the finale at speed. Chamber-sized orchestras suit it best; a hundred players make it sound heavier than the writing wants. Compare it with Mendelssohn's Italian Symphony for the same combination of youth and polish."],"tchaikovsky-symphony-no-1-winter-daydreams":["Four movements, three quarters of an hour. Tchaikovsky gave the first two titles: Dreams of a Winter Journey, and Land of Desolation, Land of Mists. The opening is all thin, cold air — flute and bassoon in octaves over shivering strings — before a bigger, warmer second theme arrives. The slow movement builds from muted strings to a broad oboe song. A light scherzo turns into a waltz for its trio, and the finale sets a Russian folk tune against a fugal battle and a blazing G major coda.","He was twenty-six, newly installed as a theory teacher at the young Moscow Conservatoire, and this was his first large orchestral work after leaving St Petersburg. It nearly broke him: he wrote through the night, stopped sleeping, suffered hallucinations and was told by a doctor he had come close to insanity. His old teachers Anton Rubinstein and Nikolai Zaremba rejected the score; Nikolai Rubinstein eventually conducted the complete symphony in Moscow in 1868.","Listen closer: the slow movement's main melody is borrowed from Tchaikovsky's own discarded overture The Storm, and the finale's folk song, The Gardens Bloomed, is stated bare by the low strings before the fugue picks it apart. In the trio of the scherzo he quotes a waltz from a piano sonata written two years earlier — the first appearance of the ballroom triple time that would run through his whole output.","The two movements Rubinstein allowed in 1867 went unnoticed; the full premiere was warmly received, and Tchaikovsky remained fonder of the piece than of much that followed, calling it a sin of his sweet youth. It is still the least played of his symphonies, usually on the grounds that the finale's rhetoric outruns its material. The 1874 revision, now standard, tightens the first movement considerably.","Start with the first movement and listen for how little Tchaikovsky puts in the middle of the texture — the cold comes from the empty space between flute and bass. Then jump to the scherzo's waltz. Pair it with the Serenade for Strings to hear the same lyrical gift before the fatalism set in."],"tchaikovsky-symphony-no-2-little-russian":["The shortest and sunniest of the six, about thirty-three minutes. A solo horn opens with a Ukrainian version of the song Down by Mother Volga, unaccompanied, and the whole first movement grows around it. The second movement is a wedding march lifted from a discarded opera. The scherzo skitters. The finale takes the folk tune The Crane and runs it through a set of ever more brilliant variations, stopping dead once for a huge gong stroke before the final sprint.","Tchaikovsky wrote most of it in the summer of 1872 while staying with his sister at Kamenka in Ukraine, then still called Little Russia, where he collected songs from the household staff and local peasants. The Balakirev circle in St Petersburg, usually hostile to Conservatoire-trained composers, adored it — Rimsky-Korsakov's set had been arguing for exactly this kind of folk-based symphony. Nikolai Rubinstein conducted the Moscow premiere in 1873 to real success.","Listen closer: the finale's variations on The Crane are the clearest thing Tchaikovsky ever wrote in the manner of Glinka's Kamarinskaya — a short tune repeated unchanged while the orchestra around it changes completely. The nickname was not his: the critic Nikolai Kashkin coined it. And the second movement's march began life in his destroyed opera Undina, one of several rescues from that score.","Success did not satisfy him. In 1879 he threw away the entire first movement and wrote a new one, shorter and tauter, and cut the finale; that revision is what orchestras play. A few conductors prefer the 1872 original for its greater expansiveness, and recordings of both exist. Critics have generally ranked it below the late three while conceding that the finale is the most purely entertaining thing he wrote for orchestra.","Go to the finale first and wait for the gong — a single enormous stroke that halts the music mid-flight. Then return to the opening horn solo and notice how the same handful of notes carries the movement. Try it beside Borodin's Second Symphony, written in the same years out of the same folk impulse."],"tchaikovsky-manfred-symphony":["Nearly an hour, in four movements, after Byron's dramatic poem. Manfred's theme — a dark, sagging phrase for bassoons and bass clarinet — opens the work and returns in each movement. A shimmering scherzo depicts the Alpine fairy appearing in a waterfall's spray; a pastorale gives the mountain shepherds a long oboe tune; the finale plunges into the caverns of the underworld with a fugue, a bacchanal, and finally an organ chord over which Manfred dies absolved. The largest orchestra Tchaikovsky ever assembled.","The programme was not his. Vladimir Stasov had drafted it for Berlioz, who declined; Balakirev then pressed it on Tchaikovsky for years, sending detailed instructions about keys and moods. Tchaikovsky resisted, then in 1885 gave in and worked at it obsessively at his new house at Maidanovo, complaining that it was costing him more effort than anything he had written. Max Erdmannsdörfer conducted the Moscow premiere the following spring.","Listen closer: Balakirev's letters specified a first movement in F sharp minor and an ending with the Dies irae; Tchaikovsky kept the plainchant and changed the key. The scherzo is scored so lightly — divided muted strings, harp, high woodwind — that the waterfall really does sound like spray. And Manfred's own theme appears in the finale played by the organ, an instrument Tchaikovsky used nowhere else.","He swung wildly about it, first calling it the best thing he had done, later proposing to destroy every movement but the first and salvage the rest as a symphonic poem. Toscanini and later Muti and Jansons made the case for it whole; others still cut the finale's coda. Its awkward position — programme symphony written after Tchaikovsky claimed to have outgrown them — keeps it on the edge of the repertoire.","The scherzo is the way in: four minutes of the lightest orchestral writing of the 1880s. Then take the first movement whole and follow the sagging bassoon phrase wherever it resurfaces. A conductor unafraid of the finale's organ, and a hall that has one, makes a real difference."],"tchaikovsky-symphony-no-5":["Four movements, about three quarters of an hour. Two clarinets in their lowest register state a slow, trudging motto at the very start; it returns in every movement, each time in a different guise. The Andante cantabile carries one of the most famous horn solos ever written, twice interrupted by a shattering intrusion of the motto. A waltz stands where a scherzo should be. The finale turns the motto into a major-key march and drives it to a hammering, much-argued-over coda.","Written in a single summer at Frolovskoye, ten years after the Fourth, during a period when Tchaikovsky feared he was written out. He had just returned from his first international conducting tour and was newly conscious of a European audience. In his notebook he sketched a private programme for the first movement — complete resignation before Fate — though he never published it and later spoke of the symphony's meaning only in the vaguest terms.","Listen closer: the motto is not a fate hammer-blow but a slow funeral-march tread, and its transformation into the finale's major-key march is achieved almost entirely by changing the harmony beneath the same notes. In the Andante, the two intrusions land at exactly the point where the melody has climbed highest, which is why they feel less like interruptions than like something the tune has summoned.","Tchaikovsky conducted the St Petersburg premiere himself and afterwards decided the piece was insincere, a mass of colour without genuine feeling, and that audiences sensed the falsity. A Hamburg performance changed his mind. Critics have argued ever since about whether the triumphant finale is earned or willed; Soviet-era readings took it at face value, while conductors from Mravinsky onwards have found ways to make its brightness sound strained.","Listen for the horn solo in the second movement, then go back to the opening clarinets and hear the same notes creeping in underneath everything. The waltz is the palate cleanser. Compare Mravinsky's Leningrad recording, hard and fast, with a broader modern one to hear how differently the finale can be argued."],"balakirev-symphony-no-1":["Four movements lasting about three quarters of an hour, built almost entirely from folk-derived material. A brooding slow introduction gives way to an allegro whose main theme keeps circling back on itself in the manner of a Russian song. The scherzo is fleet and light-footed; the Andante opens with a long clarinet melody over harp, one of the most beautiful pages in Russian orchestral music. The finale takes two folk tunes and whips them into a dancing, brass-crowned close.","Balakirev began it in 1864 as the natural leader of the group later called the Mighty Handful, then abandoned it. A nervous collapse in 1871 drove him out of music altogether and into a railway clerk's job; he returned slowly in the 1880s and finished the symphony only in 1897, more than thirty years after starting. By then his pupils Rimsky-Korsakov, Borodin and Mussorgsky had long since overtaken him, and his style had scarcely moved.","Listen closer: the four-note figure of the slow introduction is the seed of nearly every theme in the work, including the finale's dance — a Lisztian technique Balakirev had absorbed decades earlier. The Andante's clarinet melody is not a genuine folk song but Balakirev's own invention in folk manner, a distinction his colleagues took very seriously and audiences almost never notice.","The 1898 premiere, conducted by the composer, impressed the Petersburg circle but the work arrived too late to influence anyone. Beecham took it up in England and made a famous recording, arguing that it was the finest Russian symphony before Tchaikovsky's last. Most later critics have thought that overstated while agreeing that the slow movement is superb; the symphony survives mainly through advocacy rather than habit.","Start with the Andante and give it the full ten minutes — it needs the space. Then go to the finale for the sheer sound of a Russian orchestra at full tilt. Beecham's recording remains the most persuasive; Svetlanov's is heavier and more Russian in colour, and worth hearing beside it."],"glazunov-symphony-no-5":["Four movements, a little over half an hour, and the most immediately likeable of Glazunov's eight. A grand unison call opens the first movement before a genial, spacious allegro. The scherzo is the famous movement: pizzicato strings, triangle and flutes moving at a glittering trot, closer to ballet than to symphony. The Andante spins a long, warm melody for strings and horn. The finale is a broad Russian dance that gathers weight until the opening call returns to crown it.","Glazunov was thirty, already professor-material in St Petersburg and the bridge between Balakirev's nationalists and the Conservatoire he would later direct. He dedicated the score to Sergei Taneyev, Tchaikovsky's most rigorous pupil — a deliberate gesture across the old factional divide. These were the years just before his ballets Raymonda and The Seasons, and the symphony's scherzo and finale plainly belong to the same imagination.","Listen closer: the opening unison call is not a theme so much as a frame — it returns transformed at the very end of the finale, closing the symphony on the gesture that opened it. In the scherzo, Glazunov keeps the whole string section plucking for minutes at a time so that a single bowed entry sounds like a change of lighting. The orchestration is flawless and almost never doubles for safety.","Contemporaries treated it as proof that Russia now had a symphonist with real technique; later critics, especially after 1917, used the same technique against him, calling the music well made and short of urgency. The scherzo escaped as an encore piece. Recent advocacy from conductors such as Serebrier and Järvi has restored the whole set of eight to record, if not yet to the concert hall.","Play the scherzo first — three minutes that explain why Glazunov's ballets work. Then hear the finale for the swing of Russian dance rhythm handled by someone with Conservatoire discipline. Compare it with Borodin's Second, which Glazunov himself helped reconstruct from memory."],"rachmaninoff-symphony-no-2":["An hour of music in four movements. It begins with a slow, dark motto in the low strings that seeds almost every later theme. The scherzo drives hard, then stops for a fugal middle section and a snarling brass version of the plainchant Dies irae. The Adagio is the heart: a clarinet unwinds a melody nearly three minutes long over hushed strings before the whole orchestra takes it up. The finale is a blazing march that keeps opening out into further song.","Rachmaninoff's First Symphony had been destroyed at its 1897 premiere — Glazunov conducting, reportedly drunk, César Cui comparing it to a depiction of the plagues of Egypt — and the failure cost him three years of composing. A decade on, having found his feet as a conductor at the Bolshoi, he took his family to Dresden and worked in deliberate obscurity for two years. He conducted the St Petersburg premiere himself and it won the Glinka Prize.","Listen closer: the motto's shape is a slow chant-like curve, and the Dies irae fragment that erupts in the scherzo is a cousin of it, so the symphony's brightest and darkest ideas are versions of the same line. The Adagio's clarinet melody almost never repeats a phrase; it simply keeps going, which is why it feels endless in the good sense and why it is so hard to accompany.","For decades conductors performed it with large cuts sanctioned by the composer, sometimes losing a quarter of the score, on the assumption that audiences would not sit still for an hour. André Previn's uncut recordings in the 1970s largely ended the practice and the complete version is now standard. Old complaints of formlessness have quietened; the argument now is over tempo in the Adagio, where too slow a reading stalls.","Start with the Adagio and simply follow the clarinet without expecting a tune to come round again. Then hear the scherzo's opening for contrast. Choose an uncut recording; and if you know the 1970s ballad Never Gonna Fall in Love Again, you already know the melody it borrowed."],"rachmaninoff-symphonic-dances":["Three movements, thirty-five minutes, and the last music Rachmaninoff wrote. The first is a hard-edged march that stops halfway for an alto saxophone — an instrument he had never used before — to sing a long, lonely tune. The second is a waltz that keeps being derailed by muted brass, danced as if in a haunted ballroom. The finale is a fight between the Dies irae plainchant and a Russian Orthodox chant, ending with the word Alleluia written into the score.","Written in a rented house at Huntington on Long Island in the summer of 1940, with Europe at war and Rachmaninoff nearly seventy, a celebrated pianist who had composed almost nothing for years. He dedicated it to Eugene Ormandy and the Philadelphia Orchestra, the ensemble whose sound he loved most, and worked with the arranger Robert Russell Bennett on the saxophone writing. He had originally thought of it as a ballet score for Fokine, who died before anything came of it.","Listen closer: at the close of the first movement the strings quietly play the main theme of his First Symphony, the piece booed off in 1897 and never performed again in his lifetime. Nobody in the audience could have recognised it. In the finale, the chant he sets against the Dies irae is Blessed art Thou, O Lord from his own All-Night Vigil, and beside the final chord he wrote Alleluia and I thank Thee, Lord.","The 1941 premiere was politely received and the piece took thirty years to be recognised as his finest orchestral score. Critics now read it as a deliberate summing-up, a private accounting with his Russian past, though some resist the biographical reading and hear simply a superbly professional dance suite. The saxophone solo has become a standard audition excerpt.","Listen for the saxophone about four minutes into the first movement, then for the quiet string phrase that ends it. Ormandy's Philadelphia recording carries the composer's blessing; Ashkenazy and Jansons find more menace in the waltz. Hear it alongside the All-Night Vigil and the chant connection becomes obvious."],"elgar-symphony-no-2":["Four movements, just under an hour, opening with a surging theme that seems already in mid-flight. The Larghetto is a funeral march in all but name, with a passage for solo horn and strings that repeatedly climbs and falls back. The Rondo is fast and increasingly frantic, culminating in a passage where the timpani hammer a single rhythm through the whole orchestra. The finale does not end in triumph but withdraws, the opening theme returning softly and dissolving.","Elgar headed the score with Shelley's line Rarely, rarely, comest thou, Spirit of Delight, and dedicated it to the memory of King Edward VII, who had died in 1910 while the symphony was in progress. He was at the peak of his official standing and privately miserable, mourning both the King and his friend Alfred Rodewald, and unsettled by the Edwardian world visibly ending around him. He conducted the London premiere himself.","Listen closer: Elgar said the hammering rhythm in the Rondo came from a memory of a delirious noise in his head, and asked that it sound like a mind unable to stop. In the Larghetto the funeral tread is set against a rising figure borrowed from the first movement, so the mourning music and the Spirit of Delight are made of the same material. The score also hides a dedication to an unnamed friend Elgar never identified.","The premiere audience sat on its hands — Elgar reportedly turned to the leader and asked what was the matter with them, saying they sat there like a lot of stuffed pigs. It was for years the less loved of his two symphonies, until Adrian Boult and later Barbirolli argued that its refusal to end in glory was the whole point. It is now often ranked above the First.","Begin with the Larghetto, which stands on its own as a lament. Then take the finale and notice that the volume drops, rather than rising, in the last three minutes. Boult's several recordings are the classic advocacy; Elgar's own 1927 discs are fast and unsentimental, and change how the piece sounds."],"vaughan-williams-symphony-no-6":["Four movements played without a break, barely half an hour. The first erupts immediately in grinding dissonance, then finds a broad tune for the strings. The slow movement is dominated by a three-note rhythm repeated on trumpets and drums, over and over, growing enormous. The scherzo is a violent fugue with a leering tenor saxophone tune at its centre. Then the Epilogue: twelve minutes of quiet counterpoint marked never to rise above the softest dynamic, no crescendo anywhere, ending on an unresolved chord.","Vaughan Williams was in his mid-seventies when he wrote it, immediately after the Second World War and after the serene Fifth Symphony had led people to expect a peaceful old age. Adrian Boult conducted the 1948 premiere with the BBC Symphony Orchestra. Within two years it had been performed around a hundred times, an extraordinary number for a new symphony, and was widely read as a response to the war and the atomic bomb.","Listen closer: the three-note figure that dominates the slow movement is never developed, only repeated at different pitches and volumes, which is why it feels less like a theme than like something mechanical closing in. The tenor saxophone in the scherzo plays a tune Vaughan Williams described as vulgar on purpose. In the Epilogue the strings never once play louder than a whisper, and the final chord contains two keys at the same time.","Critics reached at once for war and nuclear imagery. Vaughan Williams denied all of it, telling one interviewer that he did not know the meaning and quoting Prospero instead: we are such stuff as dreams are made on. He revised the scherzo in 1950. Debate continues over whether the denial should be taken at face value; either way, the Epilogue remains one of the strangest endings any symphony has.","Do not sample the Epilogue on its own — its effect depends on the forty minutes of noise before it. Play the symphony straight through, at a decent volume, and resist reaching for the dial when the last movement starts. Boult's recordings, made with the composer's involvement, remain the reference."],"bax-symphony-no-3":["Three movements and about fifty minutes, densely scored for a large orchestra with harps, celesta and a full brass section. A solo bassoon opens alone with a winding, modal tune before the orchestra swells around it. The middle movement is a nocturne of the kind Bax wrote better than anyone in England, with long horn calls over shifting string harmony. The finale begins as a march, works itself into violence, and then subsides into an Epilogue of extraordinary calm, the music thinning until only a few instruments remain.","Bax wrote it at Morar on the west coast of Scotland, where he retreated most winters to work, drawn by the same northern landscape that had earlier pulled him to Ireland and Norway. He was by then established enough to be performed by Henry Wood, who conducted the 1930 premiere with the BBC Symphony Orchestra. This was the middle of a run of seven symphonies written between 1922 and 1939, none of which held the repertoire.","Listen closer: the Epilogue is in C major, but Bax spends most of the symphony avoiding any settled key at all, so the arrival feels less like a resolution than like weather clearing. The bassoon tune of the opening reappears, transformed and slowed, in that final section. Bax also keeps the harps busy almost continuously, a habit from his Celtic tone poems that gives the loudest passages an odd inner glitter.","It was his most successful symphony from the start, and Barbirolli's 1943 recording — made in wartime with the Hallé — kept it alive when the rest sank. Critics have long complained that Bax's symphonies are over-scored and short of structural spine; defenders answer that the Third's Epilogue is among the finest slow endings in British music. The Chandos cycle of the 1980s finally made all seven audible.","Go to the Epilogue, the last eight minutes, and then start again from the bassoon at the beginning to hear where the tune came from. Barbirolli's old recording has the most conviction; Handley and Thomson are clearer. Try it beside Sibelius, whom Bax revered, to hear both the debt and the distance."],"tippett-symphony-no-4":["A single continuous movement of about half an hour, though the shapes of a four-movement symphony are buried inside it. It opens and closes with amplified breathing produced by a wind machine, so the whole work is framed as one life drawing breath and losing it. Between those breaths the orchestra runs through fast fugal music, a slow section of glinting high woodwind and harp, a scherzo of tumbling brass, and long lyrical spans for strings that keep being interrupted.","The Chicago Symphony Orchestra commissioned it for Georg Solti, who gave the first performance in 1977. Tippett was in his seventies and freshly interested in American orchestras, whose brass and percussion he wrote for lavishly after visits in the early 1970s. He described the piece as a birth-to-death cycle, a subject he had circled in his operas, and he wanted the physical sound of breathing rather than a metaphor for it.","Listen closer: the breathing is genuinely hard to produce, and Tippett authorised various solutions — a wind machine, a synthesizer, a heavy-breathing recording — none of which fully satisfied him. Structurally the piece is a set of variations on the intervals of the opening horn call, so the fast, slow and dancing sections are transformations of one small idea, arranged so that the returns come faster and faster as the work nears its end.","Chicago critics found the breathing effect literal-minded; British reviewers were warmer, hearing a late flowering of Tippett's fractured lyricism. Solti recorded it soon after. The score's difficulty — very exposed brass, awkward string figuration — has kept performances rare, and arguments persist over whether the breath device is a stroke of directness or a gimmick attached to otherwise abstract music.","Listen twice. The first time, follow the breathing and let the rest wash past; the second time, ignore it and track the horn call's returns. Solti's Chicago recording is the authorised reading; Hickox is more lyrical. Compare it with Tippett's Child of Our Time to hear how far his language travelled in thirty-five years."],"nielsen-symphony-no-4-inextinguishable":["Four sections played without a break, about thirty-six minutes. It opens with everything at once — a violent clatter of competing keys — before a clarinet-led theme establishes some order. A gentle intermezzo for woodwind follows, then a long string lament. In the finale two sets of timpani, placed at opposite sides of the platform, hurl thunderclaps at each other and at the orchestra while the strings fight to hold their tune. The clarinet theme returns at the very end and wins.","Written in Copenhagen during 1915 and 1916 with the First World War two borders away and Denmark anxiously neutral. Nielsen wrote a preface explaining the title: the work should express what he called the elemental will to live, music being life and, like it, inextinguishable. He was fifty, well established as a Danish institution, and about to enter the most experimental decade of his career. He conducted the premiere himself.","Listen closer: the very first bars stack two keys against each other, and the whole symphony is a slow argument for one of them, E major, which only fully arrives in the last minute. The two timpanists are instructed to play in different keys, so the duel is a genuine harmonic fight rather than mere noise. Nielsen also asks the strings in the slow section to play with no vibrato at first, which drains the colour out deliberately.","Danish audiences took to it immediately; abroad it waited decades. Nielsen's international breakthrough came only in the 1950s and 1960s, when British and American conductors — Martinon, Bernstein, Horenstein — discovered that his symphonies solved problems Sibelius had solved differently. Modern debate centres on the timpani placement, which many halls cannot manage, and on how literally to take the programmatic preface.","Listen for the moment the second timpanist enters in the finale; if the recording is any good you should hear the two drums coming from different places. Start the work from the very first bar without reading anything — the opening is the point. Blomstedt's San Francisco cycle is a good modern entry."],"nielsen-symphony-no-5":["Two movements only, thirty-five minutes. The first begins with violas oscillating between two notes, apparently going nowhere, until a bassoon and then a clarinet lay a melody over the top. A side drum enters and grows; at the climax it is told to improvise freely, as if determined to stop the orchestra playing at all costs, drowning a serene clarinet solo. The second movement is a fast allegro that breaks into two fugues, one slow and one furious, before a broad affirming close.","Nielsen finished it in 1922 in a Denmark that had come through the war intact but shaken, and he was explicit that the symphony was about the fight between order and the forces that destroy it. He was by then fifty-seven, conducting and teaching in Copenhagen, in poor health and increasingly radical in his musical language. He conducted the first performance himself, in Copenhagen, to a puzzled reception.","Listen closer: the side drum's instruction is written as prose in the score rather than as notes, one of the earliest passages of authorised improvisation in a symphony. The oscillating violas at the start have no melody, no key and no direction — Nielsen is deliberately starting from nothing. And the clarinet that survives the drum attack later returns, unaccompanied and calm, as if nothing had happened.","The Danish premiere confused people; the turning point came at the 1950 Edinburgh Festival, where Erik Tuxen's performance with the Danish State Radio Orchestra astonished British critics and effectively launched Nielsen abroad. Since then it has been regarded as his finest work. Arguments continue about how loud and how anarchic the drummer should be, and whether the second movement's final pages resolve the first movement's crisis or merely change the subject.","Everything hangs on the side drum, so listen for its first quiet entry and follow it until it takes over. Then hear the clarinet that comes after. Tuxen's historic Edinburgh performance survives on record; Blomstedt and Vänskä offer modern clarity. Play it after the Fourth to hear the same fight fought with sharper weapons."],"stenhammar-symphony-no-2":["Four movements, some forty-eight minutes, scored for an ordinary orchestra with no extra colour instruments — deliberately austere after the lush First. The opening movement is built on a stern, modal theme in the strings that keeps turning back on itself. A quick scherzo dances in an off-kilter meter drawn from Swedish folk fiddling. The Adagio is long, dark and hymnlike. The finale is a large double fugue, unfashionably strict for 1915, which gathers all the earlier themes into its argument.","Stenhammar was the leading Swedish musician of his generation, conductor of the Gothenburg orchestra and a formidable pianist. Hearing Sibelius's Second Symphony had shamed him: he withdrew his own First and spent years studying Renaissance counterpoint with Andreas Hallén before daring to write another. He worked on this one from 1911 to 1915 and conducted the Gothenburg premiere himself, with Europe at war and Sweden neutral.","Listen closer: the finale's fugue subject is a version of the very first theme of the symphony, so the strictest music in the piece turns out to be the plainest. The scherzo's limping rhythm comes from the polska, a Swedish dance in three whose second beat is stretched — folk material handled without a single quoted tune. Stenhammar also avoids the harp and the tuba entirely, keeping the sound lean.","Swedish critics recognised it at once as a national landmark; outside Scandinavia it went almost unheard until recordings by Neeme Järvi and Herbert Blomstedt in the 1980s and 1990s. The usual complaint is that the fugal finale is more admirable than exciting. Its defenders point out that the counterpoint is what makes the last pages land, and that Stenhammar's restraint is the opposite of a limitation.","Start with the scherzo for the folk limp, then take the finale whole and follow the fugue theme as the brass take it over. Blomstedt's Gothenburg recording is the natural first choice. Play it beside Sibelius's Second, the piece that provoked it, and hear a very different Nordic answer."],"william-schuman-symphony-no-3":["Two parts of two sections each, half an hour: Passacaglia and Fugue, then Chorale and Toccata. The Passacaglia begins with a bare viola line that is answered by each string section in turn, a half step higher every time, building a wall of sound out of one idea. The Fugue is brassy and driving. The Chorale gives a long trumpet melody over strings; the Toccata starts with an unaccompanied snare drum and ends with the whole orchestra hammering in unison.","Schuman was thirty-one, teaching at Sarah Lawrence College, and had been taken up by Serge Koussevitzky, who conducted the Boston Symphony premiere in 1941 — months before America entered the war. The Baroque titles were a deliberate provocation: Schuman wanted to show that American music could use old forms without sounding European. The piece won the first New York Music Critics' Circle Award and made his reputation almost overnight.","Listen closer: the entire symphony grows from the seven-note viola theme of the opening. It is inverted for the Fugue, slowed and harmonised for the Chorale, and chopped into rhythmic fragments for the Toccata, so four apparently different movements are one idea seen from four angles. Schuman also keeps whole families of instruments together — strings alone, then brass alone — which gives the orchestra its characteristic hard-edged blocks of colour.","Critics greeted it as the arrival of an American symphonist who owed nothing to Copland's folk manner. Bernstein championed it for decades and recorded it twice. Later assessments have questioned whether the relentless rhythmic drive sustains half an hour, and Schuman's ten symphonies have largely fallen out of the repertoire; the Third remains the exception and is still the piece his reputation rests on.","Listen for the opening violas and then count the entries as each section climbs higher — the effect is architectural. Skip to the Toccata's snare drum opening for contrast. Bernstein's New York recording has the necessary aggression; compare it with Roy Harris's Third Symphony, written four years earlier on similar ambitions."],"sessions-symphony-no-2":["Four movements, under half an hour, densely contrapuntal and constantly changing meter. The opening is nervous and quick, its lines overlapping so that no single tune stands clear for long. A short scherzo follows. The slow movement is the emotional centre — a long, grieving span for strings and woodwind that builds to a sustained climax and then falls away. The finale is fast, hard and brilliantly scored, ending abruptly rather than in any gesture of triumph.","Sessions worked on it from 1944 to 1946, teaching at Berkeley and increasingly regarded as the intellectual conscience of American music — the composer other composers read. He dedicated it to the memory of Franklin Roosevelt, who died in April 1945 while the symphony was under way. Pierre Monteux conducted the San Francisco premiere in 1947. Sessions had spent the 1920s and 1930s in Europe absorbing Stravinsky and Schoenberg without adopting either wholesale.","Listen closer: the slow movement is written as a long, unbroken melodic line handed from instrument to instrument, so no single player ever seems to be singing it — a technique Sessions took from Schoenberg but used for grief rather than for colour. The meter changes almost bar by bar throughout the outer movements, which is why the music feels propulsive without ever settling into a groove.","It won the New York Music Critics' Circle Award and is generally counted his best symphony, though Sessions never acquired a public. The standing complaint — that his music is admirable, difficult and forbidding — has stuck since the 1950s, and his nine symphonies are performed far less often than his influence as a teacher of Babbitt, Carter's contemporaries and dozens of others would suggest. Recordings by Prausnitz and Dixon have kept it available.","Go to the slow movement and follow the melody as it is passed between instruments rather than trying to hold onto any one line. Then take the finale for its sheer energy. It rewards repeated listening more than most; do not expect a tune to stick on first acquaintance."],"diamond-symphony-no-4":["Three movements and barely a quarter of an hour, the most concise American symphony of its decade. The first movement is buoyant and open-hearted, with clean diatonic lines and a rhythmic snap that comes from the same world as Copland's ballets. The second is a slow, spare Adagio for strings, largely two-part writing, whose austerity is the point. The finale is short, bright and rhythmically playful, ending almost before it has settled — no peroration, no summing up.","Diamond wrote it in 1945 as the war ended, one of a group of young American composers Koussevitzky and Bernstein were actively promoting. Bernstein conducted the Boston Symphony premiere in 1948. Diamond had studied with Nadia Boulanger in Paris in the 1930s alongside a generation of Americans, and her insistence on clarity of line is audible in every bar. He was still in his early thirties and at the height of his standing.","Listen closer: the Adagio is written mostly in two voices, with the harmony implied rather than filled in, so the ear supplies chords that Diamond never actually plays. In the outer movements he shifts the accent within the bar constantly, producing a lilt that sounds like jazz syncopation but is written out as changing meter. Nothing in the score is doubled for safety, which is why it sounds so transparent.","The premiere was well received and Bernstein recorded it, but Diamond's tonal idiom fell badly out of favour in the 1950s and 1960s as academic serialism took hold; he spent years in Italy and watched his eleven symphonies disappear. Gerard Schwarz's Seattle recordings in the 1990s brought them back, and critics reassessed the Fourth as an unusually well-proportioned piece rather than a conservative one.","It is short enough to hear twice in a sitting, which is the best way in. Listen to the Adagio for how much Diamond leaves out. Then compare it with Copland's Appalachian Spring for the shared American openness and with Barber's Adagio for the difference between two kinds of restraint."],"bernstein-symphony-no-3-kaddish":["Forty minutes in three large movements for speaker, soprano soloist, mixed chorus, boys' choir and a very big orchestra. The Aramaic Kaddish — the Jewish prayer for the dead, which never mentions death — is sung three times, in three settings, from a frantic twelve-note choral outburst to a lullaby of great tenderness. Between them a narrator speaks, in English, addressing God directly: pleading, accusing, comforting Him, and finally negotiating a truce. The boys' choir enters near the end and changes the whole colour of the piece.","Bernstein was writing it when John F. Kennedy was assassinated in November 1963 and dedicated the finished score to his memory. The Israel Philharmonic gave the premiere in Tel Aviv the following month with the composer conducting. He wrote the spoken text himself, in the same years he was arguing publicly about whether tonality had a future, and the symphony's collision of twelve-note writing with unashamed tunefulness is that argument staged.","Listen closer: the twelve-note row that opens the work is gradually bent towards tonality as the piece proceeds, so the theological argument and the musical one resolve together. The lullaby setting of the Kaddish for soprano is in the same key that the narrator's final speech settles into. Bernstein was so uneasy about his own text that he cut it heavily in 1977, and later authorised Samuel Pisar, a Holocaust survivor, to write a replacement.","Reviews were harsh from the start and remain divided: the music is widely admired, the narration widely disliked as self-dramatising, with Bernstein's own crisis of faith placed uncomfortably in the foreground. Some conductors now use Pisar's text, others Bernstein's revision, others commission new words. The purely musical case for the piece — the choral writing especially — has grown steadily stronger as the debate about the speaker has continued.","Start with the soprano's lullaby setting of the Kaddish, which needs no context at all, then hear the whole work once to understand what surrounds it. Try more than one narrator: the difference between readings is enormous. Compare it with Chichester Psalms, written two years later, for Bernstein at peace instead of at war."],"honegger-symphony-no-3-liturgique":["Three movements of about ten minutes each, titled from the Latin Mass for the dead: Dies irae, De profundis clamavi, Dona nobis pacem. The first is a violent, grinding assault with the whole orchestra at maximum pressure. The second is a slow lament that rises to one enormous outcry and subsides. The third begins as a mechanical march that gets heavier and more brutal until it collapses; then, out of the wreckage, a solo flute and high strings play a slow rising figure, and the symphony ends in radiant quiet.","Honegger spent the war in occupied Paris, refusing to leave, and emerged exhausted and ill. Paul Sacher commissioned the symphony for his Zurich orchestra and Charles Munch conducted the 1946 premiere. Honegger, a Swiss national who had made his career in France, wrote that the work depicted modern humanity crushed between blind mechanised forces and the longing for peace — an unusually explicit programme from a composer associated with the anti-Romantic Les Six.","Listen closer: the march in the finale is a deliberate machine, built from a rhythm that never varies while the orchestration thickens around it — the composer of Pacific 231 turning his locomotive into something far more sinister. The bird-like flute figure that follows is the same shape as the Dies irae theme from the first movement, turned upside down and slowed, so peace is made from the material of terror.","Munch made it famous, and Karajan's 1969 recording with the Berlin Philharmonic gave it an unlikely afterlife in Germany. Critics who admired Honegger's inter-war astringency found the wartime symphonies too rhetorical; others regard the Third as the strongest French symphony of the century. The three Latin titles are sometimes read as sincerely religious and sometimes as a bitter borrowing of liturgical language, and Honegger did not settle the question.","Listen for the last four minutes: after the march collapses, wait for the flute. Then go back and hear the Dies irae theme in the first movement to catch the connection. Karajan's recording is the classic; Munch's earlier one is rougher and angrier. Play it after Vaughan Williams's Sixth for two very different post-war endings."],"martinu-symphony-no-6":["Three movements, under half an hour, and not really a symphony at all — Martinů called it Symphonic Fantasies and abandoned regular movement forms. It opens with a strange fluttering in the divided strings, a sound often compared to a swarm of insects, over which fragments of melody drift in and out. The second movement is faster and more angular, with brass chorales cutting across it. The third gathers everything into a long, surging span and ends on a chord that fades rather than resolves.","Martinů had left Czechoslovakia for Paris in 1923 and fled the Nazis to America in 1941; by the early 1950s he was living in New York, unable to return home after the communist takeover, and had recently suffered serious after-effects from a fall. He dedicated the score to Charles Munch, who conducted the Boston Symphony premiere in 1955 — the same partnership that had launched Honegger's Third a decade earlier.","Listen closer: in the second movement Martinů quotes his own opera Julietta, the dream-play he had written in Paris in 1937 and regarded as his most personal work — a memory of an unreachable European life surfacing inside an American commission. The fluttering string figure that opens the symphony is produced by many players moving very fast in slightly different patterns, so no individual line is audible, only the shimmer.","It won the New York Music Critics' Circle Award and is generally reckoned the finest of his six symphonies, all written after he turned fifty. Martinů's reputation has always suffered from sheer volume — some four hundred works — and from being claimed by neither the Czech nor the American tradition. Recent Czech and British recordings have made the case that the Sixth belongs with the major post-war symphonies.","Start at the very beginning and simply listen to the texture for the first two minutes before worrying about themes; the shimmer is the subject. Then listen for the brass chorale in the second movement. Bělohlávek's Czech recordings are idiomatic; Munch's Boston premiere account survives and is worth hearing for its urgency."],"pettersson-symphony-no-7":["One unbroken movement lasting about forty-five minutes, with no repeats, no clear sections and no relief for long stretches. It grows from a few small intervals stated at the start and cycles through wave after wave of dense, dissonant orchestral pressure, the brass and percussion building and collapsing. Then, around three quarters of the way through, the strings begin a slow melody over a walking bass that lasts nearly ten minutes — the one sustained consolation in the piece — before the darkness closes back in.","Pettersson was a Swedish violist from a violent, impoverished Stockholm home who came to composition late and worked in near-total obscurity, increasingly crippled by rheumatoid arthritis that eventually confined him. He wrote seventeen symphonies, most of them long, single-movement and unrelenting. Antal Doráti conducted the Stockholm Philharmonic premiere in 1968 and recorded it, an act of advocacy that made Pettersson internationally known almost overnight in his late fifties.","Listen closer: the long string melody is not new material. It is the same handful of intervals from the opening, finally allowed to unfold at length instead of being cut off, which is why its arrival feels earned rather than sentimental. Pettersson wrote without a piano and often without leaving his flat, and the scoring shows it — the orchestra is treated as one enormous instrument rather than as families of colour.","Doráti's recording caused a sensation in Sweden and divided critics elsewhere, who split between hearing genuine tragic power and hearing forty-five minutes of undifferentiated anguish. The comparison usually reached for is Mahler, though Pettersson refuses Mahler's irony and variety of surface. His symphonies remain rarely programmed, partly for their length and difficulty, and partly because concert planners struggle to place them.","Do not sample it. Set aside the full forty-five minutes and let the pressure accumulate; the string melody only works because of what precedes it. Doráti's premiere recording remains the standard, with Segerstam and Lindberg offering broader modern readings. Play something short and bright afterwards."],"rautavaara-symphony-no-7-angel-of-light":["Four movements, about thirty-five minutes, scored for a large orchestra used mostly for slow-moving, luminous chords rather than for argument. The first movement rises out of near-silence in the low strings and gathers a wide, glowing harmony above it. A quicker second movement brings brass fanfares and pulsing woodwind. The third is the slow centre, a long span of hovering string sound. The finale builds a broad hymnlike climax and then withdraws, ending as quietly as the symphony began.","Rautavaara had moved through serialism in the 1960s and out the other side into an unapologetically tonal, mystical late style. He wrote that angels had preoccupied him for decades — a series of works including Angels and Visitations and Angel of Dusk precede this one — and insisted the angels of his titles were archetypes, vast and impersonal, not the illustrated kind. The symphony was commissioned in the United States and became his most widely played work.","Listen closer: the harmony moves so slowly that the ear starts to hear the orchestra as a single sustained instrument with changing inner colours, a technique closer to Sibelius's Tapiola than to any symphonic model. Rautavaara builds most chords from intervals stacked in fourths rather than thirds, which is why the sound is rich without ever settling into a conventional key.","The Ondine recording with Leif Segerstam and the Helsinki Philharmonic was a commercial success and earned a Grammy nomination, unusual for a contemporary symphony. Critics divided predictably: some heard a late-flowering visionary, others heard mood music, and the phrase spiritual minimalism was applied whether or not it fit. Rautavaara's own view was that he had simply stopped writing music he did not believe in.","Start with the third movement, which asks nothing of you but attention to sound. Then hear the whole work in one sitting, preferably loud enough that the quiet passages are still audible. The Segerstam recording is the one to seek. Compare it with Sibelius's Tapiola for the same Finnish way of making an orchestra glow."],"glass-symphony-no-1-low":["Three movements of about forty minutes, each taking its title and its raw material from a track on David Bowie and Brian Eno's 1977 album Low: Subterraneans, Some Are, and Warszawa. Glass keeps the album's harmonic shapes and slow, brooding moods but rebuilds them as orchestral music — repeating figures in the strings, chords cycling through unexpected keys, brass sustaining long lines above. The result sounds neither like a rock arrangement nor like a conventional symphony but like a third thing built from both.","Glass had known Bowie and Eno since the 1970s, when Low itself was made partly under the influence of the minimalist scene Glass helped create; the borrowing runs in both directions. By 1992 Glass was fifty-five, famous for the opera trilogy and film scores, and beginning a series of symphonies that would eventually run past a dozen. Dennis Russell Davies, his long-term orchestral advocate, conducted the first performance and has recorded most of the cycle.","Listen closer: Glass does not quote Bowie's melodies so much as extract the chord progressions underneath them and let those cycle, so listeners who know the album recognise the harmony before they identify any tune. The third movement, Warszawa, keeps the original's slowly rising bass but stretches it far longer than the song allowed. Glass also uses no percussion battery at all, which is why the orchestra sounds unexpectedly soft-edged.","Classical critics were sniffy about the source material and minimalist critics about the orchestral packaging; audiences liked it and it launched Glass's symphonic career. A second Bowie-derived symphony, Heroes, followed in 1996. The lasting question is whether the piece is a genuine symphony or an extended set of variations wearing the title, a debate Glass has largely declined to enter.","If you know the album, play it first; the pleasure is in hearing what Glass keeps and what he throws away. If you do not, start with the third movement and let the rising bass carry you. Dennis Russell Davies's recordings are the authoritative ones and the Brooklyn Philharmonic original still has a rawness later versions smooth away."],"schnittke-symphony-no-1":["An hour-long assault in four movements that also functions as theatre. The players walk onto the stage individually while the music is already going, improvising as they arrive; a solo violinist and a pianist break into free jazz; a trumpeter wanders off and plays from the back of the hall. Chopin's funeral march, waltzes, Baroque cadences, a marching band, the Dies irae and shreds of Beethoven and Tchaikovsky collide with dense clusters. At the end the orchestra leaves the platform one by one and then returns.","Schnittke was working as a film composer in Moscow, where the studios paid the bills and the concert hall was closed to him. He assembled the symphony from 1969 to 1972 out of the collision between his day job and his ambitions, and called the method polystylism: quotation not as decoration but as subject matter. The Union of Composers blocked a Moscow performance; Gennady Rozhdestvensky conducted the premiere in Gorky, a closed city, in 1974.","Listen closer: the exit and return at the end is lifted from Haydn's Farewell Symphony, but Schnittke reverses the joke — his players come back, and the return is more disturbing than the departure. The improvised jazz sections are genuinely free, so no two performances match. And the constant tuning-up noise at the start is notated: the disorder is composed, which is the whole point of the piece.","Soviet officialdom treated it as a scandal and it went unperformed in Moscow for years. Western critics who met it later split between hearing a savage, funny indictment of a culture drowning in its own quotations and hearing an undisciplined collage. Its influence on Schnittke's own later, tauter music is not in doubt, and the polystylistic method he announced here shaped a generation of Soviet and post-Soviet composers.","Watch it if you can find video; half the effect is visual. Otherwise listen for the entrances at the start and the departures at the end, and let the quotations arrive without trying to catalogue them. Rozhdestvensky's recording carries the authority of the premiere. Follow it with the Concerto Grosso No. 1 to hear the same method under control."],"vivaldi-estro-armonico-op-3-no-10":["Four solo violins and a cello step out of the string band; three movements, about ten minutes. The opening Allegro is built from a cascade, the violins entering one after another with the same tumbling figure until four independent lines are falling at once. The central Largo is the strange part: the four soloists play slow broken chords in four different rhythms, a shimmer with almost no tune in it. The finale returns to the cascade, brisk and blunt.","Vivaldi published the twelve concertos of L'estro armonico in Amsterdam in 1711, using Estienne Roger's engraved plates rather than the older Italian movable type, so clean cheap copies travelled north quickly. He was teaching violin at the Pio Ospedale della Pietà, a Venetian orphanage whose girl musicians drew visitors from across Europe. This was the set that carried Italian ritornello form to Germany and England and made his name well beyond the lagoon.","Bach copied this concerto out and rebuilt it for four harpsichords and strings, moving it down to A minor; that arrangement survives as BWV 1065 and is the clearest evidence we have of what he took from Italy. Listen again to the Largo: the four rhythmic layers are written out exactly, not improvised, which was unusual in 1711. Vivaldi is composing a texture rather than a melody.","Opus 3 was reprinted through the eighteenth century, then Vivaldi disappeared from view for two hundred years and returned in the 1920s and 1930s largely through the Bach connection, when scholars tracing Bach's models found the Italian originals. Stravinsky's jibe that Vivaldi wrote the same concerto six hundred times is still quoted; period-instrument groups since the 1970s have answered it by playing faster, rougher and with far more rhythmic bite.","Start with the first minute and count the entries as each violin joins the falling figure. Then let the Largo blur the pulse. Afterwards play Bach's four-harpsichord version and hear the same music turned into machinery. Period bands bring out the grit; smoother modern-orchestra recordings can make it sound merely decorative."],"vivaldi-four-seasons-autumn":["Three movements, about eleven minutes, each following a sonnet printed above the music. The first is a peasants' harvest dance that keeps stumbling: the solo violin slurs and wobbles as the drinkers get drunk, and then falls asleep. The slow movement is the sleep itself, muted strings holding soft spread chords with the harpsichord rippling underneath and no melody at all. The finale is a hunt, with horn calls in the strings, a fleeing animal and gunshots.","Autumn is the third of the four concertos that open Il cimento dell'armonia e dell'inventione, published in Amsterdam in 1725 and dedicated to the Bohemian count Václav Morzin. Vivaldi had by then spent two decades at the Pietà and was also running opera seasons; descriptive violin writing was a selling point in a crowded print market. The sonnets, probably his own, are printed alongside the parts with letters keying each line to the exact bar.","The drunk in the first movement is composed, not acted: Vivaldi writes deliberately smeared scales and repeated notes that slip out of time, and the soloist is meant to sound unsteady rather than brilliant. In the sleeping Adagio he asks the continuo to arpeggiate quietly throughout, and gives the violins no tune to play, so the whole movement is atmosphere. The finale's opening figure is a horn call transferred to strings.","The Four Seasons were popular in Paris in the 1720s, then forgotten with the rest of Vivaldi until Bernardino Molinari's 1942 recording and, decisively, Louis Kaufman's of 1947. Ubiquity has cost them: they are the most recorded pieces in the repertory and are treated by many listeners as background. Period performances from the 1980s onward, and Max Richter's recomposition, have argued for hearing the violence and oddity again.","Skip to the slow movement first, before the famous outer ones have worn you down; two minutes of unmoving harmony is where the concerto is strangest. Then take the finale and follow the chase. Read the sonnet as you listen. Try a period-instrument version, then Richter's, and hear how much interpretation the piece can carry."],"bach-violin-concerto-e-bwv-1042":["Three movements, about eighteen minutes, for one violin against a small string band. The opening Allegro begins with three bare chords, as if the orchestra were clearing its throat, then runs for nine minutes in a wide arch that returns to its beginning exactly. The Adagio sets a long singing violin line over a short bass pattern that repeats without pause underneath. The finale is a rondo in dance rhythm, the same cheerful tune coming back four times.","Bach probably wrote it during his years at Köthen, roughly 1717 to 1723, where Prince Leopold kept a fine instrumental band and, being Calvinist, wanted no church music. Bach had the violinist Joseph Spiess and a group of skilled players at hand, and no cantata to produce each week. He had also spent years copying and rearranging Vivaldi, and this concerto is where that study turns into something with a different centre of gravity.","The first movement is not the usual chain of ritornellos: it is a large three-part shape, with the whole opening section returning bar for bar after a long middle passage, which is closer to an aria than to a Venetian concerto. In the slow movement the bass figure repeats about twenty times, and the drama is entirely in how the violin keeps refusing to line up with it. Bach later remade the whole work as a harpsichord concerto in D.","Only two solo violin concertos by Bach survive complete, and several harpsichord concertos are widely thought to be arrangements of lost violin originals, so the E major has carried more weight than its length suggests. Nineteenth-century performance made it broad and thick; Joseph Joachim's edition shaped a century of playing. Since the 1970s period bands have taken it faster and lighter, and the argument now is mostly about how much ornament the soloist should add.","Listen for the three chords at the very start, then notice when they come back near the end — that return is the whole design. Go to the Adagio next and follow only the bass, then only the violin. Compare a modern-orchestra reading with a one-player-per-part period version; the difference in weight changes the piece completely."],"handel-concerto-grosso-op-6-no-6":["Five movements for string orchestra with a small group of soloists stepping out of it, about seventeen minutes. A grave opening in dotted rhythms leads to a fugue that starts quietly and thickens. Then comes the heart of the work: a Musette marked Larghetto, nearly eight minutes long, a drone bass under a slow pastoral tune that darkens into the minor and back. Two short quick movements finish, the last a tight dance.","Handel wrote the whole set of twelve concertos in about a month, between late September and the end of October 1739, and John Walsh published them by subscription the next year. Handel needed instrumental music to play between the acts of his oratorios, which were drawing the London audience that had abandoned his Italian operas. He priced the set for domestic players too, and the subscription list runs to hundreds of names, including the royal family.","The Musette is far longer than convention allowed, and Handel knew it; he sometimes performed it on its own, and the autograph shows him working out where to cut. The drone imitates a bagpipe, a rustic sound deliberately dropped into a set that otherwise looks back to Corelli's Roman elegance. Several movements in Opus 6 recycle Handel's own earlier keyboard and organ material, a habit of borrowing he practised openly.","Opus 6 was admired steadily where most Baroque music was forgotten, and Handel's concertos stayed in English amateur repertory throughout the nineteenth century. Twentieth-century performance made them heavy and slow; period-instrument recordings since the 1980s have restored the dance rhythms and the improvised ornament. The unresolved question is size: Handel's own band was modest, but he wrote for oratorio nights in a large theatre, and the concertos take both readings.","Go straight to the Musette and give it the full eight minutes; if that movement holds you, the rest will. Notice the drone under everything and the point where the harmony turns dark. Then hear Corelli's Opus 6 to see what Handel inherited, and a Brandenburg concerto for the contrast in how Bach handles the same forces."],"mozart-violin-concerto-no-5-turkish":["Three movements, about half an hour. The first has a trick built into it: the orchestra sets off briskly, then stops, and the soloist enters not with the expected tune but with a slow dreaming Adagio over throbbing strings before the fast music resumes. The Adagio proper is a long, unhurried song. The finale is a courtly minuet-rondo that is twice interrupted by a stamping minor-key episode with the cellos and basses striking the strings with the wood of the bow.","Mozart was nineteen, concertmaster of the Salzburg court orchestra under Archbishop Colloredo, and wrote all five of his violin concertos within a single year, 1775, probably for himself or for the court's Italian leader Antonio Brunetti. Vienna and Salzburg were in the middle of a fashion for anything Turkish, a taste fed by the Ottoman military bands heard in Habsburg lands, and janissary noise had already reached the theatre and the opera house.","The nickname is misleading: the loud episode in the finale is Hungarian or gypsy in colour rather than Ottoman, and Mozart lifted much of it from his own earlier ballet music. The instruction for the low strings to hit with the wood of the bow is a rare notated effect in 1775. Note too that the slow interlude in the first movement is thematically unrelated to everything around it, a private aria dropped into a public argument.","Brunetti apparently found the original slow movement of another of the concertos difficult, and Mozart wrote replacements, which has left a small tangle of alternative movements across the set. The Fifth has always been the most played of the five. Debate now centres on cadenzas, since Mozart left none: Joachim's romantic ones held the field for a century, and players increasingly write or improvise their own in period style.","Wait for the moment the violin enters in the first movement — the surprise is the slow tempo, not the notes. Then jump to the last two minutes of the finale for the wood-of-the-bow episode. Compare a big modern orchestra with a small period band; at Salzburg forces the soloist stops being a star and becomes the leader of a conversation."],"mozart-clarinet-concerto":["Three movements, about twenty-eight minutes, scored without oboes or trumpets so that nothing competes with the soloist's colour. The first movement runs long and calm, the clarinet slipping between its bright top and its dark low register within a single phrase. The Adagio is one unbroken song, stated, decorated, and stated again. The finale is a rondo whose tune keeps returning, each time with the solo line leaping further across the instrument's range.","Mozart finished it in Vienna in October 1791, weeks before he died, for Anton Stadler, a fellow freemason and clarinettist in the imperial wind band to whom he already owed the clarinet quintet. Stadler played a modified instrument with an extended lower range, built with the maker Theodor Lotz, and Mozart began the work as a concerto for basset horn before rewriting it for that instrument. It was his last completed piece for orchestra.","The autograph is lost, and the version everyone plays is a posthumous printed edition that raised the low notes Stadler's basset clarinet could reach and ordinary clarinets could not. Reconstructions that put those notes back reveal phrases that suddenly plunge an octave lower than expected, which changes the shape of whole passages. Stadler, who owed Mozart money and later pawned scores, may be why the manuscript vanished.","The concerto has never been out of favour, but performance practice has moved sharply. Since the 1980s players such as Antony Pay and Eric Hoeprich have taken up reconstructed basset clarinets, and most serious recordings now at least acknowledge the lost low register. Older accounts favour a warm, even, romantic tone; period instruments give a reedier, more uneven sound with real differences between registers, which is what Mozart was writing for.","Take the Adagio first, and listen for how little happens: one tune, almost no drama, and the whole weight of the piece in it. Then hear the first movement and follow the soloist's leaps between high and low. Try a basset clarinet recording alongside a modern one; the dropped octaves are audible immediately once you know to expect them."],"weber-clarinet-concerto-no-2":["Three movements, about twenty-three minutes, and the brightest of Weber's clarinet works. The first movement is broad and confident, the soloist entering high and staying there, with long chains of rapid arpeggios that sweep across the whole instrument. The middle movement is a Romanza in G minor, an operatic scene in miniature with recitative-like passages where the clarinet seems to speak rather than sing. The finale is a Polacca, a swaggering polonaise full of leaps and trills.","Weber wrote it in Munich in the summer of 1811 for Heinrich Baermann, weeks after the first concerto and the concertino, in a burst of work that came from hearing Baermann play. He was twenty-four, moving between courts after a financial scandal had driven him from Stuttgart, and living on what performances brought in. The clarinet's keywork was still changing rapidly, and Baermann's soft, flexible tone determined what Weber dared to write.","The Romanza is opera smuggled into the concert hall. Weber breaks the melodic line with pauses and unaccompanied phrases that behave exactly like sung recitative, ten years before Der Freischütz made that manner famous. Listen also to how the fast passages sit: they are laid out to fall under the fingers of Baermann's particular instrument, and on a modern clarinet with different keywork some of them are harder than they sound.","Baermann toured both concertos across Europe and made them the core of clarinet repertory. The printed editions carry so many of his own alterations that players must still choose between Weber's text and the tradition of the man he wrote for. Critics have long ranked the second concerto below the first for depth while conceding it is the more brilliant vehicle, and it remains a fixture of competitions and auditions for that reason.","Start with the Polacca finale if you want the fun, then go back to the Romanza and listen for the moments where the clarinet stops singing and starts talking. Compare it with the first concerto's chorale passage to hear the two sides of Weber. A period clarinet recording shows how much of the brilliance comes from the instrument's own unevenness."],"mendelssohn-piano-concerto-no-1":["Three movements running without a break, about twenty minutes, which is short for a Romantic concerto and deliberately so. The orchestra plays seven bars and the piano crashes in; there is no long introduction and no cadenza anywhere. Brass fanfares act as hinges, carrying the music from the stormy first movement into a quiet E major Andante and then, with a second fanfare, into a scampering finale whose fast repeated notes never let up.","Mendelssohn wrote it in Munich in October 1831, near the end of the long European tour his father had insisted on, and played the premiere himself on 17 October with the Munich court orchestra. He was twenty-two and already a public figure. The concerto is dedicated to Delphine von Schauroth, a young Munich pianist he admired, and it was written fast: he complained in letters about being pressed for a new piece and produced this one in a few days of concentrated work.","The linking fanfares are the structural idea. By writing them into the score Mendelssohn removed the applause between movements and turned three pieces into one continuous span, a solution Liszt studied closely before writing his own concertos. Notice also what is missing: no orchestral exposition, no cadenza, no place for the soloist to improvise. The virtuoso is inside the argument rather than standing outside it.","It was a hit immediately and stayed one for decades; Liszt could play it at sight and used it to show off. The twentieth century was harder on Mendelssohn generally, and this concerto was dismissed as brilliant and empty beside the violin concerto. It has recovered slowly, helped by recordings on early pianos where the light, quick action makes the running passages sound like conversation rather than display.","Listen for the trumpet-and-horn fanfare that ends the first movement, then hear the second one arrive before the finale; those two moments explain the design. Then follow the Andante's tune, which is the concerto's only real breathing space. Compare a modern concert grand with an 1830s instrument to hear how much lighter Mendelssohn's textures were meant to be."],"bruch-violin-concerto-no-1":["Three movements, about twenty-five minutes. The first is titled Vorspiel, prelude, and behaves like one: quiet drum roll, woodwind phrase, and the violin answering in free rhapsodic sweeps twice over, then a short fast section that dies away and runs straight into the slow movement without stopping. That Adagio is a long chain of three melodies, one growing out of the next. The finale is a gypsy-flavoured dance in double stops, brilliant and accelerating.","Bruch worked on the concerto for years and was never confident about it. Otto von Königslöw played the first version in Koblenz on 24 April 1866. Bruch then sent the score to Joseph Joachim, who went through it in detail, and the revision Joachim premiered in Bremen on 7 January 1868 is the version played today. Bruch was in his late twenties, holding minor German posts, and the concerto was his first real success.","The opening movement is a prelude that refuses to end: Bruch never gives it a proper close, and its main theme returns at the very end of the movement only to dissolve into the Adagio. He worried this was a formal fault and asked Joachim whether the piece could still be called a concerto; Joachim said yes, and pointed out that Spohr had done something similar. The finale's main theme is Bruch's own invention, not a folk tune.","Bruch sold the manuscript outright and earned almost nothing from the work that made him famous, a fact he complained about for the rest of his life, along with the way visiting violinists asked only ever to play this one piece. Critics have often treated it as sentimental beside Brahms, and its permanent place near the top of popularity polls has counted against it in academic writing. Violinists have never wavered.","Start at the beginning and notice that the violin's first entries are unaccompanied gestures, not a tune — Bruch is warming the instrument up in public. Then let the Adagio run without interruption and hear the three melodies hand over to each other. Follow it with Brahms's concerto, written a decade later for the same violinist, to hear two answers to the same advice."],"saint-saens-cello-concerto-no-1":["One continuous movement of about nineteen minutes, in three linked sections. There is no orchestral introduction at all: a single chord, and the cello is already away, storming up and down in triplets. The middle section is the surprise, a delicate minuet in B-flat with muted strings dancing quietly while the soloist floats a high line above them. The music then returns to the opening material, gathers a new broad melody, and closes fast in A major.","Saint-Saëns wrote it in 1872 for Auguste Tolbecque, a cellist, instrument maker and early collector of old instruments, and it was played at the Paris Conservatoire concerts on 19 January 1873. That was significant: the Conservatoire society was conservative and had resisted living French composers. France was rebuilding its concert life after the defeat of 1870, and Saint-Saëns had just helped found the Société Nationale de Musique to give French instrumental music a platform.","The concerto is built on continuous transformation rather than repetition: the driving triplet figure of the opening keeps reappearing inside the minuet's accompaniment, so the dance is haunted by the storm. Notice that the soloist almost never rests — Saint-Saëns writes an unbroken line for nineteen minutes, which is why cellists treat the work as a stamina test as much as a display piece. The A major ending is reached only in the final bars.","Shostakovich taught it and admired its economy; Rachmaninoff called it one of the finest cello concertos. Casals took it up early and it entered the repertory permanently. Nineteenth-century French critics were divided on whether the single-movement design was daring or merely convenient, and the same argument attaches to Saint-Saëns generally, whose fluency has been read both as mastery and as a way of avoiding difficulty.","Notice that there is no introduction — the cello is playing within a second, which is rare enough to be startling. Then wait for the minuet and listen to the muted strings underneath, playing a dance the soloist is not quite part of. Pair it with Schumann's cello concerto, another continuous single span, and then with Dvořák's for the opposite approach."],"lalo-symphonie-espagnole":["Five movements, about half an hour, a violin concerto that calls itself a symphony. The first movement opens with a hard unison gesture and a soloist who plays in fierce double stops. Then a Scherzando with a habanera swing and plucked strings, a short Intermezzo, a dark Andante, and a Rondo finale whose bouncing tune keeps being flung upward. Spanish rhythms — habanera, seguidilla — run through all five, along with guitar-like plucking in the orchestra.","Lalo, of Spanish descent on his father's side and living in Paris, wrote it in 1874 for Pablo de Sarasate, who premiered it on 7 February 1875 at the Concerts Populaires. Paris was in the middle of a craze for Spanish colour; Bizet's Carmen opened five weeks after the premiere. The Société Nationale had been founded in 1871 to promote French instrumental music, and Lalo, long neglected as a composer, suddenly found himself in demand at fifty-one.","The Intermezzo, the third movement, was cut from performances for nearly a century — Sarasate seems to have dropped it, and printed editions marked it optional — so most nineteenth- and twentieth-century listeners heard a four-movement work. It is now usually restored. Notice too that the title is not a joke: the orchestra carries real symphonic argument between the solo passages rather than merely accompanying, which is why Lalo refused to call it a concerto.","Tchaikovsky heard it in 1878 and wrote to von Meck that it was fresh, light and full of piquant rhythm, and his own violin concerto followed within weeks. Later criticism has been snobbish about the Spanishness, pointing out that Lalo was a Frenchman writing picture-postcard Spain, a charge that also lands on Bizet and Chabrier. Spanish composers themselves, Albéniz among them, took the compliment and returned it.","Begin with the second movement for the habanera lilt and the plucked strings behind the violin. Then hear the finale, which is where the piece grins. Listen for the third movement in whichever recording you choose — many older ones omit it. Play Tchaikovsky's concerto immediately afterwards and the debt is unmistakable."],"tchaikovsky-rococo-variations":["About eighteen minutes for cello and a deliberately small orchestra — pairs of woodwind, two horns, strings, no trumpets or drums. A short introduction, then a neat eighteenth-century tune, then seven variations that grow steadily more elaborate: one in the minor with the cello musing alone, one a slow lament with woodwind answering, and a final variation that turns into a headlong coda. The scoring stays transparent throughout, so the soloist is never fighting for room.","Tchaikovsky wrote it in Moscow late in 1876 for Wilhelm Fitzenhagen, the German cellist who taught at the Conservatory alongside him, and Fitzenhagen gave the premiere on 30 November 1877. This was the period of Tchaikovsky's disastrous marriage and of the beginning of Nadezhda von Meck's patronage. His love of Mozart was lifelong and unfashionable in a Moscow that expected Russian subjects, and here he wrote a homage to the eighteenth century rather than to folk song.","The version everyone plays is not Tchaikovsky's. Fitzenhagen reordered the variations, cut one entirely, and moved the brilliant ending forward; he made the changes in the published score and Tchaikovsky, exhausted and preoccupied, let them stand. The original order was published only in 1941, and a few cellists now perform it. The theme is not a genuine rococo melody but Tchaikovsky's own, written to sound like one.","Fitzenhagen's edition dominated for a century, and the argument about whether to restore Tchaikovsky's text divides cellists still: the original is more shapely, the revision more effective in a hall. Rostropovich recorded the familiar version and defended it. The work has also suffered from being classed as a charming trifle, which underrates how difficult the writing is and how carefully the small orchestra is balanced against the soloist.","Listen first for the theme, plain and short, and then count how far each variation travels from it. The slow minor-key variation near the end is where the piece stops being a game. Check the sleeve notes to see which edition you are hearing, and if you can, find a recording of Tchaikovsky's original ordering to compare the two endings."],"bruch-scottish-fantasy":["Four movements with a slow introduction, about half an hour, and a harp given a soloist's prominence beside the violin. The introduction is dark and processional, brass and low strings suggesting a ruin; the violin enters as a lament. Then a lively dance movement with drone harmonies imitating bagpipes, a broad slow movement, and a finale marked in the manner of a war march. Each movement takes an existing Scottish folk tune and sets it rather than developing it.","Bruch composed it in Berlin in the winter of 1879 to 1880, at a time when he was steeped in printed collections of national melodies, which he mined for many works. He dedicated it to Pablo de Sarasate, who played it in Hamburg and made it his own. Bruch had never been to Scotland. He was working from books, in a Europe where Ossian, Walter Scott and the vogue for Highland romance had made Scotland an imaginative territory for German composers.","The tunes are identifiable: Auld Rob Morris in the first movement, The Dusty Miller for the dance, I'm a' doun for lack o' Johnnie in the slow movement, and Scots Wha Hae, the Bannockburn song, in the finale, where Bruch scores it as a march. The harp is not decoration but a stand-in for the clàrsach, and Bruch keeps returning the violin to open-string drones so it can imitate a fiddle rather than a concerto soloist.","Bruch resented being known for a handful of works and for borrowed melodies, and critics have used the Fantasy to argue that his gift was arrangement rather than invention. It nevertheless survived on the strength of great performances — Heifetz's 1947 and 1961 recordings above all, which established its tempos and its glitter for everyone since. Scottish musicians have generally regarded it with amused tolerance rather than offence.","Go to the second movement first for the bagpipe drones and the fiddle rhythm. Then take the finale and listen for Scots Wha Hae arriving in the brass. Notice the harp throughout; if you cannot hear it clearly, the recording balance is wrong. Heifetz is the reference, but slower modern readings give the folk tunes more room to breathe."],"dvorak-violin-concerto":["Three movements, about thirty-two minutes. The orchestra states four bars, the violin answers immediately, and the exchange sets the tone for a first movement that is compressed rather than expansive — it never makes a full return to its opening but slides directly into the slow movement. That Adagio in F is the emotional centre, a long lyrical span with horn calls. The finale is a furiant, a Czech dance in cross-rhythm, with a dumka-like slower episode in the middle.","Dvořák wrote it in 1879 at the suggestion of the publisher Simrock, for Joseph Joachim, who had championed Brahms's concerto the year before. Joachim asked for changes; Dvořák revised in 1880 and again in 1882, cutting and reworking the first movement. Joachim never played it in public. František Ondříček gave the premiere in Prague on 14 October 1883. Dvořák was newly famous outside Bohemia after the Slavonic Dances, and Brahms was his advocate at every stage.","The join between the first and second movements is the point of contention: Dvořák removed the orchestral recapitulation so that the music runs straight through, and Joachim, a classicist, disliked exactly that. Listen for how the first movement simply stops arguing and opens into the Adagio. The finale's cross-rhythm is a genuine furiant, in which the beat groups shift from three to two while the metre stays put — the same device runs through the Slavonic Dances.","Joachim's silence hurt the work's early reputation and it has always trailed the cello concerto in popularity, dismissed for years as loosely built. That judgment has softened as the compression of the first movement began to look deliberate rather than clumsy. Some recent performers have gone back to Dvořák's 1879 manuscript version, which restores material Joachim's objections removed, and the comparison is now a live scholarly question.","Listen for the moment the first movement gives up its formal return and slips into the Adagio; it happens quietly and is easy to miss. Then take the finale and try to feel the beat regrouping under the dance. Hear it beside Brahms's concerto, written for the same violinist three years earlier, and Dvořák's cello concerto for what he learned in between."],"brahms-double-concerto":["Three movements, about thirty-three minutes, for two soloists who spend most of the piece in dialogue rather than competition. It opens oddly: four orchestral bars, then a long unaccompanied cello recitative, a woodwind phrase, an unaccompanied violin answer, and only then the movement proper. The Andante begins with a horn call the soloists take up in octaves, a wide calm tune. The finale is a rondo in Hungarian colour, light on its feet after the weight of the first movement.","Brahms wrote it in the summer of 1887 at Thun in Switzerland and it turned out to be his last orchestral work. Its purpose was personal: he and Joseph Joachim, friends for thirty-five years, had fallen out badly when Brahms took the side of Joachim's wife during their divorce. The concerto was the approach Brahms could make. Joachim and the cellist Robert Hausmann played it in Cologne on 18 October 1887 with Brahms conducting, and the friendship was partly repaired.","In the finale Brahms quotes the Viotti Violin Concerto No. 22 in A minor, a piece he and Joachim both loved and had discussed for decades — a private signal, in the same key, that only the dedicatee would catch at once. Notice also the opening: two soloists introduced separately, unaccompanied, before any argument begins, which is Brahms staging a reconciliation in sound before he composes one.","The premiere was respectfully received and the work then spent decades under suspicion. Clara Schumann found it unlovable; the standard complaint was that the two soloists thicken the texture and the writing is grey. Advocates answer that the greyness is the subject. It is now firmly in the repertory, though performances still divide over whether to play it as a warm chamber-music dialogue or as a full symphonic argument with soloists attached.","Listen to the first three minutes closely: the cello alone, the violin alone, then both together. Everything after follows from that introduction. Go next to the Andante for the horn call and the octave melody. Try a recording where the two soloists play regularly together rather than two visiting stars; the piece is a duet, not a contest."],"sibelius-violin-concerto":["Three movements, about thirty-three minutes. The violin enters in the second bar over a soft haze of divided strings, and the whole first movement grows from that one line; where a cadenza would normally come near the end, Sibelius puts it in the middle and makes it carry the development. The slow movement is a broad dark song over low woodwind. The finale is a hard-driving dance in three, the soloist in rapid double stops over a stamping orchestra.","Sibelius had trained as a violinist and auditioned unsuccessfully for the Vienna Philharmonic; the concerto is the work of a composer writing for the career he did not have. He was drinking heavily and deep in debt in 1903. The first version, premiered in Helsinki on 8 February 1904 with the underprepared Victor Nováček, went badly. Sibelius withdrew it, cut and rewrote, and the second version was played in Berlin on 19 October 1905, Karel Halíř soloist, Richard Strauss conducting.","Putting the cadenza in the middle changes what it is for: instead of a display appended before the close, it becomes the movement's argument, the soloist alone working out material the orchestra then takes up. Sibelius also thins the orchestration around the violin so relentlessly that the soloist rarely has to force. The 1904 original, longer and more difficult, was locked away by the family and only permitted a recording in 1991.","Berlin critics in 1905 were lukewarm, and the concerto took decades to establish itself, helped by Heifetz's 1935 recording. Tovey's description of the finale as a polonaise for polar bears stuck. Since the original version became available the two texts have been compared closely, and most listeners conclude Sibelius was right to cut, though the first version's third movement has its defenders. It is now among the most recorded of all violin concertos.","Listen to the entry, second bar in, and hear how little the orchestra does under it. Then wait for the cadenza and notice that the orchestra stays away for a long time — that is the structural surprise. The finale is best taken loud. Compare Heifetz's speed with a broader modern account, and if you can find it, hear the 1904 version."],"elgar-violin-concerto":["Three movements and close to fifty minutes, one of the longest concertos in the repertory. The orchestra plays a full exposition before the soloist arrives, and the violin then spends the work turning that material over rather than displaying it. The slow movement in B-flat is broad and hushed. The finale runs fast until it stops for an accompanied cadenza in which the strings, instructed to strum their instruments with the fingertips, murmur behind the soloist as earlier themes drift back.","Elgar wrote it in 1909 and 1910 for Fritz Kreisler, who had asked for a concerto and who gave the premiere with the London Symphony Orchestra on 10 November 1910, Elgar conducting. Elgar was at the height of his public standing, recently knighted, and simultaneously miserable and uncertain. He worked on it partly at Careggi near Florence and partly in Sussex, helped through the writing by Alice Stuart-Wortley, whom he called Windflower and whose name attaches to two of its themes.","The score carries a Spanish motto: Aquí está encerrada el alma de ..... — herein is enshrined the soul of, followed by five dots. Elgar never said whose, and the five dots have been read as a five-letter name; Alice Stuart-Wortley is the usual guess, Julia Worthington another. The strummed strings in the finale's cadenza are Elgar's own invention, notated precisely, and produce a sound closer to a distant guitar orchestra than to pizzicato.","The premiere was a triumph and the concerto was played constantly for two decades, then faded as its length and its emotional register fell out of fashion outside Britain. The sixteen-year-old Menuhin's 1932 recording with Elgar conducting remains the document everyone returns to. Modern debate is mostly about pacing: the work can sag badly if the soloist treats the reflective passages as slow, and the finest performances keep it moving.","Give yourself the whole finale rather than sampling, and wait for the moment the orchestra falls to a whisper and the strings begin to strum — about five minutes from the end, and the reason the piece exists. Then go back to the opening and hear the themes it recalls. Menuhin and Elgar first; then a modern recording to hear what fifty minutes sounds like without a period microphone."],"prokofiev-piano-concerto-no-2":["Four movements, about thirty-two minutes, and among the hardest concertos anyone plays. The first movement opens quietly, almost as a lullaby, and then hands the pianist a cadenza of nearly five minutes that swells from a single line into crashing many-note chords while the orchestra waits. The second movement is a two-and-a-half-minute perpetual motion, the soloist in unbroken sixteenth notes. Then a heavy stamping Intermezzo, and a finale that alternates savagery with a long folk-flavoured melody.","Prokofiev wrote the first version in 1912 and 1913 as a Petersburg Conservatory student and played it at Pavlovsk on 5 September 1913 to catcalls; a critic wrote of cats on the roof. He dedicated it to Maximilian Schmidthof, a close friend who had shot himself that spring after sending Prokofiev a farewell letter. The score was lost after the Revolution, when the flat storing it was ransacked, and Prokofiev rewrote it from memory and sketches in 1923 in Bavaria.","The cadenza is not an interlude but the first movement's development, which means the pianist alone carries the structural weight for a quarter of an hour's music — a design Prokofiev may have taken from watching how little the orchestra does in the concerto's opening bars. He said the 1923 rewriting amounted to a new work, so the 1913 piece the Pavlovsk audience booed does not really survive; what we hear is the mature composer reconstructing his younger self.","The scandal of 1913 became part of the legend, and for decades the concerto was regarded as a young man's assault. It was overshadowed by the Third, which is shorter, brighter and easier to programme. Its standing has risen sharply since the 1990s as pianists sought repertoire beyond the standard concertos, and it is now treated as the more searching of the two, with the dedication to Schmidthof read into its darker movements.","Go to the first-movement cadenza and stay with it; the piece stands or falls on whether that build convinces you. Then take the two-and-a-half-minute second movement, which is over before you settle. Compare it with the Third Concerto to hear how much Prokofiev tidied himself up. Recordings by pianists who can play it quietly are worth seeking out."],"gershwin-concerto-in-f":["Three movements, about thirty-two minutes, in the standard concerto shape but filled with dance-band material. The first opens with timpani and a Charleston rhythm before the piano enters alone with a long tune. The slow movement begins with a muted trumpet playing a blues over quiet clarinets, a nightclub scene lifted into the concert hall; the piano answers with something faster and brighter. The finale is a hammering toccata that gathers up the earlier themes and drives to a blazing close.","Walter Damrosch heard Rhapsody in Blue and commissioned a full concerto for the New York Symphony; Gershwin signed the contract before he had written a note and, by his own account, bought a book on musical form. He wrote it during 1925 while also producing a Broadway show, and gave the premiere at Carnegie Hall on 3 December 1925. Crucially he orchestrated it himself, having left Rhapsody in Blue to Ferde Grofé, and hired an orchestra to try passages out.","The blues in the slow movement is scored with real care: the muted trumpet is accompanied by three clarinets in close harmony, a dance-band voicing rather than a symphonic one, and Gershwin marks it to be played without vibrato at the start. Listen for the first movement's Charleston rhythm returning inside the finale, transformed and speeded up; the concerto is more thematically cross-linked than its reputation for tunefulness suggests.","Reviews in 1925 split along the line that dogged Gershwin all his life — praised for freshness, doubted for craft — and Prokofiev, who heard him play it, admired the tunes and thought the structure amateur. The charge of formal weakness has faded as the piece has been examined rather than assumed. Jazz musicians have generally found it more written-out than jazz; classical players have found it harder to swing than it looks.","Start with the muted trumpet at the top of the slow movement, then let it run and hear the piano cut across the mood. Go back for the Charleston at the opening and listen for its return in the finale. Compare it with Rhapsody in Blue to hear what a year and his own orchestration did, and with Ravel's G major concerto, written six years later by an admirer."],"nielsen-flute-concerto":["Two movements, about nineteen minutes, for a small orchestra with no other flutes and with a bass trombone that behaves like a hostile second soloist. The first movement keeps being derailed: the flute begins a lyrical idea, the timpani or the trombone interrupt, and the music has to find a new key to continue in. The second is lighter, with a marching tune, and ends with the bass trombone sliding down a rude glissando underneath the flute's last cadence.","Nielsen wrote it in 1926 for Holger Gilbert-Jespersen, who had joined the Copenhagen Wind Quintet, whose five players had so impressed Nielsen that he promised each of them a concerto; he completed only two before he died. He was sixty-one, with a failing heart, and finished the piece in Italy in a rush. The premiere in Paris on 21 October 1926 used a provisional ending because the real one was not ready; the final version followed a few months later.","The concerto has no home key. Nielsen starts in one place and lets the argument between instruments push the music somewhere else, a procedure he called progressive tonality and used in his symphonies too; the flute's search for a stable key is the plot. The bass trombone is the antagonist, chosen because it is the crudest instrument he could set against the most refined, and the closing glissando is a joke with an edge to it.","The concerto travelled slowly outside Denmark and was long treated as a curiosity from a composer known abroad, if at all, for the symphonies. Nielsen's international standing rose steeply after the centenary of 1965, and flautists then discovered a concerto that gives the instrument character rather than decoration. Editions have needed care, since the Paris ending circulated for a time alongside the definitive one.","Listen for the bass trombone. Every time it enters, the flute has to change course, and following that quarrel will carry you through the piece. Then notice that the music never settles where it began. Pair it with Nielsen's clarinet concerto, written two years later and far more violent, and with the wind quintet that started the whole plan."],"stravinsky-violin-concerto":["Four movements, about twenty-two minutes, with Baroque titles: Toccata, Aria I, Aria II, Capriccio. Each begins with the same wide three-note chord, struck like a signature. There is no cadenza and almost no display; the violin is treated as the leading voice in a chamber ensemble, often doubled by a bassoon or a horn in ways that colour rather than accompany. The two Arias are slow and songlike, the outer movements dry, bouncing and rhythmically sharp.","The publisher Willy Strecker pushed Stravinsky to write a violin concerto and introduced him to Samuel Dushkin, an American violinist who advised on the solo writing without being allowed to make it more comfortable. Stravinsky, living in France and deep in his neoclassical years, had no interest in the nineteenth-century virtuoso concerto and said so. Dushkin gave the premiere in Berlin on 23 October 1931 with the composer conducting, and the two then toured it across Europe.","The chord that opens every movement — a low D, an E above it and a high A, spanning an eleventh — is the work's passport. Dushkin said he told Stravinsky it was impossible; he tried it at home and found it playable, and it became the piece's identifying gesture. Note also that Stravinsky never lets the soloist stand alone: the writing constantly pairs the violin with a wind instrument, so the star is always in company.","Reception was cool where audiences wanted a concerto and warmer where they wanted Stravinsky. It gained a second life through dance: Balanchine choreographed it as Balustrade in 1941 and again in 1972 as Stravinsky Violin Concerto, which is how many people now encounter it. Critics have argued over how much Dushkin contributed to the solo part; the surviving sketches suggest advice on layout rather than composition.","Listen for the chord at the start of each movement and use it to mark your place. Then follow one of the Arias and notice which instrument is shadowing the violin at any moment. If you can, watch the Balanchine ballet; the choreography makes the rhythmic joints visible. Compare it with Stravinsky's Octet for the same dry wit in a different medium."],"ravel-piano-concerto-g":["Three movements, about twenty-two minutes, and the outer two are pure high spirits. It starts with a whipcrack — a slapstick, two boards struck together — and a piccolo tune over rippling piano. There are jazz slides in the trombone, blue notes in the woodwind, a harp glissando near the end of the first movement. The slow movement is the opposite: a solo piano melody, unaccompanied, more than three minutes long, later handed to the cor anglais. The finale is a brilliant two-way scramble.","Ravel began it in 1929 intending to play the premiere himself on a world tour, then found his technique unequal to it and handed the solo part to Marguerite Long, who gave the first performance in Paris on 14 January 1932 with Ravel conducting. He had recently returned from a four-month American tour where he heard jazz in Harlem clubs and met Gershwin. He was also writing the left-hand concerto simultaneously, and the two works could hardly be less alike.","The slow movement's long melody is in three-time in the right hand over a left hand in a rolling two, so the two hands never quite agree — Ravel said he wrote it two bars at a time with Mozart's clarinet quintet open beside him. Listen too for the E-flat clarinet and piccolo, instruments he uses for brightness and edge rather than volume, and for the whipcrack that opens and effectively closes the outer frame.","The premiere was a success and the concerto has never left the repertory, though it is regularly set against the left-hand concerto as the lighter of the pair. Ravel's own description of it as a concerto in the spirit of Mozart and Saint-Saëns has been quoted to argue both that it is deliberately unserious and that its lightness is hard-won. Recordings by Marguerite Long, made with the composer's involvement, remain a reference point.","Start with the whipcrack and let the first movement run; it is over in nine minutes. Then take the slow movement from the beginning and count the piano's long unaccompanied stretch before the orchestra arrives. Listen for the cor anglais picking up the tune. Follow it with the left-hand concerto for the shock of the contrast, and with Gershwin for the shared vocabulary."],"rachmaninoff-rhapsody-paganini":["A single span of about twenty-four minutes: twenty-four variations on Paganini's last caprice, played without a break but grouped so that they behave like three concerto movements. Rachmaninoff plays a joke at the start — the first variation comes before the theme, which the strings then announce plainly. The middle group slows and darkens; the eighteenth variation is a broad romantic melody that seems unrelated to anything. Then the pace rebuilds to a headlong finish that stops on two quiet notes.","He wrote it in seven weeks in the summer of 1934 at Villa Senar, the modernist house he had built above Lake Lucerne, and premiered it in Baltimore on 7 November 1934 with the Philadelphia Orchestra under Stokowski. He was sixty-one, living as a touring pianist since leaving Russia in 1917, and composing little. It became the last of his works to enter the popular repertory, and he played it constantly for the rest of his life.","The famous eighteenth variation is the theme turned upside down: take Paganini's tune, invert every interval, slow it down, and that melody appears. Rachmaninoff knew what he had and reportedly said it was for his manager. Running against it is the Dies irae, the medieval plainchant for the dead, which enters in the seventh variation and stalks the piece thereafter — a tune he used obsessively, here set against a theme long associated with a violinist rumoured to have sold his soul.","It succeeded at once and was the one late work critics allowed him, at a time when Rachmaninoff was widely written off as a survivor from the previous century. Fokine made a ballet on the Paganini legend to it in 1939 with the composer's help. The eighteenth variation's afterlife in film has made the whole piece hard to hear freshly, and its wit and its structural tightness are still underrated.","Do not wait for the eighteenth variation; listen instead for the Dies irae, four notes descending, when it first appears about a third of the way in, and count how often it returns. Then hear Paganini's caprice itself so the inversion becomes obvious. Rachmaninoff's own 1934 recording is fast and dry, which is a useful corrective to lush modern accounts."],"bartok-viola-concerto":["Three movements joined by short linking passages, about twenty-one minutes. The viola begins alone over a soft timpani stroke with a rising, questioning phrase, and the first movement stays dark and searching. A brief bridge leads to a slow movement of hushed religious calm, which Bartók marked to be played with a kind of stillness. Another bridge, faster, sets up a finale in Hungarian dance rhythm, bright and rough, ending abruptly.","William Primrose, the Scottish viola virtuoso, commissioned it in 1944. Bartók had left Hungary in 1940, was living in New York in poor health and near poverty, and was working on it in the last weeks of his life alongside the third piano concerto. He died on 26 September 1945 leaving the viola concerto as thirteen pages of sketches on unnumbered sheets, with the orchestration barely indicated. He had told Primrose the piece was essentially finished.","What we hear is a reconstruction. Tibor Serly, Bartók's friend and pupil, spent four years ordering the sketch pages, deciding where movements began and ended, and inventing an orchestration; Primrose premiered that version in Minneapolis in December 1949. A second edition by Bartók's son Peter and Nelson Dellamaggiore appeared in 1995, restoring readings Serly had changed. Players now choose between texts, and no one knows exactly what Bartók intended.","For decades the Serly score was simply the concerto, and violists were grateful to have any major twentieth-century work for the instrument. Scholarly access to the facsimile sketches in the 1990s turned that gratitude into argument: how much of the harmony and nearly all of the scoring is Serly's, and some critics now hear his hand as too thick. The music beneath is not in doubt, and the concerto remains the viola's central piece.","Listen to the opening viola phrase over the drum and hear how exposed the instrument is — no orchestra to hide behind, which is the point of the whole piece. Then follow the slow movement's stillness. Compare a Serly-edition recording with a 1995-edition one; the differences in scoring are audible. Walton's viola concerto makes an instructive companion."],"ligeti-violin-concerto":["Five movements, about twenty-eight minutes, for a small orchestra that sounds permanently slightly out of tune. It begins with the solo violin and a second violin playing the same line, one of them retuned, so the unison beats and wavers. A Passacaglia grinds through a repeating bass. In the fourth movement the wind players put down their instruments and play ocarinas and slide whistles, producing a wheezing toy-shop chorus. The finale runs to a cadenza the soloist partly improvises, and then stops mid-gesture.","Ligeti wrote a three-movement version in 1990 for Saschko Gawriloff, withdrew it, and completed the five-movement score for a premiere in Cologne on 8 October 1992 with Peter Eötvös conducting. He was in his late sixties and had spent the 1980s absorbing very unlikely material: sub-Saharan African polyrhythm, Caribbean music, the player-piano studies of Conlon Nancarrow, and the natural harmonics that lie outside the twelve-note keyboard. All of it feeds this concerto.","One violin and one viola in the orchestra are deliberately mistuned to the natural harmonics of the open strings, so their notes sit a fraction away from everyone else's and the harmony shivers rather than sounding wrong. The ocarinas are not a gag: cheap, imprecise and unblendable, they give Ligeti pitches no orchestral instrument can produce. The horn also plays natural harmonics, which is why some of its notes sound flat.","It was recognised quickly as one of the few late-century concertos to enter the repertory rather than the archive, and it has been taken up by a generation of violinists who treat its cadenza as a compositional opportunity — Patricia Kopatchinskaja's version is far removed from Gawriloff's. Critics have debated whether the microtonal shimmer is a genuine harmonic system or an effect; Ligeti himself said he wanted impurity, not a new tuning theory.","Listen to the first minute for the beating between the two violins and decide whether it sounds broken or alive; everything follows from that. Then wait for the ocarinas in the fourth movement. Compare two recordings for the cadenza alone, since no two soloists play the same one. Ligeti's piano études make a good companion for the same rhythmic obsessions."],"john-adams-violin-concerto":["Three movements, about thirty-three minutes, in which the soloist almost never stops playing. The first movement sets a long unbroken violin line spinning above an orchestra that pulses steadily underneath, the two rarely lining up. The middle movement is a chaconne over a slow repeating bass, with two synthesizers colouring the harmony, and the violin drifting far above it. The finale is a hurtling toccata that speeds up in stages until it seems to be running away from the soloist.","Written in 1993 on a joint commission from the Minnesota Orchestra, the London Symphony and New York City Ballet, and premiered in Minneapolis on 19 January 1994 with Jorja Fleezanis and Edo de Waart. Adams had come out of the Bay Area minimalist scene and, after the operas Nixon in China and The Death of Klinghoffer, wanted to test whether a pulse-driven idiom could sustain a long melodic line. It won the Grawemeyer Award in 1995.","Adams described the solo part as a hyper-melody: a line so continuous that it functions as a separate stratum rather than a voice in the texture, and the orchestra beneath it operates on a different clock entirely. The chaconne carries a title from a Robert Hass poem, Body through which the dream flows, and its bass is a slow descending pattern of the kind Purcell and Bach used for lament, running under distinctly non-Baroque harmony.","Early critics were split between those who found the first movement's floating line hypnotic and those who found it aimless, a divide that still shows up in reviews. Its place has been secured by violinists — Leila Josefowicz above all, who has played it worldwide and made it a modern staple. The synthesizers date the sound for some listeners, and Adams has adjusted registrations over the years in response.","Track the violin alone in the first movement and notice that it never rests; then try instead to follow the orchestra's pulse and feel how the two slide against each other. Go to the chaconne for the slow descending bass. Josefowicz's recording is the reference. Follow it with Adams's Harmonielehre to hear the same harmonic language without a soloist."],"salonen-violin-concerto":["Four movements, about thirty minutes, titled Mirage, Pulse I, Pulse II and Adieu. The soloist opens alone at high speed, notes streaming out before the orchestra can catch up. Pulse I is slow, built over a barely moving beat with the violin sustaining long lines; Pulse II is the opposite, a hard-edged dance driven by drum kit, electric guitar and keyboards. The last movement is a long farewell in which the orchestra thins away and the violin is left playing quietly alone.","Salonen wrote it for Leila Josefowicz in 2008 and 2009, and it was premiered in Los Angeles on 9 April 2009 in his final season as music director of the Los Angeles Philharmonic, a post he had held for seventeen years. He conducted the premiere and then left to compose more. The valedictory title of the finale is not accidental. The concerto won the Grawemeyer Award in 2012 and reached an unusual audience after a passage was used in an Apple advertising campaign.","Salonen builds the solo part from what a violin does naturally rather than from abstract material — fast repeated bowing, wide leaps, harmonics — because he wrote much of it by watching Josefowicz play and asking what fitted her hands. The rock instruments in Pulse II are used for attack, not colour, and the drum kit is notated precisely rather than left to a groove. The final movement quietly recalls the opening flurry, slowed almost to stillness.","Reviews were warm and the concerto has been played more than most new works of its scale, partly because Josefowicz took it up as a signature piece and partly because Salonen's conducting career opens doors. Sceptics have called it effective rather than searching, and have questioned how much of its success is the composer's podium standing. Its survival through many performances by other violinists is the better argument.","Start with Pulse II if you want to know quickly whether the piece is for you — drum kit, guitar and violin at full tilt. Then go back to the opening flurry, and finish with the last movement, which is where the concerto says what it is about. Josefowicz's recording with Salonen conducting is the one to hear first."],"puccini-nessun-dorma":["Three minutes that open the third act of Turandot. Calaf stands alone in the palace garden before dawn; the princess has decreed that nobody in Peking shall sleep until his name is discovered. Harp and low strings rock underneath, an offstage chorus of women floats the tune back to him from somewhere in the city, and the aria ends with three cries of Vincero, the last on a held high B that most tenors stretch far beyond the printed value.","Turandot was Puccini's last opera, on a libretto by Giuseppe Adami and Renato Simoni after Carlo Gozzi's eighteenth-century fable. He worked on it at Torre del Lago and Viareggio through the early 1920s and died in Brussels in November 1924, of throat cancer, before finishing the final duet. Franco Alfano completed the score from the sketches. La Scala gave the premiere in April 1926 under Toscanini, who stopped the orchestra at the point where Puccini's own manuscript ends.","Much of Turandot's exotic colouring comes from genuine Chinese tunes Puccini took off a music box owned by a friend who had served in China, including the melody that becomes the princess's own theme. And this aria is not a dead end: the tune returns at the very close of the opera, blazed out by the full orchestra, so the last thing an audience hears is Calaf's promise of victory rather than anything the princess sings.","The 1926 premiere was famous less for the singing than for Toscanini laying down his baton mid-scene. For decades the aria was one tenor showpiece among many; Pavarotti's recordings and the 1990 football World Cup made it the single most recognised piece of opera anywhere, often heard by people who could not name the opera. Scholars still argue about Alfano's ending, and Luciano Berio wrote an alternative completion in 2001.","Listen for the offstage women's voices, which most recordings place too close; they should sound like a city talking in its sleep. Compare Pavarotti under Mehta with Jussi Bjorling or Franco Corelli for very different ideas of the final note. Then play the last five minutes of the whole opera and hear the tune come back."],"puccini-o-mio-babbino-caro":["Two and a half minutes of pleading inside a fast, snapping comedy. Lauretta asks her father Gianni Schicchi to let her marry Rinuccio, and threatens, sweetly, to throw herself off the Ponte Vecchio into the Arno if he refuses. The orchestra rocks in a gentle triple lilt, harp and strings doubling the voice, and the whole aria is essentially one arching phrase sung twice, rising to a soft high note and sinking away.","Gianni Schicchi is the comic third panel of Il trittico, three one-act operas premiered together at the Metropolitan Opera in New York on 14 December 1918, weeks after the Armistice; Puccini could not cross the Atlantic to attend. The story is expanded from a handful of lines in Dante's Inferno, where the real Schicchi is punished among the impersonators for forging a dead man's will. Puccini set it in Florence in 1299.","The sweetness is a tactic. Lauretta is working her father in front of a room full of greedy relatives, and the orchestra's syrup is part of the joke; the surrounding music is all chatter and cross-talk, so this single lyrical island lands like a performance within a performance. The melody comes back near the end of the opera, when the lovers are left alone on stage, which is the only time it means what it says.","Torn out of its frame it became a concert and film staple, used in A Room with a View in 1985 and in advertising ever since, generally played straight and slow. Opera writers grumble that this turns a comic manoeuvre into a greeting card. Sopranos still use it as a first audition piece precisely because it is short, exposed and impossible to fake.","Hear it inside the opera first, with the relatives squabbling on either side of it, and the difference is startling. Then compare a full-voiced reading with a light lyric soprano who keeps it conversational. Renata Scotto, Victoria de los Angeles and more recent lyric sopranos all take very different views of how sincere Lauretta is being."],"puccini-boheme-che-gelida-manina":["In a freezing Paris garret the candle has gone out, Mimi has dropped her key, and in the dark Rodolfo takes her hand: what a frozen little hand, let me warm it. The aria begins almost as speech over quiet strings, turns into an introduction of himself as a poet with nothing but dreams, and opens out into a long climbing phrase crowned by a high C. It leads straight into her reply and their duet.","Luigi Illica and Giuseppe Giacosa built the libretto from Henri Murger's Scenes de la vie de boheme, sketches of student poverty in 1840s Paris. Puccini had lived that life himself as a conservatory student in Milan, pawning his coat. Ruggero Leoncavallo was setting the same book at the same time and the two composers quarrelled publicly about it. The premiere was at the Teatro Regio in Turin on 1 February 1896, conducted by the 28-year-old Toscanini.","Nothing in this aria is new. Rodolfo sings over material already heard in the opera's opening bars, when the bohemians are burning his manuscript for warmth, and the soaring love theme launched here is the music the orchestra plays alone in the last act as Mimi dies and Rodolfo is the only person on stage who has not yet realised it. The tune returns; the situation does not.","Turin's critics were cool, finding the piece slight and sentimental beside Verdi, and one influential review predicted it would leave no mark. Within three years it was playing everywhere. The high C is the one practical controversy: Puccini sanctioned a version a semitone lower, and tenors who take it still get letters.","Play the three linked numbers in order, Rodolfo's aria, Mimi's reply and the duet, which are really one twelve-minute scene. Jussi Bjorling with Victoria de los Angeles is the classic account of the line; Pavarotti and Mirella Freni under Karajan is the plush one. Then go straight to the final act."],"puccini-un-bel-di-vedremo":["Cio-Cio-San, abandoned for three years and refusing to know it, describes the day Pinkerton's ship will return: a thread of smoke on the far horizon, the white ship in the harbour, the climb up the hill, and how she will hide to make him call her name. It begins almost whispered over a high sustained violin line, and the orchestra swells to double her at the climax before she drops back to a spoken certainty.","Puccini saw David Belasco's one-act play in London in 1900 without following the English, and knew at once he wanted it. He gathered Japanese melodies from a music box, from recordings, and from the wife of the Japanese ambassador in Rome, and worked on the score while recovering from a car crash that nearly killed him in 1903. The La Scala premiere in February 1904 was shouted down; a revised version at Brescia three months later succeeded.","Listen to the climax: the orchestra plays her melody in bare unison with her rather than harmonising it, so at the emotional peak she is musically alone on stage. The whole aria is in the future tense about an event that will not happen, and the tune returns in the orchestra at the end of the opera, over her death, as the ship's arrival finally destroys her.","The 1904 fiasco was noisy enough that Puccini blamed an organised claque; he withdrew the score, split the long second act and cut back Pinkerton's worst behaviour. Five versions exist, and the harsher 1904 original is now sometimes revived. Modern debate centres on orientalism and on how Cio-Cio-San is staged and cast, questions that make the aria harder to hear as a simple lament.","Follow the words; the aria is a narrated film scene, and singers who act it beat singers who only sing it. Listen for the solo violin holding the opening in the air. Renata Scotto, Victoria de los Angeles and Maria Callas each solve the ending differently, from quiet conviction to open desperation."],"puccini-tosca-e-lucevan-stelle":["On the roof of the Castel Sant'Angelo an hour before his execution, the painter Cavaradossi tries to write a farewell letter and cannot. A solo clarinet plays the melody first, alone and low; the tenor half-speaks it before he sings it, remembering a summer night and a garden gate; and it breaks off on the line that he has never loved life so much, with the orchestra crashing in over him. Three minutes, no repeats.","Tosca comes from Victorien Sardou's melodrama, which Puccini saw with Sarah Bernhardt in the title role. The action is pinned to real Roman places and to real politics: June 1800, with Napoleon's advance on Italy deciding who lives. The premiere at the Teatro Costanzi in Rome on 14 January 1900, under Leopoldo Mugnone, took place amid a reported bomb scare, with the theatre nervous about political demonstrations.","The clarinet gets the tune before the singer does, which makes the aria a memory being played back rather than a statement being made. Puccini's librettists reportedly thought the number redundant, since the audience already knows the man is doomed, and he had to insist on keeping it. Earlier in the same act he notates the actual matins bells of Rome, pitched from churches he had someone check for him.","Critics in 1900 found Tosca crude next to Verdi, and Joseph Kerman's 1956 verdict, a shabby little shocker, stuck to the whole opera for a generation. The aria itself never lost its audience. Argument now runs the other way: the score's economy and its blunt use of torture and execution look modern, and the piece is one of the three most performed operas in the world.","Listen to the clarinet's opening solo before anything else, then notice how the tenor avoids singing on the beat at the start. Giuseppe di Stefano for the sob, Franco Corelli for the sheer sound, Jonas Kaufmann for the words. Then hear how few minutes are left of the opera afterwards."],"puccini-tosca-vissi-d-arte":["The police chief Scarpia has named his price for Cavaradossi's life and is waiting. Tosca sinks to the floor and asks God why this is her reward. The orchestra thins to almost nothing under a voice that starts low and unaccompanied in feeling; the melody climbs in slow steps to a single held high note on the question, then falls back to a spoken-sounding close. About three minutes in which no action takes place at all.","Rome, June 1800: an opera singer, a republican painter and a chief of police in one room. Puccini and his librettists built Act Two as a tightening screw, and this aria is the one place where the screw stops turning. He was 41, at the height of his powers after La boheme, and writing for the Roman theatre where the story is set, with Sardou himself looking over the project.","The character is a singer, so the audience is watching a diva sing about having given her life to art, in a room where a knife is lying on a supper table she will use within minutes. The orchestral accompaniment is deliberately impoverished; Scarpia never leaves the stage, and the aria's calm is what he is enjoying while he waits.","Puccini himself is said to have doubted the number because it halts the drama dead. Maria Jeritza, having fallen during a rehearsal, sang it lying face down and the effect became a tradition in Vienna and New York. Callas's 1953 recording under Victor de Sabata is the standard reference. Feminist readings now dwell on what it means that the prayer is sung by a woman about to commit murder.","Time it: three minutes in which the plot does not move, dropped into the tightest act Puccini wrote. Listen for how little orchestra there is under the first phrases. Callas for the words, Leontyne Price for the line, and then any staging that keeps Scarpia visibly waiting."],"puccini-turandot-signore-ascolta":["The slave girl Liu begs Calaf not to strike the gong that will commit him to the princess's riddles and, if he fails, to execution. Flutes and high strings hold a thin pentatonic haze; the vocal line moves in small steps and then lifts to a soft high note before falling away almost unaccompanied. Under three minutes, and by far the quietest music in an opera otherwise built on gongs, brass and crowds.","Liu does not exist in Gozzi's fable or Schiller's version; Puccini and his librettists invented her, and she is often connected with Doria Manfredi, the young servant in the Puccini household who took poison in 1909 after his wife Elvira publicly accused her of an affair. The court case that followed damaged him badly. He wrote Liu's music last, in the final year of his life, and it was the last of the opera he completed.","Liu is the only character scored without the exotic percussion machinery: no gongs, no Chinese tunes, no ceremonial brass. Puccini gives the fairy tale's one ordinary person a plain, unlocated sound, so her music feels as if it belongs to a different opera. Her aria is immediately answered by Calaf's reply to her, so the two form a single scene, not a solo.","At the 1926 premiere Toscanini put down his baton shortly after Liu's death scene, since that is where Puccini's completed score stops; the opera Puccini actually finished therefore ends with her, not with the princess. Whether Alfano's or Berio's completion should follow remains an open question, and some productions still end where Toscanini did.","Hear the two arias together, hers and Calaf's answer, and notice the sudden loss of colour when the orchestra drops the percussion. Then jump to her death scene in the last act. Any recording with a genuine lyric soprano rather than a big dramatic voice suits the part better."],"puccini-madama-butterfly-humming-chorus":["Night falls on the house above Nagasaki harbour. Cio-Cio-San, her maid Suzuki and the child settle at the paper screen to watch for Pinkerton, and an unseen chorus hums, mouths closed, over muted strings and harp. There are no words at all, and no soloist; the melody rises and turns back on itself twice and then thins out. It runs on into an orchestral interlude and the dawn music of the following morning.","Puccini needed a whole night to pass on stage with three people who do not move and nothing to say. His solution was to have the village he never shows do the singing. In the two-act revision made for Brescia in May 1904, this vigil flows without a break into the interlude and into a dawn scored with notated birdsong, so the audience sits through the night with her in real theatrical time.","It is the only extended wordless chorus in Puccini's operas, and the closed-mouth instruction matters: the sound is deliberately hard to place, more like an instrument than a crowd, and it comes from offstage, so the audience hears a community that never appears and never helps. Nothing happens for three minutes, which is exactly the point of the scene.","The Milan audience of February 1904 lost patience with the long second act, and the vigil was among the stretches most obviously in trouble; the revision tightened everything around it but left this untouched. Excerpted for choirs and used in film soundtracks ever since, it is now often heard as a mood piece, which drains the waiting out of it.","Do not play it alone. Start ten minutes earlier, from Cio-Cio-San's decision to keep watch, and let it run through the interlude into the dawn; the point is duration. Then compare Faure's Pavane for a similar trick of a melody that refuses to arrive."],"albeniz-asturias":["Six minutes built on one obsessive idea: a single note hammered over and over in the middle of the keyboard, interrupted by heavy chords low in the left hand, exactly the way a flamenco player alternates thumb and strummed fingers. The drive keeps up for two long spans; between them comes a slow middle section, spare and free in rhythm, like a singer's lament with the guitar barely touching the strings. Then the hammering returns and evaporates.","Albeniz wrote the piece in 1892 as the Prelude to a set called Chants d'Espagne, when he was a touring virtuoso shuttling between Madrid, London and Paris and turning out picturesque Spanish pieces for the concert platform. A publisher later gave it the title Asturias and slotted it into the Suite espanola as its fifth number, which is why a piece full of Andalusian flamenco carries the name of a green province on the northern Atlantic coast.","Not one note of it was written for guitar. Every guitarist plays a transcription, made first by Francisco Tarrega and popularised by Andres Segovia, and shifted from G minor to E minor so that the open strings ring under the repeated note. What began as a piano imitating a guitar came back as a guitar imitating a piano imitating a guitar, and the second version is now the famous one.","The mislabelling is a standing joke among Spanish musicians, and the guitar version has almost entirely displaced the original in public awareness. Pianists argue that the printed text is the real piece and that the guitar loses its sustained bass; guitarists reply that the writing plainly wants open strings. The Doors quoted it in Spanish Caravan, and film and advertising have used it for decades.","Hear a guitar performance first, Segovia or John Williams, then Alicia de Larrocha at the piano, and decide which instrument the music thinks it is on. Listen for the slow middle section, which most listeners forget is there. Then try Scarlatti's Spanish sonatas for the same guitar-in-a-keyboard trick 150 years earlier."],"albeniz-iberia-evocacion":["The opening piece of a cycle of twelve, and the quietest thing in it. A dance rhythm is present from the first bar but never allowed to become a dance: the pulse is smudged, the harmony slides sideways, and melodies surface in the middle of the texture and sink again. The writing is so dense that Albeniz uses three staves in places. It ends without settling, drifting away rather than closing.","Albeniz composed the four books of Iberia between 1905 and 1909 in Paris and Nice, in his final years and in poor health with the kidney disease that killed him at 48. He was living among Debussy, Dukas, Faure and d'Indy, and the harmony shows it. Blanche Selva, a formidable young French pianist, gave the first performances of all four books, the first of them in Paris in 1906.","Two dances are folded into one piece. The rocking rhythm underneath is a jota, from Aragon in the north; the melodic curl on top belongs to the fandango of the south. Evoking Spain, Albeniz starts by refusing to pick a region. The title also warns you off literal scene-painting: this is a memory of a place, printed on paper so crowded that Albeniz reportedly wished he could write it more simply.","Pianists rank Iberia with the hardest music in the repertoire, and its reputation for unplayability slowed its spread; Selva's advocacy, and later Alicia de Larrocha's, made the case. Enrique Fernandez Arbos orchestrated five of the pieces, and his versions are still played, to the irritation of those who hear the cycle as inseparable from the keyboard. Whether it is nationalist or impressionist remains an open argument.","Start here rather than with the loud numbers; it teaches the ear what Albeniz's late harmony does. Then play Debussy's Reflets dans l'eau from three years later and hear two composers doing similar things with the same instrument. De Larrocha's cycle is the reference; more recent recordings play it faster and drier."],"albeniz-tango-d":["Under three minutes, and deliberately slight: a drooping tune decorated with little grace notes, set over a lazy syncopated bass that leans back on every second beat. A brighter middle section lifts the register and adds a countermelody, then the opening returns almost unchanged. Nothing is developed and nothing is difficult; the whole piece is a single gesture repeated until it charms.","One of six short pieces published as Espana: Six Album Leaves, written for the drawing room and for the encore slot at the end of a recital. Albeniz was living in London at the time under contract to the banker and amateur librettist Francis Money-Coutts, who paid him a salary in exchange for operas on English subjects, and pieces like this kept the household running between commissions.","The rhythm is not the Argentine tango, which barely existed outside Buenos Aires in 1890. It is the older habanera pattern that crossed from Cuba to Spain, the same lilt Bizet borrowed for Carmen's entrance. The piece is also almost never heard as written: Leopold Godowsky's elaborated version and countless guitar arrangements have quietly replaced the plain original.","Beside the four books of Iberia it looks like small change, and writers on Albeniz have often treated the salon pieces as work he had to do rather than work he wanted to do. It sold in enormous quantities, which is a kind of verdict, and guitarists and cellists still programme it.","Two versions in a row make the point: a plain piano reading, then a guitar arrangement, and hear how much of the character sits in the bass. Follow it with Bizet's Habanera for the shared rhythm, and Piazzolla for what the word tango came to mean forty years later."],"albeniz-granada":["A serenade, and shaped exactly like one: a repeating figure in the right hand imitates plucked strings, the thumb of the left hand marks a slow bass, and a plain singing melody sits between them. A middle section thickens into full strummed chords and rises in temperature, then the opening tune returns and the piece fades on the same plucking with which it began. About five minutes.","Written in Madrid in 1886, when Albeniz was 26, giving recitals and producing short character pieces named after Spanish cities for a public that wanted local colour. The Suite espanola was assembled by publishers over decades and only partly by the composer; several of its eight numbers were attached to it long after he wrote them, and some after he died.","For a piece about the city of the Alhambra there is almost no Moorish colouring: no exotic scales, no ornamental melisma, just an Andalusian night serenade written by a Catalan trained in Brussels and Leipzig. The Granada here is imagined from a distance, years before the composer soaked himself in southern song for Iberia; comparing the two is a lesson in what he learned.","Tarrega's guitar transcription made it a guitarist's standard, and for many listeners the guitar is the piece's home instrument. Critics who value the late Albeniz treat the early city pieces as postcards; defenders point out that the postcards taught him the vocabulary the later work stretches. The suite's tangled publishing history still confuses concert programmes.","Julian Bream or a good classical guitarist first, then de Larrocha's piano, and note how much the piano adds in the bass. Play it back to back with Evocacion from Iberia, written nineteen years later, for the distance travelled."],"albeniz-sevilla":["A sevillanas, the clattering triple-time dance of the Seville fair, taken at a brisk clip with sharp accents and cross-rhythms that keep landing a beat early. Halfway through the dance stops and a copla arrives: a slow, ornamented tune over a plain strummed accompaniment, as if a singer had stepped forward. Then the dance resumes and drives to a bright, abrupt finish. Around five minutes.","Written in Madrid in 1886 alongside Granada, in the same run of city portraits later gathered as the Suite espanola. Albeniz was a young virtuoso building a career on Spanish material at a moment when Paris and London wanted Spain badly, and when Spanish composers were arguing about whether a national school could be built out of dance forms rather than opera.","The form is not imposed on the dance, it is the dance: real sevillanas alternate danced sections with sung coplas, and Albeniz simply writes both. In the copla the left hand thrums repeated chords in the exact position a guitarist's hand would take, while the right imitates a singer bending notes. The pianist is being asked to be two performers.","Along with Asturias it was among the first Albeniz pieces guitarists appropriated, and the transcription tradition has kept it in circulation. Commentators divide it from the later Iberia as folkloric surface against genuine synthesis; players note that the rhythmic sleight of hand here is already harder than it sounds and points directly at the mature style.","Count into the piece in threes and enjoy losing the count, which is the intention. Listen for the change of character at the copla. Compare with Falla's dances for a rougher, later version of the same street, and with a live sevillanas for the real thing."],"mahler-symphony-no-5-adagietto":["The fourth of five movements, and the only one in the whole symphony without a single wind or brass instrument: strings and one harp, nothing else, in a work that elsewhere uses an army. A long violin melody rises in slow waves over plucked harp chords, is pulled back twice by darker harmonies, and reaches one full-throated climax before subsiding. It does not end so much as hand over: the finale begins without a pause.","Composed in the summers of 1901 and 1902 in the lakeside hut at Maiernigg on the Worthersee, where Mahler wrote everything while on holiday from running the Vienna Court Opera. Between those two summers he met and married Alma Schindler, nineteen years younger and a composer herself. Willem Mengelberg, who knew him, wrote in his score that the movement was a declaration of love sent to Alma instead of a letter.","The melody is a quotation of Mahler's own song of the previous summer, Ich bin der Welt abhanden gekommen, which sets a poem about withdrawing from the world entirely. Strip the words away and the same tune reads as a love letter; that double meaning is the movement. Mahler's own performances are documented at around seven or eight minutes, which is startling next to what conductors do with it now.","Visconti used it throughout Death in Venice in 1971 and turned it into funeral music for a whole generation of listeners; Bernstein had already conducted it at Robert Kennedy's requiem mass in 1968. The tempo argument is unusually sharp: Mengelberg's timings and Mahler's marking suggest a flowing piece, while famous recordings stretch it towards twelve minutes and treat it as an elegy.","Never play it alone the first time. Start at the scherzo before it and let it run into the finale, so you hear it as an interruption rather than a picture. Then compare a fast reading, Abbado or Boulez, with Bernstein's slow one and decide which is the love letter."],"mahler-symphony-no-2-resurrection-finale":["The finale alone runs longer than many complete symphonies. It opens with a shriek from the full orchestra, then empties out: horns call from offstage, far away and deliberately not quite in time with the hall, and a march of the dead assembles and passes. At the great summons, percussion crashes and dies to a solitary flute and piccolo trilling like a last bird. Then the chorus enters, unaccompanied and barely audible, on Klopstock's resurrection hymn, and the movement builds to organ, bells and a blaze of E flat major.","Mahler had the first four movements and no ending for years. The breakthrough came at Hans von Bulow's funeral in Hamburg in March 1894, when a boys' choir sang Klopstock's chorale Aufersteh'n and Mahler, sitting in the church, decided on the spot. He set two of Klopstock's stanzas and wrote the rest of the text himself. The first complete performance was in Berlin in December 1895, with Mahler conducting.","The offstage band is a spatial instruction, not an effect: Mahler asks for horns and trumpets placed at a distance and marked to sound detached from the orchestra's tempo, so the hall itself becomes part of the scoring. He also wanted the chorus to enter seated and so quietly that listeners cannot tell where the sound begins, and the plainchant Dies irae shape lurks inside the march that precedes it.","Berlin in 1895 split down the middle, with critics who admired the sound appalled by the theology and the length. The symphony languished until the Mahler revival after 1960, when Klemperer, Solti and Bernstein made it a choral society's ambition; it is now one of the most performed of all long orchestral works. Whether a Jewish-born convert's resurrection music is sincere, ironic or simply theatrical is still argued.","Come in at the offstage horn calls rather than the start, and give the quiet stretch before the choir enters your full attention; the effect depends entirely on how close to silence a conductor dares to go. Klemperer for weight, Rattle for detail, and a recording with a real acoustic rather than a close-miked one."],"mahler-symphony-no-1-third-movement":["A solo double bass, alone, plays the nursery round known in Germany as Bruder Martin and elsewhere as Frere Jacques, in the minor and pushed high into a register where the instrument sounds strangled. A soft timpani tread keeps time; bassoon, tuba and oboes join the canon one by one. Then a village band bursts in, clarinets whining, cymbals and bass drum grinning, playing something cheap and sentimental. The funeral reassembles and shuffles off.","Written in Leipzig in 1888, when Mahler was 28 and a jobbing opera conductor with no reputation as a composer. He attached a programme, later withdrawn, pointing at a well-known woodcut in which the forest animals cheerfully carry the dead hunter's coffin. The premiere in Budapest in November 1889, billed as a symphonic poem, baffled its audience; the funeral march drew the most hostility.","The band episode is a self-quotation: it is the last of Mahler's Lieder eines fahrenden Gesellen, the wanderer lying down under the lime tree, dropped into a funeral without explanation. The sour E flat clarinet, the cymbal on the offbeat and the sliding thirds are the sound of the klezmer bands of Mahler's Bohemian childhood, brought into a concert hall that had no category for them.","Hissed in Budapest and treated for decades as evidence of bad taste, the movement was exactly what later listeners came to value: Adorno built his reading of Mahler's irony on such collisions, and the mixture of kitsch and grief now sounds like the century's own procedure. The one practical question left is whether the opening should be one player or the whole bass section, which changes the character completely.","Listen for whether your recording uses a solo bass, as Mahler wrote, or several players hiding the strain; the discomfort is the point. Then track the round through the orchestra and count how many instruments take it up. Bernstein and Kubelik take opposite views of how vulgar the band should sound."],"mahler-symphony-no-4-finale":["The symphony ends not with a peroration but with a song. A soprano describes a child's heaven where the saints dance, the angels bake bread and the butchering of the ox is described with cheerful relish. Mahler's instruction is that she sing with childlike, cheerful expression and entirely without parody. Sleigh bells and a scampering orchestral refrain separate the verses; the last verse slips into a new key, harp and low strings thin out, and the music simply stops.","The song came first. Mahler wrote Das himmlische Leben in 1892 as one of his Wunderhorn settings, considered it as a finale for the Third Symphony, and then built the Fourth backwards from it, so that the whole symphony is an approach to a tune already written. He completed the symphony in 1900 and conducted the premiere in Munich in November 1901, to hostile reviews.","The symphony begins in G major and ends in E major, drifting into a key it has not previously visited and staying there, which is why the close feels less like an arrival than a disappearance. The sleigh bells that opened the first movement return here to close the circle. And the text, sung sweetly, is a menu: the lamb, the ox and the fish are all slaughtered, with Herod as the butcher.","Contemporaries thought it a retreat, thin and old-fashioned after the enormous Second and Third, and Munich's critics said so. It is now the usual door into Mahler for newcomers, being the shortest and lightest of the symphonies. The recurring practical question is who should sing it: Mahler wanted a woman sounding childlike, not an actual child, though boy trebles are occasionally used.","Read the text before you listen, then listen for how completely the music refuses to comment on it. Judith Raskin with Szell is the classic account of the tone; Elly Ameling and later lyric sopranos vary the innocence. Then hear the slow movement that precedes it, which is where the symphony does its real work."],"mahler-ich-bin-welt-abhanden-gekommen":["Six or seven minutes in which almost nothing happens. A cor anglais and a horn trade a slow, wandering line before the voice enters low and unhurried: I have become lost to the world, and it may well believe me dead. Mahler keeps the orchestra to chamber size throughout, with harp and a few strings, and refuses any climax. The last line, about living alone in one's heaven, one's love and one's song, descends into silence.","Composed in the summer of 1901 at Maiernigg, weeks after a haemorrhage had nearly killed him and months before he met Alma Schindler. It is one of five settings of Friedrich Ruckert made across two summers, never conceived by Mahler as a cycle and published in no fixed order. He told a friend that this one was, quite simply, himself.","The following summer the melody became the Adagietto of the Fifth Symphony, stripped of words and given to strings and harp, which means the most famous love music Mahler wrote began as a song about renouncing the world. Notice too that the score has no loud dynamic anywhere and no percussion: the orchestral apparatus he was famous for is entirely stood down.","Long overshadowed by the Kindertotenlieder, the song moved to the centre of the repertoire largely through Kathleen Ferrier's recording with Bruno Walter in 1952. Singers now take it far slower than Mahler's own indications suggest, and the piano version, once treated as a rehearsal aid, has been reclaimed as a legitimate alternative with a very different, drier intimacy.","Play it immediately before or after the Fifth Symphony's Adagietto and listen for the same melody wearing different clothes. Ferrier, Janet Baker and Christa Ludwig each find a different weight in the last line. Follow the German text; the song turns on one word, dead, sung as if it were good news."],"mahler-lied-von-erde-abschied":["Half an hour, longer than the five movements before it put together. Low gong strokes and an oboe's free recitative open it; the singer half-speaks of waiting at evening for a friend who is late. A long orchestral interlude follows, a funeral march that thins to almost nothing, and then the friend arrives, says he is going into the mountains, and leaves. The last minutes repeat one word, ewig, ever, over celesta, harp and mandolin, on a chord that never closes.","Written in 1908 and 1909 after the worst year of Mahler's life: his elder daughter died of scarlet fever and diphtheria at four, he was forced out of the Vienna Opera, and a doctor found the heart defect that would kill him. He set Hans Bethge's loose German versions of Tang dynasty poems, published as Die chinesische Flote, and superstitiously refused to number the work as a symphony. Bruno Walter conducted the posthumous premiere in Munich in November 1911.","The final lines, about the earth blossoming again everywhere in spring, forever and forever, are not Bethge's and not Chinese: Mahler wrote them himself and repeated the last word seven times. The closing chord adds an extra note that leaves it hanging, and the score simply instructs the players to die away; the mandolin, an instrument no symphony orchestra kept on staff, is there to make the last pages sound like something plucked in the dark.","Walter's premiere established the work at once, but a century of argument followed over whether the alternating movements should go to a contralto or a baritone, a choice Mahler left open. The piano version, prepared in his lifetime, has been revived. Postcolonial readings question the fantasy of the East built into Bethge's translations, while Britten and Shostakovich both wrote works that answer this one directly.","Give it the whole thirty minutes in one sitting, at night, with the text beside you; excerpting it destroys it. Ferrier with Walter in 1952 remains the emotional benchmark, Christa Ludwig with Klemperer the grander one. Listen for the moment the orchestra stops and one instrument is left holding the line."],"mahler-symphony-no-6-andante":["The one movement of the Sixth that is not under threat. A broad, unhurried melody in a key remote from the symphony's grim A minor unfolds in the strings, is taken up by a solo horn, and wanders into open country where cowbells sound from a great distance, marked to be played irregularly and softly, as a real herd would. The tune returns fuller each time, reaches one warm climax and then withdraws, leaving the bells still ringing somewhere off.","Composed at Maiernigg in the summers of 1903 and 1904, the happiest stretch of Mahler's life: he was directing the Vienna Opera at the peak of his authority and his two daughters were small. He conducted the premiere in Essen in May 1906 and witnesses described him as visibly distressed at the rehearsals. Alma's memoir attaches personal portraits to several themes, but her account was written decades later and is unreliable.","Where this movement belongs is a genuine unsolved problem. Mahler performed it third, after the scherzo, in Essen, later reversed the order, and left no final instruction; conductors and editions still disagree, and the symphony sounds like a different piece depending on the choice. The cowbells, meanwhile, are the one indication Mahler explained: not a picture of the Alps, but the last earthly sound heard from a great height.","The Sixth carried the nickname Tragic from early on, though Mahler did not authorise it. Twentieth-century argument focused on the finale's hammer blows, two or three depending on the version, and on the movement order, where Erwin Ratz's mid-century edition and the later critical revision take opposite sides. The Andante itself has never been controversial, only its address.","Find two recordings that place it differently, third and second, and hear how the symphony changes shape. Listen for the horn taking over the melody, and for the exact moment the cowbells enter. Barbirolli's account is the warm one; Abbado's the clear one."],"mahler-symphony-no-9-adagio":["Twenty-five minutes that begin with a hymn for full strings, harmonised so thickly it sounds like an organ, and proceed by alternating that warmth with passages so thin they use three or four instruments at a time. The great central climax collapses rather than resolving. In the last pages the players drop away one by one until a handful of strings is left, phrases are separated by long silences, and the score's final instruction is that the music die away.","Written in 1909 and 1910, mostly at Toblach in the Dolomites, in the shadow of his daughter's death, his own diagnosis and, in the summer of 1910, the discovery of Alma's affair with the architect Walter Gropius. Mahler orchestrated the score completely but never heard a note of it; Bruno Walter conducted the premiere in Vienna in June 1912, a year after Mahler's death.","In the closing bars Mahler quotes himself: the falling phrase that keeps returning is from the last of the Kindertotenlieder, where the dead children are said to be resting as if in their mother's house. Alban Berg, reading the score, wrote to his wife that the movement was saturated with the premonition of death, and the silences in the final page are notated exactly, so the rests are part of the music.","For half a century the Ninth was thought unplayable and was rarely programmed. The Mahler revival made it central, and two readings compete: Bernstein's, which hears the whole symphony as a body failing, and a cooler modern view that treats it as a formal experiment in dissolution. Schoenberg's circle heard it as the last symphony written before tonality gave way, a claim that says more about them than about Mahler.","Listen to the last five minutes first, then go back and hear the whole movement arrive there. Watch, if you can, rather than only listen: seeing players stop one by one makes the ending legible. Karajan's live Berlin recording of 1982 and Barbirolli's are the two accounts most often argued over."],"wolf-verborgenheit":["Three minutes, and shaped like a door closing twice. The outer sections are hymn-like: the piano moves in slow block chords while the voice asks the world to leave it alone, since it neither knows what it grieves for nor what it wants. The middle rises abruptly, joy piercing through the clouds, the piano surging with it, and then the opening returns almost note for note, as if the interruption had been a mistake.","1888 was Wolf's astonishing year. Living rent-free in a friend's cottage at Perchtoldsdorf outside Vienna, he set forty-three poems by Eduard Morike in a few months, sometimes finishing two or three in a day, after long periods in which he could write nothing at all. He had spent the previous years as a savage newspaper critic, championing Wagner and attacking Brahms, and had almost no income of his own.","The song's argument is that pleasure is dangerous, and the setting proves it structurally: the ecstatic middle section lasts only a handful of bars before the withdrawn opening music shuts it down and gets the last word. Wolf, according to friends, came to resent this song for being the one people always asked for, since he considered it the most conventional thing in the volume.","Wolf's songs spread through singers and through the Hugo Wolf Society founded by admirers in the 1890s rather than through publishers or concert institutions. Syphilis destroyed his mind in 1897 and he died in an asylum at 42. The perennial argument about his work is whether his intensely detailed word-setting serves the poems or crowds them out; Verborgenheit is usually cited by the defence.","Follow the German text; the whole design turns on the return of the first line. Dietrich Fischer-Dieskau with Gerald Moore for the diction, Elisabeth Schwarzkopf for the shading. Then hear a Schubert or Schumann setting of a comparable poem and notice how much more the piano is asked to do here."],"wolf-italian-serenade":["Seven minutes in a single movement for string quartet. Plucked chords set up a guitar strumming under a light, quick tune that keeps circling back; between its returns come episodes where the cello breaks into what sounds like recitative, a singer trying to get a word in over an accompaniment that will not stop. The strumming always wins, and the piece ends with a shrug rather than a flourish.","Wolf wrote it in three days in May 1887, a few months after his father's death and while he was scraping a living reviewing concerts for a Viennese weekly. The title probably nods to Eichendorff, whose wandering musicians and night serenades Wolf was setting at the time. He later planned to expand it into a multi-movement Italian serenade and began an arrangement for small orchestra, but finished neither.","The whole piece is one melody re-lit from different angles, which is a song composer's habit rather than a chamber composer's: instead of developing themes against each other, Wolf simply keeps re-presenting the tune with new company. The cello's interruptions are staged, not structural, and the joke is that nobody in the ensemble listens to the soloist.","It is the only instrumental piece by Wolf in general circulation, which colours how it is heard: some argue it shows a chamber composer lost to song, others that it is a song without words wearing a quartet's clothes. Both the quartet original and the unfinished orchestral version are played, and ensembles disagree about how heavily to lean on the pizzicato.","Take the quartet version first and listen only for the cello's interruptions, which are easy to miss under the strumming. Then hear a few songs from Wolf's Italian Songbook for the same world with words. It is short enough to play twice in a sitting, which is the best way to catch its structure."],"wolf-kennst-du-land":["Six minutes, long for a song and closer to an operatic scene. Mignon asks three times whether her listener knows the land where lemon trees bloom, the house with its marble statues, the mountain path with the dragon in its cave. Each verse is set to the same rising shape but pitched higher and scored more thickly, and each ends with the cry Dahin, there, there. By the third the piano is playing something like an orchestral tutti; the postlude collapses.","Written during the Goethe outpouring of late 1888, when Wolf set fifty poems in a few months. He knew exactly whose company he was joining: Beethoven, Schubert, Schumann and Liszt had all set this poem, and Schubert's version was in every drawing room. Mignon is the mute, damaged child of Goethe's novel Wilhelm Meister, remembering an Italy she was stolen from. Wolf orchestrated the accompaniment a few years later.","The refrain is different every time. Wolf sets Dahin higher and more desperately at each return, and the last one does not resolve but breaks, leaving the piano to finish the sentence. The accompaniment is not accompanying: it plays the landscape, the marble hall, the mule feeling its way along the path and the dragon in the cave, so the singer describes what the pianist is already showing.","Contemporaries found it overloaded, and the comparison with Schubert's simple strophic Mignon remains the standard classroom argument about how much a song can carry. Wolf's defenders answer that Goethe's Mignon is a broken child, not a folk singer, and that the strain is the meaning. It is now generally counted the peak of his Goethe volume; the orchestral version divides opinion further.","Hear Schubert's setting first, then this, back to back, and the century between them becomes audible. Follow the three refrains and mark how high the last one climbs. Schwarzkopf and Jessye Norman take opposite approaches, chamber intimacy against operatic scale."],"richard-strauss-also-sprach-zarathustra-opening":["Under two minutes. Double basses, contrabassoon and the organ's lowest pipes hold a single note in the dark, so low that in a good hall it is felt before it is heard. Four trumpets climb three notes; the whole orchestra answers with a hammered chord, first dark, then bright; timpani pound in pairs. The sequence happens three times, growing each time, until the organ lands with its full weight on a C major chord and the tone poem's real argument begins.","Strauss wrote it in Munich in 1896, at 32, already notorious for Don Juan and Till Eulenspiegel, and conducted the premiere in Frankfurt that November. Nietzsche's book was the fashionable text of the decade among German artists. Strauss was careful to say he was not setting philosophy but writing music suggested by the book, and he laid out an orchestra with six horns, an organ and a brass section built for a cathedral.","The opening states the contest that runs for the next half hour: the key of C, standing for nature, against the key of B, a semitone away, standing for humanity. Strauss never settles it. At the very end of the work the basses sit on one key while the woodwinds sit on the other, high above, and the piece stops without deciding, one of the first great unresolved endings in orchestral music.","The 1896 reception was mixed, with critics divided over whether an orchestra could carry philosophical freight at all. Then Kubrick used ninety seconds of it in 2001: A Space Odyssey in 1968 and the sunrise became the most quoted orchestral gesture of the century, severed from everything after it. Conductors complain, reasonably, that the remaining thirty minutes, including a fugue and a long Viennese waltz, are almost never heard.","Play the opening, then keep going: the fugue for the men of science and the waltz that follows are the parts nobody knows. Fritz Reiner's 1954 Chicago recording is the one that sold the piece and still sounds enormous. Listen for the organ, which many recordings under-record; if you cannot feel the first note, the balance is wrong."],"richard-strauss-rosenkavalier-trio":["Near the end of the third act three high voices sing at once and at cross purposes: the Marschallin giving up her young lover, Octavian caught between gratitude and desire, Sophie bewildered by both. The words are largely lost, deliberately, in the overlap, while the orchestra holds a slow harmonic ground beneath. It resolves into a plain, almost nursery-simple duet for the young couple, after which a small page runs on to retrieve a dropped handkerchief and the opera ends.","After the scandals of Salome and Elektra, Strauss and his librettist Hugo von Hofmannsthal deliberately built a comedy, set in an invented Vienna of the 1740s complete with waltzes that the real Maria Theresa never heard. The premiere at Dresden in January 1911 under Ernst von Schuch was a public event on a scale opera rarely reaches; special trains were run from Berlin, and the work paid for itself many times over.","The trio is in the Marschallin's key, the one she sang in during her Act One monologue about clocks and time, so her renunciation is musically prepared two hours in advance. Her line is the lowest of the three and sinks steadily as she withdraws. Around it, the shimmering celesta, flutes and high harmonics of the silver rose return, an artificial sound Strauss invented for an object that does not exist.","It was the biggest commercial success of Strauss's life and, to modernists, the moment the composer of Elektra changed sides; the charge of reaction has followed the opera ever since. Against it stands the Marschallin, now among the most admired roles in opera, and the argument that the anachronistic waltzes are a knowing fake. Schwarzkopf under Karajan in 1956 remains the recording others are measured against.","Do not start at the trio. Hear the Marschallin's first-act monologue about stopping the clocks in the middle of the night, then come to the trio and the effect doubles. Listen for which voice is lowest. Then let the final duet and the handkerchief play out; the ending is a joke and a farewell at once."],"richard-strauss-four-last-songs-im-abendrot":["Nine minutes for soprano and a very large orchestra used very quietly. Eichendorff's poem describes an old couple who have walked hand in hand through joy and trouble and now rest above a quiet valley at sunset; two larks climb into the dusk. Two flutes trill for the larks. The voice asks whether this, perhaps, is death, and holds the question; the orchestra answers with a rising and falling figure, and the flutes are still trilling after the singing has stopped.","Written in Switzerland in 1948, where Strauss was living in hotels at 84 while a denazification tribunal considered his years as president of the Reich Music Chamber and his efforts to protect his Jewish daughter-in-law and grandchildren. The Munich and Dresden opera houses he had worked in were rubble; he had just written Metamorphosen in response. He died in 1949 without hearing these songs, and never chose their title or their order.","The orchestra's answer to the question about death is a self-quotation: it is the transfiguration theme from his own tone poem Tod und Verklarung, written in 1889, fifty-nine years earlier, so an old man replies to himself as a young one. The larks outlast the couple: the flutes go on trilling after the voice has finished, and the last sound in the piece belongs to the birds, not the people.","The publisher assembled four songs, invented the title Four Last Songs and fixed the running order after Strauss's death; a fifth late song, Malven, surfaced in 1984 and unsettled the tidy narrative. Kirsten Flagstad and Furtwangler gave the premiere in London in 1950. The hardest question is not musical: whether this is honest valediction or an evasion of the years he spent accommodating a regime.","Read the Eichendorff poem first; it is eight lines and the whole song is in them. Listen for the flutes at the very end. Schwarzkopf with Szell in 1965 and Jessye Norman take opposite views of weight and pace. Then play Tod und Verklarung and catch the quotation coming back."],"richard-strauss-don-juan":["Seventeen minutes in one unbroken span, beginning mid-leap: an upward rush of strings with no introduction at all, as though the piece had already been running before the audience arrived. A swaggering horn theme establishes the hero. Two long love episodes intervene, one carried by a solo oboe over hushed strings. The hero's theme returns in unison horns at full cry, and then everything stops: a bare dissonance in the trumpets, a silence, and a few dark notes. He dies of disgust, not in battle.","Strauss was 24 and second conductor at Weimar, the city where Liszt had invented the symphonic poem forty years earlier. His source is not Mozart or Byron but Nikolaus Lenau's unfinished verse drama, in which Don Juan, sated and bored, drops his guard in a duel and lets himself be killed. The Weimar players told Strauss the parts were unplayable; the premiere in November 1889 made him internationally famous overnight.","Orchestral musicians know this piece as a job interview. The oboe solo is among the longest and most exposed in the repertoire, and the horn passages are standard audition excerpts across the world, which means thousands of players have learned these two minutes without ever performing the whole work. The ending is notated silence: Strauss writes the rests out, so the emptiness is measured, not merely stopped.","Success was immediate and international, and conservatives who thought music should not narrate had to argue with full houses. The piece established the tone poem as the genre of the 1890s and made a 25-year-old the most discussed composer in Germany. Modern debate is mostly about tempo: Strauss's own recordings are faster and lighter than the heroic tradition that followed him.","Two places to listen hard: the oboe solo, and the last thirty seconds, where a piece this loud simply stops being alive. Rudolf Kempe's Dresden set is the reference for the tone poems. Then play Till Eulenspiegel and hear the same trick of a single character built from one horn theme."],"richard-strauss-till-eulenspiegels-lustige-streiche":["The strings open as a storyteller clearing his throat: once upon a time. A solo horn then plays Till's tune, a lopsided phrase that will not sit straight in the bar, and a small high clarinet answers with a squeal that is his laugh. Fifteen minutes of episodes follow: riding a horse through market stalls, mocking clergy, courting a woman and being refused, being arrested. A drum roll, a trial, a downward slide and a shriek as he hangs. Then the storyteller returns.","Munich, 1895. Strauss had planned an opera on the German folk trickster and abandoned it, keeping the character for orchestra; the score's subtitle describes it as the old rogue's tale set in rondo form. Franz Wullner conducted the premiere in Cologne in November 1895. Strauss refused to publish a detailed programme, telling one conductor that the audience should be left to work out the jokes for themselves.","Everything is spun from two ideas only, the horn's tune and the clarinet's laugh, twisted through variation after variation, which is why the piece hangs together despite being a string of pranks. The execution is orchestrated as a physical event: a wind instrument's squeal cut off, then a downward glissando. And the frame matters, since the storyteller's music returns after the hanging, so Till survives inside the telling.","A hit at its premiere and never out of the repertoire since. The horn solo is one of the instrument's great tests and a fixture of orchestral auditions. Strauss's German folk hero was later put to work by the cultural apparatus of the Third Reich, a use his own conduct in the 1930s makes uncomfortable rather than irrelevant. Conductors argue mainly about how broad the comedy should be.","Do not chase the story too literally; listen instead for the two themes coming back in disguise. The clarinet's laugh is the easiest thing to track. Kempe for wit, and any live recording for the horn player's nerve. Then try Peter and the Wolf, which learned its narrative technique here."],"richard-strauss-salome-dance-seven-veils":["Nine minutes of orchestra alone, dropped into a one-act opera that otherwise never stops talking. Salome dances for her stepfather in exchange for a promise he has already made. A drum tattoo opens it; an oboe writhes through an eastern-sounding scale; the music passes through waltz episodes, slows to something almost tender, and works up to a series of shrieking climaxes before stopping dead. She has finished, and she asks for the head of Jokanaan on a silver charger.","Strauss set Hedwig Lachmann's German translation of Oscar Wilde's French play, cutting the text himself rather than employing a librettist. The premiere at Dresden in December 1905 was a scandal and a triumph together. Vienna's censor blocked it, and the Metropolitan Opera gave a single performance in January 1907 before withdrawing it under pressure. The royalties paid for Strauss's villa at Garmisch, a fact he enjoyed pointing out.","Strauss composed the dance last, after the rest of the opera was finished, and it is stitched together from motifs the audience has already heard, so the dance is a summary of the drama rather than a diversion. The score requires a dramatic soprano capable of a murderous vocal role also to dance for nine minutes; nothing in it forbids using a double, and most houses do.","Censorship followed it around Europe; Mahler fought and failed to get it staged in Vienna. Modern productions have to solve a harder problem than the censors did: the number is written as a striptease performed for a stepfather, and directors now stage it as anything from literal to nightmarish. Musically it is sometimes dismissed as the opera's cheapest stretch, though the orchestration is ferociously accomplished.","Hear it inside the opera, with Herod's pleading before it and Salome's demand after, not as a concert item where it makes no sense. Listen for the exact bar where the music stops. Birgit Nilsson under Solti is the reference recording for the whole score."],"richard-strauss-ein-heldenleben":["Forty-five minutes in six linked sections without a break. The hero strides in on a huge theme for horns and cellos that covers most of the orchestra's range in a few bars. His critics arrive as chattering woodwinds and two sour tubas, all talking at once and none listening. His companion is a solo violin, given a long unaccompanied stretch in which she flirts, nags, sulks and finally softens. Then a battle, with offstage trumpets and percussion, works of peace, and a quiet close for violin and horn.","Written in 1898, in Strauss's last year in Munich before he moved to the Berlin Court Opera. He was 34, wealthy, married to the soprano Pauline de Ahna, and openly writing a self-portrait; the critics caricatured in the second section were the Berlin press, and he alternately denied and half-admitted the autobiography in letters. He conducted the premiere in Frankfurt in March 1899.","The works of peace section is a catalogue of his own back catalogue: some thirty quotations from Don Juan, Till Eulenspiegel, Also sprach Zarathustra, Tod und Verklarung, Don Quixote and even his failed first opera Guntram, paraded as trophies. The solo violin is not a decoration but a character with dialogue, and the score marks her moods in words, so the concertmaster is effectively cast in a role.","Accusations of colossal vanity started immediately and have never stopped; Romain Rolland told Strauss so to his face in their correspondence. The piece fell out of favour in the mid-century as bombast and came back as a virtuoso showpiece for orchestras that want to prove something. The concertmaster's solo is one of the great orchestral tests and often the reason a recording is remembered.","Listen first for the concertmaster, who is playing a person rather than a cadenza. Then, in the works of peace, try to catch the self-quotations; even three or four is enough for the joke to land. Reiner in Chicago and a live Berlin recording show the two available temperaments, precision and sheer weight."],"richard-strauss-morgen":["Four minutes, and the singer is silent for the first quarter of them. The piano, or in the orchestral version a solo violin, plays a slowly rising melody alone; when the voice finally enters it does so in the middle of a phrase, not at its start, and never sings the tune the instrument is playing. The poem promises that tomorrow the sun will shine again and two lovers will walk down to the shore. The instrument finishes the sentence the voice abandons.","One of four songs published as Opus 27 and given to the soprano Pauline de Ahna on their wedding day, 10 September 1894. Strauss was 30 and had just taken up a post at the Berlin Court Opera; Pauline sang them for years, and their marriage, by all accounts combative, lasted 55 years. The poet, John Henry Mackay, was a Scottish-born German anarchist writer, an unlikely source for a wedding present. Strauss orchestrated the song in 1897 with a solo violin taking the piano's line.","The voice never states the melody. It moves around and beneath the instrumental line, which is why the song feels like something overheard rather than performed. Mackay's poem begins with the word And, opening in the middle of a thought, and Strauss's setting mirrors that exactly by starting the accompaniment before there is anything to accompany and ending it after the singing stops.","One of the most recorded songs in German, and one of the most easily oversweetened; the orchestral version with a soaring solo violin tips some listeners into resistance, while the piano version keeps the reticence Strauss wrote. Singers disagree about vibrato and pace to an unusual degree, since the vocal line is so plain that any indulgence shows.","Time the wait before the voice enters, and notice that you have already heard the whole tune by then. Try both versions in a row, piano then orchestra. Fischer-Dieskau, Schwarzkopf and Jessye Norman offer very different amounts of restraint, and the restrained ones tend to win."],"amy-beach-gaelic-symphony-second-movement":["The second of four movements in a symphony in E minor, and the one that makes the case most economically. A solo oboe sings a lilting siciliana over plucked strings, unhurried and slightly melancholy; the middle breaks into a quick, light scherzo that scatters the tune through the woodwinds; then the oboe returns and the movement closes as quietly as it opened. Around eight minutes, and every theme in it comes from Irish and Scottish folk melody.","Boston in the mid-1890s. Dvorak, then directing the National Conservatory in New York, had publicly told American composers to build a national music on Black spirituals and Native American song. Beach answered in the Boston press that the population she came from was largely of British and Irish descent, and that its inherited songs were its folk music, then wrote a symphony to demonstrate it. The Boston Symphony premiered it under Emil Paur in October 1896.","The tunes came from a printed anthology of Gaelic melodies rather than from any field collecting, which is part of the argument: Beach is claiming an inheritance through books, as an immigrant culture does. She also draws on her own earlier song writing elsewhere in the symphony, so the folk material and her own voice are deliberately hard to separate.","Boston's critics were largely admiring, though most framed the achievement as remarkable for a woman; George Whitefield Chadwick wrote to congratulate her and called her one of the boys, which was meant and taken as high praise. Then a century of near-silence, as her whole output dropped from view. Recordings from the 1990s onward, and a wave of programming since, have put it back into the American repertoire.","Start here rather than with the large first movement; the oboe theme is the fastest way into her sound. Then play the largo of Dvorak's New World Symphony from three years earlier and hear two answers to the same question about what American music should be made of."],"amy-beach-piano-quintet-f":["Three movements, about twenty-seven minutes, for piano and string quartet. The strings begin alone, slow and searching, before the piano arrives with a crash and takes charge; the writing for it is a virtuoso's, since she wrote it for herself to play. The slow movement floats a long duet between violin and cello over rippling keyboard figures. The finale drives hard and then turns back at the last moment to the searching music of the opening.","Written in 1907, four years after the death of her husband, Dr Henry Harris Aubrey Beach, whose marriage terms had restricted her public performing for twenty-five years. Widowed at 35, she was rebuilding a career as a concert pianist and played the piano part herself at the first performance in Boston. The models are explicit and contemporary: Brahms's chamber music and Cesar Franck's chromatic harmony, both of which Boston audiences knew well.","The string phrase that opens the work is drawn from Brahms's own Piano Quintet in F minor, transplanted and shifted up a semitone into F sharp minor. It is homage and correction at once, and it recurs, transformed, at the end of the finale, so the whole piece is framed by another composer's idea taken over and finished differently.","Well received in its day and then forgotten along with the rest of her output for most of the twentieth century. The revival since the 1990s treats it as among the strongest American chamber works written before Ives, and the old charge that it is derivative Brahms now runs into the counter-argument that the borrowing is deliberate, signposted and structural.","Begin with the slow movement, then hear the whole thing. Play the opening minute of Brahms's F minor Quintet immediately before it and the relationship is unmistakable. American chamber ensembles have recorded it repeatedly in the last two decades, and the newer accounts tend to be faster and less reverent."],"amy-beach-romance-violin-piano":["Six minutes built on one long arching tune, handed first to the violin over rippling piano figuration that never settles into simple chords. The middle section climbs into the instrument's highest register and thickens into double stops for a short, full-throated climax, then the opening melody returns quietly, an octave lower, and the piece closes without a final flourish.","Written for Maud Powell, the first American violinist of international rank, who premiered it with Beach at the piano at the World's Columbian Exposition in Chicago in 1893, in the Women's Building, at a festival that gathered music by women from many countries. Beach was 26 and largely self-taught as a composer, having been refused a conventional training and having taught herself orchestration from treatises she translated.","The violin writing is Powell's rather than generic. She had premiered concertos across Europe and was known for a big, singing tone in the high register, and the piece is built to show exactly that: the double stops and the sustained top notes at the climax are placed where her strengths were, so the Romance is a portrait of a player as much as a mood piece.","It sold heavily as sheet music, was played across America by amateurs and professionals, and then vanished with the rest of her catalogue. Violinists rebuilding the American repertoire brought it back in the last thirty years. Some hear a salon piece, others the same long-breathed melodic instinct that makes her songs work; nobody disputes that it is grateful to play.","Six minutes, so play it twice. Listen for the piano, which is doing far more than accompanying. Then compare Clara Schumann's Three Romances from forty years earlier, written for a violinist she loved, for the same genre used to the same purpose."],"amy-beach-year-s-at-spring":["Ninety seconds, and almost all of it climbing. Browning's lines from Pippa Passes, the year at the spring and the day at the morning, are set as one continuous ascent with no verse structure to rest on: the piano hammers full chords underneath, the voice rises step by step, and the whole thing arrives on a high, ringing declaration that all's right with the world. Then it stops, having repeated almost nothing.","Published in 1899 as the first of Three Browning Songs, the most commercially successful music Beach ever wrote. Robert Browning was the fashionable poet of the American reading public, and Browning societies met in cities across the country. The song was taken up by the women's music clubs whose network of concerts and commissions sustained American composers, and Beach in particular, when institutions would not.","There is no repeat to lean on: the song is a single sixteen-bar arc, which is why it feels breathless and why singers find it harder than its length suggests. The piano part is more demanding than the vocal line. And Browning's optimism is borrowed from a scene in which Pippa sings it while walking past people who are plotting a murder she never learns about, a context the song leaves entirely outside.","It sold in vast numbers and became something close to an anthem for American women's clubs, then was held against her as parlour music by mid-century critics who valued difficulty. Recent scholarship treats the Browning songs as her most concentrated writing, and sopranos still use this one as an encore, where its ninety seconds of pure ascent do exactly what an encore should.","Play it twice in a row; it is over before the ear has finished. Listen to the piano rather than the voice on the second pass. Then read the scene in Pippa Passes, which changes what the last line sounds like more than any performance can."],"janacek-sinfonietta-opening-fanfare":["Two and a half minutes for brass and timpani alone: nine trumpets, two bass trumpets, two tenor tubas and drums, with no strings and no woodwind anywhere. Three short ideas are stated, stacked on top of each other, and repeated at different pitches without development. The scoring is deliberately raw and open, the chords spaced like an outdoor band's, and the rhythm keeps landing a beat away from where the ear expects. The same fanfare returns, with the full orchestra swarming under it, to end the fifth movement.","Janacek wrote it in 1926, at 72, for a mass gymnastics festival of the Sokol movement, the patriotic Czech athletic organisation, in a republic only eight years old. He said the idea came from hearing a military band play in a park at Pisek while sitting with Kamila Stosslova, the married woman 38 years younger with whom he was obsessed. He first called the piece a Military Sinfonietta and attached Brno landmarks to its five movements.","The fanfare players are extras. Nine trumpeters sit through the middle of the piece with nothing to do, waiting for the finale, which is an expensive way to write and one reason the Sinfonietta is programmed less often than its fame suggests. The metre is the other oddity: the phrases will not divide evenly, so conductors have to decide where the beat actually falls.","Success came at once, at home and then abroad through Talich and later Kubelik, and the piece has never left the repertoire. Its strangest afterlife came in 2009, when Haruki Murakami built the opening of 1Q84 around it and sent recordings up the sales charts in Japan. Performance debate now centres on whether the brass should sound like a professional orchestra or like the amateur band that inspired it.","Play the fanfare, then jump straight to the last two minutes of the finale and hear it come back with the whole orchestra underneath; the piece is a frame, and hearing both ends first makes the middle make sense. Charles Mackerras's recordings are the standard, and the loudest available."],"janacek-string-quartet-no-2-intimate-letters":["Four movements, about twenty-six minutes, in which almost nothing behaves the way quartet writing is supposed to. Short phrases are repeated obsessively rather than developed; the music stops mid-gesture and starts somewhere else; the viola is pushed high and shrill; players are asked to scrape near the bridge for a thin, glassy sound. Tenderness and violence sit a bar apart, and the finale hurtles to an ending that arrives before the argument is settled.","Written in three weeks in January and February 1928, when Janacek was 73. He told Kamila Stosslova in his letters that the quartet was about her; he wrote her more than seven hundred over eleven years, almost none of them answered in kind. He died that August of pneumonia after a search for her son on the Hukvaldy hills, and never heard the piece; the Moravian Quartet gave the premiere in Brno five weeks later.","He first wrote the second part for viola d'amore, an old instrument whose extra sympathetic strings vibrate untouched, so that the woman in the piece would literally resonate without being played. It proved impractical and the part went to ordinary viola, which changes the sound of the whole quartet. His working title was Love Letters; he changed it to Intimate Letters rather than hand his feelings, as he put it, to fools.","Janacek revised it up to the last weeks of his life and an earlier version survives, so ensembles must choose. A handful now reinstate a viola d'amore for at least part of the work, with mixed results. Once regarded as the eccentric late work of a provincial, it is now placed with Bartok's quartets as one of the century's essential contributions to the medium.","Do not expect argument and resolution; expect interruption. Start with the second movement, the most immediately gripping, then hear the whole in order. The Czech ensembles, the Talich, Skampa and Pavel Haas quartets, play it with a roughness that suits it better than polish does."],"janacek-overgrown-path-good-night":["Three minutes, and the seventh of the ten short pieces that make up the first book. A soft repeating figure high in the right hand ticks away like a music box or a bell heard through a wall, and never stops or changes; underneath it a plain melody rises, turns and sinks. The piece circles the same handful of bars, refuses to build to anything, and ends on the same high figure, thinning out rather than closing.","The pieces began around 1900 as contributions to an anthology of music for harmonium, the instrument in every Moravian parlour, when Janacek was an unknown provincial teacher and choirmaster in Brno with no reputation beyond it. He assembled and published the ten pieces as a piano cycle in 1911. His daughter Olga had died in 1903 at twenty, and he attached the cycle's grief to her in later remarks, though several pieces predate her illness.","The method that would drive everything he wrote afterwards is already here in miniature: fix one small figure, never vary it, and move the harmony underneath until the same notes mean something different. The titles were added after the music was written, so the programme is retrospective; the pieces were sounds first and memories second.","The cycle spread slowly and is now the most played of his piano music, though pianists still disagree about how much of the harmonium's plainness to keep. The biographical reading, that the whole book mourns Olga, is contested precisely because of the dates. What is not contested is that these ten pieces contain the seed of the late operas.","Play the whole of Book One in order; it takes half an hour and the individual pieces gain enormously from their neighbours. Rudolf Firkusny, who studied with Janacek as a boy, is the direct line; Leif Ove Andsnes and other modern pianists find more colour and less plainness."],"janacek-taras-bulba-finale":["The third and last movement of an orchestral rhapsody, about eleven minutes, titled The Prophecy and Death of Taras Bulba. The old Cossack, nailed to a tree and burned alive, prophesies that a Tsar will rise from Russian soil whom no power on earth will resist. The music builds from a keening repeated figure through a grinding march to a final blaze in which organ, bells and full brass hammer one rising phrase over and over, louder each time, without ever developing it.","Janacek took the story from Gogol's novella and began the score in 1915, in the middle of a war in which Czech conscripts were being sent to fight Russians on behalf of Vienna. He finished it in 1918 as the Habsburg empire collapsed and Czechoslovakia was founded. He was a lifelong Russophile who founded a Russian club in Brno, taught himself the language and sent his daughter to St Petersburg. The premiere waited until Brno in October 1921.","The ending does not resolve so much as insist: one phrase repeated at rising volume, which is his speech-melody principle scaled up from a single voice to a crowd. The organ, rare in a concert orchestra and never used in the earlier movements, is held back for the prophecy alone, so its entry is the moment a man's death becomes a nation's argument.","Celebrating Slavic solidarity was politically risky while Austria still ruled, which is part of why the premiere waited six years. Critics have always found the three movements unevenly weighted, with the finale dwarfing what comes before, and some conductors compensate by driving the first two. It is now heard both as war music and as the score in which Janacek's orchestral voice arrives complete.","Read the last chapter of Gogol's novella first; the piece is unusually literal. Then listen for the organ's entry, which is the structural event of the movement. Mackerras with Czech orchestras is the benchmark, and the piece rewards volume more than most."],"janacek-glagolitic-mass-intrada":["Under two minutes of orchestra with no singing at all, framing a Mass that otherwise has soloists, chorus and a ferocious organ solo. Dotted rhythms are hammered out by brass over timpani, bright and metallic, repeated at different pitches rather than developed, more processional than liturgical. It sounds like a fanfare for an open-air ceremony, which is roughly what it is, and the same music returns as the last thing heard in the work.","Composed in three weeks in the late summer of 1926 at the spa town of Luhacovice, when Janacek was 72. The text is not Latin but Old Church Slavonic, the liturgical language of the ninth-century mission of Cyril and Methodius to the Slavs, and he meant the piece as a celebration of Slavic culture and the new Czechoslovak state rather than of the Church. The premiere was in Brno in December 1927.","Where the Intrada belongs is a real editorial puzzle. In the version Janacek first wrote it opens the work as well as closing it, and that original also had wilder timpani parts and irregular metres that were smoothed out before the premiere. Paul Wingfield's reconstruction of the 1990s restored them, so there are now two quite different Masses in circulation under one title.","The premiere succeeded immediately. When a reviewer described him as a devout old man, Janacek wrote back to deny both halves, insisting he was neither old nor a believer, which has shaped every discussion of the piece since. Most performances still use the smoothed published score; the reconstructed original is harsher, harder to play and, to many ears, more convincing.","Hear the Intrada, then skip to the organ solo near the end, which is the wildest music in any twentieth-century Mass, then return for the closing Intrada. Mackerras recorded the reconstructed original version; compare a few minutes of it with a traditional performance and the differences are obvious."],"delibes-sylvia":["A ballet in three acts, about an hour and three quarters, on a pastoral tale from Tasso: the huntress Sylvia, sworn to Diana, is wounded by Eros and eventually allowed her shepherd. Delibes writes for a large orchestra with four horns, harp and a battery of percussion, and gives the dances real symphonic weight. The famous numbers are the brassy Cortège de Bacchus, a long horn melody in the nocturne, and a pizzicato polka for strings alone.","Delibes had already written Coppélia for the Paris Opéra in 1870; Sylvia was the follow-up, staged at the new Palais Garnier on 14 June 1876 with choreography by Louis Mérante and the Italian ballerina Rita Sangalli in the title role. French ballet was then in decline as an art form, kept alive as a spectacle for subscribers. Delibes's ambition was to make the pit as interesting as the stage, and he scored accordingly.","Listen for how little the score behaves like dance accompaniment. Themes are assigned to characters and then transformed rather than repeated, and the harmony shifts under a tune to change its meaning — the technique of contemporary opera, applied to divertissements. The pizzicato polka is a joke about orchestration: no bow touches a string for the whole number, and the effect is of an orchestra tiptoeing.","The premiere was a moderate success and the ballet dropped out of the repertory for decades; only Frederick Ashton's 1952 staging for the Royal Ballet, made for Margot Fonteyn, restored it. Tchaikovsky, who saw the score in 1877, thought it far better than his own Swan Lake and said so more than once. Modern productions usually cut and reorder the third act, which is more procession than plot.","Start with the Cortège de Bacchus, then double back to the nocturne and follow the horn line as it wanders. Compare the pizzicato polka with any orchestral number by Delibes's contemporaries and notice how much wittier the scoring is. A good full recording under a conductor who takes the tempos as dance tempos rather than concert ones repays the length."],"tchaikovsky-swan-lake":["Four acts, around two and a half hours, telling of Prince Siegfried, the swan-maiden Odette and the sorcerer's daughter who impersonates her. The signature sound is an oboe melody over rippling harp and strings, first heard as the swans appear and returning at every crisis, finally blazing out on full brass. Around it Tchaikovsky sets national dances, a long violin-and-cello duet for the lakeside adagio, and a set of thirty-two whipping turns for the impostor.","The Bolshoi Theatre in Moscow commissioned it in 1875 for a modest fee. Tchaikovsky, then thirty-five and teaching harmony at the Moscow Conservatory, had never written a full evening of ballet and studied the scores of Adam and Delibes first. The Russian ballet orchestra was used to accompanying rather than competing, and the theatre's musicians reportedly found the writing difficult. The premiere, on 20 February 1877 with choreography by Julius Reisinger, was poorly danced and thinly reviewed.","The swan theme is built on the plainest possible rising minor arpeggio, which is exactly why it survives transformation — it can be a lament, a waltz, or a fanfare without losing its identity. Listen also for the moment the same music returns in the major at the very end: whether the lovers die, ascend or simply escape depends on which ending a production chooses, and the score has been made to support all three.","For eighteen years the ballet was considered a failure of Tchaikovsky's, not of its choreographer. The 1895 Mariinsky revival by Marius Petipa and Lev Ivanov, two years after the composer's death, cut, reordered and reorchestrated the score under Riccardo Drigo, and it is essentially that version the world still dances. Scholars now argue over how much of the 1877 original is worth restoring; a few companies have tried.","Begin with the Act II lakeside scene — oboe, then the violin and cello duet — and hear how long Tchaikovsky is willing to let a slow number last. Then jump to the Act III Black Swan music for the contrast in colour. Any decent full recording works; if you can, watch a staging rather than only listening, because the score's proportions were designed around bodies."],"tchaikovsky-sleeping-beauty":["A prologue and three acts, close to three hours, on Perrault's tale. The whole evening turns on two ideas stated in the first minute: a snarling chromatic figure for the wicked fairy Carabosse and a broad, rising melody for the Lilac Fairy who cancels her curse. Between them Tchaikovsky writes the grandest sequence of formal dances in the repertory, including a long garland waltz, a violin solo for the Rose Adagio, and a final act of fairy-tale character numbers.","Ivan Vsevolozhsky, director of the Imperial Theatres, planned the piece himself as a French court spectacle for Alexander III's St Petersburg, and set Marius Petipa to send Tchaikovsky detailed instructions specifying the character, metre and exact bar-count of every number. Tchaikovsky, far from resenting the constraint, worked faster than usual and called it some of his best music. The premiere at the Mariinsky Theatre on 15 January 1890 cost more than any previous Russian ballet.","The score is an argument between two harmonic worlds, and it is audible: Carabosse's music slides by half-steps and never settles, the Lilac Fairy's moves in clean, wide steps and always cadences. Listen for how the Lilac Fairy theme is quietly present in the panorama that carries the prince to the castle. The Act III wedding also quotes a French court tune, planting Versailles inside a Russian imperial evening.","The Tsar's only recorded comment was 'very nice'. Critics found it too symphonic for dancing, a complaint that dogged Tchaikovsky's ballets for a generation. Serge Diaghilev's lavish 1921 London staging nearly bankrupted him but converted Western opinion, and the Royal Ballet's 1946 reopening of Covent Garden with it settled the work's status. Debate now concerns how much of Petipa's choreography survives authentically and how heavily to cut the mime.","Listen first to the prologue: three minutes gives you both governing themes and the whole plot in miniature. Then the Rose Adagio, where a single violin line has to sustain an unnaturally slow tempo — that difficulty is the point. Compare the panorama with Tchaikovsky's symphonic slow movements. A complete recording under a conductor from the ballet pit tends to breathe better than a concert one."],"tchaikovsky-nutcracker":["Two acts, about ninety minutes. The first is a Christmas party, a battle between toy soldiers and mice, and a journey through a snowstorm sung by a wordless children's choir; the second is almost plotless, a sequence of short character dances — Spanish, Arabian, Chinese, Russian, a reed-pipe trio — framing a waltz of flowers and a duet built on a slowly descending scale. The celesta, then brand new, gives the Sugar Plum Fairy her glassy tune.","Commissioned as a double bill with the opera Iolanta for the Mariinsky, on a story by E. T. A. Hoffmann as adapted by Dumas. Tchaikovsky disliked the scenario, complained about writing to order for children and toys, and worked on it while his sister was dying. He heard the celesta in Paris in 1891 and had one shipped to St Petersburg in secret, afraid that Glazunov or Rimsky-Korsakov would use the new instrument first.","The grand duet is built on nothing but a descending scale, stated plainly and then harmonised differently on each repetition — the simplest possible material carrying the evening's emotional weight. Listen too for the snowflake chorus: children singing without words, used as an orchestral colour rather than as voices. And the party scene's toy instruments are real ones, ordered from Paris, not orchestral imitations.","Reviews of the December 1892 premiere were mixed to hostile, mostly about the choreography, which Lev Ivanov took over when Petipa fell ill; the concert suite Tchaikovsky extracted was an immediate hit. The ballet's dominance is a much later, largely American phenomenon, following George Balanchine's 1954 New York staging. Modern productions increasingly rework the Act II divertissements, whose national caricatures are now discussed as a problem rather than a convention.","Skip the suite and hear the whole second half of Act I: the battle, the transformation, then the snow. The moment the children's voices enter over the strings is the one to wait for. Compare the celesta solo with the harp writing around it. Any recording from a company orchestra with a real theatre acoustic will suit better than a very polished studio one."],"glazunov-raymonda":["Three acts and an apotheosis, a little over two hours, on a thin medieval plot: a Provençal noblewoman waits for her crusader betrothed while a Saracen knight courts her. The music is far better than the story. Act I has a long dream sequence with a violin solo; Act II turns Spanish and Middle Eastern; Act III is a Hungarian wedding, with a czardas, and dances whose accents fall on the fourth beat rather than the first.","Written for the Imperial Theatres and Marius Petipa, then nearly eighty, who again supplied bar-by-bar instructions. Glazunov was thirty-two, professor at the St Petersburg Conservatory and heir apparent to Rimsky-Korsakov's orchestral tradition. The premiere at the Mariinsky on 19 January 1898 starred the Italian ballerina Pierina Legnani. It was the last full-length ballet Petipa created that has stayed in the repertory, and the last major score of imperial Russian ballet.","The Hungarian third act is not decoration but structural: Glazunov saves his most rhythmically odd material for last, so the evening gets stranger as it proceeds rather than grander. Listen for the czardas rhythm displaced against the bar line in the final variations — dancers count these in a way that fights the music deliberately. The Act I dream is scored with a piano in the orchestra, unusual for the period.","Well received in 1898 and never quite forgotten in Russia, Raymonda was slow to travel because its libretto embarrassed twentieth-century producers, and the Saracen characterisation is now openly criticised. Rudolf Nureyev's stagings from the 1960s and Balanchine's Raymonda Variations kept the music alive in the West. Most companies outside Russia still perform the Act III suite alone, which is why the score's real ambition is under-appreciated.","Go straight to Act III and count along with the czardas until you lose the beat — that is the intended effect. Then hear the Act I dream scene for the violin solo, which is closer to a concerto than to ballet accompaniment. Compare it with Tchaikovsky's Sleeping Beauty of eight years earlier; the orchestral sound is heavier and the harmony richer."],"bartok-miraculous-mandarin":["A one-act pantomime lasting about half an hour, on a scenario by Menyhért Lengyel: three thugs force a girl to lure men into a room to be robbed. The third victim, a Mandarin, cannot be killed — smothered, stabbed and hanged, he keeps glowing — until the girl embraces him. Bartók opens with a screaming traffic-jam of overlapping woodwind and brass, and builds the seduction dances over a clarinet line that grows more chromatic with each repetition.","Begun in 1918 in the last months of the Habsburg empire and finished in the chaos after the collapse of the Hungarian Soviet Republic, when Bartók's position in Budapest was precarious. Expressionist theatre was then at its height in central Europe, and the scenario's urban brothel setting belongs with the same appetite for violence and the modern city. Political conditions delayed the score for years; Bartók orchestrated it in 1924.","The Mandarin's own theme barely changes — a stiff pentatonic shape that stays outside the chromatic churn around it, which is what makes his refusal to die audible rather than merely staged. Listen for the wordless chorus entering only at the end, as an eerie colour. The chase fugue near the close is strict counterpoint used for pure panic, a device Bartók reused in his string quartets.","The Cologne premiere on 27 November 1926 caused an uproar and the mayor, Konrad Adenauer, had it withdrawn after one night; a Budapest staging was blocked until after Bartók's death. For decades the concert suite, which ends before the final scene, stood in for the whole. Opinion has completely reversed, and the complete score with chorus is now regarded as Bartók's most violent and most modern orchestral achievement.","Insist on the complete version, not the suite — the ending is the point of the piece. Listen first to the opening minute for the sound of a city, then to the three seduction dances, tracking how the clarinet line loses its footing each time. Pair it with Stravinsky's Rite of Spring, written six years earlier, and note how much more urban and less ritual Bartók's violence sounds."],"poulenc-les-biches":["A ballet in eight short numbers, half an hour long, with no plot: twenty young women and three athletic men drift through a drawing room on a summer afternoon and nothing quite happens. Poulenc writes a brisk overture, a rondeau, a mock-solemn adagietto for a woman in blue velvet, and a final galop. A small chorus sings fragments of seventeenth-century French verse, mostly bawdy, from the pit.","Serge Diaghilev commissioned it for the Ballets Russes' Monte Carlo season, having heard Poulenc's early Rapsodie nègre. Poulenc was twenty-four. Bronislava Nijinska choreographed; Marie Laurencin designed the pastel décor. The premiere on 6 January 1924 came in the middle of Diaghilev's post-war turn away from Russian subjects towards contemporary French chic, the same impulse that had produced Satie's Parade and Milhaud's Le train bleu.","The title means both 'the does' and, in Parisian slang, something closer to 'the darlings', and the ambiguity is the whole subject — the ballet is about desire that will not declare its object. Musically the joke is on the eighteenth century: the adagietto is a straight-faced Mozartian aria that keeps sliding into wrong keys. Listen for the chorus's texts, which are far coarser than the polite music carrying them.","An immediate hit in 1924 and the piece that made Poulenc's reputation beyond the salon. Critics then and since have argued about whether the elegance is a mask for real feeling or simply good manners; Poulenc's later religious works have made most listeners decide in favour of the mask. Nijinska's choreography, unusually, survives in reconstruction, so the original ambiguity can still be seen as well as heard.","Hear the version with chorus if you can find it, then compare the purely orchestral suite to see what is lost. Start with the adagietto and wait for the harmony to slip. Set it beside Bartók's Miraculous Mandarin from the same years: the same subject, sex in a room, treated as farce rather than horror."],"copland-billy-the-kid":["A one-act ballet of about thirty-five minutes, framed by a procession of pioneers walking west over a plain, hollow-sounding open harmony. Between the two processions come a frontier-town street scene, a gunfight in which Billy kills the man who shot his mother, a card game, a night in the desert, Billy's capture, escape and death. Copland quotes real cowboy songs — 'Git Along Little Dogies', 'The Old Chisholm Trail', 'Goodbye Old Paint' — but sets them off-kilter.","Lincoln Kirstein commissioned it for his touring company Ballet Caravan and handed Copland a book of cowboy songs; Eugene Loring choreographed and danced the title role at the premiere in Chicago on 16 October 1938. Copland, a Brooklyn-born modernist who had studied with Nadia Boulanger in Paris, was consciously simplifying his language for a wider American audience during the Depression — the same decision behind El Salón México and, later, Rodeo.","The famous 'open prairie' sound comes from spacing rather than melody: Copland leaves the middle of the chord empty, so the same notes that would be ordinary in close position sound like distance. Listen to what he does to the borrowed tunes — 'Goodbye Old Paint' arrives in two keys at once during the card game, and the gunfight is built from a cowboy song hammered into a percussion ostinato.","Reviewers in 1938 recognised at once that an American sound had been found, and the concert suite spread faster than the ballet. Later criticism has been harder on the scenario, which romanticises an outlaw and stages Mexican characters as local colour. Copland himself worried that the accessible style would be mistaken for his whole output. The suite is now played far more often than the complete ballet, which has the better ending.","Start with the opening procession and listen for the gaps in the harmony rather than the tune. Then the gunfight, for what an orchestra can do with almost no melody. Compare it directly with Rodeo and Appalachian Spring to hear the same technique tightened over six years. A recording of the complete ballet rather than the suite gives you Billy's death, which the suite omits."],"khachaturian-gayane":["A four-act ballet, over two hours, set on an Armenian collective farm: Gayane's husband turns out to be a saboteur, the border guards intervene, and the community celebrates. The score is a sequence of Armenian, Kurdish, Georgian and Russian dances scored with heavy percussion and a large orchestra including saxophone. The best-known number, the Sabre Dance, is ninety seconds of xylophone and trombone glissandi; the Adagio that Kubrick later borrowed is its slow opposite.","Written during the war, when the Kirov Ballet had been evacuated from besieged Leningrad to Perm, then called Molotov, where the premiere took place on 3 December 1942. Khachaturian, an Armenian trained in Moscow, was working within Soviet cultural policy that demanded folk material, optimism and a plot approved by the state. He won a Stalin Prize for it. The libretto has been rewritten repeatedly since, each version quietly removing more politics.","Khachaturian rarely quotes actual folk melodies; he writes new ones over the drone-and-drum textures of Armenian dance, which is why the tunes sound traditional and are not. Listen for the duduk-like wailing lines given to cor anglais and oboe. The Sabre Dance was written in a hurry, at the theatre's insistence, days before the premiere, and Khachaturian resented being remembered for it.","Praised officially in 1942 and popular immediately, the ballet was then rewritten so often — 1952, 1957 and later — that no standard version exists, and companies outside the former Soviet Union almost never stage it. The suites carry its reputation instead. Critics have long treated Khachaturian as a colourist rather than a symphonist; recent writing has been more interested in how he negotiated Armenian identity inside Soviet demands.","Do not start with the Sabre Dance. Begin with the Adagio, then Gayane's first-act solo, and only then the famous ninety seconds, which lands differently once you know the surrounding music. Listen for the cor anglais imitating the Armenian duduk. A Soviet-era recording with a Russian orchestra gives a rougher, more percussive sound than modern Western ones."],"prokofiev-cinderella":["Three acts, about two and a half hours. Prokofiev keeps the classical ballet forms — a grand waltz, variations, a coda — but fills them with his own sour harmony, so the ballroom music tilts. Twelve strokes of midnight are staged as a genuine emergency, with a clock ticking in the percussion, chromatic scales pouring downward and the waltz collapsing. The stepsisters get deliberately clumsy dances; the four seasons fairies get a suite of miniature concertos.","Begun for the Kirov in 1940, interrupted by the German invasion and by work on War and Peace, and finished in 1944. Prokofiev had returned permanently to the Soviet Union in 1936 and was writing under conditions that rewarded lyricism and fairy tale over experiment. The Bolshoi premiere on 21 November 1945, choreographed by Rostislav Zakharov with Olga Lepeshinskaya, came six months after the war ended and was received as a celebration.","Cinderella has no single theme but three, and they do different jobs: one for her loneliness, one for her longing, one that only appears once she is loved. Listen for the amoroso duet, whose melody Prokofiev reuses in his Eighth Piano Sonata. The midnight scene borrows the mechanical grotesquerie of his own earlier ballets, and the stepsisters' music quotes the tuning-up sounds of a bad village band.","The 1945 premiere was a popular success and the Stalin Prize followed, but the choreography was widely thought inferior to the score, a judgement that has stuck. Frederick Ashton's 1948 London version, with two men in drag as the stepsisters, established the ballet in the West; Rudolf Nureyev's 1986 Hollywood setting and Alexei Ratmansky's stagings have since pulled it in other directions. The three orchestral suites are heard more than the ballet.","Go to the end of Act II and listen straight through the midnight scene — three or four minutes in which a ballroom turns into a machine. Then the amoroso duet for the tune at its plainest. Compare the whole with Prokofiev's Romeo and Juliet of a decade earlier: warmer, less angular, and written by a man with less room to manoeuvre."],"bernstein-fancy-free":["A twenty-five-minute ballet for three sailors on shore leave in wartime New York. A blues song plays on a jukebox before the curtain, then a syncopated fanfare launches the action: the sailors meet two women, and to settle who partners whom they dance a competition of three solos — a galop, a slow waltz, and a Latin danzón. A brawl, and the sailors are alone again as a third woman walks past.","Bernstein was twenty-five and had become famous four months earlier by taking over a New York Philharmonic concert at a few hours' notice. Jerome Robbins, a young dancer at Ballet Theatre, brought him the idea; the two worked partly by long-distance telephone, Bernstein playing new numbers down the line. The premiere at the Metropolitan Opera House on 18 April 1944 got twenty-two curtain calls, and the collaboration led straight to the musical On the Town.","The opening drum riff is a rhythm Bernstein reused for the rest of his life; you can hear its cousin in the Prologue to West Side Story thirteen years later. Listen for how the three solos are characterised entirely by rhythm, not melody — the danzón lifts its pattern from Cuban dance records Bernstein had been collecting. The jukebox song is Bernstein's own, written to sound borrowed.","The critics were unanimous in 1944 and the ballet has never left the repertory, but its reputation has been complicated by what followed: Bernstein spent decades worrying that his theatre music disqualified him as a serious composer, and reviewers obliged by treating Fancy Free as charming juvenilia. More recent assessments read it the other way, as the first successful fusion of jazz rhythm with ballet structure in America.","Listen from the jukebox blues straight through the three variations, and try to describe each sailor from his rhythm alone. Then play the Prologue of West Side Story and hear the family resemblance. Bernstein's own recordings, conducted with more swing than most, are the ones to seek; a straight classical reading flattens it."],"britten-prince-of-the-pagodas":["Britten's only full-length ballet, three acts and over two hours, on a King Lear-shaped fairy tale by John Cranko: a good princess and a cruel sister, a salamander prince, a journey to the Pagoda Kingdom. The first two acts are court music, brittle and processional. Then the Pagoda Kingdom arrives and the orchestra changes species entirely: vibraphone, celesta, xylophone, gongs and two harps in layered ostinatos, a Western orchestra doing its best to be a gamelan.","Covent Garden had wanted a Britten ballet for years. He accepted in 1954, then in early 1956 travelled through Bali with Peter Pears and spent a fortnight transcribing gamelan music with the help of Colin McPhee's earlier notations. He came home with notebooks and rebuilt the third act around them, writing under real pressure and conducting the premiere himself on 1 January 1957.","The gamelan material is not vaguely oriental colour: Britten transcribed specific Balinese pieces and reproduced their layered structure, in which slow gongs, a middle melody and fast decorating instruments run simultaneously at different speeds. Listen for the tuned percussion entering in strict proportional layers. The same technique reappears three years later in the Sumidagawa-derived church parable Curlew River and in the Balinese sonorities of Death in Venice.","The 1957 reception was lukewarm: too long, said the critics, and Cranko's scenario was thought muddled. Britten later authorised cuts and never returned to full-length ballet. The score's stock rose after Kenneth MacMillan's 1989 staging with a new scenario, and rose further as scholars traced how much of Britten's late style begins here. It is still performed far less than the music deserves.","Skip to the Act II arrival in the Pagoda Kingdom and listen to nothing but the percussion for two minutes. Then compare with an actual Balinese gamelan recording — the resemblance is startling and deliberate. If the full ballet is too long, the concert suite covers the essentials; look for a recording that keeps the tuned percussion forward in the balance."],"birtwistle-punch-and-judy":["A one-act opera of about an hour and three quarters for five singers and fifteen players, built on the seaside puppet show. Punch murders his child, his wife, a doctor, a lawyer and finally the hangman, and each killing is set as a formal number — a passion chorale, a nightmare, a quest — so the violence arrives on schedule. The band is dominated by wind and percussion, with no violins, and the vocal writing is jagged, high and shouted as often as sung.","Written for the English Opera Group on a commission arranged through Benjamin Britten's Aldeburgh Festival, with a libretto by the pianist Stephen Pruslin. Birtwistle was in his early thirties, teaching school music in Dorset, and belonged to the Manchester group that had set itself against the mild English style Aldeburgh represented. The first performance on 8 June 1968 took place in Britten's own festival, which sharpened the provocation considerably.","The structure is a ritual, not a plot: the same sequence of events repeats four times with variation, like verses of a ballad, so the horror comes from recurrence rather than surprise. Listen for the 'melodramas', short instrumental passages that function as scene-dividers and always use the same instruments. Punch's murders are each preceded by a countdown in the percussion, which tells you what is about to happen before it does.","Britten and Pears reportedly left during the first performance, and several critics called it unlistenable; others recognised at once that post-war English opera had a new direction. It has never become popular but has never gone away either, and is now routinely described as the founding work of British music theatre after Britten. Staging debates centre on how literally to present the puppet-booth frame.","Read a synopsis first; the ritual structure is much clearer when you know the pattern is going to repeat. Begin with the opening melodrama and the first murder, then skip to the hanging. Listen for the wind band's colour with no violins to soften it. A recording with a strong Punch — the part is nearly impossible — matters more than the conducting."],"sondheim-sweeney-todd":["Two and a half hours of almost continuous music, on Christopher Bond's version of a Victorian penny-dreadful: a barber returns from transportation to find his wife destroyed and his daughter taken, and begins cutting throats; his landlady bakes the bodies into pies. The score is dense and contrapuntal — a quartet in which four characters sing different music simultaneously, a waltz that will not resolve, a comic duet about the flavours of various trades — over a recurring plainchant-derived ballad for the chorus.","Sondheim had seen Bond's play in London in 1973 and wanted to write something genuinely frightening, closer to the film scores of Bernard Herrmann than to Broadway. Harold Prince, who directed, insisted on an industrial-revolution frame and a vast factory set. The production opened at the Uris Theatre on 1 March 1979 with Len Cariou and Angela Lansbury, won eight Tony Awards, and lost money.","The Dies irae, the medieval chant for the dead, is buried in the opening ballad and returns throughout — once you have heard it you cannot unhear it under the love music. Listen also for the way Johanna's theme is stolen and sung by three different men, each claiming her. Sondheim wrote the harmonic language deliberately in Herrmann's idiom, having asked him for lessons years earlier and been refused.","Reviews in 1979 were admiring but nervous about whether Broadway audiences would sit through it, and the tour struggled. Opera houses solved the problem: New York City Opera staged it in 1984, and it has since been performed by companies from Chicago to Copenhagen, which reopened the question of what it is. Sondheim always resisted the word opera. Recent small-scale stagings have argued the piece works better with eight players than eighty.","Start with the opening ballad and hold the Dies irae shape in your ear for the rest. Then A Little Priest, a comic number entirely built of puns, and Johanna's quartet, where four vocal lines run at once. Compare an opera-house cast with a Broadway one; the words survive better in the second. Follow a libretto — the rhymes go past very fast."],"ades-powder-her-face":["A chamber opera in two acts, about an hour and three quarters, for four singers who take more than twenty roles between them, and fifteen players. It moves backwards and forwards through the life of a disgraced English duchess, from her wedding to her eviction from a hotel. The band plays tangos, foxtrots and a slow waltz through a distorting filter — accordion, saxophone, bass clarinet — so the dance music of the 1930s arrives smeared and out of tune.","Adès was twenty-three, still a student in Cambridge, when the Almeida Theatre commissioned it; the librettist Philip Hensher based the story on Margaret Campbell, Duchess of Argyll, whose 1963 divorce case had been a scandal of photographic evidence and public humiliation. The premiere at the Cheltenham Festival on 1 July 1995 made Adès instantly famous in Britain, partly for a wordless aria unmistakably depicting a sexual act.","The tango is the opera's engine: nearly every number is a warped dance, and the further the Duchess falls the more the dances lose their footing. Listen for the way the same four singers keep returning as her judges, servants and lovers, which makes the whole cast feel like a single hostile crowd. The interludes quote 1930s popular styles precisely enough to be recognised and then bend them microtonally.","The premiere drew as much comment for its subject as its music, and some early reviews treated it as a scandal piece by a clever young man. Twenty-five years of revivals have moved the argument: the opera is now generally read as being about how a woman was destroyed by publicity, and productions have grown more sympathetic to her. It remains one of the most performed operas written since 1990.","Listen to the opening scene and the final eviction back to back — the same music, the same voice, thirty years apart. Then any of the dance interludes for how a tango can be made to totter. Compare it with Adès's later The Exterminating Angel for how his orchestral writing grew. The original Almeida recording, with Jill Gomez, is still the reference."],"golijov-ainadamar":["An opera in three 'images' lasting about eighty minutes. The actress Margarita Xirgu, dying in Uruguay, remembers Federico García Lorca, who was murdered by Falangist gunmen in 1936 near a spring called Ainadamar, the fountain of tears. Lorca is sung by a mezzo-soprano. The score layers a symphony orchestra with a flamenco cantaor, hand-clapping, sampled gunshots used as percussion, and long ecstatic vocal lines over a slowly turning ostinato.","Commissioned by Tanglewood, where it was first heard in 2003, then substantially rewritten for Santa Fe Opera in 2005 with the director Peter Sellars; that version is the one performed. Golijov, an Argentine of Eastern European Jewish descent living in the United States, had already mixed liturgical, klezmer and Latin American materials in La Pasión según San Marcos. David Henry Hwang wrote the libretto.","The gunshots are notated as a rhythmic pattern, not a sound effect — they enter as a bulería, a flamenco rhythm, so the execution is danced. Listen for the recurring water music, harp and high strings, marking the fountain across all three images. Lorca sung by a woman is not a stylisation of the trouser-role tradition but a deliberate refusal to let the character be fixed.","The Tanglewood version was thought promising but shapeless; the 2005 revision was widely praised, and the recording won two Grammy awards. Golijov has since been criticised over borrowed material in other works, which has coloured discussion of his collage method here, though Ainadamar's sources are credited. The Metropolitan Opera's 2024 production settled its place in the repertory.","Start with the flamenco singer's entrance and the gunshot rhythm — two minutes that explain the whole method. Then Margarita's final aria for the long-breathed melody. Play it near Golijov's La Pasión to hear the same collage instinct on a bigger canvas. The Sellars-era recording with Dawn Upshaw is the one to hear first."],"heggie-dead-man-walking":["Two acts, about two and a half hours, following a Louisiana nun who becomes spiritual adviser to a condemned murderer and, at his request, witnesses his execution. The music is unashamedly tonal: a hymn tune sung by children opens the opera and returns at the end; there are gospel, blues and Elvis-inflected numbers alongside a long confrontation scene between the nun and the victims' parents. The final scene is an execution staged in near-silence.","San Francisco Opera's general director Lotfi Mansouri commissioned a first opera from Heggie, then working in the company's public relations office. Terrence McNally adapted Sister Helen Prejean's 1993 memoir, insisting the crime be shown rather than described. The premiere on 7 October 2000 had Susan Graham as Sister Helen and Frederica von Stade as the killer's mother. American opera at the time was preoccupied with whether new work could reach a general audience.","The opening hymn, 'He Will Gather Us Around', is Heggie's own, written to sound like a hymnal entry, and its harmony is progressively soured each time it returns until the last statement, where it is finally allowed to be simple. Listen for the low, dry percussion that ticks under the prison scenes — a clock the audience is not supposed to notice consciously. The murder is scored without voices at all.","Reviews in 2000 divided along a familiar line: critics who wanted new opera to sound new found the idiom too easy, while others argued that accessibility was the point and that the piece works in the theatre. The commercial verdict was decisive — more than seventy productions worldwide and a Metropolitan Opera staging in 2023 have made it the most performed American opera of the century.","Listen to the opening hymn, then jump to the same music at the close and hear what has been taken out of it. The confrontation scene with the parents is the dramatic test — four voices arguing at once. Compare it with Adams's The Death of Klinghoffer for two opposite answers to the same question about violence and opera."],"benjamin-written-on-skin":["Fifteen scenes, ninety-five minutes, for five singers. A wealthy landowner commissions an illuminated book; the artist and the landowner's wife Agnès become lovers; the husband kills the boy and feeds her his heart, and she throws herself from a window. Angels narrate, and the characters announce their own stage directions in the third person. The orchestra is transparent rather than loud, with glass harmonica and a bass viol picking out lines that seem to come from another century.","Commissioned by the Aix-en-Provence festival with several co-producers and premiered there on 7 July 2012, conducted by Benjamin, directed by Katie Mitchell, with Barbara Hannigan as Agnès and Christopher Purves as the Protector. The libretto by Martin Crimp reworks a thirteenth-century Occitan razo about the troubadour Guillem de Cabestany. Benjamin, famously slow-working, had written only one short stage piece before.","The narration device does the heavy lifting: because characters say 'says the Protector' before speaking, the audience hears the story as an object being made, which is exactly what the illuminated book on stage is. Listen for the glass harmonica, which sounds only around the angels and the book — an instrument used as a doorway between the medieval and modern layers. Agnès's vocal line rises steadily through the opera and never comes down.","The premiere was one of the most warmly reviewed of any twenty-first-century opera, and productions followed in London, Munich, Paris, New York and beyond within three years — unusually rapid for new work. Discussion has centred on whether the cool narrative frame distances the violence too much, and on Hannigan's performance, which shaped how the role is now sung. Benjamin and Crimp have since written two more operas together.","Begin with the opening scene, where the angels stop time and set up the storytelling rule; the rest depends on it. Then Agnès's last scene, the highest and quietest music in the piece. Listen for single instruments stepping forward alone. Watch a filmed staging if you can — the visual layering of past and present clarifies the libretto's method."],"korngold-adventures-of-robin-hood":["About sixty-five minutes of music for a Technicolor adventure film, written almost continuously beneath the action. Korngold treats it as an opera without singing: Robin, Marian, King Richard and the villains each have themes that develop and combine, the sword fight is scored as a scherzo with the blades' rhythm built in, and the love scene is a full late-Romantic orchestral song. The brass writing is heroic in the manner of Richard Strauss, with a Viennese sweetness underneath.","Korngold, a Viennese prodigy whose opera Die tote Stadt had been an international success in 1920, had been coming to Warner Bros. since 1934. He initially refused Robin Hood, thinking it beyond him; while he hesitated, Germany annexed Austria in March 1938. He took the job, and it saved his life — his family stayed in California and his Vienna property was confiscated. He later said the film had rescued him.","Korngold worked at the piano watching a projection, composing to the frame, and his themes are constructed so they can be stretched or compressed to fit a cut without losing shape — the technical problem that defines film scoring. Listen for material recycled from his own concert works: the score reuses ideas from two earlier symphonic overtures. Some cues are in effect miniature sonata forms squeezed into ninety seconds.","It won the Academy Award for best score in 1939 — the first time the award went to the composer rather than the studio's music department — and became the template for Hollywood's symphonic style. Concert critics long dismissed Korngold as a talent wasted on films, a verdict that reversed from the 1970s; John Williams's Star Wars is openly a homage. Restorations and re-recordings since the 1970s have let the full score be heard apart from the dialogue.","Find a complete re-recording rather than the film soundtrack, where dialogue and effects cover the detail. Start with the main title, then the archery tournament, then the final duel. Play the Star Wars main title straight afterwards and the debt is unmistakable. Korngold's own violin concerto reuses several of these tunes, which is another good way in."],"walton-henry-v":["An hour of music for Laurence Olivier's film of Shakespeare's play, ranging across four styles as the film changes worlds: pastiche Elizabethan consort music for the Globe Theatre scenes, plainsong and modal choral writing for the French court, a long orchestral charge for Agincourt with the horses' rhythm in the strings, and a quiet string elegy, 'Touch her soft lips and part', lasting barely ninety seconds.","Made as wartime propaganda and released in November 1944, five months after the Normandy landings, with an on-screen dedication to the British commandos and airborne troops. Walton had already written scores for Olivier and for propaganda features including The First of the Few. Recording was done under blackout conditions with players borrowed from wherever they could be found. Olivier reportedly cut picture to fit the music rather than the reverse in the Agincourt sequence.","The Agincourt charge is built by acceleration alone: one rhythmic figure, repeated at rising speed for over two minutes with almost no melodic change, so the tension is purely physical. Listen for the moment the strings stop dead as the arrows are released. The Globe Theatre music is not simply antique pastiche — Walton uses real sixteenth-century tunes, including the Agincourt Song, and harmonises them wrongly on purpose to sound reconstructed.","Contemporary reviewers treated the film as a national event and the score as part of it. For decades only Muir Mathieson's short suite was available; Christopher Palmer's 'Shakespeare Scenario' of 1990, with a speaker delivering the text, restored much more and prompted a reassessment. Walton's film music is now taken seriously enough that the debate has shifted to whether it is better than his symphonies, which some critics argue.","Listen to 'Touch her soft lips and part' first, ninety seconds that carry more than the battle. Then the Agincourt charge, timing the acceleration. The Palmer Shakespeare Scenario with a speaker gives the fullest picture; the Mathieson suite is shorter and more concert-shaped. Compare with Prokofiev's Alexander Nevsky, another wartime film score built around one great battle."],"herrmann-vertigo":["About seventy-five minutes of music for Alfred Hitchcock's film about a detective who remakes one woman into another. The prelude sets two arpeggios spiralling in opposite directions at once, a musical staircase that never arrives. A slow habanera accompanies the pursuit; the long scene where the woman is transformed unfolds over a rising sequence that keeps promising a resolution and withholding it for nearly four minutes. Harps, muted brass and divided strings dominate; there are almost no tunes.","Herrmann's fourth film for Hitchcock, recorded in late 1957 and early 1958. An American Federation of Musicians strike meant the score had to be taped in Vienna and London under other conductors, with Herrmann unable to record it himself in Hollywood. Film scoring at the time still assumed melody and sentiment; Herrmann, a former radio conductor who had championed Ives, was systematically removing both.","The harmonic engine is a chord that never resolves — Herrmann adapts the opening of Wagner's Tristan und Isolde and simply refuses to let it move, which is why the score feels like unrelieved longing rather than desire fulfilled. Listen for the moment near the end when the resolution finally arrives, exactly once. The love scene's orchestration deliberately swells and recedes like breathing, without any melody to follow.","The film failed in 1958 and the score attracted little notice; both were reclaimed decades later, and Vertigo now regularly tops critics' polls of the greatest films. Herrmann's music is central to that reversal, and the score has been re-recorded many times, with arguments over which of the surviving sessions represents his intentions given that he conducted none of them. Its influence on later composers, Sondheim among them, is direct.","Play the prelude twice and follow only the two spiralling lines. Then the transformation scene, and count how long the harmony holds off. Compare with Wagner's Tristan prelude to hear what was borrowed. Seek a full re-recording rather than the thin original tracks; several conductors have made the case, and they differ audibly in tempo."],"herrmann-psycho":["Fifty minutes of music for strings alone — no wind, no brass, no percussion. Herrmann called it a black-and-white score to match the film. The main title is a sequence of hard, stabbing chords over a driving figure that never lets up; the long drive through the rain is a repeated ostinato that simply refuses to develop; and the murder in the shower is fourteen bars of violins playing a shrieking downward-glissando figure at the top of their range, in unison, with no harmony at all.","Hitchcock shot Psycho cheaply with his television crew and Herrmann worked for a reduced fee. Hitchcock's instruction was that the murder should have no music, and after seeing the sequence cut with Herrmann's violins he doubled the composer's salary. The strings-only decision was partly budgetary and partly aesthetic; Paramount had no faith in the picture, and it became the most profitable film of Hitchcock's career.","The shriek is not a sound effect but ordinary notation: violins bowing hard on a high note, then sliding down, all playing the same line. The horror comes from unison, the total absence of harmony to cushion it. Listen for how the same interval that opens the main title reappears, slowed almost past recognition, in the final scene about the mother. Herrmann never lets the strings play a full warm chord anywhere in the film.","Reviewers in 1960 were largely preoccupied with the film's violence, though the score's contribution was noted quickly. It became the most imitated cue in cinema, so widely copied that it now reads as cliché — a fate that obscures how strange it was. Herrmann and Hitchcock fell out six years later over Torn Curtain, and Herrmann's rejected score for that film remains a subject of argument.","Listen to the main title and the shower cue with your eyes closed, apart from the images, and hear how little material there actually is. Then the rain-drive ostinato for the opposite quality — monotony as dread. Compare the string writing with Bartók's for the same forces. A full concert performance of the score, several exist, is worth an hour."],"rozsa-ben-hur":["Around two and a half hours of music, among the longest scores written for any film, for William Wyler's Roman epic. Rózsa builds it from a handful of themes — a brass fanfare for Rome, a modal melody for Judaea, a chorale for Christ that is only ever heard on strings and never with brass. The galley scene is scored as a drum ostinato at three increasing speeds; the chariot race, famously, has almost no music at all, only hooves.","Rózsa had scored Quo Vadis and Julius Caesar for MGM and by 1959 was the studio's specialist in antiquity. He spent months in libraries and museums studying surviving fragments of Greek notation, Roman instrument reconstructions and Jewish liturgical chant, and had replica instruments built. MGM had staked the studio's future on the film. Rózsa won his third Academy Award; the film won eleven, a record it held for decades.","The Christ theme is a rule as much as a tune: Rózsa scores it only for strings and organ, never for the brass that carries Rome, so the two power structures are audibly different substances. Listen for the Judaean material, built on modes taken from synagogue chant rather than invented exotically. The rowing sequence uses a hortator's drum as the only rhythmic source, and the tempo changes are literal.","Acclaimed at once and for decades the reference point for the historical epic score. Later criticism has noted that the ancient research produced a sound more Hungarian than Roman, since Rózsa filtered everything through the modal language of Bartók and Kodály — a fair objection that does not much damage the music. The complete score has been re-recorded several times, and those recordings reveal structure the film obscures.","Start with the overture and the Rome fanfare, then find the Christ theme and notice that no brass ever touches it. The galley sequence is the best three minutes for pure rhythm. A full re-recording beats the film tracks. Compare with Korngold's Robin Hood twenty years earlier to hear how much harder and more modal Hollywood's epic sound had become."],"rota-the-godfather":["Barely forty minutes of music across a three-hour film, which is part of its effect. A solo trumpet plays the main theme unaccompanied over the opening, a minor-key melody that sounds like something remembered rather than composed. A waltz carries the Sicilian scenes, played by mandolin and accordion; a slow, dark 'Immigrant' theme underlies the family's history. Rota keeps the orchestra small and the scoring dry, and leaves long stretches entirely silent.","Francis Ford Coppola wanted an Italian composer and overrode Paramount's preference; Rota, then sixty-one, was known internationally as Fellini's composer and in Italy as a conservatory director and opera composer. He wrote the score in Rome, working from the film rather than with the director in the room. The picture became the highest-grossing film released to that point, and the trumpet theme entered popular culture immediately.","Rota's method is deliberate anachronism: the tunes are written to sound like Sicilian folk songs and Neapolitan café music of decades earlier, so the score functions as the family's memory of itself rather than as commentary. Listen for the waltz, which is in triple time when almost nothing else in the film is, marking Michael's Sicilian exile as a different world. Coppola's father Carmine wrote the source music heard on screen.","Rota's Academy Award nomination was withdrawn when it emerged that the love theme reworked music from his own score for Fortunella of 1958; he won three years later for The Godfather Part II. The episode has fuelled a long argument about self-borrowing, which Rota did constantly and openly. Critical opinion has moved from treating him as a gifted tunesmith to reading the film scores as a coherent body of work.","Listen to the opening trumpet solo alone, without the film, and notice that nothing accompanies it. Then the Sicilian waltz for the change of world. Play Rota's music for Fellini's La strada or 8½ next: the same plain melodies used for comedy and grief. A soundtrack album gives you the cues in order; re-recordings tend to over-orchestrate."],"morricone-the-mission":["Roughly fifty-five minutes of music for Roland Joffé's film about Jesuit missions among the Guaraní in eighteenth-century South America. Three musical worlds are set going separately and then combined: a solo oboe melody, played by a priest to win the Guaraní over; a Renaissance-style choral motet for the European church; and drums and voices for the indigenous community. In the central sequence all three run at once, each keeping its own rhythm.","Morricone at first refused, saying the film was better without music. He had by then written some four hundred scores, including the Leone westerns that had made his name, and was working in Rome with his usual orchestrator and choir. The subject required pastiche of eighteenth-century sacred style, which he had not attempted before; he studied the period's Jesuit music, some of which survives in South American archives.","The three layers are constructed to be combinable from the start — each is written so it can be superimposed on the others without clashing, which is a compositional problem solved before a note is heard on screen. Listen for the oboe theme reappearing inside the choral motet as an inner voice. The percussion patterns are drawn from surviving Guaraní practice rather than generalised jungle drumming.","It lost the Academy Award to Herbie Hancock's Round Midnight, a result still cited as evidence of the category's oddity; Morricone did not receive a competitive Oscar until 2016. 'Gabriel's Oboe' became a concert and crossover standard, arranged for almost every instrument, which has partly obscured the score's structural ambition. Recent writing treats The Mission as the summary of his method rather than as a departure.","Hear the three elements separately first — the oboe theme, the choral motet, the drums — then the sequence where they combine, and try to keep all three in your ear at once. That is the whole piece in miniature. Compare with the Leone westerns for the same technique applied to whistling and electric guitar. The original soundtrack recording remains the best-balanced."],"greenwood-there-will-be-blood":["About forty minutes of music for Paul Thomas Anderson's film about a Californian oil prospector. The opening ten minutes of the film have no dialogue and one sustained, wavering string cluster that slowly widens until it is unbearable. Elsewhere Greenwood writes a hammering piece for strings and percussion over a drilling rhythm, a plain tonal cello line for the moments of apparent tenderness, and passages where the strings are made to buzz and scrape.","Anderson had used Greenwood's BBC commission Popcorn Superhet Receiver as temporary music while editing and then asked him to score the film; parts of that concert piece stayed in. Greenwood was the guitarist of Radiohead and had scored one previous film. His models were explicit: Krzysztof Penderecki's string works of the 1960s and the ondes martenot writing of Olivier Messiaen, both of which he had studied as a listener rather than at a conservatory.","The buzzing string sound is produced by ordinary means used wrongly — players bow close to or behind the bridge, and clusters are tuned a fraction apart so the notes beat against each other. Listen for the ondes martenot, an electronic instrument from 1928, sliding underneath the strings in the derrick scenes. The tender cello melody is in a plain key, which in this context registers as more sinister than the noise.","The Academy declared the score ineligible because it incorporated pre-existing concert music, a ruling widely criticised at the time and now read as a sign the category's rules had not caught up. Reviewers treated the film's sound as inseparable from its dread. Its influence has been enormous: string clusters as a signifier of unease became standard in prestige cinema within a few years, often much less well handled.","Watch or listen to the first ten minutes with nothing but the strings for company and notice when your shoulders tighten. Then find the drilling cue. Play Penderecki's Threnody afterwards to hear the source. Greenwood's concert piece Popcorn Superhet Receiver, easily found, gives the same material without the pictures and is a fair test of whether it stands alone."],"desplat-the-grand-budapest-hotel":["Forty minutes of music for Wes Anderson's film about an invented central European republic between the wars. Desplat throws out the symphony orchestra and builds the score from a balalaika orchestra, cimbalom, zither, harpsichord, church organ, alphorns and a yodelling choir, plus a great deal of pizzicato and hand percussion. Most cues are short, fast and mechanical, ostinato figures ticking under a wistful tune, with one slow organ piece for the monastery chase.","Desplat's third film for Anderson, whose visual world is built from symmetry, miniature and invented nationality; the score had to sound like a country that does not exist. Desplat hired the Osipov State Russian Orchestra's balalaika players in Moscow, recording them separately, and assembled the ensembles piece by piece rather than writing for a standing orchestra. He won the Academy Award for it in 2015, his first after six nominations.","There is almost no legato in the score: nearly every instrument chosen plucks, strikes or ticks, so even the sad music cannot sustain a line, which is exactly the film's relation to its own nostalgia. Listen for the balalaika tremolo standing in for the string tremolo a conventional score would use. The yodelling choir sings nonsense syllables invented for the film's fictional Zubrowka.","Reviewers noted at once that the score does an unusual amount of the film's work, establishing a place and period that exist nowhere. The Academy Award confirmed a shift in taste towards chamber-scale, texture-led scoring. Sceptics argue Desplat's manner has become a formula easily imitated across prestige cinema, and that the invented-folklore approach edges towards pastiche of real central European traditions.","Start with the main title, then any of the chase cues, and count the instruments that can sustain a note — there are almost none. The organ cue is the outlier and lands harder for it. Compare with Rota's mandolin and accordion writing for a similar trick done fifty years earlier. The soundtrack album is short and works straight through."],"johannsson-arrival":["Forty-five minutes of music for Denis Villeneuve's film about a linguist deciphering an alien language. The central sound is human voices treated as an alien one: a vocal ensemble singing slow, sliding clusters that are then looped, layered and processed until the singers become texture. Under them sit long contrabass notes, a slow woodwind pulse and a recurring low knocking. Very little of the score has melody; almost all of it moves by imperceptible degrees.","Jóhannsson's second film with Villeneuve after Sicario. He had built his earlier albums around single conceptual objects — a dismantled IBM mainframe, a closed Icelandic factory — and applied the same method here, working with the Danish vocal group Theatre of Voices under Paul Hillier. He recorded the singers first and then treated the results electronically, so the score's raw material is entirely human before it becomes strange.","The vocal writing imitates the alien script the film invents: circular, with no beginning or end, phrases that turn back on themselves so you cannot hear where a line started. Listen for how the knocking pulse is never quite regular. The film's opening and closing use Max Richter's On the Nature of Daylight rather than Jóhannsson's music, a licensing decision that shaped how the score is remembered.","The score was ruled ineligible for the Academy Award because of the Richter track's prominence, which caused considerable annoyance; it won other prizes. Jóhannsson died in 2018 at forty-eight, shortly after his score for Blade Runner 2049 was replaced, and Arrival is now heard partly as a summary of what was lost. Critics divide over whether the voice-processing is genuinely original or an elegant assembly of existing techniques.","Listen to the main cue, 'Heptapod B', on headphones and try to find where each vocal phrase begins; the difficulty is the design. Then play Richter's On the Nature of Daylight to hear the piece the film borrowed. Compare with Greenwood's string writing for two very different routes to unease. The soundtrack album stands alone better than most."],"debussy-clair-lune":["The third of four movements in the Suite bergamasque, and the only one anybody plays. Roughly five minutes for solo piano, marked to be played very expressively, moving mostly in slow arpeggios under a melody in thirds. The opening is bare, almost hesitant; the middle swells into rolling broken chords that spread across the keyboard; then the first idea returns thinned out, ending on chords that fade rather than resolve. Nothing loud happens anywhere in it.","Debussy drafted the suite around 1890, in his late twenties, poor, living in Paris on odd jobs and the patience of friends. He held the manuscript back for fifteen years and let it be printed only in 1905, by which time La Mer was finished and his reputation made. He revised the writing before publication and renamed this movement, which had begun life as Promenade sentimentale. The title comes from Verlaine, whose poems he had already set as songs.","Listen for how rarely the harmony actually settles. Debussy keeps sliding between chords a third apart rather than the expected fifth, so the piece drifts instead of stepping. The middle section's left hand is a single rocking figure that never changes shape for over a minute, and all the sense of movement comes from the right. The closing bars thin out to two voices and stop rather than conclude.","Its fame has worked against it: transcribed for every instrument, used in films and advertisements, it is often played too slowly and too sweetly. Pianists argue about the middle section, where Debussy's pupils reported he wanted movement rather than reverie. The Suite bergamasque as a whole is early, uneven work, and scholars disagree about how much of the 1905 print reflects 1890 and how much a mature composer tidying up.","Find a recording that takes under five minutes and hear how much more shapely it becomes. Notice the pedalling: the sound should blur but never smear. Then play the suite's other three movements, especially the Passepied, to hear the eighteenth-century pastiche this famous movement grew out of."],"debussy-prelude-l-apres-midi-d-un":["Ten minutes for a modest orchestra with no trumpets and no timpani, opening with an unaccompanied flute curling down and back up through a wide, slippery interval. Harp glissandi and horns answer. The piece moves in waves rather than sections, building once to a warm string melody at the centre and then dissolving back into the flute, with small tuned antique cymbals ringing twice in the closing bars.","Written between 1892 and 1894 as the first of a planned three-part response to Mallarmé's poem The Afternoon of a Faun; Debussy never wrote the other two. Mallarmé, who had feared music would flatten his verse, told him it went further than the poem. The premiere came in Paris on 22 December 1894 and was encored on the spot. Nijinsky's scandalous ballet on the score, made for Diaghilev in 1912, gave it a second and noisier life.","The flute's first note is the pivot of the whole piece: the same melody returns again and again with different harmony underneath, so that one note means something new each time. Listen for the antique cymbals, small tuned discs Debussy asked for specially, which appear only at the very end, and notice that the strings are muted for much of the piece, which is why the loud passages never sound heavy.","Boulez's remark that modern music awakens with this piece has hardened into a cliché, but the point stands: the phrase begins and ends where it pleases rather than where the bar-line says. Early critics called it formless. Later analysts found a careful symmetry underneath. Performance argument now turns on how much the tempo should breathe, and on whether the flute solo should sound sensual or cool.","Listen to the first thirty seconds three times before going on; everything grows from that flute line. Then follow the same melody through its returns and notice that the accompaniment is different every time. Compare an older French recording under Munch or Martinon with a modern one for the difference in vibrato and rubato. Ravel's Daphnis makes a good companion."],"debussy-mer-dialogue-du-vent-et-mer":["The third and last panel of La Mer, about eight minutes, for a large orchestra used with unusual precision: divided strings, two harps, and a percussion section that mostly whispers. It opens on a low rumble and a wailing oboe over tremolo, then hurls short motifs across the orchestra in rising gusts. Themes from the first movement return, and the piece ends with a huge brass surge and three hammered chords.","Debussy finished La Mer in March 1905 at Eastbourne on the English Channel, in the middle of the scandal that followed his leaving his wife for Emma Bardac; Parisian friends dropped him. He had spent childhood holidays by the Mediterranean at Cannes and liked to claim he had wanted to be a sailor. The Paris premiere, on 15 October 1905 under Camille Chevillard, was under-rehearsed and coolly received.","Debussy called the three movements symphonic sketches rather than a symphony, but they are tied together: the horn call from the opening movement comes back near the end of this one. He rewrote the closing brass entry in 1909, deciding the original was too reticent, so two versions circulate. The Hokusai wave printed on the first edition's cover was Debussy's own choice, not the publisher's.","Early listeners complained they could not smell the sea; Pierre Lalo wrote that he neither saw nor felt it. Within twenty years it was standard repertoire and Toscanini had turned it into a virtuoso showpiece. Conductors still split over the 1909 revision, over how loud the closing brass should be, and over whether the piece is a seascape at all or a study in orchestral colour that happens to be named after water.","Start at the end and work backwards: hear the final ninety seconds, then find the same material earlier in the movement. Listen for the way loud passages are built from many quiet layers rather than one big sound. A recording with clear inner detail rewards the effort more than a lush one. Follow it with Britten's Sea Interludes for a colder sea."],"debussy-sunken-cathedral":["Six minutes of solo piano, the tenth of the twelve Préludes of Book I. It begins with bare fifths in a slow, bell-like haze, gathers into a huge chordal chorale played at full volume with the sustaining pedal down, then sinks back through rippling figures to the opening stillness. The Breton legend behind it, the drowned cathedral of Ys rising from the sea at dawn, is printed at the end of the piece rather than the start.","Book I of the Préludes was written quickly in the winter of 1909 and 1910, when Debussy was ill, in debt, and working slowly on everything else. He was by then France's most famous living composer and a reluctant public figure who conducted his own music badly. The legend of Ys was in the air: Lalo's opera Le roi d'Ys had been a Paris success, and Breton folklore was fashionable in Symbolist circles.","A puzzle sits in the middle of the piece. A piano roll Debussy recorded in 1913 plays a long central passage at double the notated speed, which suggests an error in the printed score; many pianists now halve those bar-lengths and find the piece holds together better. Listen too for the left hand's low octaves under the chorale, which are the bells, still tolling underwater.","The prelude became the emblem of everything people meant by musical Impressionism, a label Debussy rejected in print. Critics who dislike it call it an effect made with the pedal; pianists who love it point to the exactness of the voicing. The roll-against-score tempo argument, pressed by Roy Howat among others, remains unsettled, and recordings divide clearly into two camps.","Listen first for volume alone: the piece is one slow crescendo to the chorale and a slow fade after it. Then choose two recordings, one that follows the printed note-values and one that doubles the speed of the middle, and decide which cathedral you believe. Gieseking and Zimerman make a good pair for the comparison."],"debussy-arabesque-no-1":["Four minutes of solo piano, the first of two Arabesques. A rising arpeggio in the right hand sets a gentle triple swing going, then a melody in even notes crosses over it, so that the two hands are moving in different rhythms almost throughout. A short middle section turns firmer and briefly darker before the opening returns. It is the easiest famous Debussy to play and the least characteristic.","Composed in his mid-twenties, around 1888 to 1891, when Debussy was fresh from the Prix de Rome and earning money from teaching and salon work. Paris publishers wanted attractive piano pieces for amateurs, and Durand printed these in 1891. The manner is closer to Massenet and to the salon Fauré than to anything Debussy would write five years later, but the free, decorative line that gives the pieces their title already interested him.","The two hands almost never agree: three notes against two for pages at a time, which is why the piece sounds as though it is floating slightly free of its own pulse. Debussy also refuses to end phrases where the ear expects, cutting one short here and stretching one there. The middle section is built from the same rising arpeggio, thickened and pushed into a darker key.","Musicologists tend to file the Arabesques as juvenilia and move on; teachers keep them alive, since they sit at the point where an intermediate pianist can first sound like a professional. The piece has been arranged, sampled and used as hold music often enough that hearing it plainly takes effort. Debussy never repudiated it, but he also never wrote anything like it again.","Count the left hand's triplets while following the right hand's melody and you will hear why it shimmers. Try it after the Suite bergamasque to gauge how much distance Debussy covered in a few years. Any clean, unsentimental recording works; avoid the very slow readings that treat it as mood music."],"debussy-reverie":["Four minutes of quiet solo piano. The left hand keeps a soft rocking accompaniment going almost without a break while the right hand sings a long, stepwise melody that rises and sinks back. A slightly more animated middle section adds a little warmth, then the opening returns. There are no sharp corners, no fast passages and almost nothing above a moderate volume anywhere in the piece.","Written about 1890, when Debussy was in his late twenties and short of money, and sold outright to the publisher Fromont for a flat fee. When it was reissued in 1905 and began selling well, Debussy wrote angrily to the publisher that the piece was no good, that he had dashed it off for money, and that he now had no control over it. He was in the middle of La Mer and embarrassed by his salon past.","The rocking left hand is the whole trick. It stays on the same few chords for long stretches, so the harmony barely moves while the melody wanders across it: the static bass that becomes a signature of the later Debussy is already here. Notice too that the melody hardly ever leaps. It is built from small steps, which is why it sounds vocal rather than pianistic.","It has had a strange afterlife. In 1938 the melody was fitted with words as the popular song My Reverie and became an American hit, which is how a great many people first met Debussy. Serious critics ignore the piece; pianists play it as an encore. The composer's own dismissal is its most quoted review, and it is worth remembering he was judging a fifteen-year-old work by later standards.","Play it directly after the Arabesque and listen for the family resemblance: both are melodies floating over an unchanging pulse. Then jump forward to the Préludes to hear what became of that texture. Look for recordings that keep it moving, around four minutes, rather than the drifting six-minute versions common on relaxation albums."],"debussy-golliwog-s-cakewalk":["The last of the six pieces in Children's Corner, about three minutes for solo piano. It is a cakewalk, the syncopated American dance that reached Paris with touring minstrel troupes, complete with a strutting bass, accents pushed off the beat, and grace notes that imitate a banjo. The middle section slows to a mock-solemn quotation punctuated by giggling chords, before the strut returns and ends with a flourish.","Debussy wrote Children's Corner in 1908 for his daughter Claude-Emma, called Chouchou, then three years old; the dedication is to her, with an apology for what follows. The titles are in English, partly because she had an English governess. Ragtime and cakewalk sheet music was selling in Paris at the time, and the golliwog was a popular storybook doll whose racial caricature is now the piece's most uncomfortable feature.","The mock-solemn middle quotes the opening of Wagner's Tristan und Isolde, the most loaded few bars in nineteenth-century music, and then answers it with a snigger. Debussy marks the passage to be played with great feeling, which is the joke. He had worshipped Wagner as a young man, made two pilgrimages to Bayreuth, and spent his later years attacking him in print.","The title is usually printed as Debussy spelled it, Golliwogg, and concert programmes increasingly explain rather than repeat it; some pianists rename the piece, others let the caricature stand as evidence of its moment. Musically it is admired as one of the first serious European responses to ragtime, predating Stravinsky's by a decade. Whether Debussy heard the music as African American or as generic exotica is still debated.","Listen for the sudden change of manner at the Tristan quotation, roughly halfway through; it is unmissable once you know the Wagner. Compare Debussy's stiff, fully notated syncopation with an actual Joplin rag to hear a European hearing ragtime at a distance. Then try the rest of Children's Corner, especially Doctor Gradus ad Parnassum, a parody of piano practice."],"debussy-girl-with-flaxen-hair":["Three minutes of solo piano, the eighth prelude of Book I and the plainest music Debussy published after 1900. A gently curving melody in bare fifths and thirds opens alone, is answered by soft chords, and returns twice more, each time slightly richer. There is no development, no climax and no fast music: the piece is one tune, presented three ways and then allowed to stop.","Written in the winter of 1909 and 1910 alongside the rest of Book I. The title comes from a poem by Leconte de Lisle, one of his Scottish-flavoured pieces, which Debussy had already set as a song more than twenty years earlier and evidently never forgot. He had recently been diagnosed with the cancer that would kill him, and much of the late piano music turns towards this kind of plainness.","The melody is pentatonic, using only five notes, the pattern you get from the black keys of a piano, which is why it sounds like a folk song nobody wrote. Debussy learned the appeal of such scales partly from the Javanese gamelan he heard at the Paris Exposition of 1889. Notice also that the piece never once uses a loud dynamic, and that the final chord is deliberately left thin.","It is the prelude most often extracted, arranged for violin, cello, guitar and orchestra, and used to represent Debussy in anthologies, which has made serious writers slightly suspicious of it. Pianists prize it as a test of tone: with no technical difficulty to hide behind, everything depends on voicing and pedalling. Recordings vary in length by nearly a minute, mostly through how long the silences are held.","Listen for the three appearances of the tune and how the harmony underneath thickens each time. Play it beside The Sunken Cathedral from the same book to hear how differently Debussy uses the same bare intervals. If it helps, try a violin arrangement first, then return to the piano original and notice how much the pedal adds."],"debussy-string-quartet-andantino":["The slow third movement of Debussy's only string quartet, about eight minutes, marked to be played gently and expressively. The players are muted for most of it. A long melody passes from first violin to viola to cello over quiet repeated chords, rises to a single warm climax, then subsides. It is the least busy movement of a quartet otherwise full of pizzicato, cross-rhythm and hard edges.","Composed in 1893 and first performed in Paris that December by the Ysaÿe Quartet, to whom it is dedicated. Debussy was 31, still unknown outside a small circle, and writing under the shadow of César Franck's method of running one theme through every movement, a technique he otherwise mocked. He never wrote a second quartet, though he promised Ysaÿe one. The following year he finished the Faun and stopped needing other people's forms.","One theme runs through all four movements, transformed each time, in exactly the manner Debussy claimed to despise. Here it arrives slowed almost past recognition, in a remote key, under mutes. Listen for the point where the mutes come off and the sound opens as if a window had been raised, and for the way the melody is handed downwards through the instruments rather than kept by the first violin.","The premiere puzzled critics and disappointed Chausson, whose reaction stung Debussy enough to be remembered. Within a decade the quartet was a French classic, and Ravel's quartet of 1903 is unimaginable without it, a debt Ravel acknowledged and Debussy resented. Modern argument is mostly about sound: how much vibrato, how literally to take the many small tempo instructions, and whether gut strings suit it.","Hear this movement on its own first, then the whole quartet, tracking the shared theme through all four movements. Follow it immediately with Ravel's quartet, whose slow movement answers this one. For a contrast in style, compare a modern group such as the Quatuor Ébène with a mid-century French ensemble, where the sliding between notes is far franker."],"debussy-reflets-dans-l-eau":["Five minutes of solo piano, opening the first book of Images. Three quiet chords fall like drops, and then the whole keyboard begins to ripple: fast, soft figures in both hands surround a melody that keeps sinking downwards. The piece builds to one large cascade running the full range of the instrument, then settles back to the opening chords and ends very quietly, low down.","Written in 1905, the same year as La Mer, and published by Durand. Debussy had scrapped an earlier version and told his publisher he had rewritten it on new principles, borrowing the language of chemistry to describe what he was doing with sound. The instrument itself was changing: heavier stringing and a fuller sustaining pedal made the kind of blurred, layered texture he wanted physically possible.","The three falling chords of the opening are a signature that returns throughout, recognisable however much water is thrown over them. Much of the piece uses a scale built entirely from whole steps, which has no leading note and therefore no pull towards home, and that is why the music can hang motionless. The final low note is marked to be held until it disappears of its own accord.","Ravel had published Jeux d'eau in 1901 and believed his own water piece came first; Debussy's admirers argued the influence ran the other way. The quarrel poisoned relations between them and still colours how the two composers are compared. Pianists disagree about pedalling: too much and the ripples turn to soup, too little and the piece sounds like an exercise. The sparer approach now prevails.","Listen for the three opening chords and count how often they return. Then hear Ravel's Jeux d'eau immediately afterwards and judge the priority argument yourself. Michelangeli's famously exact recording and a warmer, blurrier one make an instructive pair, since the piece can be played as engineering or as atmosphere."],"debussy-syrinx":["Three minutes for one unaccompanied flute: no accompaniment, no repeats, and barely any sense of a regular bar. The line unfolds in long descending phrases, each beginning higher than the last one ended, separated by pauses the player has to judge alone. It closes on a low note that fades to nothing. Almost every modern flute recital includes it, and it made the solo flute a serious modern instrument.","Written in 1913 as incidental music for Gabriel Mourey's play Psyché, where it is played offstage as the god Pan's last music before he dies; Debussy called it La flûte de Pan. Louis Fleury played it from manuscript and kept the piece to himself for the rest of his life; it was printed only in 1927, after his death, under the title Syrinx. Debussy had used the same Pan imagery in the Faun nineteen years earlier.","The printed score is not entirely trustworthy. Fleury's manuscript has no bar-lines and different phrasing from the published edition, and it carries stage cues showing exactly where in the play each phrase falls; flautists who have seen it play the piece more freely. Listen also to the first phrase, which sinks by chromatic steps in much the same way as the flute at the start of the Faun. The same god, twenty years on.","Editions are the live controversy: the 1927 print, a later edition based on the Fleury manuscript, and various performers' compromises all circulate, differing in phrasing, dynamics and even the odd note. Beyond that the piece is universally admired and universally overplayed in conservatories. Its standing as the ancestor of unaccompanied wind writing, from Varèse to Berio, is not disputed.","Listen in a quiet room with nothing programmed after it. Follow the shape of each phrase down to its lowest note and notice how much of the piece is silence. Compare two players for how differently the pauses can be timed; the difference in total length can be nearly a minute. Then hear the opening of the Faun and recognise the same voice."],"sibelius-finlandia":["Nine minutes for full orchestra. It opens with snarling low brass and timpani, answered by dark wind chords; a nervous, hammering fast section takes over, driven by strings and cymbals; then, near the end, brass and woodwind give out a broad, simple hymn, taken up by the strings before the fanfares return to close. The hymn tune is Sibelius's own, not a folk song, though nearly everyone assumes otherwise.","Finland was under Russian rule and, in 1899, under a new wave of censorship. Sibelius wrote this as the final tableau of a patriotic gala in Helsinki, a fundraising evening of historical scenes staged in defiance of the ban on political meetings. He revised it in 1900 for the Helsinki orchestra's European tour, and at home, under the censor's eye, it was performed under innocuous titles such as Impromptu.","The hymn is not a quotation and had no words when Sibelius wrote it. He extracted it as a separate piece in 1900, and Veikko Koskenniemi supplied Finnish words only in 1940, during the Winter War. It has since been fitted with texts in many languages and is sung in churches that have no idea where it came from. Listen for how little of the piece it actually occupies: barely ninety seconds.","Sibelius grew tired of it and feared it had crowded out his symphonies, a fear largely justified in his lifetime. Critics have long noted that the structure is crude by his standards, a sequence of blocks rather than an argument. Its political effect is not in doubt: it did the work of an anthem for a country that did not yet exist, and Finland has repeatedly declined to make it the official one.","Hear the whole nine minutes rather than the hymn alone; the hymn only means something after the noise before it. Then listen to the choral version, Finlandia-hymni, sung in Finnish. For the orchestral piece, Finnish orchestras tend to take it faster and less grandly than others, which suits its origins as theatre music."],"sibelius-symphony-no-2-finale":["The last of four movements, running straight out of the scherzo without a break, about fourteen minutes long. A striding tune in D major arrives over rocking strings and grows by accumulation rather than argument. A long central section darkens, with woodwind laments over a slow, repeating bass figure, before the main theme returns and the brass drive the symphony to a broad final cadence that takes almost a minute to arrive.","Composed in 1901 and 1902, partly in Italy at Rapallo, where Sibelius had gone with his family on a stipend to escape the Finnish winter and his Helsinki drinking companions. Finland was still under Russian pressure, and the Helsinki premiere on 8 March 1902 was heard at once as a political statement; his friend and champion Robert Kajanus described the finale as a promise of brighter days. Sibelius denied any programme.","The finale's main theme is built from almost nothing, a rising three-note step, repeated and extended, and that shape has been circulating since the symphony's opening bars. In the dark central section, listen for the bass line pacing up and down under the woodwind, a figure Sibelius holds for far longer than is comfortable; the return of the main theme over it is the moment the whole symphony turns.","Audiences adopted it instantly and it remains his most played symphony, while critics have often preferred the leaner Fourth and Seventh and called this finale bombastic. The nationalist reading, encouraged by Kajanus rather than the composer, dominated for decades and is now treated cautiously: the sketches show material begun for a Don Juan piece and a Dante project, with no Finland anywhere in sight.","Listen for the transition out of the third movement, where an oboe line stretches upward and the finale simply arrives; it is one of the great joins in the repertoire. Then follow the main theme's returns and note how each is scored more heavily than the last. Broad and brisk recordings differ startlingly in the final two minutes, so hear one of each."],"sibelius-violin-concerto-first-movement":["Sixteen minutes, the longest movement of the concerto. The violin enters in the second bar, high and alone over shivering muted strings, and hardly stops thereafter. The writing is ferociously hard, with long stretches in the highest positions and chains of wide stretches and octaves, and the movement contains a full unaccompanied cadenza placed unusually early, where the development would normally be, so the soloist carries the argument rather than decorating it.","Sibelius had trained as a violinist and once auditioned unsuccessfully for the Vienna Philharmonic; this is the concerto he could not have played himself. The Helsinki premiere in 1904 went badly with an under-prepared soloist, and he withdrew the piece, cut it hard, and produced the 1905 version now played, first heard in Berlin under Richard Strauss. The original was locked away by his family for most of a century.","The cadenza is not an ornament but the development section itself, which is why the movement can sound as though the orchestra has been dismissed. Listen also to the opening accompaniment: divided violins holding a soft, static chord, an icy sound Sibelius liked enough to reuse elsewhere. The 1904 original, finally recorded in the 1990s, has extra solo writing and shows how ruthlessly he pruned.","Early German critics were unkind, and the composer's own doubts kept the piece marginal until Heifetz's recordings made it central. The release of the 1904 original divided opinion: some hear a bolder work, most agree Sibelius was right to cut. Today's arguments are about tempo, about how icy the opening should sound, and about how far the soloist should play the finale like a folk fiddler.","Follow the soloist's line without worrying about structure the first time; the movement is one long span. Then listen again for the cadenza and notice that nothing accompanies it. Heifetz is the fast, glittering benchmark, Ginette Neveu the darker one. If you can find it, hear the 1904 original once for comparison."],"sibelius-swan-tuonela":["Nine minutes for a small, dark orchestra: strings divided into many parts, muted brass, harp, no flutes at all, and a single cor anglais that carries almost the whole melody. The swan's song unwinds slowly over a low, shifting string carpet, interrupted once by a surge and a soft drum roll. The line is so unbroken that the player has to steal breaths where they can.","One of four Lemminkäinen legends drawn from the Kalevala, Finland's national epic, composed between 1893 and 1895 and revised twice afterwards. Tuonela is the Finnish land of the dead, ringed by a black river on which a swan floats and sings, and the hero must cross it. Sibelius originally wrote the music as a prelude to a Kalevala opera he abandoned, and salvaged the best of it here.","The cor anglais is the swan, and Sibelius gives it no rival: the oboe is silent for long stretches so that nothing else in that register can compete. Listen for the strings, divided into many separate parts and often bowed near the bridge for a glassy, colourless sound, and for the solo cello that briefly answers the swan near the end, the only real dialogue in the piece.","The Lemminkäinen Suite was slow to establish itself and Sibelius suppressed two of its movements for fifty years, but this one was played separately from the start and became his calling card abroad. Critics have sometimes found it static, which is rather the point. Current debate concerns the order of the four movements, which Sibelius himself changed, and whether the suite holds together as a whole.","Listen for the cor anglais entry and then stay with that single line for the whole piece; everything else is background. Play it in a dark room. Then hear the other three Lemminkäinen movements, especially the headlong Return, for the contrast. Recordings that keep the string sound cold rather than plush serve it best."],"sibelius-valse-triste":["Five minutes for a small orchestra of strings, one flute, clarinet, two horns and timpani. It begins almost inaudibly with hesitant string chords, gathers into a slow waltz that keeps stopping and starting, twice rushes forward into something frantic, and then breaks off. The pauses matter as much as the notes: the whole piece is a dance that cannot keep going, ending on a few bare, very quiet chords.","Written in 1903 as one of six numbers of incidental music for Kuolema, a play by Sibelius's brother-in-law Arvid Järnefelt. In the scene, a dying woman rises from her bed and dances with figures only she can see; the last partner is Death, who knocks at the door. Sibelius revised it as a concert piece in 1904 and sold it outright for a trivial sum, forfeiting a fortune in royalties.","The stage action is audible in the music: the surges are the dying woman's exertion, and the sudden silence near the end is the knock at the door. Listen for the way the waltz never completes a phrase without hesitating, and for the muted strings that keep the whole thing at a whisper. The closing chords are marked about as softly as an orchestra can play.","It became a salon and cinema staple, played by café bands across Europe, and Sibelius came to resent bitterly the sale that gave him nothing from its success. Serious critics dismissed it as a trifle for decades; more recently its economy has been re-examined, and conductors have restored the complete Kuolema score. The main performance argument is speed: taken too fast it becomes a Viennese waltz, which it is not.","Listen for the silences and count them. Then hear it beside Sibelius's Fourth Symphony, written eight years later, to meet the same composer at his least charming. Compare a light, quick reading with a slow, funereal one and decide how much of the ghost story you want. Neeme Järvi and Paavo Berglund both avoid wallowing."],"sibelius-symphony-no-5-finale":["About nine minutes. Fast, whirring strings open it, and out of that texture the horns begin a slow, rocking figure of two notes swinging up and down, around which everything else is built. A troubled middle section darkens the picture before the horn theme returns in full brass under a soaring woodwind counter-melody. The symphony ends with six separate hammered chords, spaced by silences long enough to strand an audience.","Commissioned for Sibelius's fiftieth birthday on 8 December 1915 and performed that day in Helsinki, while Europe was at war and Finland still Russian. He was dissatisfied and rewrote the symphony twice, arriving at the final version in 1919, by which time Finland was independent and had fought a civil war. His diary for April 1915 records sixteen swans flying over his home, a sight he called one of the great experiences of his life.","The horn theme is the swan music: Sibelius wrote in his diary that the birds' calls suggested a swinging figure, and the theme matches what he described. Listen also for the transition earlier in the movement, where the tempo speeds up so gradually that the fast music emerges without a seam. The six final chords are not an error in the score; the silences between them are notated exactly.","Comparison of the 1915, 1916 and 1919 versions is a small scholarly industry. The first version, recorded since the 1990s, has a separate scherzo and a different finale, and hearing it makes the revision look ruthless rather than fussy. The ending divides conductors, who must decide how long to hold each silence, and audiences, who reliably clap in the gaps.","Count the last six chords and try to predict the gaps; you will get it wrong. Before that, wait for the horn theme to emerge from the string tremor a couple of minutes in. Compare a broad reading such as Karajan's with the leaner Finnish tradition, and listen once to the 1915 original for the road not taken."],"sibelius-karelia-suite-alla-marcia":["The third and shortest movement of the Karelia Suite, about four minutes. A quiet, quick march starts in the strings, passes to the woodwind and then to full brass, the volume rising step by step until the whole orchestra is playing the same jaunty tune, at which point it stops. There is no development and almost no contrast: the piece is one long crescendo built out of repetition, and it works.","Written in 1893 for a student pageant in Viipuri, in Karelia, the eastern region shared between Finland and Russia and central to the Finnish national imagination. Sibelius, then 27 and newly famous at home for Kullervo, supplied music for a series of historical tableaux; the performance was chaotic and much of the music inaudible. He salvaged an overture and three movements, and most of the rest was lost for decades.","The march tune is not Karelian folk music, although it is meant to sound like it. Sibelius had collected runic songs in Karelia the previous year and absorbed their turns of phrase rather than quoting them. Listen for the drum, which enters quietly and late and does much of the work of building the crescendo, and notice that the movement is essentially one tune scored several different ways.","The suite has been dismissed as youthful bandmaster music and defended as the first place Sibelius's orchestral personality is clearly audible. The Alla marcia in particular is a favourite of school orchestras and, in Britain, served for years as the theme of a television current-affairs programme, which fixed it in a generation's ears. Recent performances of the reconstructed tableaux music have improved its standing.","Listen for the entries, strings, then winds, then brass, and hear a march assembling itself in front of you. Then hear the suite's Ballade, whose cor anglais solo is this movement's opposite. Finnish orchestras take it faster and lighter than British ones, and it gains from that."],"nielsen-symphony-no-4-inextinguishable-finale":["The last of four linked sections, about nine minutes, played without a break from what precedes it. It opens as a bright, striding fast movement, and then two sets of timpani, placed on opposite sides of the platform and tuned so that they cannot agree, begin firing at each other and at the orchestra. The orchestra fights through the barrage, wins, and lands in a blazing E major.","Composed between 1914 and 1916 while the war Nielsen had watched approaching consumed Europe. Denmark stayed neutral, and Nielsen, a conservatory teacher and former military bandsman, watched from close range. He wrote that music is life and, like life, inextinguishable, and put the sentence in the score as a preface rather than as a programme. He conducted the first performance in Copenhagen on 1 February 1916.","The two timpanists are the point. Nielsen asks for them to be separated as widely as the hall allows and tunes them so that the duel is a genuine collision of keys rather than a noise effect. Listen also for the symphony's opening theme, first heard half an hour earlier, returning in the last pages: the whole work is a single arch, and the four sections have no gaps between them.","Nielsen was a local hero and an international unknown for decades, and the Fourth reached wide audiences only in the 1950s and 1960s through Danish recordings and then Leonard Bernstein's advocacy. Sceptics find the timpani war theatrical. The counter-argument is that the symphony earns it: the effect is prepared across three movements, and nothing else written in 1916 does anything comparable.","Do not start here; the finale means little cold, so hear the whole symphony. Then listen for the moment the second timpanist enters and the music seems to split in two. Live performance is best for the stereo effect. Recordings under Blomstedt or Bernstein make the drums genuinely violent rather than decorative."],"nielsen-symphony-no-5-first-movement":["About twenty minutes, and unlike any other symphonic first movement. It begins with violas oscillating between two notes under a bare bassoon line, cold and directionless, and grows in slow layers. Halfway through, a side drum enters and takes over, instructed to play as though determined to stop the orchestra. The movement ends with the drum retreating into the distance and a long, calm clarinet solo left alone in the hall.","Written in 1921 and 1922, after the war and after Nielsen's heart trouble had begun, and first performed in Copenhagen on 24 January 1922 under the composer. He had abandoned the four-movement plan: the Fifth has two large movements and no title. Nielsen spoke of the work as a conflict between order and what threatens it, and of the drum as the destructive force any settled life has to face down.","The drummer's part is written out but marked to be played freely and out of step with the orchestra, as if to obstruct it; at the end Nielsen asks the player to keep going while moving away from the platform, so the sound physically recedes. The clarinet solo that follows uses the same two-note oscillation the violas began with twenty minutes earlier, now warm instead of blank.","The Edinburgh Festival performance of 1950 under Erik Tuxen made Nielsen's name in Britain almost overnight; before that the Fifth was barely known outside Scandinavia. Percussionists disagree about how far to take the improvisation: some play politely, others try genuinely to wreck the performance, which is closer to the instruction. The offstage fade is frequently bungled, and it is the hardest thing in the score to bring off.","Listen for the entry of the side drum and then decide whose side you are on. Follow the strings' repeated two-note figure from the very first bar, since it is the seed of everything. Recordings under Blomstedt, Bernstein or Osmo Vänskä differ mainly in how brutal the drummer is, so hear at least two."],"nielsen-helios-overture":["Twelve minutes describing a single day: the sun rises over the Aegean, crosses the sky and sets. It opens on almost nothing, low strings holding a bare chord, over which the horns steal in with a rising call; the light builds through a long crescendo into a broad, brassy midday and a busy fugal middle section. The end reverses the opening exactly, sinking back into the same held chord.","Nielsen wrote it in Athens in the spring of 1903, working at the Conservatory there while his wife, the sculptor Anne Marie Carl-Nielsen, studied ancient reliefs on the Acropolis for her own work. He printed a motto in the score about the sun rising from the eastern sea and sinking into the west. It was his first orchestral piece composed outside Denmark, and the light in it is not northern light.","The piece is symmetrical almost to the bar: the closing descent recomposes the opening ascent in reverse, so that the frame is the form. Listen for the fugue in the middle, an old academic device pressed into service to suggest the glare of full noon, and notice that between sunrise and sunset there is no real theme at all. The subject of the music is the changing light, and the material is deliberately plain.","Helios was played early, liked, and then overshadowed by the symphonies; it is now the Nielsen piece most likely to open a concert. Critics have called it a beautiful postcard, and there is something in that, since it is descriptive in a way Nielsen soon abandoned. Danish scholars point out how much of his later long-range crescendo technique is already present here in embryo.","Listen in the dark and let the first two minutes be almost inaudible; the piece depends on starting from nothing. Compare it with Grieg's Morning Mood, which does a similar job in three minutes and never leaves Norway. Danish orchestras under Blomstedt or Schønwandt get the balance between warmth and coolness right."],"nielsen-maskarade-overture":["Five brisk minutes of high spirits. A rushing string figure sets off at once, brass and woodwind interrupt with mock-formal fanfares, and the whole thing bustles along on quick, tumbling phrases without ever settling into a broad tune. There is no slow introduction and no let-up anywhere. It is the curtain-raiser to a comic opera and behaves like one, stopping abruptly on a full-orchestra chord.","Maskarade, first staged at the Royal Theatre in Copenhagen on 11 November 1906, is Denmark's national opera: set in 1723 and based on a comedy by Ludvig Holberg about young people who escape arranged marriages by slipping off to a masked ball. Nielsen, then in his forties and conducting at the theatre, wrote it in Danish for Danish audiences and never expected it to travel. It has barely done so.","The headlong string writing is closer to Mozart's Figaro overture than to anything Nielsen's German contemporaries were producing in 1906: the same breathless tempo, the same refusal to state a broad melody. Listen for how often the music seems about to become a proper tune and is cut off, a comic device Nielsen keeps up for the whole five minutes without once giving in.","In Denmark the opera is performed constantly and the overture is as familiar as a folk song; elsewhere the language and Holberg's local reputation have kept Maskarade rare, despite recordings and the occasional staging in translation. Critics who know it usually argue that it is the best comic opera nobody outside its own country performs, and blame the libretto rather than the music.","Play it after the Mozart or Rossini overtures it resembles and hear how much faster the Danish version talks. Then find the opera's dance sequence, the Hanedans, which is the other famous extract. Recordings under Schønwandt or Ulf Schirmer take it briskly, and anything slower loses the comedy entirely."],"satie-gymnopedie-no-1":["Three minutes for solo piano, marked to be played slowly and painfully. The left hand alternates a low bass note with a soft chord, in strict three-time, and never varies the pattern; over it a plain melody moves in long notes, mostly by step, and comes round twice. There is no climax, no acceleration and no development. The piece ends where it began, having deliberately gone nowhere.","Published in 1888, when Satie was 22, had dropped out of the Paris Conservatoire and was playing piano at the Chat Noir cabaret in Montmartre. Wagner was the reigning god of French musical life, and Satie's response was to write music with almost nothing in it. The title alludes to the festival dances of ancient Sparta, probably by way of a Flaubert novel and a friend's poem, though Satie's classical learning was thin.","The trick is in the harmony. The chord under the melody keeps changing while the bass barely moves, so each repetition of the same tune is coloured differently, and the opening chord waits a long time to resolve. Debussy orchestrated two of the three Gymnopédies in 1897 and swapped their order, so his No. 1 is Satie's No. 3, a confusion that survives in concert programmes to this day.","For thirty years the Gymnopédies were curiosities known to a few Parisians; Debussy's orchestration and then Ravel's advocacy pulled them into view, and after 1960 the minimalists claimed them as an ancestor. John Cage called Satie indispensable. The persistent criticism is that the piece is an idea rather than a composition, and its ubiquity in films and waiting rooms has made hearing it freshly almost impossible.","Play it once with full attention and once as background, and notice that it survives both, which is precisely what Satie was after. Listen to the bass, which is nearly the same throughout, and hear how the chords above it shift. Aldo Ciccolini's recordings are the classic; Reinbert de Leeuw's famously slow ones are worth hearing once."],"satie-gnossienne-no-1":["Four minutes for solo piano, written with no time signature and no bar-lines, so that the phrasing is left to the player. The left hand rocks quietly between a bass note and a chord while a melody in a minor mode, full of turns and grace notes that sound Greek or Middle Eastern, unwinds above it. Written instructions in French are scattered through the score, none of them about volume or speed.","Written around 1890, in the Montmartre years, when Satie was involved with the Rosicrucian order of Sâr Péladan and supplying music for its ceremonies. He had heard Romanian, Arab and Javanese musicians at the Paris Exposition of 1889, the same fair that gave Debussy his gamelan. The word Gnossienne appears to be Satie's own coinage, perhaps from Knossos in Crete, perhaps from the Greek for knowledge.","The written instructions are the strangest thing in the score. Across the set the player is told to play very shiny, from the tip of the thought, and to question himself; they are not performance directions in any usable sense, and Satie meant them for the performer rather than the listener. Musically, note that the melody circles the same few notes and that the piece never changes key at all.","The Gnossiennes were mostly published late, several of them after Satie's death in 1925, and their numbering is an editorial convenience; the set has grown from three to six as more pieces surfaced. Critics divide over whether the barless notation is a genuine rethinking of musical time or a piece of Montmartre provocation. Their afterlife in cinema, notably in Louis Malle's films, has fixed them as shorthand for melancholy.","Listen for the absence of a downbeat: nothing tells you where a bar begins, and good players exploit that freedom. Compare two recordings and time them, since they can differ by more than a minute over four minutes of music. Then hear a Gymnopédie straight afterwards to feel the difference between Satie in three-time and Satie set free of it."],"satie-gymnopedie-no-3":["The third and last of the set, about three minutes, marked slow and grave. The recipe is the same as its famous siblings, a bass note and a soft chord alternating in slow three-time under a plain, unhurried melody, but the writing sits lower, turns to the minor, and is even less inclined to go anywhere. It is the one many listeners have heard without knowing which number they were hearing.","Published with the others in 1888 as a set of three. Satie was living in Montmartre on cabaret work and had not yet acquired the seven identical grey velvet suits, the collection of umbrellas, or the reputation as a professional eccentric that later obscured the music. He went back to being a student in his forties, enrolling at the Schola Cantorum, having decided he needed technique after all.","Debussy's 1897 orchestration includes this piece and calls it Gymnopédie No. 1, which is why the numbering in orchestral programmes contradicts the piano score. The orchestration is worth knowing for what it reveals: Debussy gives the melody to a solo oboe and the accompanying chords to harp and strings, treating Satie's blankness as pure colour, which is one legitimate reading of it.","The three Gymnopédies are usually discussed as a single object, and No. 3 pays the price of not being No. 1. Musicians who play the set complete argue that the sequence matters, since the mood darkens across the three and stopping after the first misrepresents the whole. Satie's reputation still swings between visionary and joker, and these pieces are the main evidence in both cases.","Hear all three in order, which takes under ten minutes, and notice how little changes and how much that little does. Then hear Debussy's orchestral version and decide whether the colours help or intrude. Ciccolini and Anne Queffélec are reliable on the piano; avoid arrangements with added strings and rainfall."],"satie-je-te-veux":["A sung waltz, about five minutes, for voice and piano; Satie also made a solo piano version and a small orchestral one. The tune is frank, catchy and entirely unironic, in the manner of the Paris music halls, with a swinging refrain that comes round three times. Nothing about it is odd: no strange chords, no barless notation, no joke titles. Satie wrote it to be sold and sung.","Written around 1900 for Paulette Darty, a well-known singer of slow waltzes in the Paris cafés-concerts, to words by Henry Pacory; Satie accompanied her and worked as her arranger. He earned his living this way for years, transcribing and composing for Montmartre cabarets while his serious music went unpublished. The lyric is a straightforward invitation to bed, of the kind the halls specialised in.","The interest lies in what the cabaret work did to the concert music: the flat, repeating accompaniments of the Gymnopédies and the endless vamps of the café waltz are the same habit put to different ends. Listen to the refrain's harmony, which is completely conventional and yet moves at the same unhurried pace. Satie later made the connection explicit in his furniture music, written to be ignored.","For a long time the cabaret songs were treated as an embarrassment, work Satie did for money and did not mean. The modern view, following Ornella Volta's research into the manuscripts, is that the two halves of his output cannot be separated. The piece is now a recital regular in both versions. Singers argue over whether to deliver it straight or with a knowing wink, and straight usually wins.","Hear a French singer first, then the solo piano version, which turns the same music into something oddly melancholy. Put it beside a Gymnopédie and notice that the left hand is doing much the same job in both. It also makes an easy way into the world of French song that Poulenc inherited."],"satie-gnossienne-no-3":["Three minutes for solo piano, again written without bar-lines or a time signature. A slow, curling melody in a minor mode turns around a few notes over an accompaniment that alternates the same two chords for most of the piece, with ornamental turns giving the tune an eastern colour. It is the most compact of the set and the one that film and television reach for most often.","Composed around 1890, during the Montmartre years, and published only in 1913, by which time Satie was a Schola Cantorum graduate in his late forties being taken up by Ravel and by the young composers who would become Les Six. The Gnossiennes belong to the world of the 1889 Paris Exposition, where large numbers of visitors heard Javanese, Romanian and Arab musicians for the first time.","Nothing in the piece resolves. The accompaniment settles on two chords and simply alternates them, the melody keeps returning to the same note, and the ending stops rather than closes. Satie also writes instructions into the score that describe a state of mind rather than a way of playing, advice such as counselling yourself carefully, which suggests how he expected the music to be approached.","Satie's stock rose sharply after 1911, when Ravel programmed his early pieces and the orchestrated Gymnopédies were revived, and the Gnossiennes went from private oddity to published music within two years. The critical question has never changed: is the deliberate poverty of means a discovery or an evasion? Composers, from Cage to the minimalists, have overwhelmingly answered the first way.","Listen for the two-chord accompaniment and count how long Satie sticks with it. Then hear No. 1 for comparison, the same method more elaborately worked. Recordings vary enormously in tempo, so try a plain reading and an extremely slow one and decide how much stillness the music will take."],"scriabin-poem-ecstasy":["Twenty minutes for a very large orchestra, eight horns, five trumpets, organ and two harps, in one continuous span. It begins with a hesitant flute and violin idea, and over the next quarter of an hour dozens of short motifs are stacked, interrupted and started again, always breaking off just before they arrive. The last two minutes finally deliver: a solo trumpet climbs over the whole orchestra and organ into a held C major chord.","Written between 1905 and 1908, mostly in Switzerland and Italy, while Scriabin was living apart from his wife with Tatyana Schloezer and reading theosophy. He published a long ecstatic poem alongside the score, about the flight of the creative spirit, and wanted the two taken together. The premiere took place in New York on 10 December 1908 under Modest Altschuler, with the Russian performances following soon after.","The whole piece is a study in postponement: Scriabin builds climaxes and withdraws from them, several times over, so the final arrival lands with disproportionate force. Listen for the solo trumpet, which carries the theme associated in his sketches with the will, and count how often it is cut off before the end. The closing chord is held for over twenty seconds under a clangour of bells.","Critics have called it the most erotic orchestral piece ever written, sometimes admiringly; the composer's own account of it as a cosmic act of self-assertion is harder to take seriously than the sound itself. It fell out of favour in mid-century as excess and returned with conductors willing to let it be as extreme as written. The theosophical programme is now generally set aside.","Give it your full attention and expect to be teased for fifteen minutes. Track the solo trumpet. Then hear the last three minutes again, loud, on a system that can take it. Compare a Russian recording with Boulez's, which treats the piece as a study in orchestral colour and refuses the hysteria altogether."],"scriabin-etude-op-8-no-12":["Three minutes of solo piano, and one of the loudest small pieces in the repertoire. A hammered melody in the right hand, doubled in octaves, rides over a leaping left-hand accompaniment that has to cover huge distances at speed. Marked patetico, it sits in the awkward black-note key of D-sharp minor, hardly lets up for a bar, and ends with a plunge down the whole keyboard.","One of twelve studies written in the early 1890s and published by Belyayev, who was subsidising the young composer, which is why so much early Scriabin survives in print. He was in his early twenties, touring as a pianist, and still writing music that sounded like Chopin with a Russian accent. His hands were small and he had already injured his right one by overpractising, and the piano writing works around both facts.","The left hand is the real difficulty, not the tune: it leaps a tenth or more repeatedly, at speed, which is why a pianist with a small stretch cannot fake it. The melody is almost always doubled in octaves, so the piece has no counterpoint and no quiet moments, just weight. Scriabin played it constantly on tour, and it became the piece audiences demanded from him.","Horowitz made it a signature encore and defined how it is played: fast, dry and violent. Purists complain that his added octaves and small cuts are not Scriabin's; others reply that Scriabin was himself a free interpreter of his own music. The étude is sometimes cited to show that early Scriabin is derivative Chopin, though the physical aggression here is not Chopin's manner at all.","Hear Horowitz first for the shock, then a straighter modern account for what is actually written. Listen to the left hand rather than the melody. Then play Chopin's Revolutionary study next to it and hear how much further Scriabin pushes the same idea in the same three minutes."],"scriabin-prometheus-poem-fire":["Twenty minutes for large orchestra, solo piano, wordless chorus and a keyboard of coloured light. The piece grows out of a single six-note chord sounded at the start by muted horns and strings, and almost every harmony in the score is a version of it. The piano darts across the texture rather than leading it, the chorus enters only near the end, and the whole thing climbs to a shattering final chord.","Completed in 1910 after Scriabin's return to Moscow and first performed there on 15 March 1911 under Serge Koussevitzky, with the composer at the piano. Scriabin had convinced himself that sounds carried colours and wrote a part for a light keyboard, the clavier à lumières, meant to flood the hall with changing colour. The technology did not exist; the light part was left out at the premiere and has been realised only occasionally since.","The six-note chord is not a mystery but a working method. Scriabin treated it simultaneously as harmony and as melody, so tunes and chords are made of the same material, which is why the piece has almost no dissonance in the ordinary sense and also no resolution to move towards. The light part is written out on its own stave at the top of the score, in ordinary notation.","Contemporaries split between reverence and ridicule, and the composer's claim that this was a step towards a work that would end the world did not help. Modern opinion takes the harmonic method seriously, setting it beside Schoenberg's contemporaneous solutions to the same problem, and treats the theosophy as biography. Performances with the light part have been mounted since 1915, mostly unconvincingly.","Listen for the opening chord and then try to hear it everywhere else; once you can, the piece stops sounding formless. Notice how the piano is used as a colour rather than as a soloist. Recordings under Ashkenazy or Gergiev are secure, and filmed performances with the light part realised are worth seeing once for the idea, if not the effect."],"scriabin-piano-sonata-no-5":["Twelve minutes in one unbroken movement, the first of Scriabin's sonatas to abandon the multi-movement plan. It erupts out of a low rumbling in the bass, subsides into a slow, sweet theme marked to be played languidly, then accelerates through waves of increasingly frantic music and finally hurtles off the top of the keyboard without settling into any key at all.","Written in a few days late in 1907, immediately after finishing The Poem of Ecstasy, and published at his own expense when no publisher would take it. He prefaced it with lines from his own ecstasy poem, summoning hidden forces to life. He was 35, living outside Russia with Tatyana Schloezer, and moving away from conventional tonality faster than almost anyone else in Europe at the time.","Scriabin's performance directions are the real map. He asks for the music to be languid, then imperious, then played with growing intoxication, and the sonata's structure is a sequence of those states rather than of themes. Listen for the low rumble that opens the piece: it returns twice, each time as an interruption, and the ending simply escapes upward rather than concluding.","Pianists rank it among the hardest things in the standard repertoire, and it became a competition warhorse in the twentieth century. Critics argue about whether the later sonatas lose the sense of line that this one still has, and about whether the ecstatic markings should shape performance or be treated as private notes to himself. Sofronitsky's recordings remain the reference for the Russian tradition.","Listen for the two eruptions of the opening rumble, which divide the piece. Then follow the slow theme and hear how it is gradually sped up until it becomes the frantic music. Compare Sofronitsky's inward reading with Horowitz's blazing one, and then hear The Poem of Ecstasy for the orchestral version of the same journey."],"scriabin-nocturne-left-hand":["Five minutes for the left hand alone, and the illusion is complete: melody, bass and a rippling accompaniment sound like two hands at work. The tune is broad and Chopinesque, the accompaniment arpeggiates constantly beneath it, and the middle section grows more agitated before the opening returns. Most listeners do not notice the restriction at all until they watch the piece being played.","Written in 1894, when Scriabin, in his early twenties, had injured his right hand by overpractising showpieces, and was warned he might never recover its use. Balakirev's Islamey and a Liszt fantasy are the works usually blamed. He wrote this nocturne and a companion prelude for the hand that still worked. The injury healed, but his piano writing kept the habits of balance and pedalling it taught him.","The single hand must leap constantly between the bass note, the arpeggio and the melody, which sits in the middle of the texture and has to be brought out by weight alone. The sustaining pedal holds notes the hand has already left, so the real second player is the pedal. Listen for the melody, which is almost never at the top of the sound, where the ear expects to find it.","It is the earliest piece in the small left-hand repertoire to be widely played, and it prepared the ground for the commissions Paul Wittgenstein made after losing his right arm in the First World War, Ravel's concerto among them. Critics find it derivative, since the debt to Chopin is unconcealed, but pianists keep it because it teaches balance and pedalling better than almost anything else.","Watch a video performance before you listen properly; knowing that one hand is doing everything changes what you hear. Then listen for the melody buried in the middle of the texture. Compare it with Chopin's nocturnes for the model, and with Ravel's Concerto for the Left Hand for where the idea eventually led."],"vaughan-williams-lark-ascending":["Fifteen minutes for solo violin and small orchestra. The violin enters alone, unaccompanied and without a fixed beat, on a rising figure that climbs and hovers; the orchestra creeps in underneath with quiet, modal harmony. Two folk-like tunes follow, one of them dance-like with woodwind, before the violin climbs away on its own again and stops in mid-air. The soloist plays almost continuously and almost never loudly.","Sketched in 1914 and set aside when Vaughan Williams enlisted, at 41, as a stretcher-bearer; the story that he was arrested as a spy for writing it down on the Kent coast as war began is probably embroidered. He returned to it after the war and rewrote it for orchestra. Marie Hall, who had advised him on the violin writing, gave both the 1920 version with piano and the orchestral premiere.","The unaccompanied passages have no bar-lines and no fixed tempo; the score simply tells the soloist to play freely, so no two performances have the same shape. The tunes sound like folk songs but are Vaughan Williams's own, built from the gapped scales he had been collecting from village singers since 1903. The score carries lines from George Meredith's poem, which supplies both the title and the ending.","It has topped British radio listener polls for years, which has made critics wary of it: too pretty, too pastoral, a piece of national self-soothing. The counter-case points to what surrounds it, the war and the death of his friend George Butterworth, and argues that the calm is achieved rather than assumed. Debate continues over the original violin and piano version, now recorded and increasingly played.","Listen for the two unaccompanied solos, at the start and the end, and hear how the orchestra simply stops existing. Then hear the violin and piano version for the same music without the haze. Hugh Bean's recording with Boult is the classic; more recent readings are broader and more openly emotional."],"vaughan-williams-fantasia-theme-by-thomas-tallis":["Sixteen minutes for strings divided into three groups: a full orchestra, a small distant orchestra of nine players, and a solo quartet. The Tallis hymn tune is stated in bare, open harmony and then passed between the groups, so that answers arrive from far away. A long solo viola melody sits at the centre. The sound is deliberately archaic, with cadences that fall the wrong way by nineteenth-century rules.","Written for the Three Choirs Festival and first performed in Gloucester Cathedral on 6 September 1910, conducted by the composer, in a building whose long echo the scoring exploits. Vaughan Williams had just finished editing the English Hymnal, for which he had trawled Tudor psalters and found the tune. He was 37, had recently taken three months of lessons from Ravel in Paris, and this is where his own voice arrives.","The theme is Tallis's third setting for Archbishop Parker's psalter of 1567, and it is in the Phrygian mode, whose falling half-step at the top gives the whole fantasia its bittersweet cast. Vaughan Williams asks for the second orchestra to be placed apart from the first, so the echoes are real rather than simulated, and he twice revised the scoring, thinning it each time.","The premiere baffled some critics, one of whom thought it too long; within a generation it was regarded as the beginning of modern English music. The main argument now is acoustic: whether to record it in a cathedral, where the piece was designed to blur, or in a studio, where the counterpoint stays audible. The placing of the second orchestra varies wildly between performances.","Listen in a big, reverberant recording first and try to locate the distant group. Then hear a clearer studio version and follow the solo viola. Compare it with Tallis's original hymn tune, which lasts two minutes, to see how much Vaughan Williams grew from how little material."],"vaughan-williams-fantasia-greensleeves":["Five minutes for flutes, harp and strings. The familiar tune arrives after a brief flute introduction, played plainly, then returns decorated; between the two statements comes a different and much less familiar folk melody, faster and brighter, before the opening returns and fades. Nothing is developed or argued. It is an arrangement made with great care about texture, and it lasts exactly as long as the tunes will bear.","The music comes from Vaughan Williams's opera Sir John in Love, staged in 1929, where the tune belongs to the Falstaff story; Ralph Greaves made this concert arrangement in 1934 with the composer's approval, and it long outsold everything it came from. Greensleeves itself is Elizabethan, mentioned by Shakespeare, and by the 1930s was as familiar in England as a nursery rhyme.","The middle tune is not Greensleeves at all but Lovely Joan, a folk song Vaughan Williams had collected in Norfolk from a singer in the field, one of hundreds he took down before they were lost. Listen for the harp, used not as decoration but as the harmonic floor, which lets the strings stay very thin. The version usually heard is Greaves's, not the composer's own scoring.","It is the piece most often used to caricature Vaughan Williams as a purveyor of English nostalgia, and admirers of the symphonies tend to be defensive about it. The fair points are that he did not arrange it himself and that its popularity has distorted his reputation. Its craft is not really in question, and the opera it came from has been revived partly on its strength.","Listen for the switch to the second tune about two minutes in and notice that it is the better melody. Then find Sir John in Love, or a recording of the Lovely Joan folk song, for the raw material. Any decent English string orchestra recording will do; versions with heavy vibrato are best avoided."],"vaughan-williams-english-folk-song-suite":["Ten minutes in three movements for military band, and almost always heard in the orchestral arrangement Gordon Jacob made a year later. A brisk march built on two folk tunes; a slow intermezzo, My Bonny Boy, given to a solo oboe over a quiet accompaniment; and a closing march that stitches together several Somerset songs. The tunes are quoted straight, and the interest is in the joins and the scoring.","Written in 1923 for the band of the Royal Military School of Music at Kneller Hall, which wanted serious repertoire from serious composers. Vaughan Williams had spent twenty years collecting folk songs, cycling round Norfolk, Sussex and Somerset with a notebook and taking tunes down from elderly singers before they died with them. He had also served through the war and knew exactly what a military band sounded like.","Every tune is a real collected song with words, and most of the words are not pastoral at all: Seventeen Come Sunday is a seduction and My Bonny Boy a lament for a faithless lover. A fourth movement, Sea Songs, was published separately and is usually omitted, so the suite as played is not quite the suite as written. The countermelodies are Vaughan Williams's own, not folk material.","Band musicians regard it as a foundation of their repertoire, while orchestral critics have sometimes treated it as light music with a pedigree. Scholarly interest has shifted to the collecting itself: whose songs these were, what was quietly edited out of the words, and how much the collectors shaped what they claimed merely to preserve. Recordings of the original band scoring have become more common.","Hear the band version first, since it is the real one and the sound is coarser and better. Then compare Jacob's orchestration. Look up the words of Seventeen Come Sunday before listening and the opening march changes character entirely. Follow it with Holst's suites for military band, written a decade earlier for the same world."],"vaughan-williams-london-symphony-lento":["Eleven minutes, the slow movement of the second symphony, and the point at which the noise of the city drops away. A quiet, grey orchestral haze opens it; a cor anglais sings a long, almost unaccompanied melody over it; a viola solo answers. The middle grows troubled before the haze returns. The composer described the movement as Bloomsbury Square on a November afternoon, and the light in it is exactly that.","Written between 1911 and 1913 and first performed in March 1914, months before the war swallowed the world it describes; the manuscript was then sent abroad, lost, and reconstructed from the orchestral parts. Vaughan Williams revised the symphony repeatedly, cutting close to twenty minutes across thirty years, and the 1936 version is standard. He insisted it was a symphony by a Londoner rather than a piece of tone painting.","Listen for the street cries: a hawker's call worked into the texture so lightly that it passes for ordinary melody. The jingle of a hansom cab appears elsewhere in the symphony, and the Westminster chimes are quoted in the outer movements. The 1913 original of this movement is several minutes longer and has been recorded, so the cuts can now be heard as well as read about.","Early listeners loved the symphony and Vaughan Williams spent decades cutting it, which suggests he thought them wrong. The restored 1913 version, recorded by Richard Hickox, reopened the question: some critics prefer its sprawl, most accept the composer's judgment. The pastoral label usually attached to Vaughan Williams sits awkwardly on a symphony about traffic, poverty and fog.","Listen for the cor anglais entry and follow that single line to its end. Then hear the whole symphony, and finally the 1913 version to hear what was cut. Boult's recordings are the traditional choice; Andrew Manze's more recent cycle is lighter and clearer and makes the city sound less sentimental."],"vaughan-williams-wasps-overture":["Nine minutes, opening with the strings buzzing, a genuine imitation of insects made by rapid repeated notes, before a broad, striding tune arrives that sounds like a folk song and is not. Two or three good melodies alternate, a quiet middle section slows things down, and the buzzing returns to drive the piece to a cheerful end. It is the most extrovert music Vaughan Williams wrote before the war.","Composed in 1909 as incidental music for a student production of Aristophanes' comedy The Wasps at Cambridge, performed in Greek, part of a tradition of Greek plays that still continues there. Vaughan Williams was 36, had recently returned from three months of lessons with Ravel in Paris, and the commission handed him an orchestra to experiment with. The overture is all that is regularly played from about an hour of music.","The buzzing is scored precisely rather than left to chance, and it returns at the very end, framing the piece as a joke rather than a nature study. The big tune is Vaughan Williams's own, though it has been mistaken for a folk song often enough that the mistake has found its way into programme notes. The complete score also contains a March-Past of the Kitchen Utensils, which is exactly what it sounds like.","The overture escaped the play at once and has been a British concert opener ever since, while the complete score is rarely revived; recordings of the full suite have made the case that the rest deserves hearing. Critics note that Ravel's lessons show in the scoring and not at all in the melodies, which is the characteristic English compromise of the period.","Listen for the buzz at the start and its return at the end. Then wait for the big tune and try not to assume it is traditional. Compare it with Mendelssohn's Midsummer Night's Dream overture, written for another comedy by another young composer. Boult and Previn both take it at the right brisk pace."],"vaughan-williams-five-variants-dives-lazarus":["Twelve minutes for strings and harp, in one unbroken span. The folk tune, a modal English melody with a swinging lilt, is not so much varied as re-lit five times: low then high, thick then bare, with the harp adding a cold shimmer around it. There are no fast passages and no display of any kind; the piece moves at one slow pace throughout and ends quieter than it began.","Commissioned by the British Council for the New York World's Fair and first performed at Carnegie Hall on 10 June 1939, weeks before the war began. Vaughan Williams, then 66, had known the Dives and Lazarus tune all his life: it circulates in England under several names and several sets of words, sacred and secular, and he had already used it in the English Hymnal, where it carries the hymn Kingsfold.","The variants do not correspond to any single printed version of the tune. Vaughan Williams said he had drawn on a family of related melodies rather than one, which is why the theme keeps changing shape between statements: the piece is about the way a folk tune exists in many forms at once. Listen for the harp, which never carries a melody and functions almost as an acoustic.","It was overshadowed by the war it preceded and by the Fifth Symphony that followed, and became a repertory piece only late in the century. Critics who resist the composer's English manner find this the hardest of his pieces to dismiss, because the material is genuinely traditional and the treatment genuinely austere. Recent scholarship reads it as an elegy for a rural culture that had already gone.","Listen for the first statement of the tune, low in the strings, and then follow it through its returns without worrying about which variant you are in. Play it beside the Tallis Fantasia, the same instrument thirty years apart. Boult's recordings are the reference; Hickox and Manze are excellent and rather less reverent."],"rachmaninoff-piano-concerto-no-2":["Three movements, about thirty-five minutes. The piano begins alone with eight slow chords that swell like bells being rung one after another, and only then do the strings arrive with the long minor tune the chords were preparing. The slow movement floats a flute and clarinet melody over rippling piano figuration; the finale drives hard in march rhythm before opening into a broad tune for the strings that the whole orchestra eventually shouts back.","Rachmaninoff was twenty-seven and recovering from the collapse of his First Symphony in 1897, whose disastrous premiere left him unable to write for three years. A Moscow physician, Nikolai Dahl, treated him with hypnotic suggestion and conversation through 1900; the concerto is dedicated to him. The last two movements were played in December 1900, the whole work in Moscow in autumn 1901 with the composer as soloist and his cousin Alexander Siloti conducting.","Listen closer: the opening chords never return as chords, but the interval that swings between them keeps reappearing inside melodies for the rest of the piece. Notice too how rarely the piano is left alone once the first movement gets going - Rachmaninoff usually gives the big tunes to the orchestra and keeps the soloist accompanying, which is why the piano part is exhausting yet often inaudible as melody.","Audiences took to it immediately and never let go; critics for decades treated its popularity as evidence against it, and the 1954 Grove dictionary notoriously predicted it would not last. Film and popular song borrowed it constantly, which hardened the prejudice. Recent scholarship has been kinder, looking at how tightly the thematic material is worked and how much of the writing is genuinely orchestral rather than pianistic display.","Count the eight opening chords and hear them get louder rather than faster - that is the whole work's method in miniature. Then jump to the last three minutes of the finale for the tune everyone knows. The composer's own 1929 recording with Stokowski is faster and drier than modern performances and worth hearing as a corrective."],"rachmaninoff-rhapsody-theme-paganini-18th-variation":["Three minutes lifted out of a twenty-four-minute set of variations for piano and orchestra. The piano states a long, warm melody alone, the strings take it over and swell, and the piano decorates it in rising arpeggios before the whole thing subsides. It is the only slow, sweet stretch in a piece otherwise full of hard glitter, and it arrives at exactly the point where a listener has stopped expecting one.","Written in seven weeks during the summer of 1934 at Senar, the villa Rachmaninoff had built for himself above Lake Lucerne, and premiered that November in Baltimore with the Philadelphia Orchestra under Leopold Stokowski, the composer at the piano. He had been living as an exile since 1917 and composing very little; touring as a pianist paid for everything. He knew what he had here, remarking that this variation was for his agent.","Listen closer: the melody is not new. It is Paganini's caprice tune turned upside down - every rise in the original becomes a fall - and then set in a major key at half speed. Nothing is added; the shape is simply inverted. Rachmaninoff hides the trick by changing the harmony completely, so almost nobody hears the connection to the spiky theme that has been running for seventeen variations.","The variation became famous separately from its parent work, carried by films from 1945 onwards, and is now often played as a standalone encore in arrangements the composer never made. Purists object that hearing it alone destroys the point, since its warmth depends on the seventeen dry variations before it and the Dies irae chant that stalks the rest of the score. Concert programmers have largely stopped extracting it.","Hear it once on its own, then listen from variation eleven so you arrive at it the way Rachmaninoff intended - after the cadenza-like drift and the shift into the flat key. Then play Paganini's Caprice No. 24 and try to trace the inversion. Any recording with a conductor willing to let the strings take their time will do."],"rachmaninoff-prelude-c":["Four minutes in three blocks. Three heavy, widely spaced notes toll at the very bottom of the keyboard, answered by thick slow chords in the middle; a faster, agitated middle section runs in triplets and builds to a climax where the opening tolling returns hammered out in massive four-note chords across both hands; then it recedes to the same three notes, quiet again. The dynamic range asked for runs from the softest possible to the loudest.","Rachmaninoff was nineteen, freshly graduated from the Moscow Conservatory with the highest honours, and broke. He played the prelude himself at an electrical exhibition in Moscow in September 1892, in a group of five pieces published as Morceaux de fantaisie. Russia had no international copyright treaty, so the piece sold in enormous numbers abroad and earned him almost nothing beyond the flat fee, a grievance he repeated for the rest of his life.","Listen closer: the three tolling notes at the start are also the top voice of the chords that answer them, so the bell figure is embedded inside the harmony from the first bar rather than merely introducing it. The middle section is built from the same three notes compressed into fast triplets. The nickname The Bells of Moscow was attached by publishers, not by the composer, who disliked it.","It made him internationally famous before he had written anything he valued, and English and American audiences demanded it as an encore at nearly every recital he gave for fifty years; he came to loathe playing it. Critics have argued about whether its popularity flattened perception of his later, subtler piano music. As a piece of writing it is still admired for how much noise it extracts from a slow tempo.","Listen for the exact moment the middle section stops running and the bells come back in giant chords - that is the hinge, and pianists differ enormously in how much time they take over it. Compare a Rachmaninoff piano-roll recording with a modern concert performance; his own is startlingly brisk and unsentimental."],"rachmaninoff-vocalise":["A six-minute song with no words. The singer sustains a single open vowel of her choosing throughout while the piano supplies a slow, stepwise accompaniment that keeps sliding chromatically under the voice. The melody unfolds in one long descending arc, climbs once to a high sustained climax near the end, and settles back. Nothing repeats exactly; the line simply keeps spinning out, which is why it is so difficult to breathe.","The last of the fourteen songs of Op. 34, added after the rest were finished and dedicated to the soprano Antonina Nezhdanova of the Bolshoi. When she asked why he had given her no text, he replied that words would only get in the way of what a voice can do. Rachmaninoff revised it in 1915 and made his own orchestral version; it was among the last things he wrote before leaving Russia for good.","Listen closer: the piano is not accompanying so much as descending. Under the voice, an inner line steps downward by half-steps almost continuously, a device inherited from Baroque laments, and it is that quiet sinking, not the melody, that gives the piece its ache. Notice also that the singer never lands on a full close until the very last bar; every phrase ending is deflected onto a new harmony.","It has been arranged for practically everything - violin, cello, saxophone, theremin, full orchestra, choir - and critics have complained since the 1920s that the arrangements sentimentalise a piece written for a specific human limitation, the need to breathe. Sung performances are now comparatively rare in concert. Scholars point out that its wordlessness was a deliberate late-Romantic experiment, not a lack of a text.","Seek out a sung performance before any instrumental one, and listen for where the singer chooses to breathe - the phrase lengths are the composition. Then compare a cello version to hear what is gained in warmth and lost in strain. Good pairing: the slow movement of his Second Symphony, written from the same melodic instinct."],"rachmaninoff-piano-concerto-no-3-first-movement":["Seventeen minutes. After two bars of quiet accompaniment the piano enters with both hands in unison on a plain, narrow, chant-like tune that never rises far - one of the most unassuming openings in the repertoire, and the seed of everything that follows. The movement grows steadily denser until it reaches a cadenza of real ferocity, after which the flute, oboe, clarinet and horn quietly re-enter over the piano's murmuring, and the opening tune returns transformed.","Written in the summer of 1909 at Ivanovka, the family estate in the Tambov steppe, specifically for a first American tour. Rachmaninoff learned it on the boat over, practising on a dummy keyboard, and played the premiere in New York that November with Walter Damrosch. Two months later he played it again with the New York Philharmonic under Gustav Mahler, whose meticulous rehearsing impressed him more than anything else on the trip.","Listen closer: the opening theme moves almost entirely by step within a narrow span, like Russian church chant. Rachmaninoff denied taking it from any liturgical source and said it simply wrote itself. There are two written cadenzas - a heavy chordal one and a lighter, faster alternative - and pianists still choose between them; the choice changes the entire weight of the movement, and the composer himself usually played the lighter.","Its reputation as the hardest concerto in the repertoire has done it both favours and damage, sharpened by the 1996 film Shine. Early critics found it long-winded next to the Second Concerto; Josef Hofmann, its dedicatee, never played it. Vladimir Horowitz's advocacy from the 1930s made it a standard test of pianistic nerve, and modern scholarship stresses how economically the whole movement grows from that one bare opening line.","Play the first thirty seconds twice before going on, so you can recognise the tune later when it is buried under octaves and inner voices. Then listen straight through to the cadenza and note that the tune is still there inside the avalanche. Horowitz with Ormandy in 1978 is the celebrated document; any modern recording that keeps the opening quiet and plain is on the right track."],"rachmaninoff-symphony-no-2-adagio":["The third of four movements, around fifteen minutes and the longest slow movement in the Russian symphonic repertoire. The violins begin a soaring line, and then the solo clarinet takes over with a melody that keeps refusing to end - it extends itself for nearly three minutes without a clear cadence, the strings holding a soft cushion beneath. A more agitated middle builds and dissolves, the clarinet tune returns in the strings, and the movement ebbs away.","Rachmaninoff moved his family to Dresden in 1906 to escape the political unrest and the constant conducting work in Moscow, and wrote the symphony there in near-total privacy. It was his answer to the First Symphony, savaged nine years earlier at a badly prepared, badly conducted premiere. He conducted the first performance in St Petersburg in early 1908 and it won the Glinka Prize; the wound was closed.","Listen closer: the falling four-note motto that opened the symphony's first movement in the cellos and basses is threaded quietly through this Adagio, usually in an inner voice while the clarinet sings above it. Notice too that the great clarinet melody is accompanied by a completely separate violin line moving at its own pace - two independent tunes at once, which is why the texture sounds fuller than the thin scoring should allow.","For fifty years the symphony was almost never heard whole. Conductors made deep cuts, sometimes removing a third of the score, and the practice was defended as necessary. André Previn's uncut performances and recording in the early 1970s changed opinion decisively, and cut versions are now rare. Judgment of the work rose with its restoration: what looked like padding turned out to be the slow-burning structure.","Start here rather than at the beginning of the symphony, and time the clarinet solo - noticing how long it goes without settling is the whole experience. Then hear the finale to feel how the same material is turned brilliant. Look for a complete, uncut recording; Previn with the London Symphony remains the reference."],"rachmaninoff-prelude-g":["A four-minute march in three parts. It opens with clipped, dry chords in a stiff dotted rhythm, hands alternating like a drum and a bugle, kept deliberately unlyrical. The middle section abandons the march entirely for a long singing melody in the right hand over sweeping arpeggios in the left, in the major. The march then returns, harder and fuller, and stamps out an ending on repeated chords.","Written in 1901, the same year as the Second Piano Concerto, and published two years later in the ten preludes of Op. 23. Rachmaninoff was rebuilding a career as a composer-pianist and needed recital material of his own that was not the C sharp minor prelude everyone demanded. The Op. 23 set, together with the later Op. 32, completed his own cycle of preludes through all twenty-four keys, following Chopin's precedent.","Listen closer: the march is built on a rhythm, not a tune - there is barely a melody in the outer sections, only a rhythmic cell hammered at different pitches, which is why it survives being played at wildly different speeds. In the central episode the left hand covers more than four octaves in a single sweep; Rachmaninoff's famously enormous stretch is audible in the spacing rather than in any obvious display.","It became, with the C sharp minor prelude, one of the two Rachmaninoff pieces every conservatory student learns, and suffers from being played too fast and too loud. Critics have noted that the middle section is the real invention and the march the frame. Performance debate centres on the dotted rhythm: crisply military, as the composer played it, or softened and rounded, as many later Romantic pianists preferred.","Listen to the middle section first, then the whole thing, so the march registers as an interruption of the song rather than the other way round. Compare two performances at different tempos - the piece changes character completely. Rachmaninoff's own recording is the corrective to every heavy modern reading."],"rachmaninoff-vespers-bogoroditse-devo":["Three minutes for unaccompanied choir, the sixth of fifteen movements in the All-Night Vigil. The text is the Orthodox greeting to the Virgin, the equivalent of the Ave Maria. It begins almost inaudibly, a single line passed between the voice parts and doubled in soft parallel harmony, swells once in the middle on the words blessed art thou, and withdraws to the same hush. There is no accompaniment anywhere in the work and no instruments are permitted.","Composed in under two weeks in January and February 1915, and first sung that March in Moscow by the Synodal Choir at a concert for war relief; it was repeated five times that season. Russian church music had been recovering its own chant traditions since the 1880s under Stepan Smolensky, to whose memory the Vigil is dedicated. Within three years the Revolution made liturgical performance in Russia effectively impossible.","Listen closer: nine of the fifteen movements quote real chant, but this one does not. Rachmaninoff wrote the melody himself and called such pieces conscious counterfeits of chant - it sounds ancient because of how it moves, by step and within a narrow band, never because it is old. Listen also for how the basses are used less as a bottom line than as a slow-moving drone the harmony leans against.","Banned from Russian churches for most of the Soviet period and rarely heard complete in the West before the 1960s, the Vigil is now regarded as the summit of Orthodox choral writing and, by many, as Rachmaninoff's finest work. Argument continues over whether Western choirs can produce the low bass sound it requires - the fifth movement descends to a note most basses cannot reach - and over concert versus liturgical pacing.","Hear it sung by a Russian choir first for the weight of the bass sound, then by a Western choir for clarity of the inner parts; the difference is the whole performance-practice debate. Listen for the single swell in the middle and how little Rachmaninoff needs to make it register. The neighbouring Nunc dimittis is the natural next step."],"rachmaninoff-isle-dead":["A twenty-minute symphonic poem in one span. It opens with a rocking, lopsided rhythm in the low strings and harp - five beats to a bar, so it never settles into a comfortable sway - that continues almost unbroken for six minutes as the music climbs out of the depths. A faster central section rises to a violent climax, then the rowing figure returns and carries everything back down into the dark where it started.","Rachmaninoff saw a black-and-white reproduction of Arnold Böcklin's much-copied painting in Paris in 1907, of a boat carrying a shrouded figure to a cypress-covered rock. He wrote the piece in Dresden and conducted the Moscow premiere in spring 1909. When he later saw the painting in colour he said he would not have written the music had he seen the original first - the grey reproduction had done the work.","Listen closer: the four-note plainchant Dies irae, the medieval hymn of judgement, appears about halfway through in the low brass and then infiltrates almost every later phrase. It became Rachmaninoff's lifelong signature, returning in the Paganini Rhapsody and the Symphonic Dances. Notice too that the rowing rhythm is in five, which the ear tries and fails to hear as a waltz - the unease is metrical before it is harmonic.","Admired from the start for its orchestration and long ignored as programme music of a dated kind, it has risen sharply in estimation over the last fifty years; conductors now treat it as his most tightly argued orchestral score. The main performance argument is tempo: taken too fast, the opening becomes a scherzo and the whole design collapses. The composer's own 1929 recording is unusually urgent and has confused the question.","Look at the Böcklin painting first, then let the first six minutes run without expecting a tune - the point is the rowing. Wait for the low brass to bring in the chant. Then compare Rachmaninoff's own brisk recording with a slower modern one to hear how much the tempo changes the story."],"rachmaninoff-symphonic-dances-first-movement":["Twelve minutes, the first of three dances. A hard, clipped three-note figure snaps back and forth in the strings and woodwind over a marching bass - dry, angular, almost mechanical. The middle section stops the machine entirely and hands a long, sinuous melody to a solo alto saxophone over muted strings, the only significant saxophone solo Rachmaninoff ever wrote. The march resumes, and the movement ends with a broad, glowing tune in the strings that seems to come from nowhere.","His last completed work, written in the summer of 1940 in a rented house on Long Island, between concert tours, at the age of sixty-seven. He had been out of Russia for twenty-two years and had composed six works in that time. Eugene Ormandy and the Philadelphia Orchestra, his favourite ensemble, gave the premiere in January 1941 and received the dedication. He had first sketched the movements with the titles Noon, Twilight and Midnight, then removed them.","Listen closer: the glowing tune at the end is the main theme of his First Symphony of 1895, the work whose premiere had destroyed him as a young man and which had never been performed since. The score was thought lost; he was quoting music no living listener could recognise. On the final page of the third dance he wrote a line of thanks to God - the only such inscription in his output.","Received politely in 1941 and long treated as a nostalgic postscript, the Symphonic Dances are now widely considered his sharpest and most modern score - lean, percussive, closer to Prokofiev than to the Second Concerto. The discovery of the First Symphony's parts in Leningrad in 1944 made the self-quotation audible for the first time and transformed how the piece is read. A two-piano version by the composer is also performed.","Listen for the saxophone entry and note how completely the mood changes, then wait for the last ninety seconds and the broad string tune - knowing it is a forty-five-year-old ghost changes everything. Pair it with the Isle of the Dead to hear the same death chant used a lifetime apart. Any Philadelphia recording carries the sound he had in mind."],"holst-planets-mars":["Seven minutes of accumulating violence, the first of seven planetary movements. A single rhythm - five beats to a bar, hammered - starts almost inaudibly with the strings struck by the wood of the bow, and never stops. Over it Holst piles brass fanfares, a grinding low tune in the tenor tuba, and two chords that grate against each other because they belong to different keys. It ends with five colossal orchestral blows.","Holst began Mars in the summer of 1914, before the outbreak of war rather than in response to it, though the coincidence has shadowed the piece ever since. He was teaching at St Paul's Girls' School and Morley College and composing at weekends; neuritis in his right hand meant much of the score was written out by two colleagues acting as amanuenses. A private premiere of the suite was given at the Queen's Hall in 1918 under Adrian Boult.","Listen closer: the piece is astrological, not astronomical. Holst had been introduced to horoscopes in 1913 and called astrology his pet vice; each movement portrays a character, not a place, which is why there is no Earth. Notice that the five-beat rhythm is played by strings using the wood of the bow at the start - a scraping, dry sound that no amount of brass ever entirely covers.","It was recognised at once as something new and became the model for every subsequent musical depiction of mechanised menace, from film scores to progressive rock; Holst grew tired of being known for it. Critics have argued over whether its brutality is prophetic or merely coincidental to 1914. Performance debate centres on speed: Holst's own recordings are faster and more relentless than the ponderous readings that became standard after 1950.","Count in fives from the very first bar and keep counting; once you hear the meter, the movement makes sense as one unbroken machine. Then listen to Venus immediately after for the deliberate shock of the contrast. A recording with a big, dry, close-miked brass section serves it better than a reverberant one."],"holst-planets-jupiter":["Eight minutes and the most extrovert of the seven movements. It bursts in with rushing violins and a bouncing horn tune, piles up three or four distinct melodies in quick succession, then stops dead for the central section: a broad, slow, hymn-like tune given plainly to the strings in unison, repeated once with the full orchestra behind it. The opening bustle returns, and a short coda hurries the movement to a bright close.","Written in the same burst as Mars in 1914, and subtitled the Bringer of Jollity. Holst, a trombonist by training and a lifelong socialist who taught working adults at Morley College, was writing for the largest orchestra he ever used, including two harps, organ and six timpani. The suite reached the public in 1919 and 1920 and made him famous almost overnight, a fame he found intrusive and largely unwelcome.","Listen closer: the great central tune had a second life. In 1921 Holst adapted it to Cecil Spring Rice's words as the hymn I Vow to Thee, My Country, and named the tune Thaxted after the Essex village where he lived. He was reportedly relieved that it fitted, having complained the words were fitted to the tune rather than the reverse. Notice how the first minute contains three separate tunes that never return.","Its hymn section is now inseparable from British public ceremony, sung at royal weddings and remembrance services and adopted by rugby crowds, which has made some critics wary of the movement's sincerity. Others point out that the prodigal invention of the outer sections - tunes thrown away that other composers would build symphonies on - is the real achievement, and that the hymn is the least characteristic music in the suite.","Try to count the separate tunes in the first two minutes before the hymn arrives; most listeners find four. Then hear the hymn without words and notice how plainly it is scored the first time round. Boult, who conducted the premiere and recorded the suite repeatedly across fifty years, remains the benchmark."],"holst-planets-venus":["Eight minutes of near-stillness, placed immediately after Mars as an antidote. A solo horn sounds one rising phrase; flutes and oboes answer with slow chords that seem to hang without moving. The strings enter quietly, a solo violin takes a long lyrical line, and celesta and harps trickle through the texture in cool, glassy figures. Almost nothing happens dynamically: the movement has no climax and simply thins out into silence.","Composed in the winter after Mars, when Holst was in his forties and unfit for war service, working through the school terms and writing at Thaxted in the holidays. The subtitle is the Bringer of Peace. Astrologically Venus governs harmony and affection, and Holst's reading of the horoscope determined the character sketches; he consulted books by Alan Leo, then the most widely read astrological writer in England.","Listen closer: the answering chords in the flutes at the opening move in parallel, all the notes shifting together rather than each line going its own way - a device from French music of the previous decade that Holst had absorbed and here uses to remove any sense of forward pull. Notice also that the solo violin and solo horn never play together; the movement is a dialogue that never becomes a duet.","Long treated as the weakest link in the suite because it refuses to do anything, Venus has been reassessed as its subtlest music, and conductors who take it slowly have made the case. It is the movement most often cut when excerpts are programmed, which distorts the arch Holst designed - the suite's seven movements are laid out as a sequence of contrasted temperaments, and removing the calm leaves only noise.","Play Mars and Venus back to back with no gap; the pairing is the point, and Holst placed them in that order deliberately. Listen for the celesta, an instrument still novel in 1915. Then compare with the slow movement of Vaughan Williams's Lark Ascending for the same English stillness from the same years."],"holst-planets-neptune":["Seven minutes at the softest end of the orchestra, closing the suite. Flutes murmur in a slow five-beat pattern; harps, celesta and muted strings shimmer without ever forming a tune. Two chords from unrelated keys alternate, so the harmony hovers rather than progresses. Near the end a women's chorus enters singing on a vowel, without words, and the orchestra withdraws entirely, leaving the voices to repeat one figure until they fade past the edge of hearing.","Written last, in 1915 and 1916, and subtitled the Mystic. Neptune was the outermost planet known when Holst composed; Pluto was discovered in 1930 and he declined to add a movement, having lost interest in the suite. The chorus is placed in an adjoining room with the door left open, to be closed slowly by a stagehand during the final bars - the instruction is written into the score.","Listen closer: the ending is the first orchestral fade-out ever notated. Holst asks for the sound to be lost in the distance, an effect no composer had put in a score before recording technology made fades familiar. The chorus is not singing text of any kind, and the two hovering chords never resolve to a home key, so the piece does not end so much as stop being audible.","Understood at the time as an oddity, the fade-out is now recognised as prophetic, anticipating both electronic music and the studio conventions of the recording era; Ligeti's floating choral textures of the 1960s stand in its line. Live performances struggle with the effect - the door mechanism, the offstage balance - and many conductors cheat it with a written diminuendo. Holst thought Neptune the best thing in the suite.","Listen at low volume in a quiet room, and resist raising it as the chorus fades; the disappearance is the composition. Notice that you cannot say where the orchestra stops and the voices begin. Then hear Ligeti's Lux aeterna to find the same idea fifty years on, and Boult's recordings for the door effect done properly."],"holst-st-paul-s-suite-jig":["Three fast minutes for strings alone, opening a four-movement suite. The jig springs off in a skipping six-beat pattern, the tune tossed between violins and lower strings and answered by short, sharp chords. Midway the meter tilts sideways into groups of two against groups of three, so the dance briefly limps, then rights itself. Everything is playable by good young players: the writing is transparent, the parts idiomatic, nothing hidden in the middle.","Holst taught music at St Paul's Girls' School in Hammersmith from 1905 until his death, and the school built him a soundproof room. He wrote the suite for the school's string orchestra when the new music wing opened, drawing on English folk-dance idioms then being collected by Cecil Sharp and championed by his friend Vaughan Williams. It was designed to be played by pupils, not professionals, and it still is.","Listen closer: the metrical stumble in the middle is not a folk quotation but Holst's own habit - the same fondness for five- and seven-beat groupings that runs through The Planets, planted here in music for teenagers. The suite's finale performs a better-known trick, laying the old tune Greensleeves on top of the country dance The Dargason so that two melodies of different lengths run simultaneously.","Written for a school and long condescended to as educational music, it has held the repertoire because the string writing is genuinely expert and because it gave amateur and youth orchestras something serious to play. Scholars now read it as a laboratory for the rhythmic experiments Holst carried into the suite that made him famous. It is among the most performed British string works of the century.","Listen for the moment the beat trips in the middle and try to feel where the strong beat has moved. Then go straight to the finale for the two-tunes-at-once ending. Compare with Britten's Simple Symphony, written for similar forces two decades later; both prove that music for young players need not be simple."],"ives-unanswered-question":["Six minutes built from three layers that ignore each other. Strings, placed offstage or at a distance, play slow consonant chords at their own unvarying tempo from beginning to end, never louder than a whisper. A solo trumpet interrupts seven times with the same short crooked phrase. After each of the first six, four flutes answer, growing faster, shriller and more agitated until they collapse into mockery and stop. The seventh question receives no answer; only the strings remain.","Ives wrote it in 1908 while working full time in New York insurance - he and Julian Myrick built the largest agency in the country - and revised it decades later. It was paired originally with Central Park in the Dark under the title Two Contemplations. Nobody performed it for nearly forty years; the score sat with the rest of his output until younger musicians began excavating it, and the premiere came in 1946.","Listen closer: the three groups are meant to be conducted separately and are not synchronised. The strings represent what Ives called the silence of the Druids, who know and see nothing; the trumpet asks the perennial question of existence; the flutes are the fighting answerers. The trumpet's phrase is identical every time except the last, where one note changes - the only alteration in the whole design, and it goes unanswered.","Ignored until the postwar avant-garde adopted it, it is now read as the earliest fully realised piece of layered, non-synchronised music, decades before European composers arrived at the same idea. Debate persists over which version to play - the 1908 original or the revision - and over how far apart to place the groups. Its influence runs through Cage, Feldman and film scoring, where the offstage-string effect is now a cliché.","Listen in the dark, and try to hold all three layers in mind at once rather than following whichever is loudest. Count the trumpet's seven entries. Then hear Central Park in the Dark, its companion piece, for the same trick applied to a city at night. Recordings that genuinely distance the strings are worth seeking out."],"ives-three-places-new-england-putnam-s":["Six minutes of controlled chaos, the middle panel of a three-movement orchestral set. It opens as a boisterous holiday march and quickly becomes two marches at once, in different keys and different tempos, colliding and pulling apart. Snatches of familiar tunes surface and vanish. The centre slows into a hazy dream sequence with a distant, out-of-tune band, then everything crashes back together for a deliberately ragged, cheerfully out-of-step finish.","The place is a Revolutionary War winter camp in Redding, Connecticut, near where Ives had a country house. His programme note describes a child at a Fourth of July picnic wandering off, dozing, and seeing the Goddess of Liberty and Washington's starving soldiers. Ives composed the set around 1914 and rescored it for chamber orchestra in 1929 so it could actually be played; Nicolas Slonimsky conducted the first performance in 1931.","Listen closer: the two bands colliding come from a childhood memory. Ives's father George, a Danbury bandmaster, marched groups around the town green from opposite directions to hear what happened when they met, and taught his son to sing in one key while being accompanied in another. The tunes buried in the texture include The British Grenadiers and Yankee Doodle, quoted without warning and often only half-finished.","The 1931 premiere provoked laughter and walkouts, and one critic called the writing incompetent; the same passages are now cited as the earliest sustained use of independent tempo layers in an orchestral score. Scholars have argued fiercely about Ives's dating, since he revised old manuscripts and may have backdated some innovations. The chamber and full-orchestra versions coexist, and conductors disagree about which the piece needs.","Wait for the moment the second march arrives on top of the first and refuse to choose between them - hearing both at once is the whole experience. Listen for scraps of tunes you half recognise. Then compare Stravinsky's Petrushka, written at almost the same time, for a very different way of stacking two crowds of music."],"ives-central-park-dark":["Nine minutes. The strings play a slow, quiet cycle of chords that repeats over and over from first bar to last, unhurried and unchanging - the night itself. Over it, other music intrudes: a ragtime piano from a distant café, snatches of popular song, a street pianist practising, and eventually a runaway fire engine and a shouting crowd that build to an overwhelming din. The noise cuts off abruptly, and the string chords are simply still there, undisturbed.","Written in 1906, when Ives was thirty-one and living in New York, and paired with The Unanswered Question as one of Two Contemplations. His subtitle calls it a picture of the sounds of nature and of happenings that men would hear some thirty or so years ago when sitting on a bench in Central Park on a hot summer night. Neither piece was performed in his lifetime for decades.","Listen closer: the string cycle is a fixed sequence of chords that recurs about ten times without variation, and it is completely indifferent to everything above it - the intrusions do not modify it and it does not accommodate them. The popular tunes quoted include the ragtime song Hello, Ma Baby. The two pianos are tuned and played as separate sources, one nearer than the other, so the café music has physical distance.","Long treated as a curiosity, it is now taken seriously as an early piece of sonic realism - music that reproduces the experience of listening to a place rather than depicting it. Its layering anticipates tape collage by half a century. Editorial questions remain, since Ives left several versions and later performing editions differ in the loudness and timing of the climax, which some conductors let overwhelm the strings entirely.","Fix your attention on the quiet strings and try to keep hearing them through the din; most listeners lose them and are startled to find them still going. Listen for the ragtime piano growing closer. Then hear The Unanswered Question, its twin, and Reich's Different Trains for a later composer building music out of recorded city noise."],"ives-variations-america":["Seven minutes for organ on the tune known in America as My Country, Tis of Thee and in Britain as God Save the King. After a brief introduction the tune appears plainly, then in five variations: one running in continuous fast notes, one as a stately march, one as a Spanish-flavoured dance in three, one as a polonaise, and a final pedal-heavy romp where the melody thunders in the feet. Two short interludes separate them.","Ives was seventeen, already a paid church organist in Danbury and Brewster, New York, and played the piece at a recital in early 1892. His father George, a bandmaster and relentless experimenter, encouraged the harmonic mischief but would not allow the strangest passages in church, reportedly because they made the boys laugh out loud. Nothing of Ives's was published for decades; the variations appeared in print only in 1949.","Listen closer: the two interludes are in two keys at once, the hands playing in different tonalities simultaneously. A seventeen-year-old wrote them in 1891, more than a decade before any European composer used polytonality deliberately. They are brief, easy to miss, and were probably added slightly later than the variations themselves - which is exactly the dating problem that dogs the study of Ives's whole output.","Dismissed for years as a schoolboy stunt, the piece is now the standard exhibit for the claim that Ives arrived at modernist devices independently and early. Sceptics note that he revised his manuscripts throughout his life and that the polytonal interludes cannot be dated with certainty. William Schuman's 1963 orchestration made it popular with American orchestras, and a band version followed; organists complain both dilute it.","Hear it on a big church organ rather than in orchestration, and listen specifically for the short interludes between variations - the moment two keys grind together is the historical point. Then play the polonaise variation for sheer cheek. Compare with Bach's Passacaglia to see what a variation set on a bass line sounds like when the tradition is being honoured rather than teased."],"schoenberg-verklarte-nacht":["Thirty minutes for six string players in a single unbroken span, following the five stanzas of a poem. It opens with the cellos walking downward in a cold, even tread - two people crossing a wood at night. The music grows agitated as the woman speaks, reaches a shuddering climax, then, at the man's reply, the key turns from minor to major and the texture opens out into warm, high, slow writing that carries the rest of the piece.","Schoenberg was twenty-five, largely self-taught, playing cello in amateur orchestras and orchestrating operettas for a living. He wrote the sextet in about three weeks in September 1899 after a Richard Dehmel poem in which a woman confesses to her lover that she is pregnant by another man, and he answers that the child will be his. The Vienna Tonkünstlerverein refused it, and it waited until 1902 for a performance.","Listen closer: the rejection was blamed on a single chord that theory books said could not exist - an inverted ninth for which, a juror said, there was no classification. Schoenberg later remarked that the piece therefore could not be performed since one cannot perform that which does not exist. Notice too that the turn from minor to major happens at the exact line where the man forgives; the whole harmonic plan is glued to the poem's syntax.","Now his most performed and best-loved work, and often programmed by people who avoid everything else he wrote, which he found irritating. Early scandal centred on the poem's morality as much as the harmony. He made two string-orchestra arrangements, in 1917 and 1943, and the debate over sextet versus orchestra continues: the orchestra gives sweep, the six players give the intimacy the story needs.","Read the Dehmel poem first - it is short - and listen for the moment the harmony brightens, which is the poem's turning point made audible. Then hear the sextet and the string-orchestra versions back to back. Anyone who thinks they dislike Schoenberg should start here and then move to the Six Little Piano Pieces to hear the same ear twelve years later."],"schoenberg-pierrot-lunaire-mondestrunken":["Two minutes, the first of twenty-one settings. The piano begins a thin, glittering seven-note figure that keeps circling back on itself, joined by flute, violin and cello in lines so spare that each instrument is heard separately. The voice does not sing: it speaks on pitch, hitting each note and immediately sliding off it, a delivery Schoenberg called Sprechstimme. The poem describes drinking wine with the eyes under a full moon.","Commissioned by the actress Albertine Zehme, who wanted a concert monodrama and performed the premiere in Berlin in October 1912 in Columbine costume, the players hidden behind a screen. The texts are Otto Erich Hartleben's German versions of poems by the Belgian Albert Giraud. Schoenberg rehearsed some forty times. He had abandoned key signatures four years earlier and was living in Berlin, teaching and painting to survive.","Listen closer: the piano's opening figure has seven notes and recurs seven times in the movement. The cycle has twenty-one poems in three groups of seven and carries the opus number 21; Schoenberg, deeply superstitious about numbers, worked this arithmetic in deliberately. Notice also that the five players never all play at once in the same combination twice across the cycle - each poem gets its own instrumental subset.","The premiere divided its audience but was not the riot legend suggests; Stravinsky and Ravel came to hear it and both were shaken. It became the founding text of the twentieth-century chamber ensemble - the Pierrot quintet is still a standard grouping - and the source of a century of argument about how Sprechstimme should sound, since Schoenberg's own instructions rule out both singing and ordinary speech and no performance satisfies everyone.","Listen once for the piano's circling figure alone, ignoring the voice, then again for the voice; the two experiences barely overlap. Follow a translation, since the words are the structure. Compare a theatrical, exaggerated reciter with a near-sung one to hear the performance-practice dispute in action, then move on to number eight, Nacht."],"schoenberg-suite-piano-op-25":["Six short movements, about sixteen minutes, carrying the names of Baroque dances: Prelude, Gavotte, Musette, Intermezzo, Minuet with Trio, and Gigue. The rhythms and phrase shapes are eighteenth-century - you can hear the gavotte's upbeat and the musette's droning bass - while the notes belong to no key at all. The result is brittle, dry and often witty, with hands frequently crossing and textures kept to two or three lines.","Written across two years while Schoenberg was working out, privately, a method for organising music without keys - a problem he had wrestled with since abandoning tonality in 1908 and which had left him barely able to finish anything for a decade. He told pupils in 1923 that he had discovered something that would assure the supremacy of German music for a hundred years. Eduard Steuermann gave the first performance.","Listen closer: every note in all six movements comes from one fixed ordering of the twelve pitches, used forwards, backwards, upside down, and backwards-upside-down, at two transpositions only. Buried in that ordering is the four-note motif that spells Bach's name in German notation - a homage planted inside the machinery. The dance titles are not decoration: the old forms supply the phrasing the new method cannot.","For fifty years it was taught as the founding document of twelve-tone composition and treated with a reverence that flattened it; the reaction after 1970 was equally overstated, dismissing serialism as an academic dead end. Recent performers have simply played it as piano music - fast, light, danced - and found it far more charming than its reputation. Pianists still argue over how audible the row should be made.","Listen to the Gavotte and Musette first and ignore the method entirely; hear them as dances and note how crisp they are. Then listen for the same shapes appearing upside down. Compare with Stravinsky's Pulcinella, written three years earlier - two composers reaching back to the eighteenth century for opposite reasons."],"schoenberg-gurrelieder-prelude":["Six minutes of purely orchestral music opening a cantata that runs close to two hours. Divided strings shimmer at the top of their range while harps and high woodwind add points of light, building a texture with no melody at all for the first minutes - the impression is of light on water at sunset. A horn theme gradually condenses out of the haze, the orchestra swells to a full romantic climax, and the first singer enters.","Schoenberg began the work in 1900 as a song cycle for a Viennese competition, expanded it enormously, then broke off in 1903 and did not complete the orchestration until 1911. The text is a German translation of poems by the Danish writer Jens Peter Jacobsen, about the medieval Danish king Waldemar and his murdered lover Tove at Gurre castle. The score needs around 150 players, including eight flutes and four Wagner tubas.","Listen closer: the shimmer is made by dividing the strings into many separate parts, each playing a slightly different figure, so no individual line is audible - the sound is a statistical effect rather than a texture, a technique that reappears in orchestral music sixty years later. Also worth knowing: the orchestration was completed a decade after the notes, by a composer who had meanwhile become an atonalist finishing a late-Romantic score.","The Vienna premiere in February 1913 was a triumph, with an ovation lasting a quarter of an hour - the greatest public success of Schoenberg's life. He is said to have refused to acknowledge the applause, on the grounds that the same public had rejected the music he cared about more. Two weeks later the Skandalkonzert of his newer works ended in fighting and a lawsuit. Performances remain rare because of the cost.","Give the first three minutes your full attention without waiting for a tune; the absence of one is the point. Then let it run into the first song. Pair it with the opening of Mahler's Second Symphony for the late-Romantic scale, and with the Six Little Piano Pieces to hear the same composer, the same year, working with nine bars instead of 150 players."],"schoenberg-survivor-warsaw":["Seven minutes, over almost before it registers. A narrator speaks on pitch, in English, the recollection of a man who survived a morning round-up in the Warsaw ghetto; the sergeant's orders break in shouted German. The orchestra does not illustrate so much as flinch - trumpet calls, drum rolls, splintered string figures. At the end, as the condemned are counted off, the men's chorus suddenly sings the Hebrew prayer Shema Yisroel in unison, and the piece stops.","Written in thirteen days in August 1947 in Los Angeles, where Schoenberg had lived since fleeing Germany in 1933, after a commission from the Koussevitzky Foundation. He had returned formally to Judaism in Paris in 1933. The text is his own, assembled from survivor accounts he had heard rather than a single documented testimony. The premiere took place in Albuquerque, New Mexico, in November 1948, played by a semi-amateur orchestra.","Listen closer: everything, including the prayer, is drawn from a single twelve-note ordering - the sung Hebrew melody is not a traditional tune but is generated by the same system as the chaos before it, so redemption and terror share a source. The three languages mark three registers of the event: English for memory, German for command, Hebrew for defiance. The chorus sings only in the final ninety seconds.","The Albuquerque audience demanded an immediate repeat performance. Since then it has been the central case in the argument about whether the Holocaust can or should be represented in art; Adorno defended it, while others have objected to a compressed, dramatised narrative built on second-hand testimony. Its authenticity as document has been questioned, but its standing as the first significant musical response to the camps is not.","Read the text before listening; seven minutes is too fast to follow cold. Then listen twice - once for the narration, once for what the orchestra does under it. The entry of the chorus is the moment everything turns. Pair it with Shostakovich's Eighth Quartet or Penderecki's Threnody for the same decade's other answers."],"schoenberg-six-little-piano-pieces-op-19":["Six pieces lasting about six minutes in total, the shortest under half a minute. Each is a handful of gestures placed in silence rather than a developed argument: a rising figure answered by two soft chords, a repeated pair of notes that never resolves, a sudden fortissimo that stops. Nothing is repeated, nothing returns, and no piece has a middle section. The last is a sequence of quiet, spaced chords that fade to almost nothing.","Written in Vienna and Berlin in 1911, three years after Schoenberg had stopped using keys and in the same period as his book on harmony. Five of the pieces were finished in a single day in February; the sixth came in June, a few weeks after he attended Gustav Mahler's funeral in Vienna. He was also painting seriously that year and exhibiting with Kandinsky's Blaue Reiter circle.","Listen closer: the sixth piece is built on two soft chords that toll and are answered, and has always been heard as a response to Mahler's death - Schoenberg never quite said so. Notice how much of the set is silence: rests are as measured as notes, and the pieces are unplayable if the pianist hurries through the gaps. The dynamic markings ask for extremes at very short range.","Attacked in their day as fragments rather than compositions, and defended by Webern, whose own aphorisms followed immediately. Their reputation rose steeply after 1950 when composers looking for concentration rather than expansion adopted them as a model. The argument now is about pacing: many pianists play them too quickly and too continuously, treating six pieces as one, which erases the silence that holds them apart.","Listen to the last piece on its own, twice, before hearing the set - it is the easiest way in. Then play the whole thing and time it; the brevity is genuinely startling. Compare with Webern's Six Bagatelles for strings, written the same year, and with Liszt's B minor Sonata for the opposite ambition."],"ravel-bolero":["Fifteen minutes, one tempo, one key, one rhythm. A snare drum begins almost inaudibly and taps the same three-bar pattern without pause until the last bars. Two melodies alternate above it, each played eighteen times in all, passing from flute to clarinet to bassoon to oboe d'amore to trumpet to saxophone and onward, the orchestra thickening at every repeat. There is no development and no contrast; the only event is a sudden change of key seconds before the end, and then collapse.","Commissioned by the dancer Ida Rubinstein, who wanted a Spanish-flavoured ballet, and written in the summer of 1928 at Ravel's small house at Montfort-l'Amaury. It was staged at the Paris Opéra that November, choreographed by Bronislava Nijinska with a set showing a tavern where a woman dances on a table while the men around her grow steadily more excited. Ravel was fifty-three and at the height of his fame.","Listen closer: at the eighth repeat, the melody is doubled by a horn, celesta and two piccolos tuned to sound the harmonics above it, so the tune acquires an artificial timbre like a single vast organ stop. It is the one moment of genuine orchestral invention in a piece that otherwise refuses to invent. Ravel described the work as orchestral tissue without music, and said a woman who shouted that he was mad had understood it.","It became the most performed orchestral piece of the century and Ravel found this bewildering and slightly insulting. Critics divide between hearing a rigorous formal experiment - a single crescendo sustained for a quarter of an hour - and hearing an empty machine. Tempo is the great argument: Ravel wanted it slow and unvarying and complained about conductors who accelerated. Neurologists have also speculated about links to his encroaching brain illness.","Listen for the instrument taking each repeat and try to name them; that is the only variety on offer and it is enough. Do not expect the tune to develop. Then compare Ravel's own recorded tempo with a faster modern one, and follow it with Reich's early phase pieces to hear where relentless repetition went next."],"ravel-pavane-pour-une-infante-defunte":["Six minutes at a slow, even walking pace, in the rhythm of the old court dance whose name it takes. A simple, slightly wistful melody is stated over quiet chords, then two contrasting episodes follow - one with the tune in the middle of the texture, one warmer and higher - before the opening returns unchanged. Nothing is virtuosic; the difficulty is entirely in holding the tempo steady and the sound soft. The orchestral version gives the tune first to a solo horn.","Written in 1899 while Ravel was still a student of Fauré at the Paris Conservatoire, and dedicated to the Princesse de Polignac, a wealthy patron of new music. Ricardo Viñes gave the first performance in 1902. Ravel explained that the title was not an elegy for a real princess but an evocation of a pavane such as a little princess might have danced long ago at the Spanish court; he admitted he chose the words partly for their sound.","Listen closer: the melody moves almost entirely by step and hardly ever leaves a five-note span - the archaic feel comes from that narrowness, not from any borrowed material. In the orchestral version Ravel gives the tune to a horn with a stopped, veiled tone, then to muted strings, so the same notes change colour three times. He later criticised his own piece for its obvious debt to Chabrier and its poor form.","Instantly and permanently popular, which its composer regretted; he complained about pianists who played it as though it were a dead princess rather than a dance and insisted the tempo should not drag. Serious criticism has echoed his own verdict that the harmony is derivative and the structure slack, while conceding that the orchestration of 1910 is a small lesson in colour. It remains the piece by which most listeners first meet him.","Hear the piano original before the orchestral version, then the orchestration, and note how the horn changes the mood entirely. Keep an ear on the tempo - the good performances refuse to slow down. Fauré's Pavane makes an obvious companion, and Satie's Gymnopédies show the same Parisian taste for slow, plain beauty a decade earlier."],"ravel-daphnis-et-chloe-lever-du-jour":["Five minutes opening the ballet's third and final part. Rippling figures rise and fall in flutes, clarinets and harps, layered so densely that no single line can be picked out - the sound of running water before dawn. Over them the strings begin a long melody low down and carry it steadily upward through a slow, unbroken crescendo of about four minutes, joined by birdsong in the piccolo and a wordless chorus, until the full orchestra reaches daylight.","Diaghilev commissioned it for the Ballets Russes in 1909 and it took Ravel three years, with much friction; the scenario by Michel Fokine came from a Greek pastoral romance by Longus. The premiere in June 1912 at the Théâtre du Châtelet had Nijinsky and Karsavina dancing and Pierre Monteux conducting, and was overshadowed by the company's other productions. Ravel called the score a choreographic symphony faithful to the Greece of his dreams.","Listen closer: the sunrise crescendo is built from a slowly rising line in the bass that ascends step by step for dozens of bars while the surface figuration stays the same - the dawn effect comes from the bottom of the orchestra, not the top. The chorus sings no words and Diaghilev repeatedly urged Ravel to remove it to save money; Ravel refused, and permits a wordless-chorus-free version only reluctantly.","Judged at the time as beautiful but undramatic, and now widely considered the most accomplished French orchestral score of its era; conductors from Monteux onwards have treated the second suite as a benchmark of orchestral finish. Debate concerns whether to play the complete ballet or the suite, and whether to use the chorus. The proximity of Stravinsky's Rite of Spring, from the same company one year later, has always coloured its reception.","Listen with your attention on the bass line rather than the shimmering surface; hearing the slow ascent explains the effect. Then hear the whole third part through to the closing dance. Seek a recording with chorus, and compare Stravinsky's Firebird for the Ballets Russes sound three years earlier."],"ravel-piano-concerto-g-adagio":["Nine minutes, the middle movement of three. The piano plays alone for more than three minutes: the right hand spins a single long, quiet melody while the left keeps a slow waltz accompaniment underneath, the two hands moving in different metres so the tune floats free of its own accompaniment. The orchestra enters gradually, and near the end the cor anglais takes over the melody while the piano decorates it in filigree above.","Written between 1929 and 1931, alongside the Left Hand Concerto, for a European and American tour Ravel intended to make as soloist; ill health prevented it, and Marguerite Long gave the premiere in Paris in January 1932 with the composer conducting. He was in his mid-fifties and had recently returned from a triumphant four-month American tour where he had heard jazz in Harlem, which colours the outer movements rather than this one.","Listen closer: the two hands are in different time signatures throughout the opening solo, which is why the melody sounds unmoored though the pulse never changes. Ravel told Marguerite Long that he wrote the flowing phrase two bars at a time, with the help of Mozart's Clarinet Quintet, and that it nearly killed him. The melody has no repeats; it simply continues for over a hundred bars without returning to its opening.","Received warmly in 1932 and never seriously questioned since, though the concerto as a whole was long patronised as light next to the Left Hand Concerto. The Adagio is now the most frequently excerpted movement Ravel wrote. Performance argument turns on the opening solo: how strictly to keep the left-hand waltz, and whether the right hand should be allowed rubato or held to the notated flow that Ravel insisted on.","Count the left hand's slow three-beat pattern while listening to the right hand and try to keep both; failing to is exactly the intended sensation. Wait for the cor anglais near the end - the moment the tune changes voice. Then compare Mozart's slow concerto movements to hear the model Ravel admitted to."],"ravel-valse":["Twelve minutes. It begins as a rumble in the double basses and bassoons out of which fragments of waltz rhythm gradually assemble, as though a ballroom were coming into focus through fog. Full waltzes follow, one after another, glittering and correct. Then the machinery starts to slip: the rhythm is displaced, the harmony sours, the percussion grows violent, and the final minutes tear the dance apart into a whirling collapse that ends with two brutal chords.","Ravel had planned a tribute to Johann Strauss since 1906 and finished it in 1920, after serving as a military truck driver in the war, losing his mother, and watching the Habsburg empire disappear. Diaghilev commissioned it, heard Ravel play it through, and refused it, saying it was a portrait of a ballet rather than a ballet. The two men never reconciled. The concert premiere came in Paris in December 1920.","Listen closer: Ravel's preface in the score sets the scene in an imperial court about 1855 and describes swirling clouds through which waltzing couples may be glimpsed. He always denied that the piece was about the collapse of Vienna or the war, calling that reading a fantasy - yet the last two minutes systematically dismantle a dance the first ten have carefully built. The waltz rhythm is present in the very first bar, hidden in the bass.","The Diaghilev rejection became one of the famous quarrels of modern music, and the critical argument since has been whether the piece is a homage, a parody or an obituary. Ravel insisted on the first; most listeners hear the third. It is now staged as a ballet with some regularity and there is a two-piano version by the composer. Conductors differ on how early to let the disintegration start.","Listen for the waltz rhythm in the opening murmur before any tune arrives, then note the exact point where you first feel the beat slipping - it comes earlier than most people expect. Follow it with a Johann Strauss waltz to feel the difference between the thing and its dismantling."],"ravel-jeux-d-eau":["Six minutes of almost unbroken movement in the piano's upper half. Fast, light figures run in both hands at once, splashing up and falling back, with a simple melody threaded through the middle of the spray. Two brief moments of stillness interrupt. Near the end a rushing glissando-like cascade sweeps down the keyboard, and the piece settles onto a soft, unresolved chord. The pedal is held so that the sound blurs deliberately.","Written in 1901 while Ravel was twenty-six and still nominally Fauré's student; it is dedicated to him. Ricardo Viñes premiered it in Paris in April 1902 alongside the Pavane. The score carries an epigraph from the poet Henri de Régnier about a river god laughing at the water that tickles him. Ravel later claimed this piece contained everything his subsequent piano writing developed, and pointed out that it preceded Debussy's water pieces.","Listen closer: the melody is almost always in the middle of the texture, with fast figuration both above and below it - a distribution borrowed from Liszt's fountain piece written at the Villa d'Este, which Ravel knew well. The famous blur comes from sounding two distant chords simultaneously rather than from the pedal alone. Ravel was quietly insistent about his priority here, since Debussy's Reflets dans l'eau came four years later.","It is generally taken as the first piece of genuinely twentieth-century piano writing in France, and the question of who influenced whom, Ravel or Debussy, poisoned relations between their supporters for years. Pianists argue over pedalling: too much and the figures turn to mush, too little and the water becomes a study in scales. Ravel himself was said to play it drier and faster than the tradition that followed.","Find the tune in the middle of the texture on a second listening; most people hear only the splashing the first time. Then play Debussy's Reflets dans l'eau immediately after and judge the family resemblance for yourself. Look for a pianist with a light touch rather than a big romantic sound."],"ravel-string-quartet-second-movement":["Six minutes, the scherzo of a four-movement quartet. All four players pluck rather than bow for most of it, producing a dry, guitar-like clatter, and the music runs in two conflicting groupings at once - the upper instruments in threes, the lower in twos - so the ear cannot settle on a downbeat. A slower, bowed middle section brings back a melody from the first movement, dreamier and muted, before the plucking returns and races to a snapped ending.","Ravel wrote the quartet at twenty-seven while still competing, unsuccessfully, for the Prix de Rome, and dedicated it to his teacher Fauré, who thought the finale stunted and undersized. The first performance in March 1904 was given at the Société Nationale. Debussy, whose own quartet of 1893 was clearly the model, is reported to have told Ravel not to change a single note of it, taking the younger man's side against his teacher.","Listen closer: the plucked scherzo is not simply fast - the first violin and cello are in different metres simultaneously, which is why the rhythm feels as though it is tripping over itself. The lyrical tune in the middle is the second theme of the first movement, slowed down and transposed, so the quartet's four movements share material across the whole span rather than starting fresh each time.","It was well received and rapidly became a staple, but for decades it was programmed as a companion to Debussy's quartet in a way that framed Ravel as the imitator. Later scholarship has stressed how differently the two works handle cyclic material. Ensembles argue about how percussive the pizzicato should be, and how much to observe the muting in the middle section; the movement is often taken faster than the marking allows.","Listen for the tug between the two rhythmic groupings and try tapping along; you will change your mind about where the beat is. Then hear the first movement to catch the tune that returns here. The obvious comparison is Debussy's quartet slow movement, best heard directly afterwards."],"ravel-ma-mere-l-oye-fairy-garden":["Four minutes closing a suite of five fairy-tale pieces. Muted strings play a slow, hymn-like melody in even steps, plain and quiet, over a gently swaying accompaniment - the most unadorned music Ravel wrote. It repeats, growing a little fuller each time, and then in the last thirty seconds the harp, celesta and glockenspiel spill upward in cascades while horns and trumpets sound a rising figure, and the piece ends in a blaze of bells.","Composed for the two young children of Ravel's friends Cyprien and Ida Godebski as a piano duet they could manage, and premiered in Paris in April 1910 by two girls aged eleven and fourteen. Ravel, who was childless and famously collected mechanical toys, orchestrated the suite the following year and then expanded it into a ballet. The final piece portrays Sleeping Beauty's garden at the moment the prince wakes her.","Listen closer: the melody moves by step almost throughout and stays within a small range, close to a slow sarabande - Ravel gets the fairy-tale quality by deliberately writing simpler music than he was capable of. In the orchestral version the final bars use celesta, harp glissandi and glockenspiel together, a bell-like combination he had refined in Daphnis; the piano duet original ends far more modestly.","The suite's simplicity was suspected at first of condescension, and Ravel was accused throughout his life of an artificiality that childhood subjects supposedly exposed; the counter-argument, now standard, is that he treated children's music with exactly the same craft as everything else. The piano duet and orchestral versions are both current, and specialists tend to prefer the duet for its plainness and the orchestra for the ending.","Hear the piano duet first, then the orchestral version, and wait for the last thirty seconds - the sudden bells are one of the most effective endings in the repertoire. Then listen to Debussy's Children's Corner for the same period's other approach to writing for and about children."],"ravel-tzigane":["Ten minutes in two unequal halves. The violin plays entirely alone for the first four minutes, much of it on the lowest string, in a slow, improvisatory rhapsody full of the ornaments, sliding intervals and double stops of Hungarian gypsy fiddling. The accompaniment finally enters with a harp-like flourish, and a series of increasingly fast dances follows, each more difficult than the last, ending in a headlong presto. Every violin trick of the nineteenth century is deployed in sequence.","Written in 1924 for the Hungarian violinist Jelly d'Arányi, a great-niece of Joachim, after Ravel heard her play gypsy music late into the night at a London party and asked her to keep going. The premiere took place in London in April 1924, with a piano fitted with a luthéal attachment that could imitate a cimbalom. Ravel orchestrated it within months, and the orchestral version has prevailed.","Listen closer: the opening solo never leaves the violin's darkest register for long, and the sliding between notes is written into the score rather than left to the player - Ravel notated the improvisation. Nothing in the piece is a real folk tune; it is an invented gypsy style, assembled deliberately from Liszt's Hungarian manner and from what Ravel had heard d'Arányi play, and he described it half-jokingly as a piece of virtuoso bravura.","Some critics found it a cynical exercise in pastiche and effects, and it has never quite escaped the charge; others regard the four-minute unaccompanied opening as the boldest thing he wrote for a soloist. The luthéal version is now occasionally revived by specialists, which changes the sound of the first accompanied entry entirely. Violinists treat it as a test piece; audiences enjoy it, which has kept the doubts alive.","Listen to the unaccompanied opening with the score of expectations set aside - it is closer to a cadenza than to a concerto exposition. Note the moment the accompaniment enters and how it changes everything. Then hear Liszt's Hungarian Rhapsody No. 2 for the source of the manner, and Bartók's folk-dance settings for what a real collector did with it."],"ravel-alborada-del-gracioso":["Seven minutes, the fourth of five pieces in the piano set Miroirs. It opens with dry, hard, rapidly repeated notes and snapped chords imitating a strummed guitar, in a lopsided dance rhythm. The centre stops the dance for a long, declaiming melody in the lower register - a singer's recitative, given to the bassoon in the orchestral version. The dance returns, faster, with sweeping glissandi in both hands and a violently abrupt close.","Composed in 1905 as part of a set whose five pieces are each dedicated to a member of the Apaches, the group of young Parisian artists and writers Ravel belonged to. The title means the jester's morning song, an aubade sung under a window at dawn, and belongs to the Spanish tradition Ravel absorbed from his Basque mother, who had grown up in Madrid. He orchestrated it in 1918.","Listen closer: the repeated-note effect requires the pianist to alternate fingers on a single key at high speed, a technique borrowed from guitar tremolo and notoriously awkward; several passages are among the hardest in the piano literature for that reason alone. In the orchestral version the same figures fall to plucked strings and muted brass, which makes the guitar imitation obvious in a way the piano version keeps disguised.","Pianists have long argued that the piece is really an orchestral score written by mistake for the keyboard, and Ravel's own 1918 orchestration is used as evidence; others insist the difficulty is the expressive point. Spanish critics, including Falla, praised its authenticity of gesture despite Ravel never having lived in Spain, calling it Spain seen accurately from a distance.","Hear the piano version first and listen for the guitar - once you hear it, the strange textures make sense. Then the orchestral version, for the bassoon's central song. Falla's Spanish dances and Albéniz's Asturias make the natural companions, and show how differently a French outsider and Spanish insiders handled the same material."],"ravel-piano-concerto-left-hand":["Nineteen minutes in a single unbroken movement, played by one hand. It begins in near-darkness with a contrabassoon growling over divided double basses, the orchestra climbing slowly out of the depths for two minutes before the piano crashes in with an enormous solo declamation. A faster central section brings jazz-inflected march rhythms and jabbing brass. The music returns to the opening material, builds to a huge cadenza for the soloist alone, and is cut off by the orchestra.","Commissioned by the Austrian pianist Paul Wittgenstein, brother of the philosopher, who had lost his right arm on the Russian front in 1914 and spent his fortune commissioning left-hand works from Strauss, Prokofiev, Britten and others. Ravel wrote it alongside the G major concerto, deliberately making the two as unlike as possible. Wittgenstein gave the premiere in Vienna in January 1932.","Listen closer: Ravel arranged the writing so that a single hand covers bass, melody and inner harmony by leaping constantly, and the sustaining pedal holds the low notes while the hand is elsewhere - the illusion of two hands is entirely mechanical. Wittgenstein altered passages he found unplayable and Ravel was furious, telling him that performers are slaves. They were partly reconciled only years later.","Wittgenstein's first reaction was cool and he did not understand the piece for some time; Ravel's insistence on textual fidelity became a famous test case in the ethics of commissioning. The concerto is now generally rated above its G major twin - darker, tighter, and more original in structure. Two-handed pianists have occasionally played it, which is considered bad manners; the point of the piece is the constraint.","Watch a performance rather than only listening; the visual fact of one hand doing this is part of it. Listen for the contrabassoon at the very start, then for the first entry of the piano. Compare the sunny G major Adagio to hear how deliberately Ravel split himself in two, and Scriabin's left-hand nocturne for an earlier answer to the same limitation."],"falla-ritual-fire-dance":["Four minutes of accelerating dance from a one-act ballet. It begins with a long shivering trill low in the orchestra, out of which a short, obsessive tune emerges - only a few notes, circling and stamping, repeated at rising pitches. Sharp accents fall off the beat throughout, and the oboe carries the melody over a rhythm that never lets up. The piece tightens rather than develops, ending in a sequence of hammered chords.","Written for the gypsy singer and dancer Pastora Imperio and first staged in Madrid in April 1915 as a gitanería, a small theatrical entertainment with song and dance, then revised into the ballet El amor brujo. The story concerns Candelas, haunted by the ghost of her dead husband; in this scene she dances round a midnight fire to drive the spectre out. Falla had just returned from seven years in Paris.","Listen closer: the opening trill is the fire, and the dance's melody is built on a scale with a flattened second step, the Phrygian colour of Andalusian flamenco singing - the exotic quality comes from a specific regional practice, not from generic Spanishness. The accents deliberately contradict the bar line, imitating the heel-stamping of a dancer working against the beat rather than with it.","Arthur Rubinstein's piano transcription made it a worldwide encore in the 1920s and detached it entirely from the ballet, which is how most listeners still meet it. Critics have complained that the extraction turns a scene of exorcism into a showpiece. The ballet exists in a chamber original and a fuller 1925 revision, and specialists now often prefer the leaner first version with its raw, unpolished vocal writing.","Hear the orchestral version in the context of the whole ballet if you can, so the dance arrives as a ritual rather than a stunt. Listen for the accents that land where you do not expect them. Then hear Rubinstein's piano transcription to understand the fame, and Khachaturian's Sabre Dance for the same trick a generation later."],"falla-nights-gardens-spain":["Three linked movements, about twenty-four minutes, for piano and orchestra - though the piano is woven into the texture rather than set against it, and has almost no solo display. The first evokes the gardens of the Generalife above Granada in shimmering, shadowed sound. The second is a distant dance, heard as though across a valley, which runs without a break into the third, a livelier, gypsy-flavoured finale in the hills of Córdoba that subsides into darkness.","Begun in Paris around 1909 as a set of nocturnes for solo piano, at the suggestion of the pianist Ricardo Viñes, and reconceived as symphonic impressions with orchestra. Falla spent 1907 to 1914 in Paris in the circle of Debussy, Ravel and Dukas, who taught him more about orchestration than anyone in Spain could; he finished the score after returning home at the outbreak of war. The premiere took place in Madrid in 1916.","Listen closer: Falla insisted the piece is not descriptive and that the titles name only the emotion of places, warning against hearing it as a travelogue. The piano is used mainly as a colouring instrument - plucked chords, rapid figuration inside the orchestral fabric - so the work is a concerto in name only. Andalusian modal scales and the guitar's characteristic chord shapes underlie almost every harmony without ever appearing as quotation.","Well received in 1916 and admired by Debussy's circle, it has always sat awkwardly in programmes because it satisfies neither the concerto audience nor the tone-poem audience. Pianists complain of enormous difficulty with no display; conductors have to balance a soloist who is often accompanying. Recent scholarship treats it as the most successful fusion of French impressionist orchestration with genuinely Andalusian material.","Listen at night with the lights low and do not wait for the piano to take charge - hearing it as one blended sound is the point. Start with the second movement, the distant dance, which is the most immediately atmospheric. Then compare Albéniz's Iberia at the piano and Rodrigo's Aranjuez for the two Spanish traditions on either side of it."],"falla-three-cornered-hat-final-dance":["Seven minutes closing a two-act ballet: a jota, the fast triple-time dance of Aragon, worked up for full orchestra. It starts with hard rhythmic snaps in brass and percussion, then launches a swinging tune that passes round the orchestra, punctuated by castanets and by sudden silences where the whole ensemble stops on a beat. The material recurs with more and more instruments piled on, building to a deliberately raucous, foot-stamping conclusion.","Diaghilev saw an earlier pantomime version in Madrid and asked Falla to expand it for the Ballets Russes. The premiere at the Alhambra Theatre in London in July 1919 had choreography by Léonide Massine, who also danced the miller, sets, curtain and costumes by Picasso, and Ernest Ansermet conducting. The story, from a novel by Alarcón, has a magistrate lusting after a miller's wife and being humiliated; the finale is the villagers tossing him in a blanket.","Listen closer: the sudden orchestral silences are choreographic - the dancers freeze in them, and Massine, who had studied flamenco with a Seville dancer for the role, built the whole final scene around those stops. Falla uses real Andalusian and Aragonese dance types throughout the ballet, each attached to a specific character, so the music identifies who is on stage before the audience sees them.","A triumph in 1919 and a landmark for Spanish music abroad, though some Spanish critics resented the international packaging of national material by a Russian impresario and a Málaga-born painter working in Paris. The two concert suites drawn from the ballet are now more often heard than the staged work. Debate continues over tempo: the jota is frequently taken faster than Falla marked, turning wit into noise.","Listen for the silences and count the beats through them - they are the funniest thing in the score. Then hear the whole second suite for the miller's furious solo dance beforehand. Look for a Spanish orchestra or conductor for the rhythmic bite, and pair it with Stravinsky's Petrushka, written for the same company."],"falla-vida-breve-spanish-dance-no-1":["Four minutes of orchestral dance from a short two-act opera, played during a wedding celebration. It opens with rapid strumming figures in the strings imitating guitars, over which the woodwind toss a bright, sharply accented tune in triple time. A quieter middle section brings a slower, more sinuous melody with the flattened intervals of flamenco singing, and then the dance returns with castanets, tambourine and full brass to finish emphatically.","Falla wrote the opera at twenty-eight and won a Madrid competition with it in 1905, but the prize did not include a production and the work sat unperformed for eight years. It reached the stage only in 1913, in Nice and in French translation, after Falla had moved to Paris and made useful friends. The plot concerns Salud, a gypsy girl in Granada abandoned by her lover, who dies at his wedding.","Listen closer: the guitar imitation is written for bowed strings playing rapid repeated chords, an effect Falla worked out before he had heard any French orchestration, and which he refined for the rest of his life. The dance is diegetic - it is being played by musicians inside the story, at the wedding where the heroine will collapse - so its brightness is dramatic irony rather than local colour.","The opera has never entered the repertoire, criticised for a thin libretto and a static first act, while the two Spanish dances escaped and became concert and encore staples, especially in Fritz Kreisler's violin arrangement. Falla himself came to regard the score as apprentice work and moved sharply away from its picturesque manner. Recent revivals have argued the case for the opera as a compact verismo tragedy.","Hear the orchestral version first, then Kreisler's violin arrangement, and notice how much of the guitar effect survives the transfer. Listen for the flattened second note in the slower middle tune, the flamenco fingerprint. Bizet's Carmen dances make the obvious comparison, and show what a Spaniard heard differently."],"falla-nana-siete-canciones":["Two minutes, the fifth of seven settings of Spanish folk songs. The piano keeps a soft, rocking two-note figure going from the first bar to the last, barely changing, while the voice sings a short lullaby melody twice over, low and unhurried, curling around a few notes with the ornamental turns of Andalusian singing. There is no climax and no development; the song simply stops, still rocking.","Falla assembled the set in Paris in 1914, in the last months before the war drove him back to Spain, and it was first sung in Madrid in January 1915 with the composer at the piano. He took the melodies from published folk-song collections rather than from fieldwork, then wrote entirely new accompaniments. The cradle song is the one he said his mother had sung to him in Cádiz.","Listen closer: the accompaniment never resolves. The rocking figure alternates two harmonies that pull in different directions and the song ends on the unstable one, so the lullaby is left hanging - a child asleep, or not quite. The vocal ornaments are written out exactly, an attempt to notate the melismatic style of Andalusian cante that had always been passed on by ear.","The set became the most performed Spanish song cycle in the world and the model for how a composer might treat folk material - not harmonised in the German manner but given an accompaniment that behaves like a guitar. Purists have argued that Falla smoothed the roughness out of cante; others credit him with rescuing melodies that were disappearing. Paweł Kochański's violin arrangement is nearly as famous as the original.","Listen to it sung in Spanish with the text in front of you - it is four lines long. Then hear the violin arrangement and decide what the words were doing. The whole cycle takes twelve minutes and is best heard complete; Villa-Lobos's Bachianas Brasileiras No. 5 makes a good companion for wordless vocal warmth."],"respighi-pines-rome-pines-appian-way":["Five minutes, the last of four linked movements, and one long unbroken crescendo. It begins at the threshold of audibility: low strings, piano and organ pedal grind out a slow marching tread in the dark. A cor anglais sounds a distant call over the mist. The tread grows louder and never varies, brass enter in layers, extra trumpets play from the balconies of the hall, and the whole orchestra plus organ arrives at a shattering final chord.","The second of Respighi's three Roman scores, premiered in Rome in December 1924. The programme describes dawn on the ancient road into the city and a legion advancing towards the Capitol. Italy had been under Mussolini for two years and the imagery of imperial revival suited the regime, whose approval Respighi accepted without ever joining the party; he later signed a manifesto defending traditional Italian music against modernism.","Listen closer: the score calls for buccine, the straight bronze trumpets of ancient Rome, which no orchestra possessed - flugelhorns and modern brass are substituted, and Respighi knew they would be. Elsewhere in the suite he goes further: the third movement requires a gramophone record of a nightingale to be played over the orchestra, the first use of a recording inside a concert score.","Toscanini's advocacy made the Roman trilogy an international staple and a favourite test of orchestral power. Critics have long dismissed Respighi as a colourist without substance, and the political associations of this movement have kept the suspicion alive; others counter that the crescendo is a structural feat, not merely loud. The offstage brass are frequently reduced or cut for cost, which flattens the ending badly.","Listen on the best sound system you can find, starting from the near-silence, and resist adjusting the volume; the dynamic range from the first bar to the last is the piece. Note the unchanging tread underneath everything. Then hear Fountains of Rome for the same composer at his quietest, and Mussorgsky's Great Gate for the ancestor."],"respighi-fountains-rome":["Sixteen minutes in four sections played without a break, each a fountain at a different hour. Valle Giulia at dawn is pastoral, with oboe over misty divided strings. The Triton fountain in the morning starts with a horn call and breaks into cascading woodwind. Trevi at midday is the grand one: a broad brass theme, organ and bells, water at full pressure. The Villa Medici at sunset fades through celesta, harp and birdsong into silence.","Respighi had settled in Rome in 1913 to teach composition at the Santa Cecilia conservatory, and wrote this first of his Roman pieces in 1916 while Italy was at war. He had studied in St Petersburg with Rimsky-Korsakov a decade earlier and had also played viola in a Russian opera orchestra; the orchestration lessons are audible throughout. The premiere in 1917 went badly. Toscanini took it up the following year and made it a success.","Listen closer: each section is keyed to a time of day, and Respighi shifts the whole orchestral register upward as the day advances and back down at dusk - the structure is a single arch of pitch and brightness rather than four separate pictures. The final section quietly reuses the opening pastoral figure, so the day closes where it began, an effect most listeners feel without noticing.","The failed premiere under Antonio Guarnieri is usually blamed on under-rehearsal; Toscanini's performances made Respighi internationally famous within two years. The score has been criticised as picture-postcard writing, and defended as the most technically accomplished orchestration by any Italian of the period. It is now the least played of the Roman trilogy, precisely because it is the quietest, which most critics consider unjust.","Listen for the join between sections - there are none, and finding the seams is a good way to hear how the piece is built. Start with the Trevi section for the big tune, then go back to the beginning. Compare Debussy's La mer for water treated as motion rather than as monument."],"respighi-ancient-airs-dances-suite-no-3":["Four movements for strings alone, about eighteen minutes, arranged from sixteenth- and seventeenth-century Italian and French lute music. An Italiana walks gravely in even steps; a set of court airs follows, more varied and songful; the Siciliana, the best known, sways in a gentle lilting rhythm with the melody in the upper strings over a plucked-sounding accompaniment; and a Passacaglia builds over a repeating bass figure to a firm close. No wind, no percussion, no soloists.","Respighi was a scholarly musician as well as a colourist, editing Monteverdi and Vivaldi and combing Italian libraries for old lute tablature. He made three suites of these arrangements between 1917 and 1932; the third, for strings only, is the most restrained and was written the year he was at the height of his reputation. The 1930s in Italy encouraged a nationalist rediscovery of the country's early musical past, which the project served.","Listen closer: the sources are real and identifiable - anonymous sixteenth-century pieces, airs collected by the French lutenist Jean-Baptiste Besard, a passacaglia by the Italian guitarist Lodovico Roncalli - but the harmonies are frequently Respighi's own, thickened with added notes that no lutenist would have played. The Siciliana's melody is anonymous; its famous accompaniment, the part everyone remembers, was composed in 1932.","The suites were fashionable when the early-music revival was young and became suspect once historically informed performance arrived, since they represent exactly the romanticised approach that movement rejected. They have survived anyway, on their own terms, as string-orchestra repertoire. Scholars now treat them as documents of how the 1920s and 1930s heard the Renaissance, and note how carefully Respighi preserved the original melodic lines even while reharmonising them.","Start with the Siciliana, which you will probably recognise, then listen to a lute performance of an original piece from the same period and hear what has been added. The Passacaglia rewards following the repeating bass. Vaughan Williams's Tallis Fantasia makes the ideal companion: the same idea, another country, ten years earlier."],"respighi-birds-prelude":["Four minutes opening a five-movement suite for small orchestra. A brisk, courtly tune in even steps is announced by the strings and answered by woodwind, in the manner of a Baroque overture. Halfway through, the texture thins and short chirping figures interrupt - flute, oboe and clarinet trading brief calls that are previews of the bird pieces to come. The opening tune returns and the movement ends neatly, in perfect period manners.","Written in 1928, in the same years as the Roman scores, and drawn from harpsichord and lute pieces of the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries that depicted birds. The prelude is based on music by the Roman keyboard composer Bernardo Pasquini; the movements that follow portray a dove, a hen, a nightingale and a cuckoo, using pieces by Jacques de Gallot, Rameau, an anonymous Englishman and Pasquini again. Respighi was by then Italy's most performed living composer abroad.","Listen closer: the chirping interruptions in the middle are quotations from the four movements still to come, so the prelude works as a table of contents - a device from the Baroque suite that Respighi restores knowingly. The hen movement that follows is Rameau's La poule, one of the most famous keyboard pieces of the French eighteenth century, transferred almost note for note to the woodwind.","Long treated as a charming trifle beside the Roman trilogy, the suite has gained ground with orchestras looking for short, well-made programme openers, and with critics who value Respighi's editorial care with his sources. The recurring complaint is the same as with all his arrangements - that the added harmony sentimentalises music which was leaner and stranger than he made it sound. Period-instrument ensembles have begun playing the originals alongside.","Listen to the prelude and then to Rameau's La poule on a harpsichord before hearing Respighi's version of it; the comparison is the most enjoyable thing in the suite. Couperin's nightingale piece makes a further pairing. A chamber orchestra with clean, light articulation serves this music far better than a full symphonic body."],"bartok-concerto-orchestra-finale":["The fifth and last movement, about nine minutes. A horn call opens it; then the strings launch a whirling perpetual motion, a Transylvanian dance tune spun out at breakneck speed and handed round the sections. A fugal middle section piles the theme up in the brass, one entry on top of another, before the strings resume their spinning. The close is a blazing brass chorale in F major, with the whole orchestra used as a display cabinet of soloists.","Serge Koussevitzky commissioned the work in 1943 for the Boston Symphony, a fee arranged partly to help a composer who was ill, broke and adrift in New York. Bartok had left Hungary in 1940 rather than live under a regime allied to Hitler; American audiences barely knew him and his health was failing with the leukaemia that killed him in 1945. He wrote most of it at a sanatorium at Saranac Lake in the Adirondacks, and Koussevitzky conducted the premiere in Boston on 1 December 1944.","There are two endings. Bartok wrote a short, abrupt close first, then at Koussevitzky's suggestion added the longer coda that almost everyone now plays; both are printed and a few conductors still choose the original. Listen also for the title's real meaning: no tune here is a quoted folk melody, but the shapes, the drone basses and the lopsided accents are all borrowed from peasant playing.","Boston loved it immediately and it became the piece that finally sold Bartok to a wide public, which is precisely why some critics have treated it with suspicion ever since, hearing a hard modernist softening for a commission. The counter-argument is that nothing in the harmony is simplified, only the surface. The two endings remain a standing choice, and the second movement's mocking quotation of Shostakovich still provokes argument about what Bartok meant by it.","Start at the horn call and count how long the strings keep spinning without a break. Then go back to the third movement, the Elegia, so you hear what the finale is escaping from. Compare Fritz Reiner's Chicago recording, made by a fellow Hungarian who knew the accent, with Boulez's cooler reading; and try to catch a performance that uses the short original ending."],"bartok-music-strings-percussion-celesta-third-movement":["The slow third of four movements, roughly seven minutes of what Bartok called night music. A xylophone taps one repeated note in a pattern that speeds up and slows down again; timpani slide between pitches; the celesta pours out glassy arpeggios; violins chirp and slither like insects and unseen birds. Between these noises a chill melodic line rises and falls. The movement is a palindrome, unfolding to a central point and then folding back the way it came.","Paul Sacher commissioned it in 1936 for the tenth anniversary of his Basel Chamber Orchestra, a rare piece of luck at a moment when Bartok's position in Hungary was worsening. He had refused to let his music be played in Nazi Germany and was watching his own country's politics harden. Sacher conducted the first performance in Basel on 21 January 1937. The score directs the two string groups to sit on opposite sides of the platform with the percussion between them.","Everything in the work grows from the slow chromatic fugue subject of the first movement, and it steals into this movement in disguised, stretched-out form. Erno Lendvai's famous claim that Bartok laid the piece out by the golden section and Fibonacci numbers has been argued about for sixty years and is not settled. Listen for the xylophone's opening rhythm, which is a written-out accelerando and ritardando on a single pitch.","Recognised at once as the summit of Bartok's instrumental writing and quickly taken up by conductors who found his quartets forbidding. The Lendvai numerology divides scholars into believers and sceptics. Kubrick used this movement in The Shining, which has given several generations their first, unnerving encounter with it, and has also fixed it in some minds as horror music rather than as a nature study.","Listen in the dark and try to hear the movement as a landscape at night rather than a threat: insects, water, a distant bird. Notice the xylophone at the start and the same figure at the close. Seek a recording that respects the antiphonal seating so you can hear the two string bands answer across the stage; Reiner, Solti and Ivan Fischer are all good places to begin."],"bartok-romanian-folk-dances":["Six short dances, six minutes in all, for piano. A stamping stick dance opens; a sash dance follows; then a strange, static piece in which the right hand curls around a few notes over a drone, an imitation of a peasant flute. A gentle horn dance and a quick polka lead to a closing pair of fast dances run together. The tunes are transcribed exactly as collected; the harmonies underneath them are Bartok's.","Bartok collected the melodies between 1910 and 1912 in Transylvania, then part of Hungary, from village fiddlers and flute players, recording them on wax cylinders with a phonograph carried on peasant carts. He arranged them in 1915, during the war, when concert life had shrunk and he was working as much as an ethnographer as a composer. He orchestrated the set two years later, and Zoltan Szekely's arrangement for violin and piano followed in the 1920s.","The third dance uses a scale with a wide gap between two of its steps, the interval that gives it its faintly Middle Eastern colour, and Bartok noted the original had been played on a peasant flute. The original title was Romanian Folk Dances from Hungary; after the 1920 Treaty of Trianon handed Transylvania to Romania, the geography in the title quietly disappeared.","Szekely's violin version made the set Bartok's most-played music by a wide margin, and it is often the only Bartok a general audience has heard. Some object that the piano harmonies smooth away the raw sound of the originals, which survive on Bartok's own cylinders and can be compared directly. Others point out that Bartok never claimed otherwise: the settings are frankly settings, and the transcriptions are separately published.","Play the six through once without stopping, then listen again to the third alone and hear the drone hold still under the tune. If you can find a recording of the original cylinders, compare a field version with Bartok's setting. The violin arrangement is warmer, the piano original drier and closer to the source; the orchestral version sits between them."],"bartok-piano-concerto-no-3-adagio-religioso":["The middle movement of three, about ten minutes. Strings sing a slow hymn in bare, hymn-like counterpoint; the piano answers with plain chords, as though two voices were taking turns in a church. The centre of the movement empties out into birdsong: trills and flicks in the woodwind, the piano picking out calls high above quiet strings. Then the hymn returns, the same music now wreathed in the piano's running decoration.","Bartok wrote the concerto in the summer of 1945 at Saranac Lake, New York, as a birthday present and a source of income for his wife, the pianist Ditta Pasztory, who would be left with nothing. He was dying of leukaemia and worked against the clock, leaving the last seventeen bars unorchestrated; his pupil Tibor Serly completed them. He died on 26 September 1945. Gyorgy Sandor gave the premiere with Ormandy and the Philadelphia Orchestra in February 1946.","The chorale texture is a deliberate echo of Beethoven's Heiliger Dankgesang, the hymn of thanksgiving of a convalescent from the String Quartet Op. 132 - a sick man's music quoted by a sick man. The birdsong is not generic: Bartok had transcribed the calls of North American birds while convalescing in Asheville, North Carolina, the winter before, and they are folded into this movement note for note.","The Third Concerto has always been shadowed by its circumstances, and by the argument over how much Serly did. Some hear the late simplicity as a distillation, others as a dying man writing what would sell; the ferocious First Concerto is usually the stick they beat it with. Recent scholarship has narrowed Serly's contribution to orchestration of an essentially finished text, which has quietened the loudest complaints.","Come to this movement first and let the rest of the concerto follow. Listen for the exact moment the strings stop and the birds begin, and again for the moment the hymn comes back dressed in filigree. Then play the Beethoven Op. 132 slow movement alongside it. Among recordings, Anda with Fricsay and Zimerman with Boulez sit at opposite ends of the warmth spectrum."],"bartok-string-quartet-no-4-finale":["The fifth and last movement of a five-movement quartet, about six minutes. All four players hammer chords in unison, off the beat, so the music lands like boots on boards; a bagpipe-like dance tune grinds over a drone; the texture is closer to a percussion band than to the polite conversation a string quartet usually offers. The material is the first movement's, flattened out and turned into a peasant dance, and the ending is a brutal shared stamp.","Written in Budapest in the summer of 1928, at the point where Bartok's folk research and his most uncompromising modernism met head-on. He had just returned from an American tour and was at the height of his powers as a pianist and analyst of peasant music. The Waldbauer-Kerpely Quartet, who had championed him for two decades, gave the premiere in Budapest in March 1929. Nothing so aggressive had been asked of four string players before.","The quartet is an arch: the finale mirrors the first movement, the fourth mirrors the second, and the third stands alone at the centre as a nocturne with a long cello song over shivering chords. The fourth movement is entirely plucked, and includes the snap where the string is pulled up and released to rebound off the fingerboard with a crack - Bartok pizzicato, notated here for the first time.","Early audiences found it noise, and the quartet took a generation to enter the repertoire. It is now taught as the centre of Bartok's cycle and one of the pillars of the medium after Beethoven. The live argument is whether the arch is audible or merely visible in the score; and whether to play the finale with the ferocity of the Vegh and Juilliard traditions or the cleaner precision of later ensembles.","Listen to the first movement and the finale back to back and hear the same material walk in twice wearing different clothes. Then go to the third movement for relief. Seek out the snap pizzicatos in the fourth movement, unmistakable once you know the sound. The Vegh Quartet's recordings carry the Hungarian accent; the Takacs Quartet combine that with modern clarity."],"bartok-miraculous-mandarin-opening":["The first five minutes of a one-act pantomime. Before any curtain rises the strings start a churning, revving figure that never resolves, with horns and trombones blaring across it like traffic in a city that will not sleep. The noise resolves into a squalid room where three thugs force a girl to lean from the window and lure men off the street. Her first decoy call is a long, sagging clarinet solo, the most human sound in the scene.","Menyhert Lengyel's scenario was published in 1917; Bartok set it between 1918 and 1924, through Hungary's defeat, the short-lived Soviet republic and the arrival of Horthy's regime. Budapest would not stage it in his lifetime. The premiere took place in Cologne on 27 November 1926 and caused an uproar over its brothel setting and its violence; the city's mayor, Konrad Adenauer, had it withdrawn after that single performance.","The whole opening is spun from one restless figure passed between the strings, deliberately denied a cadence, so the city never arrives anywhere. Each of the girl's three decoy solos is more elaborate than the last, marking time as the piece tightens. When the Mandarin finally appears, his music is pentatonic, Bartok's shorthand for the outsider - and the wordless chorus that later enters marks the moment he refuses to die.","Banned in Cologne, blocked in Budapest, and for decades known mainly through the 1927 concert suite, which stops before the ending and is far more often played than the complete score. Bartok revised the close several times and never quite settled it. Modern stagings tend to treat the piece as a critique of the trade in bodies rather than an exploitation of it, and the full pantomime is now heard more often than the suite.","Play the first two minutes loud and hear it as a street rather than a story: horns as klaxons, strings as engines. Then follow the three clarinet solos and notice how each one grows. Compare the concert suite with the complete score to hear what the suite leaves out. Boulez, Fischer and Salonen have all recorded the whole thing with the chorus intact."],"bartok-six-dances-bulgarian-rhythm":["The last six numbers of Mikrokosmos, played as a set of about eight minutes. Each runs on an unequal beat: the pulse divides into groups of two and three, so a bar of, say, four plus two plus three limps forward instead of marching. The first is brisk and bright, the fourth slower and darker, the last a hurtling toccata. The hands often play in different registers with a hard, dry touch and no pedal haze.","Mikrokosmos began in 1926 as graded teaching pieces for Bartok's young son Peter and grew across thirteen years into 153 pieces rising from five-finger exercises to concert music. These six close the sixth and last book, finished in 1939 with Europe about to go to war. Bartok had written an essay on what he called Bulgarian rhythm after studying published Bulgarian song collections, and dedicated the six to the English pianist Harriet Cohen.","Bartok's argument was that the unequal beats are not an exotic effect but a record of how feet actually move in a village dance, and that a metronome-even performance destroys them. The last of the six layers two different groupings against each other. Because these pieces end a graded course, they are also a hidden autobiography of technique: everything the earlier 147 pieces teach is being cashed in here.","Mikrokosmos sits awkwardly between the studio and the concert hall, and critics have argued for decades about whether the last book is teaching material with delusions or genuine repertoire. Bartok settled it in practice by playing and recording several of the dances himself in 1940. Pianists now differ mainly over whether to lean into the limp or to keep the pulse taut, which changes the character completely.","Tap along and notice how the beat refuses to divide evenly - that discomfort is the point. Try the sixth dance first for sheer momentum, then the fourth for its darker colour. Bartok's own recordings, though dry and dated, show a harder, more percussive touch than most modern pianists use; Zoltan Kocsis and Dezso Ranki are the fuller-toned alternative."],"bartok-bluebeard-s-castle-fifth-door":["The turning point of a one-act opera for two singers. Judith has opened four doors in her husband's windowless castle; behind the fifth, the full orchestra and organ hurl out a single blazing C major chord, the loudest and widest sound in the piece, as Bluebeard shows her his whole kingdom. She sings only a few awed phrases. Then she sees that the clouds are casting bloody shadows, and the light begins, note by note, to drain away.","Bela Balazs wrote the libretto in 1910 and Bartok set it in 1911, at thirty, newly married to Marta Ziegler and just beginning the folk-collecting that would define him. The score was entered for a Hungarian competition and rejected as unperformable. It waited seven years for a stage: the Budapest Opera gave the premiere on 24 May 1918 under Egisto Tango. The vocal writing follows Hungarian speech, which stresses first syllables, so the lines fall rather than rise.","The opera begins and ends in the dark key of F sharp; the fifth door blazes in C major, the furthest possible distance from it. The whole drama is an arch between those two poles, and the return journey is a slow extinguishing. Threaded through every door is a grinding two-note figure, a semitone rubbing against itself, that means blood; once you have heard it, it turns up in the treasury and the garden too.","Rejected by a jury, then ignored, and finally recognised as one of the twentieth century's few indispensable operas. Its Hungarian text long limited its export, and singers still argue about performing it in translation. Readings have shifted from symbolist fairy tale to psychoanalytic case study to a study of loneliness rather than of a monster; whether the ending is misogynist or tragic is still contested. Kertesz, Boulez and Ivan Fischer have all recorded it memorably.","Listen to the whole hour in one sitting with the text in front of you - it is short, and the fifth door only detonates if you have sat through the dark. Notice how much longer the fading takes than the blaze. Then go straight to the seventh door. Recordings with Hungarian-speaking singers, such as Kertesz's with Christa Ludwig and Walter Berry, catch the speech rhythm best."],"stravinsky-rite-spring-augurs-spring":["The second number of the ballet, about three minutes. After the bassoon's high, curling opening has faded, the strings drop onto one thick chord and repeat it more than thirty times in hard downward bow strokes, while eight horns punch accents that fall in no predictable place - the pulse stays absolutely even and the stress lurches. A folk-like fragment appears on horn, then cor anglais and piccolo trumpet, and the texture thickens toward the Ritual of Abduction.","Diaghilev's Ballets Russes gave the premiere at the brand-new Theatre des Champs-Elysees in Paris on 29 May 1913, with Nijinsky's choreography, Nicholas Roerich's designs and scenario of pagan Russia, and Pierre Monteux conducting. Stravinsky was thirty and already famous from Firebird and Petrushka. The audience began jeering within minutes; the noise grew loud enough that the dancers could not hear the orchestra and Nijinsky shouted counts from the wings.","The famous chord is two ordinary chords jammed together a semitone apart, which is why it sounds both familiar and wrong. The accent pattern is genuinely irregular and takes several bars before anything repeats. And the tunes are not invented: Richard Taruskin and others traced a series of them to Anton Juszkiewicz's 1900 collection of Lithuanian folk songs, though Stravinsky admitted to borrowing only one.","Whether the riot was caused by the music or by Nijinsky's stamping, turned-in choreography has never been settled, and Stravinsky's own accounts changed with every retelling. The 1914 concert performances were a triumph, which suggests the dancing carried much of the blame. Millicent Hodson and Kenneth Archer reconstructed the original choreography in 1987, and hearing it with the steps restored has shifted the argument again.","Count the repeated chord aloud and try to predict the accents; you will fail, and that is the experience. Then listen to the same passage in a recording with the reconstructed choreography on film. Monteux, who conducted the premiere, left recordings decades later; Boulez is exact, Bernstein wild, and Stravinsky's own are drier and faster than you expect."],"stravinsky-firebird-finale":["The last number of the ballet, about three minutes. A solo horn sings a plain Russian folk tune over trembling strings; the melody passes to woodwind, then strings, then the whole orchestra, each time a little fuller, in one long unbroken crescendo. At the end the metre broadens into a seven-beat bar, so the final hymn tolls unevenly, with harp, bells and full brass, as Prince Ivan is crowned and the spell over the kingdom breaks.","Diaghilev's first Paris season needed a Russian ballet in a hurry after Anatoly Lyadov failed to deliver, and the commission fell to a twenty-seven-year-old Rimsky-Korsakov pupil nobody had heard of. Fokine choreographed, Karsavina danced the Firebird, and the premiere at the Paris Opera on 25 June 1910 made Stravinsky famous overnight. He later drew three concert suites from the score, in 1911, 1919 and 1945; the 1919 is the one usually played.","The horn tune is a real folk song, By the Gate, taken from Rimsky-Korsakov's published collection - the teacher's book supplying the pupil's most famous melody. The seven-beat bars at the close mean the coronation never quite squares up, a limp built into the grandest moment. Throughout the ballet the magical characters get twisting chromatic music and the human ones plain diatonic tunes, a division Stravinsky learned directly from Rimsky.","An instant success that Stravinsky spent decades half-disowning as too indebted to his teacher, though he kept conducting and rescoring it. The suite question is real: the 1919 version, for a normal-sized orchestra, is what most people know, while the complete 1910 ballet uses forces few companies can afford. Critics who prize the Rite tend to patronise the Firebird; audiences have never agreed.","Listen for the horn entry after the Berceuse fades, and then follow one instrument - the first violins, say - through the whole crescendo to hear how patiently it is built. Count the last pages in seven and feel the tilt. Try the complete ballet after the suite: the numbers the suite omits change the shape of the whole thing."],"stravinsky-petrushka-russian-dance":["The number that closes the first tableau, under three minutes. The Charlatan touches his three puppets and they jerk into life in front of the fairground crowd. The orchestra hammers out a foursquare Russian tune with a piano rattling inside the texture like a hurdy-gurdy - not a soloist, just another mechanism. Everything is loud, bright and slightly too fast, and it stops abruptly rather than ending, as if the handle had been let go.","Stravinsky began the music as a concert piece for piano and orchestra about a puppet tormenting an orchestra; Diaghilev heard it, saw a ballet, and Alexandre Benois supplied the Shrovetide fair in St Petersburg of the 1830s. The premiere was at the Theatre du Chatelet in Paris on 13 June 1911, Fokine choreographing, Monteux conducting, Nijinsky as Petrushka - a performance often called the finest acting of his career.","Petrushka's cry is two triads, C major and F sharp major, sounded together: the puppet is literally in two keys at once. Stravinsky salted the score with real street tunes, and one of them, a French music-hall song he had heard on a barrel organ, belonged to a living composer, Emile Spencer, who claimed and received royalties. The piano's function is the giveaway - it is the puppet's voice, not the pianist's.","Praised from the first night, and never as divisive as the Rite. Stravinsky reorchestrated the ballet in 1947 for smaller forces and fresh copyright, and conductors still choose between the harsher 1911 original and the leaner revision. The three-movement piano version he wrote for Rubinstein in 1921 turned the Russian Dance into a virtuoso showpiece and is sometimes accused of upstaging the ballet.","Listen for the piano and try to hear it as furniture rather than as a solo, then hear Rubinstein's descendants play the piano transcription and notice how differently the same notes behave. Compare the 1911 and 1947 scorings on record - Monteux and Boulez for the original, Stravinsky's own for the revision. Then go straight into the second tableau, where the puppet is alone in his cell."],"stravinsky-rite-spring-sacrificial-dance":["The last five minutes of the ballet, in which the Chosen One dances herself to death. The bar lengths change almost every bar - two beats, three, five - so the orchestra lands like a body hitting the floor and then falls silent, again and again. Huge chords are hurled by full brass and percussion between gaps of nothing. Near the end the flutes and strings surge upward, the whole orchestra crashes down once more, and a single flicked note is the last thing heard.","This is the conclusion of the pagan scenario Stravinsky devised with the painter and archaeologist Nicholas Roerich: a chosen maiden dances until she dies to secure the spring. It gave Stravinsky more trouble than anything else in the score - he could play the rhythm at the piano but struggled to write it down. At rehearsals for the 1913 premiere Nijinsky's dancers could not count it, and Stravinsky pounded the beat while Marie Rambert shouted numbers.","The 1943 revision Stravinsky made for a Boston performance changed the barring, not the notes: he had spent thirty years listening to conductors put the accents in the wrong places and re-notated the movement to stop them. Underneath the chaos the pulse never actually changes speed - only the length of the bars does, which is why the music feels like stumbling rather than accelerating.","Notorious from the first night and canonical within fifteen years. Conductors choose between the 1913 barring and the 1943 rewrite, and orchestras that have played it for a century still rehearse this movement more than the rest. Charges of primitivism have been answered by Taruskin's argument that the violence is specifically Russian and folkloric rather than generically savage; the debate over what the ballet is actually about continues.","Do not try to follow a beat; follow the silences instead, and hear how the music is built from landings. Then listen to the whole second part in order so this arrives where it should. Compare a performance using the 1913 barring with one using 1943 - Boulez and Salonen for precision, Bernstein and Gergiev for weight - and notice how the accents shift."],"stravinsky-firebird-infernal-dance":["The ballet's set piece of violence, about four and a half minutes. After a long fade to near-silence the whole orchestra detonates on one chord, and the monsters of Kashchei's court are forced to dance. The music limps: accents land off the beat, trombones slide between notes, the xylophone clatters, and the tune never settles into a comfortable stride. It grows more frantic until the dancers collapse and the Berceuse steals in on a solo bassoon.","Part of the 1910 Ballets Russes commission, written to Fokine's requirement for grotesque monsters under the spell of the immortal magician Kashchei. Stravinsky, twenty-seven and working fast to a deadline, had Rimsky-Korsakov's fairy-tale operas as his model and a Paris audience conditioned to expect Russian exoticism. This is where he first sounds unmistakably like himself rather than like his teacher.","The opening blow is the loudest single attack in the score and is deliberately placed after a diminuendo, so it functions as an ambush; conductors who let the preceding bars get too quiet lose the effect. Kashchei's music runs on a scale of alternating whole and half steps that Stravinsky inherited from Rimsky, while Prince Ivan's is plainly diatonic. The rhythmic displacement here is the Rite in embryo, three years early.","Always the number audiences remember, and included in all three suites. Critics who find the Firebird derivative usually make an exception for this movement, hearing the first genuinely Stravinskian rhythm. Its afterlife in film scores and cartoons has made it almost too familiar; Stravinsky's own recordings are notably steadier and less hysterical than the tradition that grew up around it.","Turn the volume up before the ambush chord and do not touch it afterwards. Follow the trombone slides - they are what makes the dance grotesque rather than merely fast. Then play it straight into the Berceuse without a gap, as the score intends. Compare Stravinsky conducting his own score with a more theatrical account such as Dorati's or Gergiev's."],"stravinsky-firebird-berceuse":["The lullaby that follows the Infernal Dance, under three minutes. The orchestra drops to almost nothing and a solo bassoon winds a slow, curling melody over muted, divided strings and harp while the monsters fall asleep. The tune passes to the oboe; the harmony barely shifts; the dynamic hardly rises above a whisper. The last held note does not stop but dissolves directly into the horn solo that opens the Finale, so there is no break between them.","Fokine's scenario required the Firebird to sing Kashchei's court into sleep before Ivan can destroy the egg containing the magician's soul. Rimsky-Korsakov, Stravinsky's teacher, had died two years earlier, and the soft doublings, harp figuration and muted strings here are directly his inheritance, deployed by a pupil who was already using them for stranger ends.","The melody is essentially built from a handful of notes and rocks between two of them, so the lullaby's rocking is in the tune itself rather than only in the accompaniment. The bassoon is used in its low, reedy register - the same instrument that three years later would open the Rite of Spring at the very top of its range, strained and inhuman. And the transition into the Finale is seamless: there is nowhere to applaud.","The number that persuaded doubters Stravinsky could write a melody, and the one most often lifted out for encores and film. It appears in all three suites. The main performance argument is tempo: some conductors stretch it past four minutes into something dreamlike, others keep it moving as a functional lullaby. Its influence on Hollywood scoring of sleep and enchantment is hard to overstate.","Listen to the Infernal Dance and this together, without a gap, to feel the collapse from noise to breath. Concentrate on the bassoon's low register and how exposed it is. Then compare a slow reading such as Ansermet's with a flowing one such as Stravinsky's own, and hear how much the tempo changes the character."],"stravinsky-pulcinella-overture":["The ballet's opening number, barely two and a half minutes. A bright, bustling Allegro for small orchestra, with a concertino of soloists set against the rest in the manner of a Baroque concerto grosso. The tune is straight eighteenth-century Italian, taken from a trio sonata then believed to be Pergolesi's. What is not eighteenth-century is the bass, which lands on the wrong notes, the accents, which shove against the phrasing, and the pungent doubling of wind and strings.","Diaghilev put a sheaf of manuscripts copied in Naples and London into Stravinsky's hands and asked him to arrange them for a ballet about the commedia dell'arte character Pulcinella. Stravinsky, thirty-seven, exiled from Russia by the revolution and cut off from the folk sources that had fed his ballets, agreed. The premiere at the Paris Opera on 15 May 1920 had Massine's choreography, Picasso's designs and Ansermet conducting.","Most of the music is not by Pergolesi at all. Later scholarship reattributed the pieces to Domenico Gallo, Carlo Ignazio Monza, Unico van Wassenaer and others - a misattribution Stravinsky inherited and never had reason to doubt. What he actually did is more interesting than arrangement: he kept the melodies and bass lines almost intact and rebuilt the harmony, scoring and accentuation underneath them, so the old music is heard through modern glass.","Stravinsky later called it the discovery of the past through which his whole later work became possible. Contemporaries were less sure: some called it theft, some pastiche, and Diaghilev was disappointed by its restraint. It launched two decades of neoclassicism across Europe. The scholarly unmasking of the sources has, if anything, raised the piece's standing, since it shows how little the identity of the model mattered to what Stravinsky did with it.","Find a recording of Domenico Gallo's trio sonatas and play the original beside Stravinsky's version - the difference is the whole lesson in two minutes. Listen for the bass line landing a step away from where you expect. Then hear the full ballet with its three singers, which is rarer than the suite and much stranger."],"stravinsky-symphony-psalms-finale":["The last of three linked movements, about twelve minutes, setting Psalm 150 in Latin. It begins with a slow, hushed Alleluia from the chorus, moves into a driving fast section over drumming timpani and two pianos, and then returns to slowness for a long closing chant in which sopranos and altos circle a handful of notes above a rocking bass in harps and pianos. Nothing swells; the music simply repeats until it settles onto a bare C major.","Koussevitzky commissioned it for the Boston Symphony Orchestra's fiftieth anniversary season. Stravinsky, living in France, had returned to the Russian Orthodox church in 1926, and inscribed the score to the glory of God and to the orchestra, in that order. Ansermet conducted the first performance in Brussels on 13 December 1930, six days before Koussevitzky gave the American premiere in Boston.","The orchestra has no violins, no violas and no clarinets - Stravinsky removed exactly the instruments that carry Romantic warmth - but adds two pianos and a harp, so the sound is bright, hard and bell-like. The closing Alleluia rocks over a repeating bass figure that never resolves, and the score asks for it to be sung evenly, without crescendo or expressive shading; Stravinsky said the ending came to him as an image of a chariot rising.","Canonised almost immediately and now the most-performed twentieth-century choral work after the War Requiem and Carmina Burana. Its deliberate coldness divides listeners: some hear genuine liturgical impersonality, others a chilly aestheticism. Conductors argue chiefly about the tempo of the coda, where too slow becomes lugubrious and too fast destroys the stasis. The absence of violins still surprises audiences seeing it live.","Listen to the coda alone first, then go back and hear the whole symphony so that its arrival is earned. Do not wait for a climax - the point is that none comes. Compare a large choral-society performance with a small, hard-edged one; Stravinsky's own recording and the Bernstein version are almost opposite in temperature, and both are defensible."],"stravinsky-soldier-s-tale-march":["The opening number of a travelling theatre piece for narrator, actors, a dancer and seven players. A jaunty march for cornet and clarinet strides over a two-note double-bass tramp - except that the tune's phrases and the bass's pulse are different lengths, so the two keep sliding out of step and the soldier's walk limps. Three minutes, dry and bright, with no cushioning strings. The same march returns at the end, hollowed out.","Written in Morges, in French-speaking Switzerland, in 1918. The war had closed theatres, the Russian revolution had cut off Stravinsky's income and estates, and he and the Swiss writer C. F. Ramuz devised a piece small enough to tour villages on a cart: a Faust story of a soldier who trades his violin to the Devil. The premiere in Lausanne on 28 September 1918, with Ansermet conducting, was the only one - the influenza pandemic stopped the tour dead.","The seven instruments are chosen as high-and-low pairs - violin with double bass, clarinet with bassoon, cornet with trombone, plus one percussionist - a jazz band as Stravinsky imagined it from printed sheet music, never having heard the real thing played. Across the work the soldier's violin degrades as the Devil gains control of it, and the March that closes the piece is taken over by percussion alone.","The founding work of the modern mixed-ensemble repertoire, and the practical answer to a century of composers with no orchestra. Its ragtime, tango and waltz numbers are the sound of European modernism reading American music off the page and getting it productively wrong. Ramuz's text and some early stagings drew on antisemitic caricature in presenting the Devil, which productions now have to confront rather than ignore.","Hear it with the narration rather than as the instrumental suite - the piece is theatre, not concert music. Tap the bass's two notes and notice how the tune keeps arriving early or late. Then compare the opening March with its return at the end and hear what has been stripped away. Small, sharp-toned ensembles serve it far better than smooth ones."],"kodaly-hary-janos-suite-intermezzo":["The fifth of the suite's six movements, about five minutes. It is a verbunkos, the swaggering recruiting dance of the old Hungarian army: heavily dotted rhythms, a proud tune that struts rather than runs, and a cimbalom - the hammered zither of Hungarian cafe bands - rattling underneath and filling every gap with tremolos. A slower, more songlike middle strain for strings gives way to the strut again, and the movement ends with a flourish.","Hary Janos, first staged in Budapest in 1926, is a Singspiel about an old soldier in a village inn who claims to have single-handedly defeated Napoleon and turned down the hand of the Empress. Kodaly extracted the six-movement orchestral suite in 1927 so the music could travel where the Hungarian dialogue could not. Conductors including Toscanini took it up at once, and it became Kodaly's international calling card.","The suite opens with an orchestral sneeze - Hungarian lore holds that a sneeze during a story proves it true - so the entire work is announced from the first bar as a lie. The Intermezzo's tune is Kodaly's own writing in the verbunkos manner rather than a collected folk melody, which is exactly the distinction Bartok policed most fiercely. Where no cimbalom player can be found, the part is usually shared out between piano and harp.","The suite long ago eclipsed the opera, which is rarely staged outside Hungary and hard to translate. Critics divide over the Intermezzo: some hear urban cafe music given orchestral evening dress, others the most idiomatic verbunkos in the concert repertoire. The presence or absence of a real cimbalom changes the piece considerably, and orchestras that hire a player are noticeably preferred by Hungarian critics.","Listen first for the cimbalom, then try to ignore everything else and follow it alone. Compare a Hungarian orchestra - Fricsay, Dorati or Ivan Fischer - with a non-Hungarian one and hear how differently the dotted rhythms are snapped. Then hear the sneeze that opens the suite, and let it colour everything that follows."],"kodaly-dances-galanta":["One continuous movement of about sixteen minutes shaped like a verbunkos: a slow, heavy introduction for cellos and horns; then a long, curling clarinet solo that becomes the piece's refrain; then a chain of dances that get faster and lighter, interrupted each time by the clarinet returning to slow everything down. The last return is brief, and the orchestra hurtles into a whirling coda in which the accelerando is written into the notes.","Commissioned in 1933 for the eightieth anniversary of the Budapest Philharmonic Society. Kodaly had spent seven childhood years in Galanta, a small market town then in Hungary and now in Slovakia, where his father was a railway stationmaster and where he first heard a celebrated Roma band. The tunes come largely from a set of Viennese prints of around 1800 that advertised themselves as containing music from Galanta.","The form is a rondo in all but name, with the clarinet melody standing in for the refrain. The printed source is the point: this is urban Roma repertory of about 1800, precisely the tradition Bartok dismissed as not authentically peasant, and Kodaly embraces it openly. He also writes the final acceleration into the note values rather than leaving it to the conductor, so the rush is fixed rather than improvised.","Popular from the first performance and still Kodaly's most-played orchestral score alongside the Hary Janos suite. The authenticity quarrel between Kodaly's stylisation and Bartok's transcription is how both composers are now taught, and this piece is Exhibit A for Kodaly's side. Fricsay, Dorati and Ivan Fischer are the reference recordings, and each treats the clarinet's rubato quite differently.","Follow the clarinet: everything else is arranged around its returns, and the piece becomes easy to hold in the ear once you count them. Then listen to Bartok's Romanian Folk Dances immediately afterwards and hear two men who collected together arrive at opposite conclusions. A Hungarian orchestra will snap the dotted rhythms in a way that no score can notate."],"kodaly-hary-janos-viennese-musical-clock":["The third movement of the suite, about two and a half minutes. One brisk fanfare tune goes round five times without ever developing, each turn dressed in different colours: bells, tubular chimes, glockenspiel, celesta, triangle, brass and drums. There are no strings at all. The result is a sound made entirely of metal, wind and skin, imitating the clockwork carillon of a palace clock whose figures parade on the hour.","In the story the peasant Hary, brought to the imperial court in Vienna, stands open-mouthed before the Emperor's mechanical clock. Kodaly placed the movement third in the 1927 suite as relief between the battle music and the songs. Its simplicity and its bright percussion made it a fixture of children's concerts and, from the 1930s, of school music broadcasts in Britain and the United States.","The strict rondo is itself the joke: a clock cannot vary, so the tune is recoloured but never developed, and the movement's refusal to go anywhere is the characterisation. The absence of strings is complete and deliberate, which is rare in orchestral music of any period. It is also one of the few pieces by Kodaly with no folk source whatever - the tune is entirely his own.","By some distance the most-played thing Kodaly wrote, and for many non-Hungarians the only one they know, which has led to a certain critical condescension - the Intermezzo is usually held to be the better music. The one real interpretive choice is whether to keep it strictly metronomic, which reads it as machinery, or to let it breathe, which makes it a memory of machinery.","Count the five turns of the tune and note what changes each time - it is a lesson in orchestration in under three minutes. Play it beside Prokofiev's Peter and the Wolf and hear two composers teaching the orchestra to children within a decade of each other. A recording with real tubular bells rather than a synthesised substitute is worth seeking out."],"kodaly-sonata-solo-cello-first-movement":["The first of three movements, about ten or eleven minutes, for one cello alone. The bottom two strings are retuned down a semitone, which lets the player sound ringing open-string drones under the melody in the home key. Over that drone comes a declamatory, modal tune that sounds improvised. The writing constantly demands two things at once - bowing while plucking with the left hand, chords hammered across all four strings - so a single instrument keeps producing the texture of two or three.","Written in 1915 in wartime Hungary, three years into the folk-collecting expeditions Kodaly shared with Bartok and at a moment when concert life had all but stopped. Nothing of this scale for unaccompanied cello had been attempted since Bach's suites nearly two centuries earlier. Jeno Kerpely, cellist of the Waldbauer-Kerpely Quartet, gave the first performance in Budapest in 1918.","The retuning is what makes the piece possible: with the lower strings a semitone down, they ring sympathetically in the sonata's key and the player can bow a melody while the drone sustains, imitating a village fiddler. The modal inflections are drawn from Transylvanian song rather than from any theory book, and the rhapsodic passages are notated in strict rhythm precisely so that they can be made to sound free.","Neglected for three decades until Janos Starker's recordings around 1950 made it famous, and it is now a rite of passage for cellists. Almost everyone retunes, though a handful have tried it at standard pitch and lost the drones. The interpretive divide is between reading it as a national epic in the manner of a bardic recitation and reading it as an abstract study in what one instrument can be made to do.","Listen for the moment you first believe there are two instruments playing; it usually comes within the opening minute. Have Bach's First Cello Suite prelude to hand for comparison, and notice how differently the two composers use a single line. Starker's account remains the benchmark; more recent players tend to be warmer and slower."],"webern-five-pieces-orchestra-op-10":["Five pieces for a chamber orchestra of roughly eighteen solo players, lasting under five minutes in total; the fourth runs about twenty seconds. Instruments enter one or two notes at a time - mandolin, guitar, harmonium, celesta, muted trumpet, a clatter of small percussion, a single violin harmonic - and then withdraw. Almost nothing is doubled and nothing repeats. Silence takes up as much of the score as sound, and the loudest marking is rare.","Written in Vienna between 1911 and 1913, when Webern was in his late twenties, conducting in provincial theatres he detested and undergoing analysis. Schoenberg had abandoned key centres in 1908 and his two pupils followed; without a key to organise long spans, forms contracted to the size of a gesture. There was no public for this music. The pieces waited until 1926 for a premiere, in Zurich, at an ISCM festival, with Webern himself conducting.","This is where the technique Schoenberg called Klangfarbenmelodie becomes fully audible: a single line handed from instrument to instrument so that changing colour, not changing pitch, does the melodic work. Most of the score is muted and the dynamics run down to four p's. The fourth piece is six bars long and contains barely a dozen notes; its clarinet line rises out of nothing and stops without cadencing.","Dismissed for decades as fragments or as jokes - the Vienna scandal concerts of 1913 set the tone. After 1945 Boulez, Stockhausen and Nono treated Webern's economy as the ground floor of everything, arguing from these pieces toward total serialism, which many now think misread an Expressionist miniaturist as a systems theorist. Recordings split accordingly: Boulez's precision against a warmer, more gestural Viennese tradition.","Listen with headphones and the volume higher than feels sensible, because half the score is at the edge of audibility. Take the fourth piece alone and repeat it five times; twenty seconds is enough to learn by heart. Then hear Mahler's Ninth afterwards to feel the scale Webern was reacting against. Boulez's complete Webern set is the standard starting point."],"webern-langsamer-satz":["A single slow movement for string quartet, about ten minutes, in a warm C minor that keeps leaning toward the major. The first violin carries a long-breathed melody over throbbing inner parts; a second, more restless idea gathers into one large climax; then the opening returns and the music settles onto a sustained, quietly glowing chord. The texture is thick and continuous, with the viola and second violin busy almost throughout - closer to a string sextet than to a classical quartet.","Written in the summer of 1905, when Webern was twenty-one and had just begun private study with Schoenberg. He had spent June walking in Lower Austria with his cousin Wilhelmine Mortl, whom he later married, and the diary he kept of that trip is unguardedly rapturous. He did not publish the piece or seek a performance; it stayed in the family's papers.","The harmonic language is Brahms filtered through Verklarte Nacht, which Schoenberg had written six years earlier - the same voluptuous chromatic writing for strings put to a shy young man's purposes. The tempo marking asks for slow playing with agitated expression, an instruction Webern would never write again after 1908. The manuscript surfaced only around 1961, and the first performance took place in Seattle in 1962, seventeen years after his death.","Its rediscovery scrambled the postwar picture of Webern as a bloodless serialist, and it is now comfortably his most-played work. Some critics find that popularity faintly embarrassing - a Romantic exception used to avoid the real music - while others argue it shows the expressive impulse that the later miniatures compress rather than abandon. Performers argue mainly over how much portamento the style permits.","Play this and then the Six Bagatelles back to back: eight years and an entire aesthetic separate them, and the same sensibility is audible in both. Listen for the climax, the only place the quartet is allowed to be loud. The Emerson and Quatuor Diotima recordings are cooler; older Viennese ensembles slide between notes in a way that suits the piece."],"webern-six-bagatelles-string-quartet":["Six movements for string quartet lasting under four minutes together; the shortest is about thirteen bars. Each is assembled from a handful of separate gestures - a harmonic that barely sounds, a plucked note, a whispering bow drawn near the bridge, one accented stab - with silence between them. Nearly everything is muted and played at the threshold of hearing. There are no themes to follow, only events, and the last of the six ends by evaporating rather than closing.","Vienna, 1911 to 1913, Webern's most concentrated years, written alongside the orchestral pieces of Op. 10. Schoenberg's circle had abandoned keys, and without tonality's long-range machinery there was nothing to sustain a long movement, so forms shrank to the length of a single idea. Webern was making his living conducting operetta in provincial theatres and loathing it, and he wrote almost nothing longer than a few minutes for the next decade.","Schoenberg's preface to the published score is the key document: he describes a whole novel expressed in a single gesture and warns that such concentration demands silence around it. Nearly every note carries its own dynamic and playing instruction. And a habit is already visible that has nothing to do with theory - a pitch, once used, tends not to return for a while, an instinct that would harden into the twelve-note method Webern adopted a decade later.","Laughed at in Vienna as music that was not there, then treated after 1945 as scripture by the Darmstadt generation. The current argument is about performance rather than value: whether to play them as fragile Expressionist aphorisms with rubato and portamento, as the Vienna tradition did, or with the clinical exactness of the postwar recordings. The difference is enormous in music this short.","Turn everything else off. Play all six twice through - it costs eight minutes - and on the second pass try to hold the whole set in your head at once. Note where the silences are longer than the sounds. Then hear Webern's Langsamer Satz for the contrast, and compare the Emerson Quartet's precision with an older, more inflected reading."],"webern-passacaglia-op-1":["About eleven minutes for full orchestra. A bare eight-note theme is plucked out by strings alone, unaccompanied, in D minor; then some two dozen variations are built over it, starting almost inaudibly with a few solo instruments, thickening to a full-orchestra climax, and subsiding into quiet. The theme is always present somewhere - in the bass, buried in an inner part, turned upside down - and the piece's shape is the shape of what accumulates above it.","Webern's graduation piece after three years of private study with Schoenberg, finished in 1908 when he was twenty-four. He had just completed a doctorate in musicology on the Renaissance composer Heinrich Isaac, editing a volume of his masses, and was about to begin a decade of miserable theatre conducting. Schoenberg thought highly enough of it that Webern conducted it at his first public concert in Vienna that November.","The choice of form is not arbitrary: Webern's doctoral work had immersed him in Renaissance counterpoint, and a strict repeating bass is an old device carried forward. The eight-note theme leaves four of the twelve available pitches unused, and Webern places those four pointedly in the variations that follow. Behind the whole conception stands the finale of Brahms's Fourth Symphony, another orchestral passacaglia over a short repeating figure.","The friendliest door into Webern and the piece conductors programme when they want him in an ordinary concert. Critics divide between hearing accomplished Mahler-and-Brahms by a gifted student and hearing the moment Webern's lifelong obsession with strict form declares itself. Performances range from the lushly Romantic to the analytically dry, and the two produce almost different pieces.","Learn the eight-note theme in the opening seconds - it is plucked and unmistakable - and then try to track it through the variations; losing it is part of the experience. Play Brahms's Fourth Symphony finale afterwards. Boulez's recording is clear and cool; older accounts under Rosbaud or Dorati let the strings sing more."],"berg-violin-concerto-second-movement":["The concerto's second and last movement, about fifteen minutes in two halves. It opens with a violent Allegro in which the soloist is battered by the orchestra, marked as a catastrophe, with hammering repeated chords and a cadenza that breaks off exhausted. Then the clarinets quietly intone a Bach chorale, harmonised as Bach left it; the solo violin takes the melody, and a set of variations builds and subsides until the violin climbs alone to a held high note over sustained strings.","Berg accepted a commission from the American violinist Louis Krasner early in 1935 and worked slowly at it until April, when Manon Gropius - the eighteen-year-old daughter of Alma Mahler and Walter Gropius, whom Berg knew well - died of polio. He finished the score in about four months and inscribed it to the memory of an angel. In December he died of blood poisoning from an insect sting, having never heard a note of it; Krasner and Hermann Scherchen gave the premiere in Barcelona in April 1936.","The chorale is Es ist genug, from Bach's cantata O Ewigkeit, du Donnerwort, and it can appear at all because of an extraordinary fit: its melody opens with three rising whole steps, and the last four notes of Berg's twelve-note row are exactly those pitches. The row itself is stacked from alternating major and minor thirds, which is why the whole work sounds tonal without ever quite being so. A Carinthian folk song is woven into the first movement.","One of the very few twelve-note works to have reached a wide public, which has made it a battlefield: proof, for its defenders, that serialism can be as expressive as anything in Mahler; proof, for its detractors, that Berg only succeeded by cheating the method. Later scholarship has uncovered further private dedications hidden in the score, complicating the public story of the angel. Krasner's own recording remains a document of the premiere generation.","Start where the clarinets enter with the chorale - the change of air is unmistakable - and then go back and hear the catastrophe that makes it necessary. Have Bach's original chorale to hand. Among recordings, Krasner's is the historical witness, while Isabelle Faust with Abbado and Louis Krasner's successors take very different views of how much to let the violin sing."],"berg-wozzeck-interlude-d":["The orchestral interlude near the end of the opera, about four minutes, played with the stage empty after Wozzeck has drowned in the pond looking for the knife he used to kill Marie. Alone in the whole score it is unmistakably in D minor. It gathers the opera's important themes into one slow, swelling lament, climbs twice to a shattering chord for the full orchestra, and then subsides into the final scene, where children play and Marie's son is told his mother is dead.","Berg saw Buchner's unfinished play Woyzeck in Vienna in May 1914 and began at once; then came the war, and three years in the Austrian army, much of it spent on clerical duties under humiliating conditions he later compared directly to his protagonist's. He finished the score in 1922. Erich Kleiber forced it through some 137 rehearsals at the Berlin Staatsoper for the premiere on 14 December 1925.","Every scene of the opera is cast in an abstract instrumental form - passacaglia, suite, rondo, sonata movement, invention - and this interlude is the invention on a key. It is the one place Berg lets a plain D minor stand unchallenged, and he does it precisely because the character is now dead and the composer steps forward to speak in his own voice. The theme that opens it is Wozzeck's own, first heard in the opera's first act.","The premiere was simultaneously a scandal and a triumph, and the opera spread quickly across Germany until the Nazis banned it as degenerate in 1933, cutting off Berg's income. Since 1945 it has become the most-performed serious opera of the twentieth century and the model for a century of opera drawn from literature. Critics still argue whether this interlude is a moment of terrible honesty or the one sentimental lapse in an unsentimental work.","Hear the interlude cold first, without knowing the plot, and then again after watching the whole opera - it is a different piece the second time. Listen for the two climaxes and for how little happens after the second. Boulez, Abbado and Kleiber's son Carlos have all conducted it memorably; a staged performance in a small house hits far harder than a recording."],"berg-lyric-suite-andante-amoroso":["The second of six movements, five or six minutes, and one of the freely chromatic rather than strictly twelve-note ones. A gentle, rocking theme in the first violin floats over plucked and murmuring inner parts; three times a scampering muted trio interrupts, each time lighter and faster; the theme returns each time altered, and the movement disappears on harmonics. Across the whole suite the movements alternate, each faster one more agitated and each slower one more desolate than the last.","Written in 1926, four years after Wozzeck, when Berg was famous. Publicly it was an abstract quartet dedicated to Alexander Zemlinsky, notable for using Schoenberg's twelve-note method in some movements and free chromaticism in others. Privately it was a diary of Berg's affair with Hanna Fuchs-Robettin, the married sister of the novelist Franz Werfel, whom he met in Prague in 1925 and could not stop writing to.","The secret came out in 1976, when George Perle found an annotated score Berg had given Hanna. The numbers twenty-three, Berg's, and ten, hers, govern tempos and bar counts throughout; the pitches A, B flat, B and F spell their initials in German note-naming. This movement is generally taken to portray Hanna's household, the scampering trios her two children. The finale, it turned out, has a suppressed vocal line setting Baudelaire, and simply stops mid-phrase.","Taken for fifty years as a formalist showpiece and then re-read overnight as autobiography, which set off a lasting argument about whether biographical decoding illuminates the music or shrinks it. A quotation from Zemlinsky's Lyric Symphony and, in the finale, one from Wagner's Tristan had always been noticed; the cipher explained why they were there. Some quartets now perform the finale with a soprano, which Berg never authorised.","Listen to the six movements in order and feel the two paths diverging - faster and more frantic, slower and emptier - until they meet in the silence at the end. Take this movement alone for its lightness, then the finale for the fall. The Alban Berg Quartett's recording is the classic; hear a version with the soprano line restored to judge the argument for yourself."],"berg-seven-early-songs-nachtigall":["The third of seven songs, about two and a half minutes. Theodor Storm's poem says that because the nightingale sang all night, the roses opened and a girl has become a woman in love. The vocal line rises in one long arc to a sustained high note at that transformation and then falls away. Beneath it the accompaniment spreads chords that swell and recede without ever committing to a cadence until the final bar, so the song floats until the last moment.","Composed while Berg was in his early twenties, studying with Schoenberg and working as a civil servant in Vienna. He wrote perhaps eighty songs in those student years and much later chose seven of them. He orchestrated the set in 1928, after Wozzeck had made him famous, and it was in that version that the songs entered concert life. He had been courting Helene Nahowski, his future wife, when they were written.","The harmony is Wagner and early Strauss, but the chords frequently move by whole steps rather than by the pull of a key - the young Berg quietly testing what happens when functional harmony is loosened, four years before he wrote anything openly atonal. The 1928 orchestration is a mature composer commenting on his own youth: the nightingale figure goes to solo woodwind, and the poem's night-long singing is never illustrated literally.","Widely used as the gentle way into Berg for singers and audiences, and the subject of the same faint suspicion as Webern's Langsamer Satz - that its popularity lets listeners keep the Second Viennese School at arm's length. Singers and conductors disagree over which version is definitive: the orchestral one is grander and Berg's own final thought, the piano original more intimate and closer to the songs' occasion.","Follow the single long rise of the vocal line to its high note; the whole song is that one arc. Hear the piano version and the orchestral version back to back and decide which you believe. Then place it beside Strauss's Morgen and Mahler's Ich bin der Welt abhanden gekommen to hear the tradition Berg was still inside."],"villa-lobos-bachianas-brasileiras-no-5-aria":["About six minutes for soprano and eight cellos. The cellos pluck a slow, swaying accompaniment while the voice sings a long wordless melody on a single vowel, floating high above them. A middle section brings words - a short Portuguese poem about the moon rising over the evening - sung more freely and lower in the voice. Then the opening returns, but hummed with the mouth closed, so the melody seems to withdraw into the singer rather than end.","Villa-Lobos wrote nine Bachianas Brasileiras between 1930 and 1945, each arguing that Bach's counterpoint and Brazilian popular music spring from the same root. He composed No. 5's Aria in Rio in 1938, while running the Vargas government's national music-education programme and conducting massed choirs of schoolchildren in stadiums. The text is by Ruth Valadares Correa, the soprano who gave the first performance.","Every movement of the Bachianas carries two titles, one Baroque and one Brazilian: this one is Aria, and in brackets Cantilena. The plucked cellos are imitating a violao, the Brazilian guitar, so that a Bach aria's continuo is quietly converted into a street serenade. The closing return is marked to be sung with closed lips, which is why it sounds like a memory of the opening rather than a repeat of it.","By a wide margin his most-played music and the piece that fixed his image abroad, arranged for every conceivable combination. Some Brazilian critics have complained that the Bachianas' cosmopolitan polish sanded down the wilder Choros, and that this Aria in particular flatters a foreign idea of Brazil. Villa-Lobos recorded it twice as conductor, with Bidu Sayao and later with Victoria de los Angeles; the Sayao performance is generally treated as authoritative.","Listen to the cellos rather than the voice on a second hearing - eight of them, divided, doing the work of a guitar. Then find Bach's Air on the G String and hear what Villa-Lobos meant by the comparison. Sayao's recording with the composer conducting is the one to start from; de los Angeles is warmer and slower."],"villa-lobos-choros-no-1":["The first and shortest of a series of sixteen, about four minutes for solo guitar. It is a chorinho - the roving street-serenade style of Rio - laid out as a rondo: a wistful minor-key tune with a characteristic dotted lilt returns between two contrasting strains, one brighter and in the major, one more restless. Everything lies under the hand of a player rather than on the desk of a theorist: open strings ring as drones, and chord shapes slide bodily up the neck.","Villa-Lobos had played in Rio's chorões, the roving bands of guitar, flute and cavaquinho, in his teens and twenties, against his family's wishes and largely in the streets and cafes. He wrote this in 1920 and dedicated it to Ernesto Nazareth, the pianist-composer of Brazilian tangos - a deliberate claim of lineage. The rest of the Choros series followed through the 1920s, most of it written in Paris.","The conceit of the series is that a choros can be scored for anything: No. 1 uses one guitar, and later numbers call for orchestra, band and chorus together. This piece is the seed of that idea. The dedication to Nazareth places popular Rio music at the head of a concert cycle rather than as decoration within it, which in 1920 was a provocation.","The piece that made classical guitarists take Villa-Lobos seriously, though Segovia's advocacy came later, with the Etudes and Preludes. Critics divide between hearing an affectionate genre miniature and hearing a manifesto for the whole Choros project. Questions of tempo and rubato tend to be settled by reference to Brazilian popular practice rather than to the printed score, which is sparse in its markings.","Listen for the open strings ringing on under the melody - that is the sound of a guitarist writing for himself. Then hear a recording of a real chorinho group and come back to it. Among guitarists, the Brazilian tradition of Turibio Santos and Fabio Zanon phrases it more freely than European players tend to."],"villa-lobos-little-train-caipira":["The fourth movement of Bachianas Brasileiras No. 2, about four minutes and the piece everybody knows. Scrapers, rattles and plucked strings start a small steam engine turning; the wheels gather speed; a whistle blows on the trumpet; and over the clatter the strings lay a broad, modal country tune, so machine and landscape are heard at once. Near the end the train brakes - the deceleration written into the note values - and stops with a hiss.","Written in 1930 for chamber orchestra and named for the caipira, the countryman of the interior. Villa-Lobos had ridden the branch-line trains that carried farm workers across the state of Sao Paulo while travelling to collect material, and the piece is a portrait of one of them. It sits inside a four-movement suite whose movements all carry double titles, one Baroque and one Brazilian: this one is Toccata.","The engine is built from percussion normally used as colour - scrapers, ratchet, chocalho - promoted here to the role of the machine itself, which is why the piece sounds more like a recording than an evocation. The tune riding above the wheels is modal and country-derived, so the industrial and the rural do not compete. And the slowing at the end is composed, not conducted: the notes themselves get longer.","Endlessly arranged, endlessly excerpted, and almost always played without the other three movements of Bachianas No. 2, which are rarely heard at all. It invites comparison with Honegger's Pacific 231 of 1923, a machine portrait with the opposite temperament - awed rather than affectionate. Brazilian critics have used it in the long argument about whether Villa-Lobos's charm cost him depth.","Listen once for the percussion alone and identify each noise as a moving part. Then listen again for the string melody and try to hold both at once. Play the whole of Bachianas No. 2 afterwards - the other movements are stranger and slower. Recordings under Villa-Lobos himself are rougher and faster than modern ones, and closer to the point."],"villa-lobos-guitar-prelude-no-1":["About five minutes. It opens with a broad singing melody in the guitar's bass strings, thumb-plucked beneath sustained chords - the tune below the harmony rather than above it, which is a rare and instantly recognisable sound. A faster middle section moves to the treble, chordal and more urban in character, before the bass melody returns. The piece ends on a quiet rising flourish rather than a resolution.","One of five preludes written in Rio in 1940; a sixth is lost. By then Villa-Lobos was Brazil's musical administrator-in-chief under Getulio Vargas, directing national music education and conducting vast patriotic choral events. Andres Segovia, who had commissioned the Twelve Etudes from him a decade earlier, took the preludes into his repertoire and they quickly became standard guitar recital fare worldwide.","Villa-Lobos attached a dedication to each prelude, naming a facet of Brazil; this one honours the sertanejo, the countryman of the interior, and its contrasting middle section is usually associated with the street-wise malandro of Rio. The melody sits on the fourth and fifth strings, where the guitar is most vocal - a guitarist's instinct rather than a composer's abstraction. The manuscript of a sixth prelude has never been found.","After Rodrigo's concerto, probably the most-played guitar music of the twentieth century. Some players find the turn to the urban middle section abrupt and less convincing than the opening. More substantially, the Segovia editions differ from Villa-Lobos's manuscript in fingering and in some notes, and players increasingly go back to the source, which changes several familiar passages.","Listen for the melody in the bass and notice how unusual it is to hear a tune underneath its own accompaniment. Then compare a recording using the Segovia edition with one using the manuscript readings. Julian Bream and the Brazilian players Turibio Santos and Fabio Zanon offer three quite different accents on the same piece."],"villa-lobos-guitar-etude-no-1":["Barely two minutes, and almost entirely one thing: a rippling arpeggio pattern for the right hand, repeated without pause, while the left hand slides a single unchanging chord grip up and down the neck. Because the fingering never changes, the open strings keep ringing against the moving notes, and the harmony turns strange without any conventional progression at all. A short chordal passage and a fast scale bring it to a stop.","The Twelve Etudes were completed in 1929 and dedicated to Andres Segovia, whom Villa-Lobos had met in Paris, where he spent most of the 1920s among the modernists; drafts of some of the material go back to his Rio years. Segovia was then campaigning to build a serious concert repertoire for an instrument the concert world despised, commissioning from Ponce, Turina and others. Villa-Lobos, a guitarist himself, wrote from inside the instrument.","The right-hand arpeggio is the ostensible study, but the real invention is the left-hand grip - a shape that keeps two open strings sounding wherever it is moved, which is why the harmonies sound like nothing in European music. The same device underlies the guitar writing throughout the Choros. Segovia's published edition altered several passages, and the manuscript has since been re-edited, so what you hear depends on the score in front of the player.","The Etudes were not published until 1953 and Segovia performed only a few of them, but they are now the technical and musical foundation of classical guitar training. The editorial argument between the Segovia print and the manuscript is live and occasionally heated. No. 1 in particular is played at wildly different speeds, from a machine-like blur to a deliberate tempo in which every ringing string can be heard.","Listen for the open strings that never stop sounding, and hear how they collide with the notes moving around them. Then play it beside Paganini's Caprice No. 24 to compare two composers writing studies from inside their own instrument. Try a fast recording and a slow one - they are almost different pieces."],"prokofiev-romeo-juliet-dance-knights":["The ballet's most famous number, about five minutes, danced at the Capulets' ball. Heavy dotted chords in the brass grind over a stamping bass tread that repeats almost without variation, giving the feuding houses a lumbering, armoured weight. A quieter middle section for flute and muted strings lets Juliet dance lightly with Paris; then the tread returns, thicker, with a tenor saxophone buried in the texture giving the sound its peculiar sourness.","Commissioned by the Kirov in 1934, transferred to the Bolshoi, and written in 1935 as Prokofiev was finally resettling in the Soviet Union after eighteen years abroad. The Bolshoi declared the score undanceable and dropped it; the original ending, in which the lovers survive, was abandoned under pressure. The ballet was first staged in Brno in 1938 and reached Leningrad in January 1940 with Galina Ulanova as Juliet.","The saxophone doubling inside the brass is what gives the knights their acid weight; take it away and the number becomes merely loud. The theme returns transformed at the end of the ballet, so the family feud audibly outlives the lovers. Prokofiev drew three orchestral suites and a set of piano pieces from the score, and those concert versions circulated for years before any company would stage the thing.","The undanceable verdict was reversed within five years. Lavrovsky's 1940 staging, with substantial cuts and reordering, became canonical in Russia, and only from the 2000s have companies gone back to Prokofiev's 1935 score with its surviving lovers - a reconstruction that divides critics sharply. The number itself now leads a strange double life in football stadiums, television titles and advertising.","Listen for the saxophone; once you have found it you cannot unhear it. Then hear the same theme where it returns near the end of the ballet. Compare the Lavrovsky version with a reconstruction of the 1935 score to see how much a staging changes the music. Gergiev and Maazel take opposite views on tempo, and the difference is enormous."],"prokofiev-peter-wolf-opening":["The opening of a twenty-five-minute story for narrator and orchestra. Each character is introduced with its instrument and its tune: Peter as a bright striding melody on the strings in C major; the bird as a fluttering flute; the duck as a waddling oboe; the cat as a clarinet stalking in its low register; the grandfather as a grumbling bassoon; the wolf as three horns in close, snarling harmony; the hunters as timpani and bass drum. Then the gate opens onto the meadow.","Written in four days in the spring of 1936 for Natalia Sats and the Moscow Children's Musical Theatre, months after Prokofiev had moved his family permanently to the Soviet Union and weeks after Pravda's attack on Shostakovich had made the terms of Soviet music brutally plain. Sats wanted a piece to teach children the instruments of the orchestra; Prokofiev wrote the text himself as well as the music.","The teaching frame is kept exactly: every character holds its instrument and its key throughout, so the plot can be followed with your eyes shut, which is the whole design. The strangest moment comes at the end, when the duck - swallowed alive - can still be heard quacking faintly inside the wolf. Sats was arrested during the purges not long afterwards, and the hurried premiere was barely noticed.","A permanent classic outside the Soviet Union almost at once, and recorded by narrators from Eleanor Roosevelt to David Bowie to Sting. The sheer volume of celebrity narrations has led some critics to file it as a novelty, which undersells orchestration as fine as anything Prokofiev wrote. Debates now centre on how much the text should be modernised, and whether the hunters' triumph needs softening for present-day children.","Listen to the introduction until you can name each instrument from its tune alone, then play the rest without the narration and follow the story purely by sound. Compare two narrators of very different temperament. Then hear Kodaly's Viennese Musical Clock and Britten's Young Person's Guide, the same teaching impulse before and after."],"prokofiev-symphony-no-1-classical":["Four short movements, about fifteen minutes, scored for the size of orchestra Haydn used. A brisk opening Allegro with tunes that leap absurd distances; a slow movement in which a high, sweet violin line floats over a tiptoeing accompaniment; a Gavotte in place of the expected minuet, heavy-footed and famous on its own; and a fizzing finale. The manners are eighteenth-century throughout; the sudden sideways slides into distant keys are not.","Written in the summer of 1917 in the countryside near Petrograd, between the February and October revolutions, and deliberately composed away from a piano - Prokofiev wanted to prove he could work in his head. He was twenty-six. He conducted the premiere himself in Petrograd in April 1918 and left Russia within weeks, travelling east across Siberia to Japan and on to the United States, not to return for years.","The joke is structural rather than decorative: Prokofiev asked what Haydn would write if he were alive in 1917, and answered by keeping Haydn's proportions and phrase lengths exactly while sliding the harmony sideways underneath them. He claimed the finale contains no minor chords at all, and it very nearly does not. The Gavotte he later reused in the Romeo and Juliet ballet, where it sounds entirely at home.","It gave neoclassicism a working example three years before Pulcinella, though Stravinsky's version proved the more influential model. Soviet critics later held it up as proof that Prokofiev could write accessibly when he chose, an argument he resented being made on his behalf. Conductors divide between chamber-sized performances with hard timpani sticks and full modern string sections that romanticise what should be dry.","Start with the Gavotte, which lasts ninety seconds, then hear the whole symphony straight through. Play Haydn's Symphony No. 104 alongside it and count how much Prokofiev kept. A small orchestra on modern instruments, played fast and light, serves it far better than a big band; compare an eighty-player account and hear the joke go flat."],"prokofiev-piano-concerto-no-3":["Three movements, about twenty-eight minutes. A solo clarinet opens with a slow, songlike line before the strings scurry in and the piano arrives at a run; the first movement then alternates that Russian lyricism with hard, motoric passagework. The middle movement is a theme and five variations on a prim, gavotte-like tune that is progressively distorted - one variation a nocturne, another a machine. The finale sets a sardonic bassoon march against a broad soaring melody and ends headlong in C major.","Assembled from sketches going back to 1913 and finished in the summer of 1921 in Brittany, in a cottage near a colony of Russian emigres that included Konstantin Balmont and, nearby, Stravinsky. Prokofiev premiered it himself with the Chicago Symphony under Frederick Stock in December 1921 to a lukewarm reception; London and Paris performances over the following two years established it.","The opening clarinet melody is among the oldest material in the score, sketched years before the rest and waiting for a home. The second movement's theme is deliberately archaic, so that the variations have something formal to dismantle. And the finale's rushing white-note runs, where the pianist simply sprints up and down the keys, are a Prokofiev signature: material a child could read, played at a speed almost nobody can manage.","Slow to catch on, then the most-played twentieth-century piano concerto after Rachmaninoff's Second. Prokofiev's own 1932 recording with Piero Coppola is a primary document and is markedly faster and drier than the modern tradition. The standing argument is whether this is a Romantic concerto in modern dress or a deliberate anti-Romantic machine; Argerich's performances made the case for ferocity and reset expectations of tempo.","Begin with the second movement's variations, which are the easiest way to hear how Prokofiev's mind works, then take the whole thing. Listen for the clarinet in the first bars and again for how the finale answers it. Compare Prokofiev's own recording with Argerich's - one lean and matter-of-fact, the other electric - and the piece changes character completely."],"prokofiev-lieutenant-kije-troika":["The fourth of the suite's five movements, under three minutes. Sleigh bells and tambourine set a three-horse sledge moving across the snow while a swinging tune, based on a Russian song, passes from strings to saxophone to brass. A slower, more wistful middle strain interrupts; then the sledge gallops off and recedes, the bells thinning until the sound has gone into the distance. It is a portrait of motion that ends by simply going away.","Prokofiev's first film score, written for Alexander Faintsimmer's 1934 satire in which a clerical error creates a non-existent officer whose entire life, career and death the Tsar's court must then invent. It was Prokofiev's first major Soviet commission while he was still based in Paris and testing whether he could work inside the USSR. He drew the concert suite the same year, substantially rescoring the film cues.","The tune is an existing Russian song, sung with words in the film; in the suite Prokofiev usually gives it to instruments, though he also wrote a baritone version that is occasionally revived. The fade at the end is achieved by thinning the orchestration rather than by asking the players to get quieter, which is why the sledge sounds as though it is genuinely getting further away rather than merely softer.","The suite has completely eclipsed the film. Its wide afterlife - the Romance was the basis of Sting's Russians in 1985, and the Troika is a fixture of Christmas advertising - has left it filed as light music, which undersells the economy of the writing. The main performance question is whether to include the baritone, which restores the film's words and changes the tone considerably.","Listen to the last thirty seconds twice and work out how the disappearance is done. Then hear the whole five-movement suite, which is funnier and stranger than the Troika alone suggests. Try a recording with the baritone part included, then one without, and see which you prefer. Reiner and Abbado are both persuasive."],"prokofiev-piano-sonata-no-7-precipitato":["The finale of the second of the three War Sonatas, about three and a half minutes, marked Precipitato - headlong. It is a toccata in a limping seven-beat bar: the left hand pounds an ostinato built from barely two notes while the right hammers chords above it. Nothing lets up. The chords thicken through the middle, the dynamic climbs, and the piece drives to a battered B flat major that sounds less like triumph than like machinery running out of track.","Sketched in 1939, the year of the show trials and the Nazi-Soviet pact, when Prokofiev began all three War Sonatas at once, and finished in 1942 in evacuation at Tbilisi while the Soviet Union fought for survival. Sviatoslav Richter learned the sonata in four days and premiered it in Moscow in January 1943. It was awarded a Stalin Prize, which tells you how the authorities chose to hear it.","The seven beats are grouped unevenly, which is what produces the lurch; try to march to it and you will fall over. The ostinato is essentially two alternating notes, so almost all the sound comes from the right hand's chords. And the sonata's slow movement quotes Schumann's song Wehmut - about being able to sing while inwardly weeping - which casts a long shadow over the brightness of this finale.","Richter's premiere and subsequent recording set the terms and are still the benchmark. The sonata is read both as a portrait of the war machine and as officially acceptable energy that says nothing dangerous, and the argument has never resolved. Pianists take the finale at very different speeds; the fastest, loudest performances tend to lose the grip of the ostinato, which is where the menace lives.","Try to tap along and notice where you trip - the seven beats will not divide. Then listen to the slow movement first and come back to the finale; the Schumann quotation changes what the ending sounds like. Richter's recording is the historical document; Pollini's is the cleanest modern account, and Yuja Wang's the fastest."],"prokofiev-symphony-no-5-first-movement":["The first of four movements, about thirteen minutes, and unusually a slow one - Andante, in a broad B flat. Flute and bassoon lay out a long striding theme; a warmer second idea follows on the strings. Rather than argue, the two accumulate: layer is added to layer over the same tread, the brass thickening, until a coda brings the theme back fortissimo with tam-tam, bass drum and piano hammering underneath, hard and gleaming rather than consoling.","Written in about a month in the summer of 1944 at Ivanovo, the composers' retreat outside Moscow, while the Red Army pushed west. Prokofiev described it as a symphony about the grandeur of the human spirit. He conducted the premiere in Moscow on 13 January 1945; artillery salutes for the crossing of the Vistula were sounding outside and he waited for them to finish before beginning. It was his last appearance as a conductor - a fall weeks later left him permanently unwell.","There is no exposition repeat and no real contest between the two themes; the movement grows by piling material over an unchanging tread, which is why the coda feels arrived at rather than imposed. The piano is used purely as percussion in the loudest passages, doubling the attack of the bass drum. The symphony's grief is not here but in the third movement's Adagio, and the two should be heard as a pair.","An immediate success in Moscow, and in America through Koussevitzky's Boston performances later in 1945. Three years later the Zhdanov decree denounced Prokofiev for formalism and the Fifth's official standing became awkward overnight. The lasting question is whether it is patriotic affirmation or something more equivocal: Western critics tend to hear irony in the coda, Russian performers often do not, and the score supports both.","Listen to the coda and decide for yourself whether it is a victory or a threat - the answer is the whole interpretive question. Then hear the third movement, which is where the grief is. Compare a Russian recording such as Rozhdestvensky's or Gergiev's with an American one such as Bernstein's, and notice how differently the brass is balanced."],"prokofiev-cinderella-midnight":["The climax of Act II, three or four minutes. A ticking, mechanical figure starts up beneath the ball music; woodblock and percussion count out twelve strokes while the waltz above is progressively torn apart by dissonant brass and shrieking woodwind, faster and more frantic with each chime. Cinderella runs; the music does not wind down but stops dead. The gaiety of the ball is not interrupted so much as eaten.","Begun in 1940, set aside for the war and for the opera War and Peace, and completed in 1944 and 1945. The Bolshoi gave the premiere on 21 November 1945 with Olga Lepeshinskaya in the title role and choreography by Rostislav Zakharov. Prokofiev, already unwell after the fall that followed the Fifth Symphony's premiere, could take little part in the preparations. It was the last of his ballets staged in his lifetime.","The clock is a character with music of its own, and it appears earlier in the act as a warning before returning here to destroy the waltz - the ballet's two idioms, tender and mechanical, colliding in one scene. The twelve strokes are not evenly spaced dramatically, because the orchestra crowds between them and the panic tightens. Prokofiev reused material from this scene in the Third Orchestral Suite drawn from the ballet.","Long treated as Romeo and Juliet's lighter sibling and programmed far less often. Frederick Ashton's 1948 English staging, with men in the stepsisters' roles, established the ballet in the West and pushed it toward comedy, which some argue drains the midnight scene of its real menace. Russian and Western companies still differ over how grotesque to let the clock become.","Count the twelve strokes and notice how much more music is packed between the later ones than the earlier. Play it against the Nutcracker's waltzes to hear a machine devouring the same kind of tune. Then hear the whole of Act II. Pletnev and Gergiev both recorded the complete ballet; the orchestral suites give you only fragments."],"prokofiev-alexander-nevsky-battle-ice":["The central movement of a seven-part cantata, about thirteen minutes. It opens in near-silence - a frozen dawn on Lake Peipus, thin high strings, a rising chill - then the Teutonic Knights advance to a braying pseudo-Latin chant in the brass over a galloping ostinato. The Russians answer with a folk-like tune, and the two collide head-on, chant against song, for minutes on end. The ice cracks, the chant sinks, and the movement subsides into stillness over the battlefield.","Eisenstein's 1938 film about the thirteenth-century defeat of the Teutonic Knights was made as explicit anti-German propaganda. Prokofiev worked with him closely, sometimes writing to cut footage and sometimes having footage cut to his music. The Nazi-Soviet pact of August 1939 got the film pulled from cinemas; the cantata Prokofiev drew from the score that year kept the music alive, and the film returned the day Germany invaded in 1941.","The knights' chant is not a real hymn: Prokofiev invented a deliberately ugly pseudo-Latin one, and in the film had the brass play close into the microphone so the sound distorted - an early use of recorded distortion as an orchestral colour. In the concert hall that effect has to be manufactured by the players alone, which is why live performances of this movement sound cleaner and less frightening than the soundtrack.","The film score is routinely described as the first in which composer and director worked as equals, and Eisenstein's writings on their method are a founding document of film-music theory. The cantata is criticised for flattening the film's montage, but it is the form in which almost everyone hears the music. The crude 1938 soundtrack has been reconstructed and re-recorded several times, with results that divide opinion.","Watch the film sequence first, then hear the cantata movement without pictures, and note what the music has to do for itself. Listen for the moment the two armies' music overlaps rather than alternates. Reiner's and Abbado's recordings are the concert benchmarks; a screening with live orchestra is worth travelling for."],"prokofiev-violin-concerto-no-2-andante":["The middle movement of three, about nine minutes, and among the simplest things Prokofiev wrote. Plucked strings and clarinets tick out an unhurried lilting accompaniment; over it the solo violin spins a long melody in E flat that rises and falls back, almost entirely by step, so the rare leaps land hard. A darker central section clouds the harmony before the tune returns wreathed in running figuration, and the movement settles onto quiet plucked chords.","Written in 1935 for the French violinist Robert Soetens, who had asked for a work of his own after touring with Prokofiev. The composer noted that he sketched it in one country, wrote the slow movement in another and orchestrated it in a third, working while on tour. Soetens gave the premiere in Madrid on 1 December 1935. Within months Prokofiev had moved his family permanently to Moscow.","The accompaniment is a barely disguised siciliano, the lilting pastoral rhythm of the Baroque, which is why a 1935 movement sounds older than its date. The clarinets beneath the solo line are a favourite Prokofiev texture - the same colour opens the Third Piano Concerto. And the melody's near-total stepwise motion is a deliberate constraint: the two or three leaps in it are the only events that need to happen.","The Second Concerto has always been easier to love than the First and harder to make sound serious. Soviet critics welcomed its lyricism as evidence of the new simplicity the state wanted, which has left some Western commentators permanently suspicious of it. Heifetz's lean, fast account and Oistrakh's warm, broad one still frame the interpretive choice, and modern players tend to fall between them.","Count how few large leaps the melody makes, and listen for the ones that do come. Then hear the finale, which is Spanish-flavoured with castanets, and the contrast will show you what the Andante is doing. Compare Heifetz and Oistrakh directly - the same notes at very different temperatures."],"milhaud-boeuf-sur-toit":["Seventeen minutes of Brazilian popular dance music strung on a rondo: one bouncing refrain returns fifteen times, each time in a different key, with a new tango, maxixe or samba between the returns. Two keys often sound at once, so the tunes carry a permanent cheerful sourness, like a band in which the trumpets have read the wrong page. The staged version is a wordless farce set in an American bar under Prohibition, played by clowns in outsized papier-mache heads.","Milhaud spent 1917 and 1918 in Rio de Janeiro as secretary to the poet and diplomat Paul Claudel, filling notebooks with the tunes he heard in the streets and cinemas. Back in Paris he assembled them in 1919 as accompaniment for a silent Charlie Chaplin film. Jean Cocteau took the score away and turned it into a surrealist spectacle instead, staged in February 1920 with the Fratellini clowns. The title comes from a Brazilian song, O boi no telhado.","Almost nothing in it is Milhaud's own invention: some thirty borrowed Brazilian popular melodies pass by, plus a Portuguese fado, and he was happy to list his sources. The trick is the refrain, which never returns in the key it left, so the piece walks steadily through the whole circle of keys while appearing to stand still. The bar the score named became the headquarters of Les Six.","The Paris premiere delighted and irritated in equal measure, and critics who wanted French music to be serious took the music-hall borrowings as a provocation, which was exactly Cocteau's intention. Milhaud later grumbled that his lightest score had buried his four hundred others. Recent scholarship has traced the individual Brazilian sources and complicated the old story of a Frenchman simply raiding a folk tradition.","Count the returns of the refrain and try to hear it change key underneath you. Then listen for the two-key clashes, easiest in the loud tutti passages. Follow it with La creation du monde, written four years later, to hear the same composer swap Rio for Harlem. A brisk French orchestra suits it better than a heavy one."],"milhaud-scaramouche-brazileira":["The third and last movement of a suite for two pianos, three minutes long and marked at samba speed. A short syncopated tune is thrown between the two players, doubled, interrupted and answered, while the accompaniment keeps a hip-swinging pattern that never lets up. The two pianos frequently sit a step apart in key, which sharpens the tune rather than blurring it. It ends with a rush up the keyboard and a flat, grinning chord.","Milhaud drew the suite from incidental music he had written for a children's theatre company in Paris, playing at the International Exposition of 1937 in a theatre called Scaramouche. The material was practical stage music, recycled for two pianists who wanted something brilliant and short. Two years later he arranged the suite for alto saxophone and orchestra, and later for clarinet, and those versions have carried it into every conservatoire in the world.","The samba rhythm is not a general impression of Brazil but a specific maxixe pattern from the notebooks Milhaud filled in Rio twenty years earlier, transplanted into a French salon. Underneath the fun, the two pianos are running a strict polytonal experiment: hold one hand's part in your ear alone and it is perfectly ordinary; the friction is entirely between the players, not within either part.","It became his most performed piece, which he found faintly humiliating, and critics have used it ever since as evidence that he wrote too much and edited too little. Saxophonists disagree, since the arrangement gave their instrument one of its few unembarrassed concert pieces. Performance argument now centres on tempo: the printed speed is fast, the tradition is faster, and the polytonal clashes disappear at either extreme.","Hear the two-piano original first, then the saxophone version, and notice how much rhythmic bite the single wind line loses and how much swagger it gains. Listen to the left-hand parts alone if you can find a slow performance. Then go back to Saudades do Brasil for the quiet version of the same material."],"milhaud-creation-du-monde":["A ballet in six linked sections, about seventeen minutes, scored for a jazz band of seventeen soloists in which an alto saxophone sits where the violas would be in an orchestra. It opens with a slow, drooping blues over saxophone and piano. The centre is a fugue whose subject enters on the double bass and is answered by trombone and saxophone, blue notes and all. After the chaos of creation resolves, the opening blues returns for a final couple left alone on stage.","The Ballets suedois commissioned it for the Theatre des Champs-Elysees, where it was first given in October 1923: a creation myth assembled by Blaise Cendrars from African sources, with sets and costumes by Fernand Leger, who dressed the dancers as moving sculpture. Milhaud had gone looking for jazz in the dance halls of London and then in Harlem, and came back convinced that its instruments and its rhythmic freedom belonged in a theatre pit.","The fugue is the point. Nobody before had written a strict academic fugue whose subject is a blues line, given to a double bass, in a band with no violins. Listen also to how the saxophone is used, not as a novelty colour but as an ordinary member of the ensemble filling the alto register throughout. The final section quietly reverses the opening: the same music, but the tempo relaxed and the dissonance drained away.","Reviewers complained that a nightclub had been carried into the theatre, and the score was not taken seriously for years. It now looks like the first fully achieved marriage of jazz language with concert form, made a few months before Rhapsody in Blue and with no knowledge of it. Modern scholarship is more uneasy about the primitivist framing of the scenario and Leger's designs than about the music.","Follow the alto saxophone from the first bar and you will hear the whole texture organise itself around it. Wait for the double bass to start the fugue, roughly a third of the way in. Then play Rhapsody in Blue immediately afterwards and decide for yourself which one sounds more like jazz and which more like a concert piece."],"milhaud-saudades-do-brasil-corcovado":["Two minutes, one of twelve short dances each named after a district or landmark of Rio de Janeiro. The right hand plays a slow, swaying tango melody; the left hand vamps steadily beneath it in a completely different key and stays there. The result is a tune you could hum resting on an accompaniment that refuses to agree with it, which sounds less like a mistake than like heat, distance and memory. It fades rather than finishes.","Saudade is the Portuguese word for a longing for something absent, and the whole set is a Frenchman's souvenir of the two years he spent in Brazil during the First World War attached to the French legation. He wrote the pieces in Paris in 1920 and 1921, after returning, and orchestrated them soon after. The dance rhythms are those of the tango brasileiro and maxixe played in Rio's cinemas and cafes at the time.","The bitonality here is unusually strict: the left hand keeps one key from beginning to end while the right keeps another, so the piece is a controlled laboratory experiment disguised as a postcard. The idiom is close to the tangos of Ernesto Nazareth, the pianist Milhaud heard playing in a Rio cinema lobby, whose syncopations he took over almost intact and then coloured with the wrong chords.","The set has always been treated as charming minor Milhaud, and the orchestral version is rarely played, though it is arguably the better one. Pianists disagree about pedalling: heavy pedal blurs the two keys into a haze, dry playing makes the clash comic. Recent Brazilian scholarship has been more interested in the collection as documentary evidence of what was actually being played in Rio around 1918.","Listen once for the tune, then once trying to follow only the left hand, and the double vision will click into place. It lasts two minutes, so play it three times. Then hear a recording of Ernesto Nazareth to catch the source, and Le boeuf sur le toit to hear what Milhaud did with the same material at full volume."],"hindemith-symphonic-metamorphosis-finale":["The fourth and last movement of a twenty-minute suite, a march of about six minutes. Woodwinds set out a neat, slightly prim tune borrowed from Carl Maria von Weber; the brass take it over and swagger with it; a side drum keeps the parade moving. Counter-melodies pile on top of each other rather than replacing one another, and the movement ends with the whole orchestra hammering the theme home over pealing brass. Nothing is developed; everything is re-scored.","The project began in 1940 as a ballet with Leonide Massine, to be built on Weber's piano duets. Massine wanted the originals left more or less alone; Hindemith wanted to rebuild them, and the collaboration collapsed. In 1943, by then a refugee teaching at Yale with his music banned in Germany, he turned the sketches into a concert suite. The New York Philharmonic under Artur Rodzinski gave the premiere in January 1944.","The word metamorphosis is exact and was chosen against the word variations: Hindemith does not decorate Weber's tunes, he re-harmonises them in his own hard-edged language and re-scores them for a percussion battery Weber never imagined. In the finale, listen for the brass chorale writing that quietly overlays the march, which is pure Hindemith laid across a borrowed skeleton, two centuries of style sounding at once.","American audiences took to it immediately and it remains his most played orchestral score, which some German critics after the war read as evidence that exile had softened him. Keith Wilson's wind band transcription made it a staple of school and military bands, spreading it far beyond concert halls. Scholars now use it to argue that Hindemith's supposed dryness was a caricature drawn from his theoretical writings, not his music.","Wait for the moment the brass seize the tune over the side drum and everything else becomes accompaniment. Afterwards, find Weber's original piano duets, which take about a minute each, and hear how little melody Hindemith needed. Compare with Britten's Young Person's Guide, written three years later on a Purcell tune with the same appetite for orchestral colour."],"hindemith-mathis-maler-engelkonzert":["The first of three movements named after painted panels, about nine minutes. It opens quietly, strings holding still while three trombones intone an old German folk hymn, one phrase at a time. A fast section follows in which several themes run simultaneously rather than in sequence, woodwind lines crossing string lines, the counterpoint always audible because each strand keeps its own colour. At the close the hymn returns in the brass at full stretch over the racing strings.","Hindemith wrote it in 1933 and 1934 while working on an opera about Matthias Grunewald, the painter of the Isenheim Altarpiece, who lays down his brushes during the Peasants' War to join a revolt and then discovers he has abandoned the only thing he can actually do. Wilhelm Furtwangler premiered the symphony with the Berlin Philharmonic in March 1934. His public defence of Hindemith that November cost him his official positions.","The trombone tune is a real medieval melody, Es sungen drei Engel, and it does not simply frame the movement: it is buried inside the fast music as a counter-line, disguised in shorter notes, before it is revealed in the brass at the end. The scoring keeps the strings and the winds in separate registral layers throughout, so the effect is of painted panels rather than blended orchestral colour.","The premiere became a political event, the ovation understood by everyone in the hall as a demonstration; Goebbels answered by denouncing Hindemith by name, and the opera itself was not staged until 1938, in Zurich. Later critics found the symphony too consonant, too well made. Historians have complicated the resistance story by documenting how long Hindemith tried to find an accommodation with the regime before leaving.","Play the last ninety seconds first so that you know what the trombones at the opening are promising. Then listen straight through, tracking the hymn as it hides inside the fast music. Compare with Vaughan Williams's Tallis Fantasia, another twentieth-century orchestra reaching back for an old tune. Berlin Philharmonic recordings keep the brass weight the piece assumes."],"hindemith-trauermusik":["Eight minutes in four short sections played without a break. A grave opening for solo viola over quiet strings, a faster and more restless passage, a slow lament, and then a final section in which the strings settle onto a hymn tune while the viola climbs above it and the music simply stops. The viola stays low and speaking rather than singing high; the string writing is spare, with long held chords and almost no vibrato-hungry melody.","Hindemith was in London in January 1936 to give the premiere of his viola concerto Der Schwanendreher with the BBC Symphony Orchestra. King George V died on 20 January and the concert was cancelled. The BBC asked for something suitable instead; Hindemith spent the following day writing this piece in a studio, and played the solo part himself that evening in a broadcast under Adrian Boult, barely twenty-four hours after starting.","The closing chorale is Bach's setting of Vor deinen Thron tret ich hiermit, and Hindemith chose it for a reason that only works in Britain: the same melody is sung in English churches as the Old Hundredth. A German refugee mourning an English king found the one tune both nations already knew by heart, and let it arrive without announcement, so that the listeners in 1936 recognised it before they understood it.","Written in a few hours to fill a broadcasting emergency, it has outlasted most of what he laboured over, which critics have found either touching or a rebuke to the notion of labour. Some hear the last section as pastiche Bach. Violists treasure it because their concert repertoire is thin, and it now appears regularly at memorials, usually without anyone mentioning the king it was written for.","Listen for the moment the busy string writing drops away and the chorale begins underneath the viola, about six minutes in; that is the whole piece in one gesture. Compare with Barber's Adagio for Strings, written for very different reasons and used for the same ones. Recordings by Tabea Zimmermann or William Primrose keep the tone plain."],"orff-carmina-burana-o-fortuna":["Under three minutes, and it opens and closes the whole hour-long cantata. Two colossal chords for chorus and orchestra, then everything drops to a whisper: the choir mutters the Latin almost under its breath over a slow, heavy drum pulse, while the orchestra adds one instrument at a time. There is no counterpoint and almost no melody, only a single long crescendo that finally releases into a shouted final line. The chorus sings in unison for most of it.","Orff found the poems in an anthology printed from a manuscript kept at Benediktbeuern abbey in Bavaria, songs written by wandering medieval scholars about fortune, drink, spring and sex. He chose two dozen and set them for a staged cantata with dancers, first given at the Frankfurt Opera in June 1937. He then wrote to his publisher instructing him to destroy everything he had composed before it.","There is nothing to develop and that is the design: one rhythmic pattern, one harmonic area, and a crescendo lasting the whole movement. The two pianos are treated as percussion instruments, not as keyboards. And because the identical music returns at the very end of the cantata, the hour between is enclosed inside a wheel that turns exactly once, which is what the poem about Fortune's wheel describes.","It succeeded instantly in Nazi Germany, which is why Orff's postwar account of himself, including his claimed links to the White Rose resistance, has been picked apart by historians and largely rejected. Musicians have always found it crude; audiences have never cared. Its adoption by film trailers after Excalibur in 1981 turned it into shorthand for doom and made it almost impossible to hear as music.","Turn the volume down and listen to the long quiet middle, which is where the piece actually lives; loud playback flattens it. Follow a translation, because the poem is a complaint, not a spell. Then hear the whole cantata in one sitting so that the return of this music at the end lands as the closing of a circle."],"orff-carmina-burana-taberna":["Three minutes of men's voices at speed. The tavern chorus begins with a chanted list of who is drinking, and the list accelerates: the mistress drinks, the master drinks, the soldier, the priest, the sister, the brother, a dozen categories piling onto each other in a single breathless span while the orchestra hammers a flat, repeated accompaniment. It ends not with a cadence but with a shout, the chorus and the drums stopping together.","This is the third part of the cantata, the tavern section, sung entirely by men. The poems come from the goliards, wandering students and unfrocked clerics of the twelfth and thirteenth centuries who wrote in Latin about gambling, drinking and the collapse of every virtue. Orff had run a Munich school of dance and gymnastics before he made his name, and it shows here: he wanted a physical, immediate effect rather than a literary one.","The number immediately before this one is the strangest in the cantata: a roasted swan sings its own lament from the platter, given to a tenor pushed into a strangled falsetto over a squawking bassoon, one of the few genuinely comic-grotesque moments in the choral repertoire. Then note what happens to the drinking list: the accumulation is the entire musical argument, with no key change and no new material at all.","The tavern poems were the part that made cultural officials in 1937 nervous, being blasphemous as well as bawdy, and they are still the reason the cantata is occasionally cut in church performances. Tenors dread the swan. Critics who complain that Orff writes lists rather than music are not wrong; the counter-argument is that lists sung by fifty men at speed do something no counterpoint can.","Get a text and translation, because the joke is entirely in the words and their acceleration. Listen to the swan's lament first, then this, so the section's black humour registers. Compare with a reconstruction of actual goliard song, sung by one voice with a lute, to hear how far Orff's medieval sound is his own invention."],"orff-carmina-burana-tanz":["A purely instrumental interlude, under two minutes, sitting in the spring section of the cantata. Strings and woodwind stamp out a village dance in a modal tune that circles a handful of notes, percussion cracking on the strong beats. A slower, heavier trio arrives, then the fast music returns and closes abruptly. There are no voices at all, and after the massed choruses on either side of it the effect is of a band suddenly playing outdoors.","The cantata's second part, on the green, gathers medieval spring poems about dancing, courting and the return of the sun, and Orff punctuates the songs with wordless dances for the staged version. His years running a school of movement and gymnastics with Dorothee Gunther fed directly into this music, as did the tuned percussion he and Gunild Keetman designed there, which became the Orff Schulwerk instruments used in schools worldwide.","The Benediktbeuern manuscript actually contains melodies, written in an early notation, and Orff ignored every one of them. Nothing in Carmina Burana is medieval music; the tunes are his, built on Bavarian folk-dance patterns he knew from home. This dance is the clearest evidence: its stamping rhythm and its trio belong to a village band of the nineteenth century, not the thirteenth, and the whole invented Middle Ages of this score follows from that decision.","It is the movement most often lifted for trailers and suites, and the one that best exposes the charge of primitivism, since it has no text to argue for it. Musicologists have made the manuscript's unused melodies a standing reproach. Conductors differ on whether to let the percussion dominate, which turns it into a folk band, or to keep it symphonic, which makes it sound merely brisk.","Listen to the whole spring section rather than this alone, so the dance arrives as relief between songs. Notice how few notes the tune uses. Then compare with Bartok's Romanian Folk Dances, which are transcriptions of real village music, and hear the difference between collecting a tradition and inventing one."],"gershwin-rhapsody-blue":["Sixteen minutes in a single unbroken span for piano and band. It opens with a clarinet trill that slides upward over two octaves in one unbroken smear, and from there proceeds as a chain of tunes rather than an argument: a strutting syncopated theme, a jaunty march, a broad slow melody at the centre that the strings take over, and a final acceleration. The piano alternates between leading the band and playing alone in cadenzas that sound improvised because some of them once were.","The bandleader Paul Whiteman announced in the New York papers that Gershwin was writing a jazz concerto before Gershwin had agreed to write one. He composed it in roughly three weeks in January 1924, sketching at the piano while Ferde Grofe orchestrated behind him for Whiteman's band. The premiere was at Aeolian Hall in February 1924, in a long concert billed as an experiment in modern music, with Rachmaninoff and Stokowski in the audience.","The opening was written as a plain rising scale for clarinet. Ross Gorman slid it as a joke during rehearsal, Gershwin heard it and told him to keep it, and one of the most recognisable sounds in American music is a player's gag that survived. The other open secret is that Gershwin did not orchestrate his own piece: Grofe made three versions, and the solo part in the original score is in places simply blank, marked to wait for the composer's nod.","Reviews split down the middle, some hearing a new American form, others dismissing it as feeble and derivative; Bernstein later admired the tunes while saying the piece could be reordered without harm. The 1924 jazz-band version, long replaced by Grofe's 1942 symphonic arrangement, has been restored by conductors who want the original's saxophone-and-banjo grit, sometimes alongside Gershwin's own piano rolls.","Start with the 1924 band version, which is leaner and stranger than the one used in adverts. Listen for the moment the strings take up the big slow tune, and hear how little preparation it gets. Then play Milhaud's La creation du monde, written the year before, and ask which composer had actually been listening to jazz musicians."],"gershwin-summertime":["The first thing you hear after the opera's opening dance: Clara sings a lullaby over her baby on a tenement porch while the men below shoot craps. Three minutes, slow, rocking between two chords for almost its whole length, with a wordless humming chorus underneath and a melody that rises, hangs, and falls back on itself. It returns twice more in the opera, sung by different women in changed circumstances, which is why it works as a marker of time passing.","Gershwin spent four years turning DuBose Heyward's novel Porgy into an opera, including a summer in 1934 on Folly Island off South Carolina, where he listened to Gullah singing and shouting in nearby churches. Heyward wrote this lyric. The opera opened in Boston and then on Broadway in the autumn of 1935 with an all-Black cast, which Gershwin insisted upon and which then restricted where in America it could be performed.","It is an original tune, not a spiritual, though almost everyone assumes otherwise: the melody's narrow, gapped shape and the wail on the long syllables imitate the idiom so exactly that it has been absorbed back into the tradition it copied. Underneath, the harmony barely moves, two chords swaying, which is why it survives being lifted out of the opera and given to any instrument at any tempo.","Arguments about a white composer writing a folk opera about Black life began before the premiere and have never stopped; Duke Ellington was among the early critics, and later Black artists from Leontyne Price to Grace Bumbry made the work theirs. The 1976 Houston Grand Opera production, restoring the full score, moved it decisively from Broadway to the opera house. Its thousands of cover versions have almost detached it from the drama.","Hear it once inside a full staged recording so you know who is singing and why, then let the covers do their work: Billie Holiday in 1936, Ella Fitzgerald and Louis Armstrong in 1958, Miles Davis with Gil Evans. Listen for the humming chorus, which nearly every popular version removes and which changes the song completely."],"gershwin-american-paris":["Eighteen minutes of tone poem with no soloist and no movements. It begins with a jaunty walking theme and real taxi horns honking across it; the walking music wanders through a street scene, and then everything slows for a long blues at the centre, a solo trumpet over saxophones, which is homesickness rather than sightseeing. A Charleston-flavoured episode lifts the mood, the walking theme returns, and the piece signs off with a grin.","Gershwin went to Paris in the spring of 1928, bought four taxi horns on the avenue de la Grande Armee, and met most of the composers then living there: Ravel, Poulenc, Milhaud, Prokofiev, Stravinsky. Ravel refused to take him as a pupil on the grounds that a first-rate Gershwin was better than a second-rate Ravel. Walter Damrosch commissioned the piece and conducted the New York Philharmonic premiere at Carnegie Hall in December 1928.","He orchestrated this one himself, unlike the Rhapsody, and the difference is audible in the wind writing. The taxi horns hold a small scandal: Gershwin labelled them A, B, C and D in the score as names, not pitches, and orchestras took them for note names for the best part of a century, so most recordings honk the wrong chord. A manuscript study restored his intended pitches only recently.","The first reviews were lukewarm, several critics finding it a string of postcards. The 1951 MGM film built round it, with Gene Kelly dancing an eighteen-minute ballet, made it universal and simultaneously made it hard to hear as concert music. Scholarly editions since the 2010s have corrected accumulated errors, and performances using the restored horns and Gershwin's own cuts sound noticeably different.","Listen for the horns in the first minute, then for the blues trumpet at the centre, which is the emotional point of the piece and is usually treated as an interlude. Compare its orchestration with the Rhapsody, which Grofe scored, to hear Gershwin's own hand. Recordings using the corrected taxi horns are worth seeking out."],"gershwin-three-preludes":["Seven minutes, three short pieces, fast-slow-fast. The first drives on a syncopated dance rhythm with a five-note figure that keeps landing off the beat. The second is a slow blues, which Gershwin described as a kind of blues lullaby: a walking bass under a drooping right-hand melody, the whole thing built like a written-out improvisation. The third is a brilliant, hard-driving finale of barely a minute, over almost before it registers.","Gershwin played five or six preludes at a New York recital in December 1926, sharing the programme with the contralto Marguerite d'Alvarez, and published only three of them the following year. He was at that point the most successful songwriter in America and spending his own money on composition lessons, having been turned down by Ravel and, later, approached Nadia Boulanger and Schoenberg with the same result.","The second prelude is the interesting one: its left hand walks a bass line in a strict pattern while the right hand plays a melody that is deliberately not aligned with it, which is a jazz pianist's texture written out in full at a time when almost nobody notated such things. Two further preludes from the recital survive under other names, so the published set of three is an editorial decision, not a design.","Long dismissed as salon miniatures, they have been reassessed as the closest thing to a document of how Gershwin actually played. Jascha Heifetz arranged all three for violin and piano and those versions are now heard as often as the originals. Pianists argue over swing: the printed rhythms are square, Gershwin's own recordings and piano rolls are anything but.","Start with the second, the slow blues, and let the walking bass carry you. Then find a Gershwin piano roll or his 1920s recordings, and notice how much freer he is than the page. Set them beside Debussy's preludes to hear what a young American took from Paris and what he flatly refused."],"gershwin-cuban-overture":["Eleven minutes in three connected sections, fast-slow-fast. Four Cuban percussion instruments are placed at the front of the platform and start the piece, and they never stop for long. A rumba rhythm drives the outer sections, with two borrowed Cuban popular tunes tossed between trumpets and woodwind. The slow centre is quite different: a long, sultry melody laid over a repeated pattern, treated in strict imitation, one instrument chasing another. Then the percussion returns and takes the piece out at full volume.","Gershwin spent two weeks in Havana in February 1932, hearing son and rumba bands and, by his own account, being serenaded outside his hotel by a sixteen-piece band at four in the morning. He wrote the piece within months, originally calling it simply Rumba, and it was premiered that August at Lewisohn Stadium in New York before a crowd of some seventeen thousand at an all-Gershwin evening.","The slow middle section is a canon, written while Gershwin was taking rigorous lessons in counterpoint and compositional systems with Joseph Schillinger, and it is there to prove that he could do it. The score also specifies where the four percussionists should stand, in a row in front of the conductor, making the piece partly a piece of staging: the instruments are meant to be seen as well as heard.","It is the least played of his orchestral works and has often been written off as a holiday snapshot with an academic middle. The counter-case has grown as Latin percussionists have taken part in recordings, which changes the rhythms audibly from a symphonic approximation into something closer to what Gershwin heard. Critics still divide over whether the canon belongs in the same piece as the rumba.","Listen to the first thirty seconds twice and separate the four percussion instruments by ear; the rest of the piece then makes sense. Wait for the canon in the middle and follow one line through it. Compare with Copland's El Salon Mexico, made from a similar trip and a similar borrowing four years later."],"gershwin-it-ain-t-necessarily-so":["Four minutes at a church picnic on Kittiwah Island. Sportin' Life, the drug dealer, entertains the congregation by taking apart the Bible stories they have just been singing about, verse by verse: Jonah and the whale, David and Goliath, Moses in the bulrushes, Methuselah's nine hundred years. He opens with a run of nonsense syllables, then a lazy, swung refrain, and the picnickers answer him in call and response, gradually joining in with what they came to deny.","Ira Gershwin wrote the lyric, and it is his sharpest work: a scepticism aria disguised as a party turn. The part was written for John W. Bubbles, half of the vaudeville act Buck and Bubbles, a tap dancer who could not read music and learned the role by ear, with the rhythms tapped out to him. It has been lifted out of the opera as a cabaret number ever since the show opened in 1935.","The opening nonsense syllables trace a melodic shape very close to the Hebrew blessing chanted before the reading of the Torah, which Gershwin had heard from childhood; the resemblance is noticed by nearly every cantor who hears it and is probably deliberate. If so, the mocking of scripture opens with a synagogue melody, which is either a joke or a confession, and Gershwin never explained which.","The number is the reason the opera keeps being accused of stereotype, since Sportin' Life is a caricature and gets the best tune. Cab Calloway, who later played the role, and the Ella Fitzgerald and Louis Armstrong recording of 1957 fixed a performance style closer to vaudeville than to opera. Productions since the 1976 Houston restoration have worked to give the character weight rather than only charm.","Hear it in a full staged recording first, with the crowd's shocked responses, which the standalone versions cut and which supply the joke. Then compare Cab Calloway with Ella Fitzgerald, and notice how much of the effect is timing rather than tune. Follow the words closely; the rhymes do most of the work."],"gershwin-man-i-love":["A thirty-two bar popular song with a verse in front of it, four minutes at a singer's usual pace. The chorus is built on one small idea: a three-note figure repeated at rising pitches while the harmony underneath slides steadily downward, so the melody seems to strain upward against a floor that keeps dropping away. Each phrase ends on a bent, flattened note. The lyric is a wish rather than a memory: she has not met him yet.","Ira and George wrote it for the 1924 show Lady, Be Good!, starring Fred and Adele Astaire, and it was cut before Broadway. They put it into Strike Up the Band in 1927, which folded. They tried again in Rosalie in 1928 and cut it once more. It became a hit anyway, through sheet music sales and through London society, where it was taken up and played by dance bands with no show attached to it at all.","The engine is the descending bass line under the rising tune, the same device that drives centuries of laments and most of the blues, and it is why the song sounds sad while the words are hopeful. Ira changed the title from The Girl I Love and back depending on who was singing it. Jazz musicians almost always drop the verse and treat the chorus purely as a chord sequence, which turns a specific wish into an abstract shape.","Three shows dropped it and it outlived all three, which is the standard argument against Broadway's judgement. Billie Holiday, Lena Horne and Ella Fitzgerald each made it theirs. Some critics find the rising-sequence construction mechanical once you notice it. The habitual omission of the verse is a real interpretive loss: without it, the listener never learns that the man in question is imaginary.","Find a recording that keeps the verse, then one that does not, and hear how the song changes meaning. Listen for the bass line falling while the tune climbs. Billie Holiday in 1939 and Coleman Hawkins as an instrumental make a good pair, one following the words, the other abandoning them entirely."],"poulenc-concerto-two-pianos-first-movement":["Eight minutes that change costume every ninety seconds. Both pianos crash in together on a hammering figure, the orchestra snapping behind them; then a music-hall tune saunters past; then a tender second idea that could have come out of a Mozart concerto. Nothing is developed in the textbook sense, it is all juxtaposition. The extraordinary part is the ending, where the orchestra falls away and the two pianos ripple quietly in overlapping patterns until the movement dissolves rather than closes.","Winnaretta Singer, the sewing-machine heiress who became Princesse Edmond de Polignac, commissioned it for her Paris salon, as she commissioned much of the best French music of the period. Poulenc and Jacques Fevrier gave the premiere at the Venice Biennale in September 1932 with the La Scala orchestra. Poulenc was thirty-three, still regarded as the lightweight of Les Six, and largely self-taught in composition.","The rippling ending is a memory of a Balinese gamelan Poulenc had heard at the Paris Colonial Exposition the year before: he took the layered, interlocking ringing and gave it to two pianos, without pretending to imitate the instruments. Elsewhere the borrowing is stylistic rather than exotic. The whole concerto is assembled from other people's manners, and Poulenc's originality lies in the joins, not the materials.","Critics who wanted a concerto to argue found this one flippant, and the charge of pastiche has followed it since 1932. The defence is that the styles are chosen and placed with complete precision, and that the gamelan coda is not a joke at all. It is now the most played twentieth-century two-piano concerto, and duo teams treat it as their calling card.","Listen once for the last thirty seconds alone, then again from the start and count how many different worlds pass in eight minutes. Compare with Mozart's concerto for two pianos, which Poulenc plainly had in mind. The Labeque sisters' recordings catch the mixture of precision and cheek the piece needs."],"poulenc-gloria-laudamus-te":["Barely two minutes, the second of six movements, and it bounces. The chorus tosses the Latin out in short, syncopated bursts with the accents landing on the wrong syllables, brass punching in between the phrases, the whole thing skipping along at a tempo closer to a dance number than to a church service. After the solemn opening movement it arrives like someone throwing open a window. The choral writing is homophonic and squarely rhythmic; nothing hides.","The Koussevitzky Foundation commissioned it, and Charles Munch conducted the premiere with the Boston Symphony in January 1961. Poulenc had come back to Catholicism in the 1930s and by 1960 had written a body of sacred music that mixed devotion with an entirely unembarrassed sensuality. When the movement's tone was questioned he said he had been thinking of Benedictine monks he once saw playing football, and of frescoed angels sticking out their tongues.","The mis-accentuation is deliberate and systematic: Poulenc pulls the Latin stresses about so that the words dance rather than declaim, which is precisely what centuries of church musicians were trained never to do. Note too how the harmony moves, sliding a third at a time into unrelated keys and back, a trick from his songs; the sacred and the secular music use exactly the same hand.","Some churchmen and critics found the levity close to irreverence, and the argument about whether a Gloria may be funny has never quite gone away. Poulenc's own defence, and the movement's obvious joy, have won. It is now among the most performed sacred works written since 1945. Performance debates centre on tempo, since his own markings are faster than most choirs attempt, and on boys' voices against women's.","Play the first movement and then this one without a gap, so the whiplash does its work. Follow the Latin text and watch where the accents fall. Then hear the soprano's Domine Deus later in the piece for the other Poulenc. A French choir and a conductor who trusts the fast tempi will make the case best."],"poulenc-chemins-l-amour":["A waltz-song of about three minutes. The piano keeps a gentle three-beat swing, the singer begins low and almost conversational, and the refrain then opens out on the title phrase with a long swooping line. The verse returns, then the refrain again, and on its last appearance the harmony steps sideways into a warmer key and does not come back the way it left, so the song ends somewhere slightly other than where it started.","Poulenc wrote it for the actress and singer Yvonne Printemps to perform in Jean Anouilh's play Leocadia, staged in Paris in 1940, in the first months of the German occupation. It is a chanson for a spoken play rather than a concert song, and its dramatic function is memory: the heroine is chasing a love affair that lasted three days, and the waltz is the thing she cannot stop hearing.","It is not really an art song at all but a boulevard waltz written for a small, actressy voice, which is why the vocal line stays low and speech-like until the one soaring phrase it saves up for. That restraint is the craft. Listen to where the piano stops filling in and lets the singer stand alone; the silences are placed as carefully as the tune.","Purists have always treated it as an encore trifle, unworthy of the composer of the Carmelites, and singers from Regine Crespin to Renee Fleming have used it as a closing number anyway. Arrangements for cello, saxophone, guitar and orchestra keep appearing. What it really documents is Poulenc's lifelong refusal to accept that a good tune disqualifies a piece from being serious.","Hear a French-speaking singer first so the words land, then a frankly cabaret version, and let the two versions argue. Listen for the key change on the final refrain. Follow it with one of Poulenc's fiercer wartime songs to hear the same composer with the charm switched off."],"poulenc-flute-sonata-cantilena":["The slow middle movement of a three-movement sonata, four unhurried minutes. The flute sings one long melody from beginning to end with almost no ornament and no display; the piano supplies quiet chords and then gets out of the way, often leaving the flute alone at the start of a phrase. The phrase lengths are uneven, so the tune never settles into a pattern. It builds to a single climax, subsides, and closes on the flute's warmest register.","The Coolidge Foundation commissioned the sonata in memory of Elizabeth Sprague Coolidge, the American patron who had funded chamber music for decades. Poulenc wrote it in Cannes and gave the first performance with Jean-Pierre Rampal at the Strasbourg Festival in June 1957, playing the piano himself. He was in his late fifties, in a period of illness and of deaths among his friends, and writing some of the plainest music of his life.","The melody is made of one gesture repeated: a phrase that lifts, reaches, and sags back a step, over and over, never quite completing its climb. The same falling shape runs through the sacred music he was writing in the same years. Note also how specifically it is written for Rampal's playing, the bright French silver-flute sound with a quick vibrato, which is why the tune sits in the middle register and never screams.","Early reviews called it slight. It is now the most played French flute sonata in existence and a fixture of every conservatoire syllabus, which has generated its own performance quarrels over vibrato and rubato in this movement, and a running complaint about errors in the first printed edition that later editions have corrected. Lennox Berkeley's orchestration of the accompaniment gets an occasional airing.","Find the recording Rampal made with Poulenc at the piano and listen for how little the pianist does. Notice where each phrase begins with the flute unsupported. Then hear the sonata's finale to catch the same melodic shape hurried up and made cheerful. Debussy's Syrinx makes a good companion for unaccompanied contrast."],"poulenc-dialogues-des-carmelites-salve-regina":["The last scene of the opera, about six minutes. Sixteen Carmelite nuns walk to the guillotine in a Paris square singing the Salve Regina in procession. As each is executed the orchestra delivers a single dry percussive stroke, and one voice disappears from the chorus. The singing continues, thinner each time, until one voice is left, and then that voice too is cut off. What follows is silence and a short orchestral postlude.","The Martyrs of Compiegne were guillotined in July 1794, ten days before the Terror ended. Their story reached Poulenc through a novel by Gertrud von Le Fort and a screenplay by Georges Bernanos. La Scala commissioned the opera and gave the premiere in Italian in January 1957, with the French premiere in Paris that June. Poulenc composed it during a period of severe depression and intense religious practice.","The dramatic mechanism is subtraction: the listener does not watch deaths, but hears the choral texture lose a voice at each stroke, so the arithmetic is done in the ear. And the last voice is not one of the sixteen. Blanche, who had fled the convent in terror, comes out of the crowd to take the empty place, and what she sings as she climbs the scaffold is not the Salve Regina at all but the Veni Creator.","French audiences in 1957 argued about an opera that sets a play's ordinary dialogue with almost no arias, and about hearing it first in Italian. Some producers find the guillotine stroke unbearably literal and mute or stylise it. It has become one of the most performed operas written since 1945, and the final scene is routinely cited as the strongest ending in postwar opera.","Go in without reading a synopsis and simply count the strokes; the piece does the rest. Listen for the moment only one voice remains. Afterwards, hear Poulenc's Gloria for the other face of the same faith. A French-language recording with Denise Duval, who created Blanche, carries the diction the scene depends on."],"poulenc-organ-concerto":["Twenty-three minutes in a single unbroken span made of seven contrasting sections. It opens with a colossal organ chord and a plunging flourish in the old improvising manner, then swings without warning between grave, chorale-like music and fast episodes that would not disgrace a fairground. There are no woodwind or brass at all: only strings, timpani and the organ, so the sound alternates between two extremes of weight. After a last outburst it ends quietly, on the grave music.","The Princesse de Polignac commissioned it in 1934, apparently wanting something not too forbidding for her salon, and Poulenc took four years. In between, a friend was killed in a car accident and Poulenc made a pilgrimage to the shrine of the Black Virgin at Rocamadour, after which he returned to the Catholicism of his childhood. Maurice Durufle played the first public performance in Paris in June 1939.","The opening flourish is deliberately modelled on Bach's G minor Fantasia, and Poulenc wanted the reference heard: the piece begins by quoting the whole tradition it then refuses to obey. Durufle advised on the registration, so most of the stop indications in the score are his rather than the composer's. Note also that the organ never gets a display cadenza, which for this instrument is close to a provocation.","The first hearers found the mixture bewildering, half church and half circus, and the composer's own description of it as being on the edge of his sacred music has been quoted ever since as an admission. The mixture is now understood to be the subject. Performances vary enormously with the instrument: a French romantic organ and a neo-baroque one produce almost different pieces, and the fast tempi remain contested.","Seek a recording on a large French romantic organ, where the piece belongs. Listen for the timpani, which is a third character and not an accompaniment. Compare with the finale of Saint-Saens's Organ Symphony to hear the opposite approach to the instrument. Then move on to the Gloria, twenty years later, for where this music was heading."],"copland-appalachian-spring-simple-gifts":["The penultimate section of the ballet, about four minutes. A solo clarinet states the Shaker song plainly, with no harmony worth the name. Five variations follow, each handing the tune to different instruments: oboe and bassoon in canon, strings hurrying it along, then the full ensemble in a slow, hymn-like statement with the melody in long notes underneath everything. After that peak the music thins out again for the ballet's quiet ending, the couple alone in their new house.","Elizabeth Sprague Coolidge commissioned it for Martha Graham, who wanted a ballet about a frontier couple on their wedding day in nineteenth-century Pennsylvania. The premiere was in the small auditorium of the Library of Congress in October 1944, with thirteen players in the pit, Graham dancing and a set by Isamu Noguchi. Graham took the title from a Hart Crane poem after the music was finished; Copland always pointed out that it has nothing to do with spring.","Simple Gifts is the only borrowed tune in the score, taken from a published collection of Shaker songs and virtually unknown before this ballet made it a national possession. What makes it sound like landscape is not the tune but the spacing: Copland separates the notes of his chords by several octaves, so the harmony has holes in it, and the ear reads the holes as distance and air.","It won the Pulitzer Prize in 1945 and was immediately used as evidence that Copland had abandoned modernism for accessibility, a charge that shadowed him for the rest of his life. The thirteen-instrument original was neglected for decades in favour of the 1945 orchestral suite; it is now often preferred for its dryness and clarity. Graham's original choreography is still danced.","Hear the thirteen-player version first, for the clarinet's bare opening statement, then the orchestral suite for the full hymn. Count the variations and note which instruments take the tune each time. Compare with Vaughan Williams's English folk-song settings, which do something similar with the same devotion and a different landscape."],"copland-fanfare-common-man":["Three minutes for brass and three percussion instruments, and nothing else: no strings, no woodwind. Timpani, bass drum and tam-tam strike first, alone. Then a single trumpet line, unaccompanied, built from wide leaps rather than steps. Horns join in harmony, then trombones and tuba, each entry separated by more drum strokes, the texture thickening one layer at a time until the full brass sounds the theme in a final blaze. The silences between the phrases are as long as the phrases.","Eugene Goossens, conducting in Cincinnati, asked eighteen American composers for fanfares to open his wartime concerts, following a British precedent from the previous war. Copland's arrived in the summer of 1942 and Goossens premiered it the following March, on the day American income tax was due, at Copland's own suggestion. The title came from a 1942 speech by Vice-President Henry Wallace announcing a century of the common man.","Copland reused the whole fanfare four years later as the opening of the finale of his Third Symphony, where the tune first steals in quietly on flutes before the brass reclaim it, so the famous version turns out to be the second half of an argument. Notice too that the drum strokes are not a march rhythm: their spacing is irregular, so the brass entries never land where the ear has learned to expect them.","It was adopted almost at once for state occasions and has since carried sporting events, space launches and a 1977 rock album, a ubiquity that has cost it critical respect. The politics have not aged into neutrality either: Copland was questioned by McCarthy's subcommittee in 1953 and a performance of his Lincoln Portrait was dropped from the Eisenhower inaugural, the common man having become a suspect phrase.","Listen to what the percussion does in the gaps, which is where the piece's strangeness lives. Then hear the finale of the Third Symphony to catch the tune walking in on flutes before the brass arrive. A recording made in a large, resonant hall suits it; a dry studio sound makes it merely loud."],"copland-rodeo-hoe-down":["The last of the ballet's five numbers, three and a half minutes of barn dance. The orchestra impersonates a country fiddle band: strings scrub open-string drones, a piano thumps like a saloon upright, woodblock and drum keep the boots moving. The tune is a straight fiddle reel, repeated with changing scoring rather than developed. Twice the whole band stops dead in its tracks, silence, and then starts again, which is the oldest trick in dance music and still works.","Agnes de Mille choreographed it for the Ballet Russe de Monte Carlo and danced the lead herself at the Metropolitan Opera in October 1942: a ranch tomboy who cannot get anyone to dance with her until the Saturday night social. Copland had already written Billy the Kid four years earlier and joked about being asked for a second cowboy ballet. The premiere reportedly drew more than twenty curtain calls.","The main tune is not a generic reel but a specific recording: Bonaparte's Retreat as played by the Kentucky fiddler William Stepp and collected for the Library of Congress in 1937. Stepp's version is idiosyncratic, straightening the old tune into an even, driving rhythm, and Copland took it over almost note for note, so what the world hears as American orchestral music is one fiddler's personal variant.","A hit from the first performance and a fixture ever since, which a 1990s beef advertising campaign made almost impossible to hear fresh. Musicologists working on the sources have shown just how much of Copland's American sound is direct quotation, which complicates the story of a composer inventing a national idiom and gives more credit to the collectors and fiddlers he drew on.","Find William Stepp's 1937 field recording online and play it first; the effect on the Copland is permanent. Listen for the two full stops. Then play Rodeo's Corral Nocturne, three minutes of quiet emptiness, to hear the other half of the same score and the same composer."],"copland-quiet-city":["Ten slow minutes in one continuous movement. Strings lay down quiet, widely spaced chords that barely move. Over them a trumpet plays short phrases that sound like signals heard from streets away, and a cor anglais answers with longer, more troubled lines that wind and double back. The two soloists never really meet. There is no climax that resolves anything; the trumpet's last call is left hanging and the strings simply stop holding it up.","It began as incidental music for Irwin Shaw's play Quiet City, about a young trumpeter walking a sleeping New York at night while his brother, who has changed his name and prospered, hears the horn and cannot stop hearing it. The Group Theatre gave it two try-out performances in 1939 and abandoned it. Copland kept the music, rescored it for these three forces, and it was first played in this form in early 1941.","The trumpet is a character, not an instrument: the phrases are written as signals blown across distance, which is why they are short, unaccompanied and never developed. Copland asks for a straight tone with little vibrato so it sounds like a real horn in a real street. The cor anglais line answering it carries the rise-and-fall shape of Jewish cantillation, the brother's conscience singing in the voice of what he left.","The play is forgotten and the ten minutes survive, which Copland found funny. It is often called his most Ives-like score, and it has become a standard memorial piece in America since 2001, generally programmed without any reference to the play. Conductors disagree over whether to place the trumpet offstage, which strengthens the distance and weakens the dialogue.","Listen late, in a quiet room, and follow the trumpet rather than the strings. Notice that the two soloists answer each other but never agree. Then play Ives's The Unanswered Question, which poses almost the same situation with almost the same instrument, and hear how differently an American asked the question thirty years earlier."],"copland-el-salon-mexico":["Eleven minutes in one movement, built as a chain of Mexican popular tunes rather than an argument. A blaring trumpet opens it; a long, wandering clarinet solo takes over; the beat keeps changing length so the dance stumbles just when you have learned to move to it. The middle slows into something sultry and slightly worn out, and then the tunes come back and race to a hard finish. The orchestra spends much of the piece imitating a dance band, shouts included.","Carlos Chavez invited Copland to Mexico in 1932, and Copland spent an evening at a Mexico City dance hall of this name, a three-room establishment with a room for each class of clientele and signs asking patrons not to drop cigarette ends on the floor because the women danced barefoot. He worked on the piece for four years. Chavez conducted the premiere with the Orquesta Sinfonica de Mexico in 1937.","Nearly every phrase can be traced to two printed collections of Mexican folk songs, and Copland could name his sources when asked. What he added was rhythmic sabotage: he shortens and lengthens the bars so the regular dance patterns keep tripping, a device he took straight into Rodeo and Billy the Kid. The tunes are borrowed; the stumble is his, and the stumble is what makes it sound modern.","Copland cared more about the Mexican reception than the American one and got it: the premiere audience approved. Later critics have called the piece tourism, and defenders point out that the borrowing was openly declared. It is also the hinge in his career, the first work in the plain, popular style that his 1920s admirers regarded as a betrayal and his later audience as an arrival.","Follow the long clarinet solo, which is the piece's best invention, then listen again for the moments the beat trips and count how often it happens. Compare with Chavez's own Sinfonia india, and with Gershwin's Cuban Overture, which came out of a similar holiday four years earlier."],"copland-clarinet-concerto-first-movement":["The first of two movements joined by a cadenza, about eight minutes. It is a slow song in a gently rocking triple meter: the clarinet spins one long, wide-spaced melody over strings, harp and piano, and simply keeps spinning it. There is no second subject, no development, no drama. The phrases lengthen and shorten against the accompaniment's regular sway so the line never quite lines up with its own pulse, and the movement drifts straight into the cadenza.","Benny Goodman commissioned it for two thousand dollars and two years of exclusive performance rights. Copland wrote much of it while travelling in South America in 1947 and finished it in 1949; Goodman asked for some of the highest and hardest passages to be eased before the broadcast premiere with the NBC Symphony under Fritz Reiner in November 1950. Jerome Robbins turned it into a ballet, The Pied Piper, the following year.","There is no other wind instrument in the piece at all, so the soloist is the only breath in the room, and the harp and piano exist to give the strings an edge the clarinet can cut through. The rocking accompaniment and the melody are deliberately out of phase, which is why a movement with no events still feels unsettled. The jazz that everyone remembers arrives only after the cadenza, in the finale.","The passages Goodman had simplified were restored from the manuscript in later editions, so recordings now differ audibly in their hardest bars, and players take sides. Critics have often called the two halves mismatched, a Boulanger-trained song followed by a Goodman-trained romp. Clarinettists do not care: they rank it with Mozart's and Nielsen's concertos, of which there are not many rivals.","Notice, early, that no other wind instrument ever plays, and the movement's loneliness explains itself. Let the long tune go by without waiting for it to return. Then follow the cadenza into the finale for the shock of the jazz. Goodman's own recording against a modern one with the restored passages makes the argument audible."],"rodrigo-concierto-aranjuez-adagio":["Eleven minutes, the middle movement of a twenty-two minute concerto. The guitar sets up a slow rocking arpeggio and the cor anglais lays a long descending melody over it, one of the most immediately recognisable tunes in music. The guitar then takes the melody and the two instruments trade it, each return more ornamented than the last, until the whole orchestra breaks out in a single anguished statement. What follows is a broken, hesitant guitar cadenza and then the tune, whispered, gone.","Rodrigo wrote it in Paris in 1939, the year the Spanish Civil War ended and the next war began, and returned with it to Spain. Blind since a diphtheria epidemic took his sight at three, he composed at a Braille writing frame and dictated the score. It was written for Regino Sainz de la Maza, who gave the premiere in Barcelona in November 1940. The title points at the gardens of the Bourbon palace at Aranjuez, south of Madrid.","For decades the official story was gardens, fountains and birdsong. Late in life Rodrigo said the Adagio came out of the loss of his and Victoria Kamhi's first pregnancy and his own near-fatal illness at the same time, so the lament had a private cause hidden behind a royal one. Listen also to the scoring: the orchestra is kept so thin that the quietest instrument on the platform is never covered, an act of engineering as much as of taste.","It made Rodrigo's name and became the most played Spanish work of the century, which has attracted the usual charge of sentimentality, not helped by a thousand arrangements. The Miles Davis and Gil Evans version on Sketches of Spain in 1960 brought both fame and a legal quarrel over rights. Guitarists still argue about amplification, which solves the balance problem and changes the sound.","Let the cor anglais have the tune first, then follow how the guitar answers each phrase rather than repeating it. Hear the whole concerto so the slow movement carries the weight the outer movements build for it. Then set a guitarist's recording beside the Miles Davis version and hear a lament become a trumpet solo."],"rodrigo-fantasia-para-un-gentilhombre":["Four movements, about twenty-two minutes, for guitar and a deliberately small orchestra. A courtly villano and a ricercare open it; then an espanoleta whose grave tune is interrupted by a fanfare of the Naples cavalry, trumpets answering across the ensemble; then a stamping dance of the axes; then a fast canary dance to finish. The scoring is light throughout, wind soloists rather than sections, so the guitar leads instead of struggling. The old dance rhythms are kept intact.","Andres Segovia, who had turned down the chance to premiere the Aranjuez, asked Rodrigo for a concerto of his own, and this was the answer: the gentleman of the title is Segovia himself. He gave the first performance in San Francisco in March 1958. By then Rodrigo was Spain's official composer in all but name, holding a chair created for him in Madrid, and writing music that looked back past the nineteenth century entirely.","Every theme comes from one book: the guitar tutor published by Gaspar Sanz in 1674, which Rodrigo had studied closely. Even the cavalry fanfare is Sanz's. He keeps the strummed seventeenth-century rhythms exactly as they stand and adds only harmony, so the piece is genuinely double-dated: baroque in its bones, twentieth-century in its colouring, with no attempt to disguise the seam.","Overshadowed by the Aranjuez and quietly preferred by many guitarists, because the lighter orchestration never fights the soloist and the writing lies better under the hand. Scholars group it with Stravinsky's Pulcinella and Respighi's Ancient Airs as part of the century's long conversation with old music. Segovia recorded it more than once and his tempi remain the reference point.","Start with the third movement, the dance of the axes, which is the most immediately gripping. Then find a recording of Sanz's originals on a baroque guitar, two minutes each, and hear how little Rodrigo changed. Respighi's Ancient Airs and Dances makes the obvious comparison from another country."],"rodrigo-concierto-aranjuez-allegro-con-spirito":["The opening movement, six bright minutes. The guitar begins alone with a strummed pattern that will not settle: the beats group in twos and then in threes, alternating, so the music dances with a permanent limp. The orchestra picks the pattern up and soloist and band chase each other through cheerful material, the tunes always short and dance-shaped. There is no cadenza and very little display; the guitar's job here is rhythm at least as much as melody.","Rodrigo had studied in Paris with Paul Dukas alongside the circle around Manuel de Falla, and this is the piece with which he came home. He described wanting the fragrance of magnolias, birdsong and fountains, images of the eighteenth-century Bourbon gardens at Aranjuez, and the sound world of the outer movements is exactly that: bright, formal, open-air. He never played the guitar, and worked with his soloist on what the instrument could do.","The alternating beat groups are an old Spanish device, the pattern that underlies the zapateado and much flamenco, and Rodrigo makes it the engine of the whole movement rather than a decoration. Notice also how thin the orchestra is: for long stretches only one or two lines play at a time, which is why a nylon-strung guitar can hold its own against a full symphony orchestra without amplification.","Usually treated as the curtain-raiser to the Adagio, which is unfair, and the movement most often shortened in arrangements. Guitarists rate it the hardest of the three to bring off, because the quiet strumming has to lead an orchestra that cannot hear it well. That balance problem, and whether to amplify, is the concerto's permanent practical argument in the concert hall.","Try to tap along and notice where the beat changes shape under you; the limp is the point. Listen for how often the orchestra is reduced to a single line. Then play the third movement, which uses the same trick faster and lighter, and finally the Adagio, so the concerto's shape registers."],"rodrigo-invocacion-y-danza":["Nine minutes for guitar alone, in two joined halves. The invocation is free and unmeasured: sustained chords, long silences, strummed flourishes that flare and die, a player thinking aloud. Out of it a dance emerges, at first hesitant, then gathering pace and insistence, with the flamenco guitarist's rasgueado and percussive strokes worked into concert writing. Fragments of familiar tunes keep surfacing and vanishing. The end flares up and stops without warning.","Rodrigo wrote it in 1961 for an international guitar competition run by French radio, which it won, and subtitled it a homage to Manuel de Falla. Falla, the composer Rodrigo most admired and the presiding figure of Spanish music, had died in Argentine exile in 1946, having refused to return while Franco ruled. Rodrigo, who had made his own accommodation with that regime, was paying a complicated debt.","The fragments are quotations, and they are all Falla: the miller's dance from The Three-Cornered Hat, gestures from El amor brujo, and, most pointedly, Falla's Homenaje pour le tombeau de Debussy, the only piece Falla ever wrote for guitar, itself a memorial. So the homage is a memorial quoting a memorial, and a listener who knows Falla hears an elegy where everyone else hears a Spanish dance.","Many guitarists consider it Rodrigo's finest work for the instrument and harder than anything in the concertos, and it is much less known simply because there is no orchestra to carry it into the concert hall. Because he dictated rather than wrote, editions differ over fingerings and occasionally over notes, and performers make choices that amount to small acts of editing.","Play Falla's Homenaje first, four minutes, so the quotations register when they appear. Then listen for the long silence before the dance starts, which most players stretch further than the page suggests. Recordings by Pepe Romero or David Russell show two quite different views of how free the invocation should be."],"walton-belshazzar-s-feast-finale":["The last ten minutes of a thirty-five minute cantata. The baritone narrates the writing on the wall and the feast; then, after a moment of near silence, the whole chorus hurls out a single word, Slain, and the piece detonates. What follows is an exultant hymn of celebration by the captive Israelites, choral blocks answering each other, hammering syncopations, and two extra brass bands placed on either side of the orchestra throwing fanfares across the hall, ending in an enormous Alleluia.","The BBC commissioned a small choral work for modest forces. Walton, then twenty-nine and known mainly as the Sitwells' protege, wrote something so large that the BBC handed it on to the Leeds Festival, where Malcolm Sargent conducted the premiere in October 1931. Osbert Sitwell assembled the text from Daniel, Psalm 137 and Isaiah. Thomas Beecham is said to have advised adding the brass bands, since Walton would never hear the piece again anyway.","Everything hinges on a gap. The writing on the wall is delivered by a small unaccompanied semi-chorus in a hush that lasts only seconds, and the silence after it is the loudest thing in the score, because the next sound is the full chorus on that one word. Listen too for the lament by the waters of Babylon, from earlier in the piece, returning transformed into the material of the final rejoicing.","The premiere astonished a festival audience raised on Elgar's oratorios, and several cathedrals refused the work as unfit for consecrated buildings, keeping it out of the Three Choirs Festival for a quarter of a century. Critics accused Walton of vulgarity and of borrowing from Bloch and Stravinsky. It is now the piece by which British choral societies measure themselves, and the vulgarity is counted an asset.","Have the text in front of you for the last ten minutes. Wait for the silence before the word Slain and do not adjust the volume beforehand. Look for a recording with the antiphonal brass bands genuinely separated in space, Previn's or Walton's own, since a compressed recording throws away the piece's main effect."],"walton-crown-imperial":["A ceremonial march of about eight minutes in the English pattern: a brisk, syncopated opening whose accents fall slightly off the beat, then a broad singing tune for strings which returns with organ and full orchestra underneath it, then the quick music again, then the big tune once more with bells. It is designed for a procession, so the sections are built to be repeated or cut on the day depending on how long the walking takes.","The BBC commissioned it for the coronation of Edward VIII, planned for May 1937. Edward abdicated in December 1936, and the march was played instead at the coronation of his brother George VI in Westminster Abbey, conducted by Adrian Boult. Walton was thirty-five, the enfant terrible of English music, and this was the commission that made him an establishment composer. The title comes from a line of William Dunbar's poem in honour of the city of London.","The march is deliberately hard to walk to. Walton puts an accent just off the beat at the start of the opening theme, so the music seems to begin a fraction late and keeps tugging against the stride, which is a joke at the expense of the genre he was being paid to supply. The score's repeat structure is practical rather than musical, written so an organist can spin it out while a procession is still moving.","Some contemporaries read the commission as a capitulation after the jazz-age Facade and the ferocity of Belshazzar, and Walton did nothing to discourage the view, though he took the second commission too, writing Orb and Sceptre for Elizabeth II in 1953. The march has outlasted the argument, and now circulates most widely in an organ arrangement played at weddings by people who have never heard the orchestral version.","Listen for the second appearance of the big tune, when the organ comes in underneath, which is the moment the piece is built for. Then compare with Elgar's Pomp and Circumstance March No. 1 and notice what Walton keeps, the shape, and what he sharpens, the rhythm. An abbey recording with real bells helps."],"walton-viola-concerto-first-movement":["A concerto that opens slowly, which was unusual then and still surprises. The orchestra gives a few quiet bars and the viola enters almost at once with a long, drooping melody in its dark lower register, unhurried and unaccompanied by any orchestral competition. About eight minutes, alternating that melancholy song with a faster, more agitated middle section, and returning at the end to where it began. The soloist is rarely asked to be brilliant and never asked to be loud.","Beecham suggested Walton write for Lionel Tertis, the man who had spent his career arguing that the viola was a solo instrument. Tertis sent the score back by return of post, saying he could not understand it. Paul Hindemith, a professional violist himself, took the premiere in London in October 1929 with Walton conducting. Tertis later apologised in print and took the concerto into his repertoire.","The opening melody's shape, a rise followed by a long sag, returns at the very end of the concerto's finale over a quiet repeating bass, so the work closes by going back to its first phrase rather than by resolving anything. The 1962 revision is not cosmetic: Walton thinned the orchestra considerably and added a harp, and the two versions balance the soloist quite differently.","Tertis's rejection is the founding story and slightly obscures the piece, which is now the cornerstone of the viola repertoire alongside Bartok's concerto. Players and conductors argue over which version to use, since Walton preferred his leaner revision while many performers want the original's weight. Frederick Riddle's 1937 recording, made with Walton conducting, is still cited as the model of unsentimental playing.","Give it the first two minutes and listen to how early the viola enters and how low it stays. Then hear a recording of the 1929 scoring against one of the 1962 revision and notice how much easier the soloist is to hear in the later one. Riddle's old recording is worth the surface noise."],"walton-spitfire-prelude-fugue":["Two linked pieces drawn from a film score, about nine minutes. The prelude is a broad ceremonial march with a striding brass tune of the kind Walton could write in his sleep and never did carelessly. The fugue is the surprise: a fast, chattering subject entering in one string section after another in strict imitation, accelerating and multiplying, until the prelude's big tune cuts across it in long notes and the two run together to the end.","It comes from The First of the Few, Leslie Howard's 1942 film about R. J. Mitchell, who designed the Spitfire and died before it flew. Walton was writing propaganda scores rather than serving, and produced several of them; he made the concert version at once. The fugue accompanies the sequence in which the aircraft is built, and the film's audiences in 1942 were watching a machine they had reason to care about.","The fugue is a real fugue, not film-music decoration: the entries follow the rules, and they were chosen because a factory floor is exactly that, one machine starting, then another, then another, until the whole shop is running. When the prelude's tune finally comes back over the top in long notes, that is the old Baroque device of crowning a fugue with a chorale, imported wholesale into a war film.","Long dismissed as wartime hack-work, and Walton never argued otherwise, though he took the craft seriously enough to write a genuine fugue for it. It is now regarded as among the best British film music, and the prelude has become a standard item at remembrance concerts. The original film tracks were unavailable for decades until reconstructions in the 1990s allowed comparison with the concert suite.","Follow the fugue's entries one section at a time; it is the clearest fugue you will hear outside Bach. Then watch the film sequence it was written for and see how exactly the music fits the assembly line. Walton's Henry V music, two years later, makes the obvious next step."],"khachaturian-sabre-dance":["Two and a half minutes that never let go. A hammering tune in xylophone and high woodwind runs flat out over a stamping accompaniment, the same figure driven round and round with no development at all. Halfway through everything drops away for a slower middle: a saxophone, or the cellos, sings a long ornamented melody over a single held note in the bass. Then the hammering returns, faster if the conductor dares, and finishes in a shout.","It was an afterthought. The theatre asked for one more dance for the last act of the ballet Gayane the day before the dress rehearsal, and Khachaturian wrote it overnight, complaining as he went. Gayane premiered in December 1942 in Perm, where the Kirov company had been evacuated as the war ran on; the plot concerns an Armenian collective farm, loyalty and sabotage, and the sword dance closes the celebrations.","The middle section is the real music: the held bass note under the melody imitates the drone that accompanies the Armenian duduk, and the tune above it uses the wide-stepped scale of Caucasian folk playing. The famous outer sections never change key or harmony, which is precisely why they work as a loop in cartoons, circuses and ice rinks without anyone noticing where the music restarts.","It became an accidental global hit, reaching the American popular charts in 1948 in a dance-band arrangement, and Khachaturian, who won every Soviet honour going, spent decades irritated at being known for one noisy encore. The 1948 denunciation of him for formalism did nothing to slow it down. Modern conductors sometimes pull the tempo back, which restores the dance and disappoints the audience.","Listen for the saxophone or cello tune in the middle, which most people have never consciously heard. Then play the whole Gayane suite, especially the Lezginka, to hear this piece in its proper company. A Soviet-era recording under the composer takes it slower than you expect, and it is worth knowing why."],"khachaturian-spartacus-adagio":["The love duet at the centre of the ballet, about nine minutes. A melody starts low in the cellos and begins to climb, and essentially never stops climbing: it rises by steps, is handed upward through the orchestra, gathers strings and then brass in unison, and arrives at a full-orchestra statement that has been delayed so long the release is almost physical. The harmony underneath moves slowly and deliberately holds the tune back. Then it subsides to where it began.","Written between 1950 and 1954 for the Bolshoi on the slave revolt against Rome in 73 BC, a subject the Soviet authorities were happy to approve, since the rebellion is against an empire safely two thousand years dead. Leonid Yakobson choreographed the Leningrad premiere in December 1956; Yuri Grigorovich's Bolshoi production of 1968 is the version that travelled, and Khachaturian collected a Lenin Prize along the way.","The endless ascent is an illusion built from repetition: one short rising figure returns again and again at higher pitches, without being developed at all, and the effect of growth comes from the scoring and the accumulating volume rather than from the material. British listeners of a certain age know the tune from a 1970s television series about a shipping line, which has permanently attached a sea voyage to a Roman slave revolt.","Western modernists dismissed it as film music before the fact, and its lush unison string writing is still cited as the sound of official Soviet art. Grigorovich reordered and cut the score so heavily that the ballet as staged is rarely the work Khachaturian assembled, a fact that irritated him. The Adagio has escaped the ballet entirely and now survives mostly on concert programmes and film soundtracks.","Count how many times the same rising figure comes back before the climax arrives; the answer explains the piece. Listen to the whole Spartacus suite for context, then compare with the balcony scene from Prokofiev's Romeo and Juliet, which reaches for the same effect with far more harmonic invention."],"khachaturian-masquerade-waltz":["Four minutes, and it begins already at speed, as though a door has opened onto a ball in progress. The main tune is in the minor, nervous and swirling, over an accompaniment that pushes rather than supports. A warmer second theme arrives, the strings sing, and then the first tune returns and grows louder without ever growing happier. It is a glittering ballroom waltz with something wrong at its centre, which is exactly what the play requires.","Incidental music for Lermontov's verse drama Masquerade, written in 1835 and staged at the Vakhtangov Theatre in Moscow in June 1941, days before the German invasion. The play turns on a masked ball, a lost bracelet, a husband's jealousy and a poisoned ice cream: the heroine dances at the ball shortly before she is killed. The waltz has to be irresistible and sinister at the same time, and Khachaturian said it was the hardest number he had to write.","Under the melody, an inner line slides steadily downward by half-steps, so the harmony is always subsiding while the dance keeps whirling, and the tune never gets to settle on a comfortable chord. That is the whole trick of the movement's double character. Once you have heard the descending line you cannot unhear it, and the waltz stops sounding merely festive.","The five-movement concert suite became a Soviet staple, and this waltz is everywhere in Russian film, television and figure skating, so much so that non-Russian listeners regularly confuse it with Shostakovich's Waltz No. 2, which does a related job with a lighter conscience. Critics have noted how easily Khachaturian wrote this in 1941 and how hard his life became after the anti-formalism campaign of 1948.","Listen twice: once for the tune, once tracking the descending line underneath it. Then put it next to Shostakovich's Waltz No. 2 and Tchaikovsky's Sleeping Beauty waltz to hear three quite different ballrooms. A Russian orchestra will take it faster and harder than a western one, which suits the play."],"khachaturian-masquerade-galop":["Two minutes flat out. A galop is a round dance in which couples run rather than step, and this one snaps: brass and percussion drive a short, sharp-elbowed tune, woodwind trills flick between the phrases, and there is a brief lyrical trio before the dash to the finish. It closes the concert suite and it is designed to leave an audience grinning, which is a legitimate thing for a piece of theatre music to do.","One of five numbers Khachaturian drew from his 1941 score for Lermontov's play into a concert suite, alongside the waltz, a nocturne, a mazurka and a romance. The dances had to sound like what a stage band would have played at a St Petersburg masked ball in the 1830s, so the composer of Armenian dance rhythms spent 1941 writing period pastiche for a Moscow theatre in the last weeks before the invasion.","The galop was a real craze of the 1820s and 1830s, so Khachaturian is imitating Lermontov's era rather than writing Soviet music, and the trio tune is deliberately banal, the sort of thing a hired band would grind out. When it returns it is scored to sound thin and cheap on purpose. Writing something knowingly second-rate and making it work is harder than it looks.","The least performed of the suite's five numbers and the most purely enjoyable, usually turning up as an encore. It has attracted almost no critical writing, which is itself revealing about how theatre music is valued. Set beside the trouble that engulfed the composer seven years later in the anti-formalism campaign, the suite's untroubled charm reads rather differently now.","Play it straight after the waltz to hear the same ballroom from the other side of the room, one dance for the guests and one for the servants' half hour. Compare with Kabalevsky's Comedians' Galop, which does the same job in the same decade. Any Russian orchestra recording of the full suite will do."],"khachaturian-violin-concerto-first-movement":["Fifteen minutes that begin with the orchestra stamping out a rhythm and the violin entering almost immediately, without ceremony, on a wiry dance-shaped tune. A second theme follows in the violin's low register, slow and turning back on itself like a folk song, over a held bass note. The middle builds through increasingly athletic writing to a long cadenza, and the return drives hard to the end. The soloist plays almost continuously throughout.","Khachaturian wrote it in about two months in the summer of 1940 at a composers' retreat outside Moscow, for David Oistrakh, who premiered it that November with the Moscow Philharmonic and made it a signature piece. It won a Stalin Prize the following year. He was then at the centre of Soviet musical life; eight years later he was denounced alongside Shostakovich and Prokofiev for formalism and had to recant in public.","None of the folk-sounding tunes is a quotation. They are his own, built on the scale patterns and the ornamented drone texture of the ashugh minstrels he grew up hearing in Tbilisi, which is why they sound authentic to listeners who cannot name a single Armenian song. And most recordings do not play his cadenza: Oistrakh wrote an alternative that many soloists prefer, so the concerto's showpiece is often not by the composer.","An immediate Soviet success and a fixture of Oistrakh's touring repertoire, while western critics have generally called it colourful and repetitive rather than searching. Jean-Pierre Rampal made a flute transcription with Khachaturian's blessing, which gave the piece a second career and a second constituency. The 1948 decree condemned the composer's other works while leaving this one comfortably in the repertoire.","Notice how early the violin enters, within the first half minute, and how little the orchestra prepares the ground. Check which cadenza is being played. Oistrakh's 1950s recording against a modern one shows how much the performing tradition has changed, and the flute version is worth a hearing for sheer curiosity."],"khachaturian-gayane-dance-rose-maidens":["Three minutes of light, quick dancing. A fluttering tune in the woodwind circles a narrow set of notes, each phrase ending with a sharp little flick, over a plucked-string accompaniment that keeps a steady strum going underneath. A broader middle section lets the strings sing briefly, and then the fluttering returns and the dance ends neatly. Everything is scaled down: no brass weight, no percussion battery, just the top of the orchestra moving quickly.","From the same wartime ballet as the Sabre Dance, set on an Armenian cotton-growing collective farm, first staged in December 1942 by an evacuated company in the Urals. The maidens' dance belongs to the harvest scenes, images of an abundant and orderly countryside offered to audiences in the third winter of the war. Khachaturian recycled a good deal of music into Gayane from an earlier ballet of his, Happiness, from 1939.","The flick at the end of each phrase is a specific piece of Armenian folk practice, the ornament a zurna player adds to close a line, transcribed for oboe and flute. The plucked accompaniment imitates the rapid strumming of the kanun. And the tune stays inside one narrow scale from beginning to end, so the whole dance holds still harmonically while appearing to be in constant motion.","Permanently overshadowed by the Sabre Dance from the same suite, and often used as the palate cleanser before it. There is a genuine practical muddle around it: Khachaturian assembled three different Gayane suites with different contents, so a record labelled Gayane suite may or may not contain this dance at all, and concert programmes are routinely inaccurate about which one is being played.","Play it immediately before the Sabre Dance and hear the same orchestra used in two opposite ways. Follow the plucked strings rather than the tune for one hearing. Then compare with Bartok's Romanian Folk Dances, where the ornaments are transcribed from actual village players rather than composed in their style."],"shostakovich-symphony-no-5-finale":["The last of four movements in a symphony of about fifty minutes; on its own it runs some twelve minutes. It opens with a hammered march for full brass and timpani over churning strings, drives to a jagged climax, then drains away into a long quiet stretch where a solo horn and harp hold the air almost motionless. From there the music grinds slowly upward until trumpets and trombones plant a blazing major chord, and the timpani beat the same note over and over to the final bar.","Shostakovich wrote the Fifth in the summer of 1937, at the height of Stalin's Great Terror and eighteen months after Pravda savaged his opera Lady Macbeth of the Mtsensk District under the headline 'Muddle Instead of Music'. Colleagues and relatives were being arrested; he kept a packed bag by the door. The premiere in Leningrad on 21 November 1937 under Yevgeny Mravinsky brought an ovation said to have lasted half an hour. A journalist's phrase, 'a Soviet artist's reply to just criticism', was attached to the score and stuck.","In the quiet centre the strings quote Shostakovich's own setting of Pushkin's 'Rebirth', written months earlier: a poem about a crude overpainting flaking away to reveal the original picture underneath. The ending's speed is the whole argument. Taken at the slow pulse written in the score, the repeated brass note sounds like a salute performed under duress; taken fast it sounds like victory. Conductors still choose sides.","Officially the Fifth restored its composer to favour, yet listeners at the premiere reportedly wept, hearing grief where the authorities heard affirmation. The memoir Testimony, published in 1979, claimed Shostakovich meant the close as forced rejoicing; its authenticity is contested. Scholars now argue less about whether the music is double-edged than about how deliberately he controlled that doubleness, and how much later politics has read itself into the notes.","Start at the hushed middle, where the texture thins to a solo horn and a harp; everything afterwards is the climb back out. Then hear the last two minutes twice, once in Mravinsky's driving Soviet-era account and once in a slower modern reading such as Bernstein's or Haitink's, and decide which ending you believe."],"shostakovich-waltz-no-2":["Four minutes of dance-band waltz. An alto saxophone sings the tune over a swaying oom-pah-pah, trombones answer, and the melody comes round again and again, each time in a new colour and a little louder, before the band fades out. Nothing develops and nothing is argued; the whole pleasure is in the tune returning dressed differently. The scoring is a Soviet variety orchestra, the sound of a park bandstand or an evening radio broadcast.","Shostakovich wrote constantly for film, theatre and light ensembles, partly for money and partly because such music was politically safe ground. This waltz belongs to the Suite for Variety Orchestra, assembled in the mid-1950s from earlier scores including his music for the 1956 film The First Echelon. Soviet cultural policy actively promoted well-made light music for parks, dance halls and radio, and ensembles of exactly this make-up played it across the country.","For decades the piece was sold as the second waltz of Jazz Suite No. 2 — a work whose full score had in fact been lost in the war and was only partly recovered, as a piano reduction, in 1999. The mislabelling stuck because a popular recording used it. Listen to the second statement of the tune: the melody moves into the strings while the saxophone keeps a countermelody running underneath.","Critics have never settled on it. Some hear charm and nothing more, the sound of official Soviet good cheer; others hear a chill under the sweetness, a waltz smiling too broadly for comfort. Its afterlife decided the matter commercially when Stanley Kubrick set it over the opening of Eyes Wide Shut in 1999, after which it became the most-played music its composer ever wrote.","Play it once for pleasure, then again following only the bass and drums, which never vary — that is why the melody's changing costume registers so strongly. Compare a silky Western orchestral recording with a Russian band version, which is rougher, brighter and closer to what a park orchestra would actually have sounded like."],"shostakovich-string-quartet-no-8":["Five movements running without a break for about twenty minutes. It begins with the cello alone tracing a four-note figure that the others take up in slow imitation; a violent fast movement follows, then a bitter waltz, then a movement broken by three hammer blows, and finally a return to the opening lament. The four notes are present in almost every bar, sometimes as melody, sometimes buried in an accompaniment, so the whole work sounds like one thought circling.","Shostakovich composed it in three days in July 1960 while staying at Gohrisch, near Dresden, having been taken to see the city's ruins for a film about the bombing. He had just been pressed into joining the Communist Party, a capitulation that appalled him. The published dedication reads 'to the victims of fascism and war'; in a private letter he said he had written it for himself, and told a friend it was his own memorial. The Beethoven Quartet gave the premiere in Leningrad that October.","The four-note figure spells his initials in German note-names, D–S–C–H. Around it he assembles quotations from his own First, Fifth, Eighth and Tenth symphonies, the Second Piano Trio, the First Cello Concerto and Lady Macbeth, plus the old revolutionary song 'Tormented by grievous bondage' and, in the third bar of the fourth movement, the funeral music from Wagner's Götterdämmerung. It is an autobiography assembled from evidence.","Presented officially as an anti-fascist memorial, it was accepted as such and widely performed. The private reading — a suicide note in music, written under duress after joining the Party — surfaced only when his letters were published, and now dominates programme notes. Some scholars warn against replacing one simple story with another; the quartet's own quotations refuse to settle into a single meaning.","Learn the four notes first: sing the opening cello line and you will hear it return everywhere, including inside the fastest music. Then listen for the three repeated chords in the fourth movement, often described as knocks at the door. A quartet with a hard, dry sound suits it better than a warm one; the Borodin Quartet's recordings are the classic reference."],"shostakovich-symphony-no-7-leningrad-first-movement":["Nearly half an hour of music in three panels. A broad, confident opening theme for strings gives way to a quiet pastoral, and then a side drum begins a tapping rhythm that never stops. Over it a jaunty little march tune is repeated a dozen times, each time louder and more thickly scored, until the whole orchestra including extra brass is screaming the same tune. The music collapses, and a long grieving aftermath follows, with a bassoon solo over sparse strings.","Shostakovich began the symphony in Leningrad in the summer of 1941 as German forces closed in, serving in the conservatory fire brigade and photographed in a fireman's helmet for the cover of Time. He was evacuated and finished the score in Kuibyshev, where it was premiered on 5 March 1942. The score was flown out on microfilm to the West. On 9 August 1942, in the besieged and starving city, an assembled orchestra of survivors under Karl Eliasberg played it and it was broadcast over loudspeakers towards the German lines.","The march tune is close enough to 'Da geh' ich zu Maxim' from Lehár's The Merry Widow that the resemblance is hard to unhear, and Bartók mocked the whole passage in his Concerto for Orchestra two years later. Shostakovich also said the theme was not a portrait of the enemy alone but of anything that crushes people, and friends reported he had begun sketching the material before the invasion.","In 1942 it was a propaganda triumph, played more than sixty times in America in a single season, with Toscanini and Stokowski competing for the premiere. The backlash followed fast: critics called the invasion episode crude and endless. Rehabilitation came with the argument, pressed hardest by the memoirist Solomon Volkov, that the terror portrayed is domestic as much as German — a claim still argued over.","Time the invasion episode and notice how little changes except volume and instrumentation; it is a lesson in orchestration disguised as a battle. Then compare it with Ravel's Bolero, built the same way for opposite ends. Bernstein's Chicago recording is deliberately enormous; the wartime Eliasberg story is worth reading before any listening."],"shostakovich-piano-concerto-no-2-andante":["The slow middle movement of a three-movement concerto, about seven minutes long and scored for piano and strings only. Muted strings lay out a calm, hymn-like theme; the piano enters underneath with slow rocking chords, then takes the melody itself in octaves while the strings hold long notes. It rises once to a full-voiced climax and subsides. There are no fireworks and almost no dissonance — unusual enough in this composer to feel like a deliberate withholding.","Shostakovich wrote the concerto in 1957 for his son Maxim, then nineteen, who played the premiere in Moscow that May as part of his conservatory graduation. It is a father's practical gift: brilliant but playable, full of scale exercises turned into jokes in the outer movements. The year also brought a thaw in Soviet cultural policy after Stalin's death, and the composer allowed himself lightness he had not risked in a decade.","The movement's shape is borrowed from the nineteenth century rather than his own idiom — the string introduction and the piano's rocking left hand recall Romantic slow movements of the Rachmaninoff kind, an inheritance Soviet criticism officially despised. Notice that the piano never plays alone against the orchestra in dialogue; it accompanies the strings for its first minute, entering as a subordinate voice rather than a soloist.","Shostakovich was dismissive of the concerto, writing that it had no redeeming artistic merit — a judgement almost nobody shares. Critics have long treated the outer movements as a slight if delightful entertainment, while this Andante travels separately as a recital encore. Its appearance in Disney's Fantasia 2000 introduced it to millions who had no idea whose music they were hearing.","Listen for the moment the piano stops accompanying and takes the tune in both hands at once — that is the movement's single event. Then hear it beside the composer's Eighth Quartet from three years later to grasp how wide his range of private voices was. Maxim Shostakovich's own recordings carry obvious authority."],"shostakovich-symphony-no-10-second-movement":["Four minutes of unbroken violence, and the shortest movement in a symphony lasting nearly an hour. Strings tear through fast repeated figures while brass punch out a march that never relaxes; woodwind shriek at the top of their range, the xylophone rattles, and the whole orchestra plays close to its limit throughout. There is no second subject worth the name, no slow passage and no let-up: it begins at full speed and stops, rather than ending.","The Tenth appeared in 1953, months after Stalin's death, and was premiered by the Leningrad Philharmonic under Yevgeny Mravinsky that December. Shostakovich had written no symphony for eight years, since the 1948 Zhdanov decree condemned him and most of his colleagues for formalism. The new symphony was debated for three days at the Union of Soviet Composers, a sign that nobody was yet sure what could be said in public.","The memoir Testimony calls this movement a musical portrait of Stalin — a claim unverifiable and widely repeated. What is verifiable is the finale's obsessive use of the composer's D–S–C–H initials, and the horn call in the third movement spelling the name of his student Elmira Nazirova. Set beside those, the scherzo's refusal to name anything at all begins to sound like a decision.","Soviet critics in 1953 split over whether the symphony was pessimistic, that being the charge that mattered. Western opinion has ranked it among the strongest of the cycle almost from the start. The portrait-of-Stalin reading now dominates concert programmes and irritates many scholars, who point out that the music was largely drafted before the dictator died.","Play it loud and once, straight through; it is too short to analyse on a first hearing. Then go to the finale and listen for the four-note initials hammered out by the whole orchestra, and decide whether the scherzo sounds like the same person. Mravinsky's steely Leningrad recordings remain the benchmark."],"shostakovich-festive-overture":["Six minutes of concert opener. A brass fanfare rips out unaccompanied, then the strings set off on a fast running tune that barely pauses for breath; a broad horn melody arrives as contrast, the running music returns, and the fanfare comes back over the top for a finish that can add an offstage brass band. Everything is bright, loud and quick, with clarinets and violins passing scales between them so fast that the notes blur.","Written in three days in November 1954 when the Bolshoi Theatre found itself without a suitable piece for a concert marking the anniversary of the October Revolution. A conductor is said to have waited at the composer's flat while pages were copied. Shostakovich was by then adept at supplying official occasions with exactly what they wanted, at speed, and the year following Stalin's death was a good one in which to be publicly cheerful.","The opening fanfare is close kin to the brass writing in Glinka's Ruslan and Lyudmila overture, the standard Russian model for this kind of curtain-raiser, and the speed of the string writing has the same source. Some of the material appears to have been salvaged from an abandoned overture of 1947. The offstage brass band is optional, which is why recordings differ so much in the last thirty seconds.","Nobody has ever claimed it is profound, including its composer. It is instead one of the most useful pieces in the repertoire: an orchestral showpiece that flatters good players and forgives an amateur band, which is why it opened the 1980 Moscow Olympics and turns up at school and youth-orchestra concerts everywhere. Critics who look for coded dissent in every Soviet bar have generally left it alone.","Listen for the clarinet's first entry after the fanfare, and try to follow one instrumental line rather than the mass of sound; the tune is being handed round faster than it seems. Compare a Russian orchestra's brassy, hard-edged reading with a Western one, and seek out a recording that uses the extra offstage band."],"shostakovich-piano-trio-no-2-finale":["The last of four movements, about eleven minutes long. The piano sets a dry, hopping dance figure and the violin adds a tune with the lilt and the sharp-edged intervals of Jewish folk music; the cello plays plucked notes underneath. The dance is repeated obsessively, growing harder and more mechanical, until the piano is hammering block chords. At the very end the music of the trio's first movement returns, high and pale in harmonics, and the dance freezes.","Shostakovich wrote the trio in 1944 and dedicated it to Ivan Sollertinsky, the musicologist who had been his closest friend and champion, who died suddenly that February aged forty-one. In the same months the first reports and photographs of the Nazi death camps were reaching Soviet readers, including accounts of prisoners forced to play and dance beside their own graves. He played the piano part at the Leningrad premiere that November.","The Jewish inflection is deliberate and dangerous: Shostakovich said such music could sound cheerful while being deeply sad, and he returned to it repeatedly at a time when official antisemitism was hardening in the Soviet Union. He reused this finale's theme sixteen years later as one of the self-quotations in the Eighth Quartet, so the dance became part of his own musical signature.","The premiere passed without official trouble, but the finale was cut from some later Soviet performances, and the trio disappeared from print for a period. Western critics long treated the Jewish material as local colour; scholarship since the 1990s reads it as protest and as identification, though how far a listener should hear specific camp imagery in the notes remains disputed.","Wait for the moment the piano abandons melody and simply pounds chords — the dance stops being a dance there. Then listen to the Eighth Quartet and catch the same tune returning. Look for a recording that keeps the tempo tight and unsentimental; the Beaux Arts Trio and Russian ensembles offer very different degrees of harshness."],"shostakovich-cello-concerto-no-1-first-movement":["Six minutes built almost entirely from four notes. The cello states them alone at the start, brusque and clipped, and then will not let them go: they are inverted, stretched, hammered in double stops and thrown between soloist and woodwind at high speed. The orchestra is small and dry, with no trumpets or trombones, and a single horn acts as the cello's shadow, echoing or contradicting it. The movement drives without pause to a hard stop.","Shostakovich wrote the concerto in the summer of 1959 for Mstislav Rostropovich, who learned it from memory in four days and gave the premiere in Leningrad that October under Mravinsky. The two men had known each other since Rostropovich was a conservatory student; the composer deliberately avoided telling him it was coming. It was among the first major works he wrote after the political thaw made public experiment safer again.","The four-note motto is a near relation of the composer's own D–S–C–H initials, bent out of shape, and it goes on to dominate all four movements. The horn is effectively a second soloist, an unusual arrangement Shostakovich borrowed from nowhere in particular; watch a performance and the player is often seated apart. The composer told Rostropovich that a Georgian song Stalin liked was hidden in the third movement.","Rostropovich's advocacy made it a repertory piece within a few years, and it is now the most played cello concerto written after 1945. Debate concerns tone rather than value: whether the movement's relentlessness is grim wit or something bleaker, and whether the concerto belongs to the composer's public or private voice. Its resemblance to Prokofiev's Sinfonia concertante, written for the same soloist, is often noted.","Memorise the opening four notes in the first ten seconds — after that you will hear them constantly, including inside the accompaniment. Follow the horn rather than the cello for one listening. Rostropovich's 1959 recording, made within weeks of the premiere, has an urgency later performances rarely match."],"shostakovich-gadfly-romance":["A four-minute song without words for solo violin and orchestra. Strings lay down a gently rocking accompaniment and the violin sings a long, unhurried melody that climbs in wide steps, falls back, and climbs again a little higher each time. A middle section darkens briefly before the tune returns, this time with the orchestra carrying it while the violin decorates above. Nothing is complicated: it is a straightforwardly beautiful tune, phrased like breathing.","Shostakovich wrote around three dozen film scores, and this one accompanied Alexander Faintsimmer's 1955 adaptation of Ethel Voynich's novel The Gadfly, a story of revolutionary conspiracy in nineteenth-century Italy hugely popular in the Soviet Union. Film work paid well and was politically unimpeachable, and he treated it as a craft rather than a compromise. The suite drawn from the score, assembled later by Levon Atovmyan, is how almost everyone knows the music.","The melody's shape — small rising steps repeated at successively higher pitches — is the same device Tchaikovsky used in his most famous romances, and Shostakovich, who could parody anything, plays it entirely straight here. The film's Italian setting leaves no trace in the music at all; the Romance is Russian salon melody, written for an audience that would recognise the accent immediately.","Long dismissed as light work beneath serious attention, the Gadfly music was rescued by television: the Romance became the theme of the British series Reilly, Ace of Spies in 1983 and has been a violin encore ever since. Critical opinion has since softened towards the film scores generally, which are now read as a laboratory for material that resurfaces in the symphonies and quartets.","Listen for the point where the orchestra takes the melody and the violin rises above it in decoration — the emotional peak arrives there, not at the loudest moment. Pair it with the Andante of the Second Piano Concerto, written two years later, to hear the same unguarded lyricism. Almost every violinist has recorded it; Vengerov's is a good place to start."],"shostakovich-jazz-suite-no-1-waltz":["Three minutes for a small jazz band of the 1930s: three saxophones, a couple of trumpets and a trombone, with violin, banjo, piano, double bass and a drummer. The saxophone leads a lopsided little waltz, sweet on the surface and slightly wrong underneath, with sliding trombone comments and a nasal violin countermelody. It is the first of three short movements, followed by a polka and a foxtrot, each barely longer than a dance-hall record side.","In 1934 a state commission set out to raise the quality of Soviet light music, which officials thought vulgar and imitative, and invited serious composers to write for jazz bands. Shostakovich, twenty-seven and newly famous for Lady Macbeth of the Mtsensk District, produced this suite for the competition; it was played in Leningrad that March. Jazz in the Soviet sense meant a variety band rather than an improvising ensemble, and nothing in the score is improvised.","There is no jazz here in the American sense at all: every note is written out, the harmony is Viennese, and the rhythm never swings. What Shostakovich takes from the idiom is its instruments and its knowingness. Listen to the violin's entry, deliberately thin and scratchy — a café-orchestra sound he uses elsewhere, notably in his ballets, whenever he wants sweetness to sound faintly disreputable.","The suite has been the victim of a long confusion, constantly mixed up with the later Suite for Variety Orchestra and its famous Waltz No. 2, to the point where record sleeves have printed the wrong one. Critics who value Shostakovich for his tragic voice have tended to file the jazz suites away as trivia; others hear the same ironic distance at work, just in miniature.","Play all three movements together, since they last under nine minutes, and notice how the band never once relaxes into swing. Compare it with Gershwin, working in the real idiom on the other side of the world at the same moment. Riccardo Chailly's Concertgebouw recording is polished; smaller Russian ensembles sound closer to a dance hall."],"messiaen-quartet-end-time-louange-l-eternite":["The fifth of eight movements, and one of two written for a single instrument with piano. The cello plays one enormously slow melody, marked infinitely slow and ecstatic, in its high register, while the piano underneath repeats soft chords in an even, unhurried pulse that never varies for eight minutes. The line climbs by small steps, hangs, and finally reaches a held high note that seems to have no natural end. Nothing else happens, and that is the design.","Messiaen was captured in June 1940 and held at Stalag VIII-A near Görlitz in Silesia. Among the prisoners were a violinist, a clarinettist and a cellist; a sympathetic German officer supplied paper and pencils and eventually an upright piano. The quartet was first played to several hundred prisoners and guards in the camp on 15 January 1941, in the cold, on damaged instruments. The title comes from the Book of Revelation, where an angel announces that there shall be time no longer.","The melody was not composed in the camp. Messiaen adapted it from Fête des belles eaux, a 1937 piece written for six ondes Martenot to accompany a water-and-light show at the Paris Exposition — sound designed for a fountain display, transplanted into a prison and reinterpreted as the eternity of Christ. The piano's chords are spaced so widely that the ear hears them as colour rather than harmony, which is how their synaesthetic composer described them.","The premiere's legend has grown in the telling: the often-repeated audience of five thousand was more likely a few hundred, and the cello had four strings rather than the three of popular accounts. Scholars including Rebecca Rischin have carefully unpicked the story without diminishing it. Musically the quartet is now accepted as the central chamber work of its century, and this movement, with its companion for violin, is the part most often heard alone.","Do not wait for anything to happen; let the pulse settle and follow the cello's climb note by note. Listen for the single highest note and how long the player must sustain it. Then hear Le banquet céleste, written when Messiaen was nineteen, to find the same slowness already fully formed. A recording with a genuinely quiet piano matters more than a famous cellist."],"messiaen-l-ascension-third-movement":["Five minutes of organ writing at full power, titled Outbursts of joy from a soul before the glory of Christ which is its own. It begins with jagged, jubilant figures thrown between the hands, breaks into a long passage where the feet play a rapid melody on the pedals alone, and ends with a toccata of hammered chords over which the highest stops shriek. The registration is deliberately harsh and brilliant, all reeds and mixtures, with none of the organ's usual warmth.","L'Ascension exists in two versions. Messiaen composed it first for orchestra in 1932–33, then transcribed it for organ in 1933–34 — except for this movement, which he replaced entirely with new music, since the orchestral third movement, an alleluia for trumpet and cymbal, would not transfer. He had just been appointed organist of La Trinité in Paris, a post he held for over sixty years, and was in his mid-twenties.","The rhythms are not the church music's usual regular beats but patterns Messiaen took from Indian tables of rhythmic cells, sometimes lengthened by half a value so that the pulse quietly limps. He also thought in colour: he saw specific chords as specific hues and chose registrations to match. The pedal solo in the middle is a rare thing in the repertoire, a melody for the feet alone at high speed.","Organists made it a competition and recital staple within a generation, and it remains one of the pieces by which French instruments and players are judged. Debate turns on tempo and on the choice of stops: whether to follow the specifications of the Cavaillé-Coll organ at La Trinité, which Messiaen knew intimately, or to adapt them to the instrument at hand. His own recordings settle little, since he played the piece differently each time.","Find a recording made on a French Cavaillé-Coll organ, ideally the one at La Trinité, since the reeds are what the piece is about. Listen for the moment the hands drop out and the melody continues in the pedals. Then hear the orchestral version of the other three movements to understand what the transcription gained and lost."],"messiaen-turangalila-symphonie-joie-du-sang-des":["The central movement of a ten-movement symphony lasting about eighty minutes, and its wildest six. A dance in constantly shifting rhythms hurls a brass theme forward while the solo piano crashes through it in glittering runs; tuned percussion — glockenspiel, celesta, vibraphone — rings above like a gamelan, and the ondes Martenot, an early electronic instrument, swoops over everything with a sliding, unearthly wail. It ends with the piano and the whole orchestra pounding the same chords in unison.","Serge Koussevitzky commissioned the work for the Boston Symphony Orchestra in 1945, telling Messiaen to write for whatever forces and length he wanted. He wrote a hymn to physical and cosmic love, taking his title from two Sanskrit words and his story from the legend of Tristan and Isolde. Leonard Bernstein conducted the premiere in Boston on 2 December 1949, with Yvonne Loriod, later the composer's wife, at the piano and Ginette Martenot on the instrument her brother had invented.","The joy in this movement is explicitly the joy of bodies: Messiaen described it as the union of two lovers imagined as a transformation on the scale of the stars. The rhythms are built from Indian patterns that add a fraction to themselves as they repeat, so the dance is never quite square. Four cyclic themes run through the whole symphony; the heavy brass 'statue theme' from the first movement is audible here, sped up almost past recognition.","Reactions in 1949 ranged from delight to open contempt: Pierre Boulez, Messiaen's own pupil, called the work brothel music, and its lushness embarrassed the serialist avant-garde for a generation. Audiences never had the problem, and by the 1970s Turangalîla was standard repertoire for orchestras able to afford it. Debate now concerns the ondes Martenot, since working instruments and players are scarce and substitutes are usually judged unsatisfactory.","Listen for the ondes Martenot first: once you can pick out its gliding electronic voice you will hear it everywhere in the symphony. Then follow the piano, which behaves like a percussion soloist rather than a concerto part. Try this movement before attempting the whole work; live performance is worth seeking out, since the percussion section alone is a spectacle."],"messiaen-o-sacrum-convivium":["A four-minute motet for unaccompanied choir in four parts, setting a short Latin text about the sacrament. The sopranos carry a slow, arching melody while the lower voices move beneath in rich, close chords that shift by tiny steps rather than resolving in the usual way. The dynamic stays hushed almost throughout, rising once near the words about future glory before sinking to a long, quiet Alleluia. There is no counterpoint to follow, only harmony changing colour.","Messiaen wrote it in 1937 at the request of a choirmaster, for practical use in church. He was then in his late twenties, organist at La Trinité and a founder of the group La Jeune France, which argued for a music of sincerity and spiritual seriousness against the fashionable dryness of interwar Paris. The text is an antiphon for Corpus Christi attributed to Thomas Aquinas, familiar to every Catholic congregation.","It is the only unaccompanied choral work Messiaen ever published, and the only place where his harmonic language appears stripped of the organ, the ondes Martenot and the percussion he otherwise relied on. The chords are drawn from his modes of limited transposition, scales that can only be shifted a few times before repeating themselves; the effect is music that seems to hover rather than travel toward a home note.","Church musicians adopted it quickly because it sounds simpler than it is, and it has become one of the most performed twentieth-century motets, sung by cathedral choirs and amateur groups alike. Critics occasionally note the gap between its modest liturgical purpose and the ambition of everything else Messiaen wrote in the 1930s; others treat it as the clearest short introduction to his harmony available.","Listen once for the melody and once for the bass, which moves so slowly it can be missed entirely. Notice how rarely the harmony gives you the resolution the ear expects. Compare it with the same composer's Le banquet céleste for organ, nine years earlier — the same stillness, a different instrument. Seek a choir with a straight, cool tone rather than a heavy one."],"messiaen-banquet-celeste":["Two pages of music that take six or seven minutes to play. Soft flute and string stops sustain slow chords that change roughly once a bar; above them a melody moves in single notes so far apart in time that the ear has to hold each one until the next arrives. Twice, drops of sound fall from the highest register — short detached notes over the sustained harmony. The pedals hold long low notes underneath. Nothing accelerates and nothing is repeated.","Messiaen composed it in 1928, aged nineteen, while still a student at the Paris Conservatoire, adapting it from an orchestral piece of the same period. It is his earliest published work and already carries the epigraph he took from the Gospel of John about dwelling in Christ. Five years later he was appointed to La Trinité, where he would play works like this to Sunday congregations for six decades, often improvising for far longer.","The tempo marking is extraordinarily slow, and Messiaen meant it: he attached to the score a note that the piece should convey the eternity of the divine banquet, which in practice means each chord must be allowed to decay in the church's own acoustic before the next arrives. The detached high notes were later described by the composer as drops of water, an image he reused for birdsong and for the wounds of Christ.","Early listeners found it static to the point of provocation, and organists complained that audiences could not tell whether the piece had begun. It is now recognised as the moment a nineteen-year-old announced a complete aesthetic — slowness as a spiritual argument — that he would spend sixty years elaborating. Performance debate concerns how slow is too slow, and whether large reverberant buildings help or blur the harmony.","Listen in a quiet room with the volume low and resist the urge to check how much is left. Count how many chord changes there are; the number is smaller than you expect. Then compare it with Arvo Pärt's Für Alina, written half a century later from the same instinct. A recording made in a French church rather than a studio gives the necessary echo."],"barber-adagio-strings":["Eight minutes for strings alone. A single melodic line begins in the first violins, stepping upward slowly, and is handed to the violas, then the cellos, each entry starting a little higher, so the music climbs in overlapping waves without ever settling. The rhythm stretches and contracts, refusing a steady beat. After a long accumulation the whole ensemble reaches a shattering held chord at the top of the range, then falls silent, and the opening returns quietly and stops unfinished.","Barber wrote it in 1936, aged twenty-six, as the slow movement of a string quartet composed in Austria during a stay funded by the American Prix de Rome. He arranged it for string orchestra and sent it to Arturo Toscanini, who returned the score without comment; Barber was offended until he learned the conductor had memorised it. Toscanini and the NBC Symphony broadcast the premiere on 5 November 1938 to a national radio audience.","The climax is built by withholding, not adding: the melody stops climbing several times and drops back before the final ascent, so the top note lands later than the ear predicts. The silence after it is written into the score as a full pause, longer than most conductors dare hold. The original quartet version, still occasionally played, sounds thinner and faster, and reveals how much the piece owes to the sheer weight of massed strings.","It became America's public music of mourning — broadcast on the death of Franklin Roosevelt, played after the Kennedy assassination and after the attacks of September 2001 — and its use in Oliver Stone's Platoon fixed it as a sound of grief for a further generation. Barber grew weary of it. Critics have periodically dismissed it as sentimental; performers note that the score is austere and that most of the sentiment arrives with the tempo chosen.","Listen for each new entry of the same rising line in a lower instrument, and count the false summits before the real one. Then time the silence at the top. Compare a fast, dry reading with a slow, thick one — recordings vary by several minutes — and try the original quartet version afterwards to hear the piece before it became a monument."],"barber-violin-concerto-first-movement":["About eleven minutes, and the concerto's longest movement. The violin begins immediately, with no orchestral introduction, singing a broad lyrical theme that unfolds in long phrases; a clarinet answers with a second, more wistful idea. The orchestra stays light and transparent for much of the movement, letting the soloist spin melody almost continuously, before a stormier central section builds real weight. The writing is difficult but never showy, and the movement ends quietly rather than in display.","The concerto was commissioned in 1939 by the Philadelphia industrialist Samuel Fels for his ward, the violinist Iso Briselli. Barber wrote the first two movements in Switzerland and finished the work in America after war broke out. A dispute followed over whether the finale was playable or substantial enough, and Briselli never performed it; Albert Spalding gave the premiere with the Philadelphia Orchestra under Eugene Ormandy in February 1941.","Barber wrote no cadenza — unusual for a concerto of this scale — so the soloist never stops to show off alone, and the first movement's difficulty is hidden inside the lyricism. Notice also how rarely the orchestra doubles the violin's melody; the accompaniment usually moves in a different rhythm underneath, which keeps the solo line exposed. The finale, the cause of the original quarrel, lasts under four minutes and is nothing like the first.","The commissioning row has been distorted by retelling: the story that Briselli found the finale too difficult is contradicted by surviving letters suggesting he thought it too slight. The concerto was slow to enter the repertoire, dismissed in the postwar decades as unfashionably tonal, then rediscovered from the 1980s onwards. It is now among the most recorded American concertos, and its unfashionable directness is exactly what performers cite.","Notice that the soloist plays from the first second — there is no waiting. Listen for the clarinet answering the opening theme, then track how Barber keeps the violin singing without pause. Compare the lyrical first movement with the finale's three-minute perpetual motion to hear the argument that split the commission. Hilary Hahn's and Isaac Stern's recordings take very different views of the tempo."],"barber-knoxville-summer-1915":["Sixteen unbroken minutes for soprano and orchestra, setting a prose poem rather than verse. The music opens with a gently rocking figure, like a porch swing, over which the singer describes a Southern evening: sprinklers hissing on lawns, a streetcar passing, families lying on quilts looking at the stars. The mood breaks twice into faster, brighter music before returning to the rocking pulse, and the piece ends with the singer alone on the words about being taken in and put to bed.","Barber set a 1938 prose piece by James Agee, who was recalling his own Knoxville childhood before his father's death; Agee later used it as the prologue to his novel A Death in the Family. Barber wrote the setting in 1947, while his own father was seriously ill and shortly after the death of his aunt, the contralto Louise Homer. The soprano Eleanor Steber commissioned it and sang the premiere with the Boston Symphony under Serge Koussevitzky in April 1948.","The text is prose, and Barber refuses to force it into regular phrases: the vocal line follows speech rhythm so closely that the accompaniment's steady rocking often falls out of step with it, which is why the piece can feel like a memory rather than a song. He later made a reduced version for chamber orchestra, which most performances now use; it thins the string sound and makes the words easier to catch.","Steber recorded it twice and championed it for decades, but it took years to become standard, partly because it is neither an aria nor a song cycle and fits awkwardly into programmes. It is now widely regarded as the finest American orchestral song, and Agee's text has drawn as much critical attention as the music. Arguments continue about whether the chamber version's clarity outweighs the full orchestra's warmth.","Read Agee's paragraph first — it takes two minutes and the music makes far more sense afterwards. Then listen for the streetcar, which Barber scores with a real sense of metal on rails. Compare Steber's own recording with a modern one, and try both orchestral versions. Stop the music at the final line rather than letting the next track begin."],"barber-agnus-dei":["The composer's own choral version of his Adagio for Strings, setting the Latin words of the Agnus Dei from the mass. The same rising line passes from sopranos to altos to tenors and basses instead of between string sections, the same overlapping climb builds to the same held climax, and the same silence follows. The difference is breath: a choir must take it, and the phrases are shaped by lungs rather than bows, which makes the long lines audibly harder won.","Barber made the arrangement in 1967, three decades after the original and in a difficult period following the critical failure of his opera Antony and Cleopatra at the opening of the new Metropolitan Opera House. By then the Adagio had long since escaped him and become public property. Choosing the Agnus Dei — the plea for mercy and for peace near the end of the mass — attached a liturgical text to music already understood as a national lament.","The words do not fit the melody comfortably, and Barber knew it: he repeats the short Latin phrases many times over, letting syllables stretch across several notes so that the text becomes a texture rather than a statement. Listen at the climax, where the choir is singing the words for peace at the loudest and most anguished moment in the piece, an intentional contradiction not present in the string version.","Choirs adopted it instantly, and it is now among the most performed unaccompanied works in the repertoire, which has drawn the criticism that it trades on the original's fame. Others argue the reverse: that a text about mercy exposes what the instrumental version leaves implicit. Performance debate concerns whether to use the optional keyboard support and whether large choirs can sustain the phrases at the marked slow tempo.","Listen to the string original immediately afterwards and note where the choir has to breathe — those seams are the arrangement's real subject. Follow one inner voice, the altos or tenors, rather than the top line. Look for a recording by a chamber choir rather than a large chorus; the harmony stays clearer at the climax."],"barber-monk-his-cat":["Two minutes, and the eighth of ten short songs in the cycle Hermit Songs. The piano begins with a light, padding figure in a lilting rhythm — unmistakably a cat crossing a room — and keeps it up almost throughout while the voice sings a conversational, half-spoken line about a monk and his white cat, each happy in his own work: the cat hunting mice, the man hunting words. It ends mid-thought, with the piano's padding simply stopping.","The Hermit Songs set anonymous verses written in the margins of manuscripts by Irish monks between the eighth and thirteenth centuries, in modern translations Barber commissioned or selected — this one from W. H. Auden's version of the poem known as Pangur Bán. The cycle was commissioned by the Elizabeth Sprague Coolidge Foundation and premiered at the Library of Congress in October 1953 by Leontyne Price, then twenty-six and barely known, with Barber at the piano.","The accompaniment is almost entirely one repeated pattern, but it is not quite regular: the beats group unevenly, so the cat's walk never falls into a march. Barber marks the vocal line to be sung lightly and without emphasis, and the singer is asked to sound as if thinking aloud rather than performing. The original marginal poem is a doodle by a bored scribe, and the setting keeps that scale exactly.","The Hermit Songs made Leontyne Price's early reputation and have never left the recital repertoire; this song and The Crucifixion are the two most often extracted. Critics who find Barber's larger works over-rich generally exempt the cycle, praising its economy. The main performance question is whether a singer should aim for medieval austerity or let the humour show, and most now let it show.","Listen to the piano alone for the first fifteen seconds and decide whether you hear a cat. Then follow the words, which are worth reading beforehand. Hear it beside the Adagio for Strings to appreciate how narrow the usual picture of this composer is. Price's recording with Barber himself at the piano is the historical document."],"britten-peter-grimes-four-sea-interludes":["Four orchestral scenes lifted from an opera, lasting about sixteen minutes together. Dawn is bare and cold: high violins and flutes in unison over a hollow gap, answered by muttering brass and rippling clarinets. Sunday Morning rings with clashing horn calls imitating church bells. Moonlight moves in heavy, slow blocks of sound with flecks of flute and harp glinting across them. Storm is violent and jagged, hurling the whole orchestra about before dying suddenly away.","Britten conceived Peter Grimes while living in California in 1941, after reading George Crabbe's poem The Borough about the Suffolk coast where he grew up. A conscientious objector, he returned to wartime England in 1942 and faced a tribunal. The opera opened at Sadler's Wells on 7 June 1945, four weeks after victory in Europe, and was received as the rebirth of English opera. He extracted the interludes as a concert suite the same year.","The sea music is not scene-setting but character: each interlude describes Grimes's mind as much as the weather, and the storm returns inside the opera as the sound of the crowd turning on him. Listen in Dawn for the enormous empty space Britten leaves between the high violins and the low brass — the orchestra's middle register is simply absent, which is what makes the cold. Bells recur throughout the opera as the voice of the community.","The premiere's impact is well documented and rarely disputed; what is argued over is meaning. Britten and Peter Pears shaped Grimes into an outsider hounded by a conformist community, and many read the opera as coded testimony about being homosexual in a country where it was criminal. Others insist Grimes remains genuinely dangerous. In concert the suite is sometimes criticised for detaching gorgeous music from an uncomfortable drama.","Listen to Dawn with your eyes closed and locate the hole in the middle of the orchestra. Then hear the interludes in their operatic places, where each precedes a scene it comments on. Britten's own recording with the Royal Opera House forces is authoritative; a modern orchestra with a big percussion section makes Storm more violent than he probably intended."],"britten-young-person-s-guide-orchestra":["Seventeen minutes that take the orchestra apart and put it back together. A stately theme by Purcell is played first by everyone, then by the woodwind, the brass, the strings and the percussion in turn. Thirteen short variations follow, each handing the tune to one family or one instrument — piccolo, oboe, clarinet, bassoon, then violins, violas, cellos, basses, harp, horns, trumpets, trombones, percussion. A fugue gathers them all up in the same order, and Purcell's theme blazes out on the brass above it.","The Ministry of Education commissioned music for an educational film, Instruments of the Orchestra, made in 1946 with the London Symphony Orchestra under Malcolm Sargent. Britten wrote the score in December 1945, six months after Peter Grimes, and gave it the formal subtitle Variations and Fugue on a Theme of Purcell. Postwar Britain was investing heavily in public arts education, and the film was shown in schools and cinemas for years.","The theme comes from Purcell's incidental music to the play Abdelazer, written around 1695 — Britten's lifelong argument that English music had a great past worth claiming was being made here to schoolchildren. The final trick is structural: the fugue's entries follow exactly the order of the variations, so when the brass declaim Purcell's original melody over the top, two different pieces are running at once and both are complete.","It has been performed and recorded more than almost any other twentieth-century British orchestral work, which has cost it critical standing: serious writing on Britten often passes over it as a schoolroom piece. Conductors disagree on the narration, which Britten made optional and later expressed doubts about; many now perform it without a word spoken. Its craft is not in dispute.","Play the Purcell rondeau first so you know the tune. Then choose one instrument you cannot usually pick out — the bassoon or the violas — and wait for its variation. In the fugue, try to hear the moment Purcell's theme returns on top; it arrives before the fugue has finished. Britten's own recording is brisk and unsentimental."],"britten-war-requiem-libera-me":["The last and longest section of a ninety-minute requiem, running over twenty minutes. It begins with a slow, grinding march in the low strings and drums that builds to a shattering climax for full chorus, soprano and orchestra. Then everything stops. A tenor and a baritone, accompanied only by a small chamber ensemble, sing a dialogue between two dead soldiers who meet in a tunnel underground. Boys' voices and a distant chorus close the work over a quietly repeated chord.","Britten was commissioned to write for the consecration of the rebuilt Coventry Cathedral, next to the ruins of the medieval one destroyed by bombing in November 1940. A lifelong pacifist, he interleaved the Latin mass for the dead with nine poems by Wilfred Owen, killed a week before the Armistice in 1918. He wrote the solo parts for an Englishman, a German and a Russian — Peter Pears, Dietrich Fischer-Dieskau and Galina Vishnevskaya — as a gesture of reconciliation. The premiere was on 30 May 1962.","Owen's poem here is Strange Meeting, in which a soldier recognises his killer: 'I am the enemy you killed, my friend.' Britten lets the English tenor and the German baritone sing together for the first time in the whole work, on the words 'Let us sleep now', while boys and chorus overlay the Latin prayer for paradise. The interval that has haunted the entire requiem resolves in the last bars, but not cleanly.","The premiere was a national event and the recording sold in unprecedented numbers, yet within a decade critics such as Stravinsky were calling the work sentimental and its pacifism too easy. Later scholarship has restored it, focusing on the rigour of the tritone scheme and the deliberate separation of the three musical worlds. Soviet authorities refused Vishnevskaya a visa for the premiere; she recorded it with Britten a year later.","Read Strange Meeting before listening. Notice the three distinct sound worlds — full orchestra for the Latin, chamber group for the soldiers, distant organ and boys for the eternal — and how they never mix until the end. Britten's own 1963 recording with the intended soloists is the essential document; hear it in a large resonant building if you ever can."],"britten-simple-symphony-playful-pizzicato":["Three minutes in which no player uses a bow. The whole string orchestra plucks throughout: a scampering, whispered tune races past in short phrases, stops dead, and starts again; a broader plucked melody arrives in the middle, then the scampering returns and vanishes with two flicked chords. It is the second of four movements in a twenty-minute suite, all of them alliteratively titled — Boisterous Bourrée, Playful Pizzicato, Sentimental Saraband, Frolicsome Finale.","Britten assembled the Simple Symphony in 1933 and 1934, aged twenty, from tunes he had written between the ages of nine and twelve, mostly for piano. He had just left the Royal College of Music, where he was frustrated by his teachers and by the conservatism of English musical life. He conducted the first performance in Norwich in March 1934 with an amateur string orchestra, and dedicated the work to his childhood viola teacher.","The material really is a child's: Britten listed in the score which early pieces each movement came from, and the plucked tune of this movement derives from a piano piece written when he was about ten. What is not a child's is the handling — the phrases are deliberately clipped short so the music sounds as if it keeps catching its breath, and the plucking must be exactly together across the whole ensemble or the joke collapses.","It has always been treated as a charming apprentice piece rather than serious Britten, and the composer encouraged that view. It has also become a staple of school and youth orchestras, which is where most people first meet it, and the pizzicato movement is regularly extracted as an encore. Scholars mining the early manuscripts have made a stronger case for the pieces' originality than the affectionate title implies.","Listen for the moment when the plucking suddenly gets loud — the change is dramatic precisely because there is no bow to help. Try the string quartet version, where four players must be even more exactly together. Then hear the Young Person's Guide from eleven years later to see what the same fascination with instrumental colour became."],"britten-ceremony-carols-wolcum-yole":["Under two minutes of welcome. Treble voices burst in on the word 'Wolcum' — medieval English for welcome — and hurl it about, one group of voices chasing another a beat behind so the greeting piles up on itself. The harp punches out bright chords underneath rather than rippling. The text welcomes Christmas and then each saint's day in turn, and the music gets faster and more crowded until it stops abruptly on a shouted last word.","Britten wrote most of the cycle in March and April 1942 aboard a Swedish cargo ship crossing the Atlantic from America back to wartime England, a dangerous month-long voyage. He had bought a volume of medieval English poetry at a bookshop in Halifax, Nova Scotia during a stop. The eleven movements for boys' voices and harp were a deliberate return to a native tradition at the moment he was returning to a country he had left, controversially, in 1939.","The whole cycle is framed by a plainchant processional, sung by the choir walking in and out, so the carols are enclosed in music a thousand years older than they are. Here Britten keeps the archaic spelling exactly as he found it and lets the overlapping entries blur the words — the effect is a crowd greeting rather than a text delivered. The harp part was written before he had consulted a harpist, and was later revised on advice.","It became a Christmas fixture in cathedrals and schools within a generation and is now among the most performed choral works of the century. The main practical debate is about voices: Britten wrote for boy trebles, but women's and mixed choirs have long sung it, and purists argue the sound was calculated for unbroken voices. Its wartime origin at sea is often sentimentalised in programme notes beyond what the evidence supports.","Follow one line of voices and hear how the others enter just behind, turning a single word into a scramble. Listen to the processional chant that opens and closes the cycle to feel the frame. A recording by a cathedral choir with boy trebles and a single harp gives the sound Britten had in mind; larger mixed choirs soften it considerably."],"britten-peter-grimes-passacaglia":["Seven minutes built over a short bass phrase that repeats without stopping from first bar to last. A solo viola plays a bleak melody above it; other instruments take up variations in turn, the music thickening and growing more violent, until the full orchestra is grinding out the same pattern at maximum weight. Then it thins again, and the viola is left almost alone. The repeating bass never changes speed and never modulates away for long, so the movement feels trapped.","This is the orchestral interlude between the two scenes of Act II of Peter Grimes, played while the stage is reset — the point in the opera where Grimes has struck the schoolmistress in front of the congregation and the town has decided to march on his hut. Britten published it separately as Op. 33b alongside the Four Sea Interludes, and it is often performed with them, though it is not one of them.","The repeating bass is taken from Grimes's own words a few minutes earlier, a bitter phrase about God having mercy upon him. The solo viola stands for the apprentice boy who cannot speak in the opera and who will shortly die — Britten himself played the viola. The form is deliberately archaic: a passacaglia is a Baroque device, and using it here casts the town's persecution as something fixed and ceremonial rather than spontaneous.","It is generally judged the most powerful purely orchestral music in the opera, and Britten returned to the passacaglia form throughout his career, in the Violin Concerto, the Cello Symphony and Death in Venice. Concert programming remains contested: pairing it with the Sea Interludes makes a satisfying suite but reorders Britten's own published groupings, and some conductors insist it should be heard only in the theatre.","Learn the repeating bass phrase in the first twenty seconds and then count how many times it comes round; the number is the point. Follow the solo viola, and notice how it survives into the quiet ending. Compare it with Bach's organ Passacaglia to see the form doing something ceremonial rather than dramatic. Britten's own recording is the reference."],"britten-serenade-tenor-horn-strings-prologue":["Ninety seconds for a single unaccompanied horn, opening a forty-minute cycle of night poems for tenor and strings. The horn plays a simple rising and falling call, unhurried, with no accompaniment and no harmony to place it. Several of the notes sound slightly out of tune against modern expectations — noticeably flat or sharp. The same music returns at the very end of the cycle as an Epilogue, this time played offstage, so the work is framed by the same call heard near and then far away.","Britten wrote the Serenade in 1943 for two performers: his partner, the tenor Peter Pears, and Dennis Brain, the twenty-two-year-old horn player he had heard in an RAF orchestra. The premiere was at the Wigmore Hall in London in October 1943, with the country at war and both men exempt or otherwise placed. The poems Britten chose — Cotton, Tennyson, Blake, Keats and an anonymous fifteenth-century dirge — all concern nightfall and what comes with it.","The Prologue is written to be played entirely on the horn's natural harmonics, using no valves, as horns did before the nineteenth century. Those harmonics are not tuned to the piano scale, so the seventh and eleventh notes sound audibly off. This is not an error and must not be corrected: it is an older instrument calling across a valley, and it is why the Epilogue, played offstage, seems to come from another century as well as another room.","Performers occasionally 'fix' the tuning with valves, which Britten's markings forbid and critics reliably condemn. Dennis Brain's recordings with Pears set the standard and cast a long shadow; his death in a car crash in 1957 at thirty-six turned them into memorials. The Serenade as a whole is now considered Britten's finest orchestral song cycle, with the Nocturne of 1958 its closest rival.","Listen for the notes that sound wrong and enjoy them rather than wincing. Then skip to the end of the cycle to hear the same music from offstage and notice the difference distance makes. Compare the Prologue with a horn passage from Mozart to hear what the valves took away. Brain and Pears under Britten remains the recording to start with."],"cage-sonatas-interludes-sonata-v":["Two minutes for a piano with screws, bolts, nuts and strips of rubber and plastic wedged between its strings, so that pressing a key produces a thud, a click, a dull bell or a muted twang rather than a note. Sonata V runs in a fast, even patter, with the same short phrase repeated and rearranged; each half is played twice, an old dance-suite convention. The result sounds less like a piano than a small percussion orchestra played by one person.","Cage wrote the twenty-piece cycle between 1946 and 1948 in New York while reading Indian aesthetics, partly through his contact with the musician Gita Sarabhai. He had invented the prepared piano in 1940 for a dance performance in a hall too small for a percussion group. Maro Ajemian gave the complete premiere at Carnegie Hall in January 1949, and the cycle won Cage grants from the Guggenheim Foundation and the American Academy of Arts and Letters.","The score is an instruction manual as much as a piece: it lists which object goes between which strings and at what distance from the bridge, and no two pianos will sound alike afterwards. Cage's stated subject is the nine permanent emotions of Indian aesthetics — the erotic, the heroic, the odious, the angry, the mirthful, the fearful, the sorrowful, the wondrous, and the tranquillity toward which they all tend. Nothing in the music tells you which is which.","It made Cage's reputation as a composer rather than a provocateur, and even critics hostile to his later chance music generally exempt the Sonatas and Interludes. Debate concerns preparation: each piano, each set of hardware and each player's measurements produce different results, so recordings vary enormously, and some scholars argue that the variability is the piece rather than a flaw in it.","Listen for keys that produce no pitch at all, only a knock — they are scattered unevenly across the keyboard. Then notice each half repeating. Try two different recordings back to back and hear how far apart the same score can sound. If you can, watch a video of the preparation being installed; it takes hours."],"cage-landscape":["Nine minutes of quiet piano with the sustain pedal held down almost continuously, so nothing is ever damped and every note blurs into the ones around it. The right hand traces a slow melody that circles through the same handful of pitches; the left hand answers in long, spaced intervals. There is no climax and no dissonance — the piece uses so few different notes that clashes cannot occur. It ends by simply thinning out and stopping.","Cage wrote it in 1948, the same year as the Suite for Toy Piano and the end of the prepared-piano cycle, for the dancer Louise Lippold. He was working constantly with dancers, above all Merce Cunningham, who became his partner for the rest of his life, and such commissions had to fit a choreographer's timings and a small budget. It predates by four years the chance procedures for which he became notorious.","The piece is built from a rhythmic structure rather than a harmonic one: the phrase lengths are fixed in proportion in advance and the notes fill them, a method Cage borrowed from his study of Indian tala and from his own percussion works. The held pedal is not colour but structure, since it makes the piano into a resonating box in which the whole gamut of available pitches is always faintly present.","For decades it went almost unnoticed, overshadowed by the scandals; it began to circulate widely only in the 1990s, largely through Stephen Drury's and Alexei Lubimov's recordings, and now appears on playlists next to Satie and Pärt. That success has provoked its own argument: whether hearing Cage as an ambient composer is a rediscovery or a domestication of a far more radical figure.","Play it quietly and let the pedal blur do its work; a recording made on a warm, resonant instrument suits it better than a bright one. Count how few different notes you hear. Then follow it with 4'33\" from four years later to trace how the same interest in unforced sound arrived at silence. The harp version is worth hearing too."],"cage-4-33":["Four minutes and thirty-three seconds in three movements during which the performer plays nothing. A pianist sits, closes the keyboard lid to mark the start of each movement, opens it at the end, and leaves. The score specifies only silence and the durations — thirty seconds, two minutes forty, one minute twenty in the first version. What the audience actually hears is the room: air conditioning, traffic outside, coughing, rain on a roof, their own breathing and, usually, growing discomfort.","David Tudor gave the first performance on 29 August 1952 at the Maverick Concert Hall near Woodstock, New York, a wooden barn open at the back to the woods, during a programme of contemporary piano music. Cage had been thinking about the piece for years, held back by fear of ridicule, and was pushed toward it by Robert Rauschenberg's blank White Paintings and by a visit to an anechoic chamber at Harvard where, expecting silence, he heard his own nervous system and blood.","There are three movements, and most listeners miss the fact that Cage marked them — the piece has a shape, with proportions worked out by chance operations using the I Ching. Later versions of the score dispense with fixed timings altogether. The title's duration is not symbolic; it results from the process. Cage's point was never that silence is music but that silence does not exist, a claim he verified physically before composing.","The Woodstock audience was reportedly offended, and a century of jokes has followed. Serious argument continues over what the work is: a piece of music, a piece of theatre, a philosophical demonstration, or a legal question — a 2002 dispute over an alleged plagiarism of it was settled out of court. Musicologists broadly accept it as the century's most consequential single work outside the concert-music mainstream.","Do not stream it; perform it. Sit somewhere with ambient noise, time four minutes thirty-three seconds in three marked sections, and listen to what is already there. Then hear a recording made in a concert hall, where the audience is audible, and note how quickly people start to fidget. Read Cage's own account of the anechoic chamber afterwards."],"cage-dream":["Eight minutes of piano played softly with the pedal held, so that notes accumulate into a soft haze rather than following one another cleanly. A single melodic line wanders slowly through a small set of pitches, doubling back on itself, occasionally supported by a held low note that rings on underneath for many bars. There are no chords in the ordinary sense, no fast passages and no loud moments. The line simply continues until it stops.","Cage wrote it in 1948 for Merce Cunningham, who choreographed and danced a solo to it; the two had begun their long collaboration a few years earlier and would work together for the rest of Cage's life. Music for dance was Cage's main livelihood in the 1940s, and it imposed useful constraints — a fixed duration, a small instrument, a texture that would not compete with movement on stage.","The whole piece uses a fixed gamut of pitches chosen in advance, so the melody is not free but is drawing repeatedly from the same small pool, which is why it sounds familiar within seconds of starting. In the Cunningham partnership, music and dance were made separately to an agreed length and only met in performance, meaning nothing in this score is designed to match a step. The two arts share time and nothing else.","Like In a Landscape, Dream lay largely unregarded during the decades when Cage was defined by controversy, and has since been adopted by listeners who know nothing of his chance procedures. Critics divide over whether this popularity is a fair correction — proof that he was always a composer of beautiful sound — or a misunderstanding that quietly ignores everything he actually argued for.","Listen at low volume and let the pedal blur accumulate; a bright modern recording spoils it. Notice how few distinct pitches there are, and how the same shapes keep returning. Then watch a film of Cunningham dancing to Cage and see how little the two agree moment to moment. Pair it with In a Landscape from the same year."],"cage-suite-toy-piano":["Five short movements, about seven minutes altogether, written for a child's toy piano — an instrument that strikes metal rods rather than strings, giving a clean, glassy chime with no sustain and no dynamic range to speak of. Cage restricts himself to nine adjacent white notes, and the whole suite is built from that handful of pitches in changing rhythms. The sound is bright, small and slightly comic, and entirely serious in construction.","Written in 1948 for a Merce Cunningham dance called A Diversion, at a point when Cage was composing for dance almost continuously and working with whatever was affordable and portable. He also arranged the suite for chamber orchestra later, which loses the joke and keeps the structure. The toy piano had never been used as a concert instrument before; a repertoire for it now exists, and Margaret Leng Tan built a career on it.","The nine-note limit is not a gimmick but the same working method as the prepared-piano pieces: fix a restricted set of sounds in advance, then build with rhythm alone. Because the toy piano cannot play loudly or softly, every expressive decision must be made through timing. The instrument's pitches are also unreliable, drifting sharp or flat, so no two toy pianos play this piece in the same tuning.","Long treated as a curiosity, it is now credited with founding a genuine instrumental repertoire, and composers from Kaija Saariaho's generation onward have written for the toy piano seriously. The orchestral arrangement divides opinion sharply: it proves the music works as abstract structure, and it removes the thing that makes the original interesting.","Listen for how quickly the notes die — there is no pedal and no resonance, so silence is constantly interrupting the line. Count the different pitches; you will not get past nine. Margaret Leng Tan's recording is the standard. Hear the orchestral version afterwards to test whether the piece survives the loss of its instrument."],"lutoslawski-concerto-orchestra-finale":["The third and longest movement, around seventeen minutes, in three linked stages. First a passacaglia: a bass theme creeps in on the double basses and repeats underneath while layer after layer is added above, until the orchestra is dense and roaring. Then a fast toccata of hammered, driving figures thrown between sections. Finally a chorale for brass rises through the din, is beaten back, and returns to end the work in a huge affirmative blaze with bells and percussion.","Witold Rowicki commissioned the concerto for the Warsaw Philharmonic, an orchestra he was rebuilding in a city eighty-five per cent destroyed in the war. Lutosławski worked on it from 1950 to 1954, under a Stalinist cultural policy that required folk material and forbade modernist experiment; he had already had his First Symphony banned. The premiere in Warsaw in November 1954 was a public triumph and made his international name.","The folk tunes are real ones, taken from the nineteenth-century collections of Oskar Kolberg, but Lutosławski uses them as raw material for abstract construction rather than as national colour, treating each melody as a set of intervals to be recombined. The passacaglia's bass theme states its full shape only once before it starts being altered, so most of the movement is a variation on something you barely heard.","The obvious comparison, made from the start, is with Bartók's Concerto for Orchestra of a decade earlier, and Lutosławski disliked the parallel. He was also later dismissive of the whole work as a compromise with the folkloric requirements of the period, though he never withdrew it. Critics now read it as the point where he learned to build large structures, which he applied for the next thirty years to entirely different material.","Catch the bass theme in the first thirty seconds — it is quiet and low and easy to miss, and everything grows from it. Then wait for the brass chorale near the end and hear how it has to fight to be heard. Compare the whole with Bartók's Concerto for Orchestra. Polish orchestras tend to play it harder and faster than Western ones."],"lutoslawski-symphony-no-3":["Half an hour in a single unbroken span, though it falls audibly into two large parts: a first half of short, interrupted episodes and a second that builds one long continuous argument to a climax and a quiet coda. Four repeated notes, hammered out by clarinets and later by the whole orchestra, punctuate the work like a signal. In several passages the players are given their own material and told to play it freely, so the orchestra blurs into a shimmering cloud with no common beat.","The Chicago Symphony Orchestra commissioned it in 1972; Lutosławski took eleven years, working through the political convulsions that produced Solidarity and then martial law in Poland in December 1981. He refused during that period to perform in state institutions, and appeared instead at church concerts and underground events. Georg Solti conducted the premiere in Chicago in September 1983, and the work won the Grawemeyer Award two years later.","The repeated four notes are his own; whether they nod to the opening of Beethoven's Fifth is a question he was asked often and answered evasively. The freely played passages use what he called limited aleatorism: the pitches and gestures are fully written out, but the players are not synchronised, so the harmony is fixed while the texture is different at every performance. Chaos is being controlled to the note.","It was immediately received as the major symphony of its decade, and is now generally judged his finest work. Critics who wanted overt political statement from a Polish composer under martial law found none, and Lutosławski insisted his music had no programme. Others hear the interruptions and the hard-won continuity as testimony despite him. Performance difficulty keeps it rarer in concert than its reputation suggests.","Listen for the four repeated notes and use them as signposts; they mark the structure. Then notice the passages where the orchestra stops playing together — the sound goes soft-edged and glittering. Compare it with Ligeti's Atmosphères for a different route to the same shimmer. Salonen's and Barenboim's recordings differ most in how ferocious they let the climax become."],"lutoslawski-variations-theme-paganini":["Six minutes for two pianos on the same violin tune that Brahms and Rachmaninoff also plundered — Paganini's last caprice, a brisk sixteen-bar melody almost designed for variation. Lutosławski runs through a dozen short variants without a break: one is a fast chase in which the two players interlock so tightly they sound like one instrument, another is a slow, sour waltz, another piles the theme on top of itself in different keys at once. It finishes at speed with both pianists hammering.","He wrote it in 1941 in German-occupied Warsaw, where he and the composer Andrzej Panufnik played piano duets in cafés for money — the only public music-making Poles were permitted, since concert halls were closed to them and Polish musicians were banned from performing serious repertoire. The duo built an arrangement library of some two hundred pieces; almost all of it was destroyed in the Warsaw Uprising of 1944. This is one of the very few survivors.","The wrong-note harmony is not modernist licence but café practice, the sound of two players entertaining an audience by bending a familiar tune just enough to make it funny. Lutosławski returned to the piece in 1978 and rescored it for piano and orchestra, adding nothing and mostly redistributing the second piano's part — a rare case of a composer treating his own wartime survival music as a finished object.","For years it was heard as an attractive trifle from a composer who later became forbidding, and it remains his most played piece by some distance. Since the 1990s the wartime café context has been documented more fully, and criticism has taken it more seriously — not as protest, which it is not, but as evidence of what musical life under occupation actually sounded like.","Learn Paganini's tune first, from the caprice itself or from Rachmaninoff's Rhapsody, then hear how quickly Lutosławski distorts it. Follow one pianist rather than both. The two-piano original is sharper and funnier than the 1978 orchestral version; the Argerich and Freire recording is the classic account."],"lutoslawski-musique-funebre":["Fourteen minutes for strings alone in four continuous sections. The cellos begin entirely alone with a bare line built from just two intervals, a very wide one and a very narrow one, alternating; other instruments enter in canon, one after another, until the texture is thick and knotted. A faster middle passage grows steadily more agitated to a violent climax of massed dissonance. Then everything recedes, and the piece ends as it began, with a single line in the cellos.","Commissioned by the conductor Jan Krenz to mark ten years since Bartók's death, Lutosławski worked on it slowly from 1954 to 1958, spanning the political thaw that followed Stalin's death and reopened Poland to Western modernism. Krenz conducted the premiere in Katowice in March 1958. It arrived at the moment the Warsaw Autumn festival was making Poland the most open musical culture in the Eastern bloc.","The whole work is generated from a twelve-note series in which the notes are separated only by the two intervals — the largest and one of the smallest available. This is a serial technique, then politically suspect, put to entirely un-serial ends: Lutosławski uses it to control the density of the sound rather than to organise melody. He said afterwards that the piece opened a door for him, and that everything he wrote later came through it.","It was recognised at once as the work in which he found his own voice, and it won the UNESCO International Rostrum of Composers prize in 1959. Debate has focused on the label: Western critics filed it as Polish serialism, which Lutosławski rejected, insisting the technique was a means of building texture. Its status as a memorial to Bartók is sometimes overstated, since it quotes him nowhere.","Listen to the opening cello line alone and try to hear the alternating wide and narrow steps; everything grows from that. Then track how many voices have entered by the time the texture solidifies. Compare it with Bartók's own night music. A string orchestra with a lean, unsentimental sound serves it far better than a lush one."],"bernstein-west-side-story-symphonic-dances":["Twenty-three minutes strung together from the show's dance music, played without pause. It opens with finger-snaps and a wary, prowling figure, moves through the yearning tune of Somewhere, a fast scherzo, and then the Mambo, in which the whole orchestra shouts the word and the brass and Latin percussion tear the place apart. A delicate cha-cha and a hesitant meeting scene follow, then a fugue built on the same prowling notes, a violent rumble, and a quiet ending on an unresolved chord.","West Side Story opened on Broadway in 1957, with choreography by Jerome Robbins, lyrics by Stephen Sondheim and a book by Arthur Laurents, transplanting Romeo and Juliet to Manhattan gang rivalry between white and Puerto Rican teenagers. Bernstein, by then also music director of the New York Philharmonic, wanted the score in the concert hall; Sid Ramin and Irwin Kostal made this arrangement with him in 1960, premiered by the Philharmonic in February 1961.","Almost everything grows from one interval — three notes spanning an awkward gap that sounds unstable to the ear, the same shape heard in the prowling opening, in the word Maria, in Cool and in the fugue. Bernstein uses it as a wound that will not close, and the work ends on it rather than resolving. The fugue itself is a strict academic form used for a gang planning a fight, which is the joke and the point.","The Broadway run was respectable rather than sensational; the 1961 film made the score famous. Critics for years treated Bernstein as a serious composer slumming, or a theatre composer overreaching, and he was wounded by both charges. The consensus has moved: the score is now taught as a model of motivic construction, and the argument has shifted to the show's Puerto Rican stereotyping and how productions should handle it.","Learn the three-note figure from the opening bars and count how many later tunes contain it. Wait for the Mambo, and hear the orchestra shout. Then hear the show's original cast recording to catch what the concert suite leaves out. Bernstein's own recordings are hard-driven; Dudamel's are more explicitly Latin in the percussion."],"bernstein-candide-overture":["Four and a half minutes at breakneck speed. A brass fanfare cracks out, the strings take off in a fast tune that keeps tripping over its own accents, and a lyrical second theme arrives on the woodwind before being swept aside. Near the end a soprano's coloratura writing from the show is handed to the orchestra, so an aria of scales and trills is played by violins and flutes instead of a voice. It ends with a headlong dash and two hard chords.","Candide, based on Voltaire's satire, opened on Broadway on 1 December 1956 with a book by Lillian Hellman and lyrics from several hands including Richard Wilbur and Dorothy Parker. It ran only two months and was considered a failure, its satire on optimism and inquisition read by many as a commentary on the McCarthy hearings Hellman had endured. Bernstein conducted the overture separately with the New York Philharmonic in January 1957, and it succeeded immediately.","The overture is stitched from the show's numbers: the fast opening comes from the duet Oh, Happy We, and the flurry of scales near the end is Cunegonde's coloratura aria Glitter and Be Gay, played by the orchestra. The rhythm is what makes it hard — the accents fall off the beat repeatedly, which is why amateur orchestras come apart in the first thirty seconds and why conductors love it.","It is said to be among the most performed concert openers written since the Second World War, and it long outlived the show, which was rewritten repeatedly over three decades in versions that still divide opinion. Critics have used the overture's success against Bernstein, arguing he was a brilliant miniaturist who never sustained a large form; his defenders point to the show's eventual rehabilitation in the 1988 Scottish Opera version.","Play it twice: once for the ride, once counting where the accents actually land. Then listen to Glitter and Be Gay and catch the passage the orchestra borrows. Bernstein's own recordings are the fastest and most reckless; compare one with a modern orchestra taking care, and decide which serves Voltaire better."],"bernstein-chichester-psalms":["Nineteen minutes in three movements, sung entirely in Hebrew. The first opens with a shouted call to awake and then breaks into a jubilant dance in an uneven seven-beat metre. The second is the heart: a boy alto sings the twenty-third psalm to a simple harp accompaniment, the trebles answer, and then the men burst in furiously with a psalm about the nations raging, the two texts colliding. The third begins with a grinding orchestral introduction and ends with the choir in near-whispered unison.","Walter Hussey, Dean of Chichester Cathedral and a determined patron of modern art, commissioned the work for the 1965 Southern Cathedrals Festival, telling Bernstein a hint of West Side Story would not be unwelcome. Bernstein was on a sabbatical from the New York Philharmonic, struggling with a twelve-tone piece he eventually abandoned; he wrote this instead, in tonal Hebrew-texted music, and conducted the first performance in New York that July.","Some of the material is salvage. Melodies discarded from West Side Story and from an abandoned musical of The Skin of Our Teeth were reworked here — the fast choral writing in the first movement began life as show music. That a Jewish American composer set Hebrew psalms for an Anglican cathedral choir, with a boy treble singing David's own psalm, was itself the commission's quiet argument.","Bernstein described the piece as the most accessible and B-flat major-ish thing he had written, and some critics took him at his word and dismissed it as a retreat from modernism. Choirs disagreed and it entered the repertoire fast. The lasting performance question is the boy alto, who must be genuinely unbroken and secure; Bernstein permitted a countertenor but forbade a woman, a restriction now sometimes challenged.","Go straight to the second movement and wait for the men's furious entry under the boy's psalm — the collision is the whole work in miniature. Listen for the seven-beat dance in the first movement and try to count it. The reduced version with organ, harp and percussion is often more vivid in a church than the full orchestral one."],"bernstein-west-side-story-somewhere":["Three minutes, and the show's one moment of stillness. The melody opens with a wide upward leap that feels like a physical reach, then falls back and gathers itself to try again, higher. The accompaniment is plain and slow, mostly sustained strings, with none of the finger-snaps or Latin percussion that drive the rest of the score. Sondheim's lyric promises a place and a time, and the setting keeps its harmony unsettled underneath, so the promise never quite lands.","In the original 1957 staging the song is not sung by the lovers at all: it belongs to an unseen voice during a dream ballet in which the gangs imagine a world without the streets they live on, before the fantasy collapses back into violence. The film and most later productions reassign it. Bernstein wrote it as the show's utopian centre, in a work otherwise about a city that permits no such place.","The opening leap is a minor seventh, and its resemblance to the slow movement of Beethoven's Emperor Concerto has been noted since the show opened; the same interval also echoes the finale of Tchaikovsky's Swan Lake. Bernstein, who spent his life quoting and teaching, never denied it. Hear also how the three-note figure that runs through the whole score is buried inside this melody, so even the escape is made of the same unstable material.","It has become a standard, recorded by hundreds of singers and detached from its dramatic function, which is exactly what Sondheim later objected to: sung alone it is a love song, and in the show it is a doomed collective fantasy. Critics also note that giving the song to the lovers, as the film does, sentimentalises a scene originally staged as an illusion.","Listen for the opening leap and then count how many times the melody tries the climb again. Hear it in the context of the dream ballet, not as a single track, and then in the Symphonic Dances where the orchestra plays it without words. Compare a Broadway belt with an operatic version — Te Kanawa's, from Bernstein's own recording — and note how differently the reach reads."],"bernstein-candide-glitter-be-gay":["Five minutes for a high soprano and orchestra, in two alternating moods. First a mournful slow section in which Cunegonde laments her ruin in long sighing phrases; then the tempo doubles, she catches sight of her jewels, and the music explodes into a fast waltz strung with trills, staccato leaps and repeated top notes, with laughing interjections between phrases. The two moods swap back and forth, self-pity and delight, and the aria ends on a sustained note at the very top of the voice.","The number belongs to the first act of Candide, sung by Cunegonde after Voltaire's heroine has been raped, sold and installed as the mistress of two men at once, consoling herself with diamonds. Barbara Cook created the role in 1956 at twenty-eight. Bernstein wrote it as a direct parody of nineteenth-century operatic display — specifically the jewel song from Gounod's Faust — for a Broadway house rather than an opera house, with a singer expected to act the joke.","The comedy is structural: every ornament is a lie. The slow lament uses genuine operatic pathos, the fast section uses genuine coloratura difficulty, and the character deploys both cynically while telling the audience she is doing so. Listen for the moment the orchestra stops dead and the soprano continues alone; the exposure is deliberate. The final high note is optional in the score, and no soprano who can sing it declines.","It has escaped the show entirely and become an audition and recital staple, which flattens the satire — sung without a stage, it can read as straight display. Critics have argued for decades over whether Candide works better as musical or as operetta; the aria is the strongest evidence for the operetta case, and opera houses now programme the whole show partly for it.","Watch a staged performance rather than listening blind; half the aria is acting. Listen for the switch from lament to waltz and how abruptly it comes. Compare Barbara Cook's original with a full operatic voice such as Kristin Chenoweth's or June Anderson's, and then hear Gounod's jewel song to catch what is being mocked."],"bernstein-mass-simple-song":["Four minutes near the start of a two-hour theatre piece. A young man in jeans, carrying a guitar, walks on after a blast of taped electronic noise and sings an unhurried folk-like melody about singing God a simple song, its phrases falling into an easy lilt. A Latin refrain of praise threads through the English words. The accompaniment stays light — guitar, a few winds, quiet strings — and the song ends without any climax, on a held note over a plain chord.","Jacqueline Kennedy Onassis commissioned a work for the opening of the John F. Kennedy Center for the Performing Arts in Washington, and Bernstein delivered Mass: A Theatre Piece for Singers, Players and Dancers on 8 September 1971 — a staged Latin mass invaded by rock bands, blues singers, a marching band and a street chorus who gradually destroy the ritual. Additional texts were by Stephen Schwartz. The Vietnam War was at its height and the FBI monitored the premiere.","This is the Celebrant's first appearance and the only moment in the work where faith is stated without irony; everything that follows dismantles it, and the Celebrant eventually smashes the sacraments on stage. The tune returns transformed at the end, sung by a boy, so the piece is framed by the same melody before and after the collapse. The taped noise it interrupts is a deliberately unlistenable Kyrie in four overlapping recorded tracks.","Reviews in 1971 were savage: Harold Schonberg in the New York Times called it cheap and vulgar, and Catholic commentators were divided over whether it was blasphemous. Bernstein was hurt for years. Revivals since the 2000s, including a widely seen Kennedy Center production, have found a substantial following, and critics now read the work's stylistic chaos as the point rather than a failure of taste.","Hear the song straight through, then listen to the electronic Kyrie that precedes it to feel what the guitar is answering. Follow it to the end of Mass to catch the melody's return. If you sample only one thing from the work, make it this; if you can, watch a staged production rather than listening to the recording alone."],"bernstein-waterfront-symphonic-suite":["Twenty minutes in a single span. It opens with a solo horn alone, playing a bleak rising phrase that will return throughout. A savage passage for timpani and percussion follows, jagged and jazz-inflected, with a wailing alto saxophone; then a warm love theme for flute and strings; then the violence again, heavier each time. Near the end the horn's opening phrase returns over a huge orchestral tread, battered but intact, and the suite closes with a slow, hard-won affirmation.","Elia Kazan's 1954 film about corruption on the New York docks was Bernstein's only original film score, written after he watched a rough cut and became convinced no one else should do it. He found the experience painful — music cut and dialogue laid over his best passages — and never scored another feature. He assembled this concert suite in 1955 and conducted the premiere at Tanglewood that August.","The unaccompanied horn opening is a deliberate risk: no chord, no context, one player exposed for nearly a minute, and everything that follows is derived from it. Kazan's film was itself an argument, widely read as his defence of having named names to the House Un-American Activities Committee. Bernstein, who was blacklisted and had his passport withheld in the same period, wrote its music, and the tension has interested scholars ever since.","The score won broad admiration in 1954 and the suite entered the repertoire steadily rather than instantly. Critics have noted how much of West Side Story is audible here three years early — the same brutal percussion, the same jazz-tinged brass, the same lyricism against violence. Debate over Kazan's testimony still colours discussion of the film and, by extension, of the music attached to it.","Give the first minute your full attention; a solo horn with no accompaniment either grips you or does not, and the whole suite depends on it. Then wait for the same phrase to return at the end and note what has happened to it. Watch the film afterwards. Bernstein's own recording is unsurprisingly the reference."],"piazzolla-libertango":["Barely four minutes long, Libertango runs on a single clipped ostinato, a repeated figure that guitar and piano hammer out under everything else, unchanged, like a machine that will not stop. Over it the bandoneon lays a long, sinuous melody in A minor that climbs and falls in wide arcs. There is no development in the classical sense: the tune returns, the accompaniment thickens, a solo breaks loose, and the whole thing closes without ceremony.","Piazzolla recorded it in Milan in 1974, at a moment when he had left Buenos Aires in disgust. Argentine tango traditionalists had spent fifteen years telling him his music was not tango; one confrontation reportedly involved a knife. In Europe he added electric guitar, organ and drum kit, courting the record-buying public rather than the dance hall. The title fuses libertad and tango: freedom from the dance floor, and from his own country's arguments.","Listen closer: the ostinato never modulates, but the harmony around it does, so the same figure keeps changing meaning, first the root of the chord, then a dissonance against it. Piazzolla also keeps his bandoneon's mechanical noise in the mix. You can hear the bellows and the click of the keys, the sound of an instrument built for street dances being used as a concert voice.","The Milan sessions horrified purists at home and made him famous everywhere else. For decades Libertango was the piece that opened doors and the piece serious critics used to dismiss him as a crossover act. Grace Jones turned it into a pop hit in 1981; Yo-Yo Ma recorded it in 1997; by then scholars had begun treating nuevo tango as a repertoire rather than a novelty. Arrangements now exist for almost every ensemble imaginable.","Start with Piazzolla's own 1974 recording to hear the sound he actually wanted, bellows noise and all, then try a string arrangement to hear how much survives without the bandoneon. Listen for the moment the ostinato drops out; the sudden hole in the floor is the whole point. Compare with the slower Oblivion for the other half of his temperament."],"piazzolla-adios-nonino":["A slow, falling melody in A minor, stated by bandoneon over sustained strings, framed by a fierce fast section that erupts and then collapses back. In the quintet version the piano opens with a long unaccompanied cadenza before the tune arrives. The shape is unusual for a tango: lament, violence, lament, with a coda that simply thins out. It lasts around eight minutes and ends far quieter than it began.","In October 1959 Piazzolla was in New York, playing club dates with a struggling ensemble, when word came that his father Vicente, known as Nonino, had died in Mar del Plata after a bicycle accident. He shut himself in a room and within a day or two reworked a 1954 piece called Nonino into this elegy. He was thirty-eight, broke, and had not yet found the quintet sound that would make his name.","Listen closer: the fast outer music is not new. It is the older Nonino, kept almost intact, with the elegy inserted into its middle. Piazzolla rewrote the piece dozens of times across thirty years, and no two of his own recordings agree; the quintet versions and the later orchestral ones differ in tempo, in where the cadenza falls, and in which instrument carries the tune.","Piazzolla returned to it at nearly every concert for the rest of his life, and it became the piece even his opponents allowed him. That made it his passport: orchestras that would not touch the electric work programmed Adiós Nonino, and it has been played at state occasions and royal weddings. Performers still argue over how much rubato the lament will take before it tips into sentimentality.","Try a quintet recording first, then a version with strings, and notice how differently the silence around the tune behaves. Wait for the piano cadenza; the piece has not really started until it ends. If the lament grips you, go next to Oblivion; if the fast music does, go to La muerte del ángel."],"piazzolla-oblivion":["One of the slowest things Piazzolla wrote: a long, stepwise melody over a rocking accompaniment that barely changes, in a dark minor. The tune is passed between instruments, bandoneon first, then violin or cello, and each time it returns it is decorated a little more. There is no fast section and no release. Around four minutes, it works as well on solo cello with piano as on full string orchestra, which is why dozens of arrangements exist.","Piazzolla wrote it in 1982 for Marco Bellocchio's film Enrico IV, an adaptation of Pirandello's play about a man who has retreated into believing he is a medieval emperor. Piazzolla was in his sixties, working steadily in Italy, and increasingly writing for screen and concert hall rather than nightclubs. The Falklands war was under way at home. The film is largely forgotten; the cue outlived it completely.","Listen closer: the accompaniment is a slow habanera, the same lopsided rhythm that underlies nineteenth-century tango and Bizet's Carmen, but played so gently that most listeners hear only a sway. The melody keeps reaching for a note above and falling back short of it. The piece never grants the arrival it keeps promising, which is what makes it feel like something remembered rather than something happening.","Oblivion has become Piazzolla's most-arranged piece after Libertango, and its ubiquity in films, adverts and competition programmes has cost it some critical respect. The debate is about tempo: cellists tend to slow it well past the film cue's pace, which some hear as depth and others as indulgence. Its defenders point out that the harmonic writing is more subtle than anything in his early tangos.","Listen once at something like the original film tempo and once to a slow cello reading; the difference in what the piece seems to be about is startling. Follow the inner voices rather than the tune, since they move constantly while the melody hangs. Then hear Milonga del ángel, written seventeen years earlier, and notice how long this mood had been forming."],"piazzolla-milonga-del-angel":["A quiet, walking piece for the quintet, built over a bass line that steps steadily downward and keeps starting again. The bandoneon sings a long, plain melody; the violin answers high above it; the guitar fills the gaps with soft chords. Roughly six minutes, almost entirely soft, with a middle section where the players loosen into something close to improvisation before the walking bass returns and closes the door.","It belongs to a loose cycle of pieces about an angel who wanders the tenement courtyards of Buenos Aires, healing and dying and rising again, begun in 1957 as incidental music for a play and extended over the following decade. Piazzolla had recently formed the first of his great quintets, the group that fixed his sound for good: no singer, no drum kit, five players who could turn on a phrase.","Listen closer: a milonga is normally a fast, jaunty older cousin of the tango, and Piazzolla is using the name against type. The rhythm is there in the bass, just drastically slowed. He also asks the string players for a scraping, unpitched noise near the bridge in places, a device tango violinists used to imitate the sound of the street, and it turns the sweetest passages slightly grey.","The Ángel pieces were slow to be recognised as a cycle; Piazzolla never fixed their order, and different recordings assemble them differently. Critics long treated them as mood music beside the more aggressive tangos, but performers have gradually made the case that this is his most sustained lyrical writing. Chamber groups now programme the set whole, which changes how the individual numbers land.","Follow the double bass, not the tune: everything else is decoration on that descending walk. Hear it next to La muerte del ángel, the violent piece it shares a world with, and the pairing makes the cycle's argument clear. Look for a recording by one of Piazzolla's own quintets, or by a string group that keeps the tango attack rather than smoothing it."],"piazzolla-muerte-del-angel":["Three and a half minutes of controlled violence. It opens as a strict fugue: a jagged subject in A minor announced alone, then answered, then piled up until four or five lines are clawing at each other. Piazzolla drives it with hard, off-beat accents and lets the double bass slap. Halfway through the counterpoint dissolves into a slower, keening passage, then the fugue subject returns and the piece stops mid-stride rather than ending.","Written in 1962 as part of the incidental music for Alberto Rodríguez Muñoz's play Tango del ángel, in which an angel is stabbed in a Buenos Aires slum. Piazzolla was working in theatre and radio while trying to keep a tango group alive in a city whose dance halls were closing. He had spent 1954 and 1955 in Paris with Nadia Boulanger, who heard his conservatory scores politely and then, on hearing him play a tango, told him never to abandon it.","Listen closer: the fugue is real, not a gesture. Subject, answer at the expected interval, countersubject, the whole eighteenth-century apparatus, run at tango speed with the accents in the wrong places. Boulanger had drilled him in exactly this. The scraping behind the bridge and the knocking on the instruments' bodies are notated rather than improvised; the players are asked to make the ensemble sound like a fight.","This is the piece that convinced sceptical musicians Piazzolla could write, whatever they thought of the electric guitar. It has become a showpiece for string ensembles, and the argument now is about arrangements: the widely played string-orchestra versions tidy away the ensemble noise the quintet made, and some players restore it deliberately. Traditionalists in Buenos Aires still object that a fugue is not a tango.","Count the entries at the start; you can hear each new instrument take up the same tune, and once you have the pattern the whole piece opens. Then listen for the moment the counterpoint gives way to the slow section, which is the stabbing. Compare it with Bach's organ passacaglia and fugue to hear exactly what Piazzolla kept."],"piazzolla-histoire-du-tango-cafe-1930":["The second panel of a four-movement history of the tango for flute and guitar. Where the first movement bustles, this one is slow and confiding: the guitar lays down a soft, rocking accompaniment and the flute spins a long line above it, breaking now and then into free, speech-like phrases. About seven minutes. The writing is intimate, with no percussion effects and no aggression, just two instruments and a great deal of space between the notes.","Piazzolla wrote Histoire du Tango in 1986 as a deliberate potted history: Bordel 1900, Café 1930, Nightclub 1960, Concert d'aujourd'hui. Each movement imagines where the tango was heard and who was listening. By 1930 the dance had come back from Paris respectable; couples no longer danced to it in cafés, they sat and listened to singers. Piazzolla, who had lived through the later stages himself, is reconstructing the one he missed.","Listen closer: this is a portrait of a style Piazzolla did not much like, the sentimental singer-led tango canción of the Gardel era, and he writes it straight, without mockery, then lets small modern harmonies slide in underneath. The flute is standing in for a singer throughout, and its free passages are written-out rubato of the kind a tango vocalist would have taken by instinct.","Histoire du Tango has become one of the most played duos in the flute repertoire, and Café 1930 the movement students learn first, which has made it a test case for how much tango feeling a classically trained player can supply. Guitarists complain the accompaniment was written by someone who did not play the instrument. Versions for violin, clarinet, cello and marimba have multiplied well beyond anything Piazzolla sanctioned.","Listen for the guitar's bass note at the start of each bar; it marks the sway everything else leans against. Play the four movements in order at least once, because the joke and the sadness both depend on the sequence. Seek out a recording where the flautist bends the line rather than playing it evenly, then hear a violin version for comparison."],"piazzolla-primavera-portena":["Spring in the port city: about six minutes of hard, driving music that opens with a fast snapping figure passed round the quintet, slows to a long lyrical middle section, then returns to the attack. The scoring is percussive, with guitar and bass struck as much as played and dry, stabbing chords from the piano. It ends with a sudden acceleration and a stop, more like a door slamming than a cadence.","Piazzolla composed his four Estaciones porteñas separately between 1965 and 1970, not as a set; Primavera came last. Buenos Aires in 1970 was under military government and its tango industry had largely collapsed, but Piazzolla's quintet was working constantly, playing to students and intellectuals rather than dancers. The title's porteña means of the port: these are seasons in one specific city, not in the countryside.","Listen closer: Piazzolla never quoted Vivaldi here. The famous Vivaldi references come from Leonid Desyatnikov's 1990s arrangement for violin and strings, made for Gidon Kremer, which threads fragments of The Four Seasons through all four pieces and inverts them, so that Argentine spring sounds against Venetian autumn, the hemispheres corrected. Many listeners now know the music only in that version.","The Desyatnikov arrangement did for the Estaciones what the Milan recordings did for Libertango: it made them concert repertoire and, at the same time, made them something Piazzolla had not written. Purists prefer the quintet originals, where the pieces are shorter, rougher and unrelated to Vivaldi. The set is now among the most programmed of all twentieth-century works for violin and strings.","Hear the quintet original and the Desyatnikov arrangement back to back; deciding which is the real piece is half the pleasure. Listen for the slow central section, where the writing is most his own. If you like the way this drives, La muerte del ángel is the same engine at higher pressure."],"ligeti-atmospheres":["Eighty-seven players, no melody, no rhythm you can tap, no percussion section. Atmosphères begins with every note of the chromatic scale sounding at once across five octaves, then lets that block of sound slowly change colour as instruments drop out and re-enter. Inside the mass each player has an independent line, but they are packed so densely that no individual voice is audible, only the shifting cloud. Nine minutes, ending with a player brushing the strings inside a piano.","Ligeti had escaped Hungary in December 1956, walking out under the Soviet crackdown, and arrived at the West German radio studios in Cologne where Stockhausen was making electronic music. Atmosphères is what he took from that year with tape: the idea that sound could be shaped as texture and colour rather than as notes and themes. He wrote it for the Donaueschingen festival, which premiered it in October 1961 under Hans Rosbaud.","Listen closer: the cloud is made of strict written counterpoint. Ligeti called it micropolyphony, dozens of canonic lines moving at slightly different speeds, each perfectly notated and deliberately too crowded to hear separately. The technique came from Renaissance church polyphony, which he had studied closely. What you perceive as a single slowly bending surface is in fact dozens of string parts each going its own way.","Donaueschingen recognised it at once as something new, and it became the emblem of the European avant-garde's turn from serial rows to sound-mass. Then Stanley Kubrick used it in 2001: A Space Odyssey without clearing the rights properly; Ligeti sued, settled, and spent the rest of his life half pleased and half irritated at being the sound of outer space. Scholars now read it as much through Bartók's night music as through Cologne.","Play it loud, in the dark, and resist listening for events; treat it as weather. Notice how the sound seems to move upward and downward without anything obviously changing. Then listen to Lux Aeterna, which does the same thing with sixteen voices, and to Bach's Third Brandenburg for counterpoint you can actually count."],"ligeti-lux-aeterna":["Sixteen unaccompanied solo voices, nine minutes of continuously shifting choral sound on the Latin communion text Lux aeterna luceat eis. It begins with a few sopranos on a single note and grows by imperceptible degrees into a dense chord that seems to hang without breathing, then thins to nothing. Individual words surface briefly and dissolve. There is almost no vibrato, no obvious pulse and no cadence in the ordinary sense.","Written in 1966 for the Schola Cantorum Stuttgart and its conductor Clytus Gottwald, one of the few ensembles then able to sing it: the score demands sixteen singers holding independent parts in tune with no instrumental support. Ligeti was living in Vienna, teaching at Darmstadt in the summers, and applying to voices the cloud technique he had developed for orchestra. The text comes from the Requiem Mass.","Listen closer: the piece is built on canons at the unison. Every voice sings the same slow melody, entering at a different moment, so the harmony is an accident of the overlaps rather than a chosen progression. Ligeti stages the entries so that consonance and dissonance drift past one another. Near the end the sopranos hold a single note for a very long time while the rest of the choir vanishes beneath them.","Kubrick used it three times in 2001, most famously over the black monolith on the Moon, again without full permission, and the association has never come off. Choral conductors treat it as a benchmark of intonation, and there is a running argument about whether it should be sung with the vibrato-less blend of a specialist group or with the fuller sound of a large choir, which blurs the canons past recognition.","Use headphones and try to track one voice from its entry; you will lose it within seconds, which is exactly the experience the piece is designed to produce. Compare it with Tallis's forty-part motet, where the same crowd of voices is arranged so you can hear every one. Look for a chamber choir of soloists rather than a large festival chorus."],"ligeti-etude-no-13-l-escalier-du":["Five relentless minutes for one pianist. A pattern of rising chromatic steps begins quietly in the middle of the keyboard and repeats, each cycle a little longer and a little louder, climbing until it occupies the whole instrument and the dynamic marking has passed beyond fortissimo into rows of f's. Near the top, bell-like chords crash in against the rising figure. The music does not resolve; it exhausts itself and drops away in the deep bass.","Ligeti wrote his eighteen piano études between 1985 and 2001, after a long block, energised by African drumming, by the player-piano music of Conlon Nancarrow and by fractal geometry. This one, which opens the second book, dates from 1993, when he was seventy and living in Hamburg. He wrote for a virtuosity well beyond his own playing: he was a modest pianist and said so, and composed at the limit of what hands can reach.","Listen closer: the title comes from the devil's staircase of mathematics, a curve that rises only in jumps and is flat everywhere in between. Ligeti builds the piece from groups of two and three quick notes that never settle into a regular bar, so the ear keeps expecting a downbeat that arrives late or early. He linked the piece to a storm he sat out on the California coast.","The études were an unexpected late triumph: composers who had written Ligeti off as a texturalist found him producing the most demanding and most played new piano music of the era. Pierre-Laurent Aimard, who premiered many of them, set the performance standard, and the argument since has been over tempo, since the score's markings are close to impossible and some pianists take them as aspiration rather than instruction.","Watch a video rather than only listening: the physical problem the pianist is solving is part of the piece. Listen for the moment the bell chords enter over the climbing figure, roughly two thirds through. Then hear Musica ricercata, written forty years earlier with only three notes, to measure how far he travelled."],"ligeti-requiem-kyrie":["The second and central movement of Ligeti's Requiem, about nine minutes for two vocal soloists, two large choruses and orchestra. The chorus enters on the word Kyrie in a dense, groping polyphony in which no line is separable, and the sound heaves between whisper and shriek. Voices are pushed to extreme registers; the choral mass thickens into something closer to a crowd than a choir, then contracts to almost nothing. It is unremittingly slow.","Ligeti worked on the Requiem from 1963 to 1965 on a Swedish commission and set only part of the liturgy, the Introit, Kyrie, Dies irae and Lacrimosa, leaving out the Sanctus and the consoling final prayers. His father and brother had died in the camps; his mother survived Auschwitz. Whatever he intended, the result is not a consolation. Swedish Radio gave the premiere in Stockholm in March 1965 under Michael Gielen.","Listen closer: the Kyrie is a strict double fugue. Each subject is given to a bundle of voices, and each bundle is itself a canon, so the score contains a fugue whose individual parts are several voices thick, twenty real lines in all. Ligeti wrote out every note; nothing is left to chance. The technique is the choral counterpart of Atmosphères, applied to a text asking for mercy.","The Requiem was recognised at once as the most extreme choral score anyone had attempted, and for years only radio choirs could perform it. Kubrick lifted the Kyrie for the monolith scenes in 2001 alongside Lux Aeterna, which is how most people first heard it. Conductors dispute how literally to take the notated pitches in the wildest passages; Ligeti insisted they were exact, not approximations.","Come to it after Atmosphères and Lux Aeterna rather than before, since the technique is easier to hear once you know it. Listen for the two soloists, whose lines are the only things in the movement you can follow. Then compare Mozart's Lacrimosa to feel how completely Ligeti has removed comfort from the same liturgy."],"ligeti-musica-ricercata-ii":["Four minutes built out of three pitches. A single note repeats in a slow, hollow rhythm high in the right hand; a second note answers below; a third arrives late and changes everything. Ligeti marks it mesto, rigido e cerimoniale, sad, rigid and ceremonial, and asks for the same figure again and again with almost no variation of speed. Near the end one note is hammered fortissimo, over and over, and then the piece simply stops.","Ligeti wrote the eleven pieces of Musica ricercata in Budapest between 1951 and 1953, under a cultural regime that would have banned every one of them. He set himself a rule: the first piece uses two pitches, the second three, and so on to the eleventh, which uses all twelve. He was working, as he later put it, from nothing, with no access to what was being written in the West. Most of the set waited until 1969 for a public performance.","Listen closer: the three pitches are neighbouring semitones, and the third is held back until late in the argument so that it arrives as an event rather than a note. Kubrick used the movement in Eyes Wide Shut for the masked ceremony, where its ritual stiffness does exactly what the Italian marking asks. The rhythm is a slow ostinato that never once falls where you expect it.","For decades the set was known mainly to Hungarian pianists; it was published in the West only after Ligeti's emigration, and this movement became famous only in 1999, when Eyes Wide Shut put it in cinemas. Scholars read the constraint-based design as Ligeti inventing minimalist and serial thinking simultaneously and privately, in a country cut off from both. It is now a standard recital opener.","Count the notes: there are only three, and hearing that fact changes the piece from bleak to astonishing. Notice how much variety Ligeti gets from register and attack alone. Then play the Six Bagatelles, where six other movements of the same set are rescored for five wind players and turn from ritual into something almost cheerful."],"ligeti-six-bagatelles-wind-quintet":["Six short movements for flute, oboe, clarinet, horn and bassoon, twelve minutes in all, ranging from a scampering opener to a bare, slow fifth movement and a wild final scherzo. The writing is dry and pointed: clipped repeated notes, folk-like turns of phrase, sudden silences, every player exposed. The fifth movement is marked in memory of Béla Bartók and consists largely of long held notes over a hollow, tolling accompaniment.","Ligeti made the Bagatelles in 1953 by rescoring six movements of his piano cycle Musica ricercata for the standard wind quintet. Hungary was still under Rákosi; Bartók's more astringent music was officially suspect and Ligeti's own was unperformable. When five of the six were finally played in Budapest in 1956, the last was left out as too dissonant to risk. The complete set waited until 1969, in Sweden.","Listen closer: the folk material is invented, not collected. Ligeti admired Bartók's method but had no interest in quoting real tunes, so the Balkan-sounding limps and irregular groupings are his own constructions in a borrowed accent. The Bartók memorial movement is the one place where the joking stops: it is almost motionless, and its held notes are the only sustained sound in the set.","The Bagatelles have become the wind quintet's most played modern work, grateful, funny and short enough for any programme, and that popularity has slightly obscured their origin. Some critics hear them as Ligeti's farewell to Bartók's world before Cologne; others point out that the harmonic experiments of Musica ricercata survive the rescoring intact. Wind players argue about how brutally to attack the repeated notes.","Start with the last movement for the sheer speed, then go back to the fifth for the contrast. Listen for the horn, which Ligeti uses as the quintet's shadow rather than its hero. If you like them, hear the piano originals; the same music in one colour instead of five is a different experience entirely."],"boulez-marteau-sans-maitre-excerpt":["A contralto and six players in a dry, shimmering world of plucked and struck sound, with no bass instrument and no piano. The nine movements interleave three cycles on surrealist poems by René Char; some are sung, others are purely instrumental commentaries on the sung ones. The music moves in short bursts separated by long silences, and the voice arrives late and often speaks as much as it sings.","Boulez composed it between 1953 and 1955, when he was under thirty, running a concert series in Paris and demolishing reputations in print; he had recently published an article announcing that Schoenberg was dead. The instrumentation came partly from his fascination with Balinese gamelan, Japanese koto and African xylophone, heard at the Musée de l'Homme. Hans Rosbaud conducted the premiere at Baden-Baden in June 1955 after enormous difficulty in rehearsal.","Listen closer: the ensemble is designed as a chain of relatives. Each instrument shares something with its neighbour, the voice with the flute through breath, the flute with the viola through line, the viola with the guitar through strings, the guitar with the vibraphone through struck resonance, so that sound slides across the group without seams. Boulez also never sets Char's poems straight; in two cycles the words have disappeared entirely.","The premiere astonished even hostile listeners, and Stravinsky admired it; it became the piece that proved postwar serialism could produce sensuous music rather than arithmetic. Decades later the analyst Lev Koblyakov worked out how the pitches were actually generated, and it emerged that Boulez had departed from his own system freely wherever his ear preferred something else, which changed the debate about the whole movement.","Do not chase the notes. Listen to the colour: the flute's breathy low register, the guitar's dry pluck, the vibraphone's halo. Take one movement at a time, and start with one of the instrumental commentaries, which are the easiest way in. Boulez's own recordings are the reference, and his later one is slower and warmer than his first."],"boulez-notations-orchestra":["Boulez takes tiny piano pieces he wrote at twenty, each exactly twelve bars long, and blows a handful of them up into vast orchestral movements for over a hundred players, some lasting ten times as long as the original. The result is brilliant, violent, minutely detailed: massed percussion, split string sections, sudden blocks of sound that stop dead. Four movements were finished by 1980 and a fifth much later; the set was never completed.","The piano Notations date from 1945, written while Boulez was studying with Messiaen and discovering Schoenberg's twelve-note method. He disowned them for decades. From 1978 he began orchestrating them at Daniel Barenboim's urging, and Barenboim conducted the first four in Paris in 1980 with the Orchestre de Paris. By then Boulez was directing IRCAM, the electronic-music institute he had persuaded the French state to build beneath the Pompidou Centre.","Listen closer: the originals are not merely orchestrated, they are recomposed. Boulez keeps the twelve-bar skeleton and expands the space between its events, so a whole vast movement is the magnification of a piece lasting under a minute. He worked on them for a quarter of a century, revising as he went, and the manuscript history is a record of a composer arguing with his younger self.","Critics disagree about whether the expansions honour or bury the originals: some hear the finest orchestral writing of the late twentieth century, others a monument built over a sketch. The unfinished state is part of the discussion, since Boulez left most of the twelve unorchestrated at his death in 2016, and his habit of endless revision means several of his works exist in this permanently open condition.","Hear the twelve piano miniatures first, which take about nine minutes altogether, then the orchestral versions; the pleasure is almost entirely in the comparison. Listen for the percussion, which Boulez uses as colour rather than as a rhythm section. Barenboim's Chicago recording and Boulez's own are the two obvious starting points."],"boulez-derive-1":["Seven minutes for six players, and among the gentlest music Boulez wrote. A six-note chord is struck and left to decay; the players trace lines out of its resonance, converging on held notes and drifting apart again. The texture is transparent, the dynamic mostly quiet, and the piece ends by settling rather than stopping. Nothing here shouts: the ear is asked to follow slow changes of colour instead of events.","Written in 1984 as a tribute to Sir William Glock, the BBC administrator who had programmed difficult new music on British radio for a decade and brought Boulez to London as chief conductor. Boulez was then dividing his life between IRCAM in Paris and the conductor's podium. The piece is a by-product of the much larger Répons, whose harmonic material it borrows; Boulez habitually spun small works off big ones.","Listen closer: the chord everything grows from is a musical cipher. It spells the name of the Swiss conductor Paul Sacher in German note-names, a six-note collection that composers commissioned by Sacher used in dozens of pieces during the 1970s and 1980s. Boulez does not present it as a theme; he dissolves it, so the tribute is structural rather than audible.","Dérive 1 is now the Boulez piece programmers choose when they want to introduce him gently, and its warmth surprised listeners who knew only the polemics. Some critics read the late works as a softening; others argue the rigour is unchanged and only the surface has altered. It has been recorded often, usually by the Ensemble Intercontemporain, the group Boulez founded to play music like it.","Listen for the vibraphone and piano: they strike, and everything else grows out of what they leave behind. Play it twice in a row, since it is short and the second hearing is where the shapes come clear. If it appeals, Le Marteau sans maître is the same ear at work thirty years earlier, harder and stranger."],"takemitsu-requiem-strings":["Nine minutes for string orchestra in a single unbroken arch. It opens low and quiet, the lines moving in narrow steps, and gathers slowly to a strained climax before subsiding to where it began. There are no themes to hold on to; the music proceeds by small intensifications of a texture that is always dense and always in motion. The harmony is chromatic, tonal at the edges, and the string sound deliberately dark.","Takemitsu wrote it in 1957, aged twenty-six, in and out of hospital with tuberculosis and very poor. He was self-taught, having discovered Western music from American armed-forces radio during the occupation, and had come to dislike the Japanese traditional music he associated with wartime nationalism. The piece is dedicated to the memory of Fumio Hayasaka, the film composer who had befriended him and who died in 1955.","Listen closer: the whole piece grows from a single small interval turned over and over, so the music seems to be circling something it cannot say. Takemitsu was already treating silence as material, and the pauses are placed as carefully as the notes; the ending does not cadence, it withdraws. Nothing in the score is Japanese in any obvious way, which at the time was a deliberate refusal.","Tokyo received it tepidly. Then Stravinsky, visiting Japan in 1959, heard it, asked to hear it through to the end, and praised it publicly, an endorsement that made Takemitsu's career almost overnight. Later critics have argued about whether the piece is derivative of Berg and Bartók or already unmistakably his own; the consensus has moved towards the second view, hearing his sense of pacing already fully formed.","Listen in one sitting with nothing else on, because the piece depends entirely on continuity. Notice how rarely the strings play loudly and how much weight the few climaxes carry as a result. Compare it with Barber's Adagio, written twenty years earlier: both are string laments, but Takemitsu refuses the release Barber grants."],"takemitsu-november-steps":["Twenty minutes in which two Japanese instruments, the biwa, a lute struck and scraped with a large plectrum, and the shakuhachi, an end-blown bamboo flute, are set against a Western orchestra divided in two and playing mostly sustained, breathing textures. The soloists do not blend; they cut across. At the centre is a long cadenza for the two of them alone, unmeasured, with the orchestra silent. The ending resolves nothing between the two worlds.","The New York Philharmonic commissioned it for its 125th anniversary, and Seiji Ozawa conducted the premiere in November 1967 with Kinshi Tsuruta on biwa and Katsuya Yokoyama on shakuhachi. Takemitsu, who had spent his early career refusing traditional Japanese music, had been converted in the mid-1960s partly by hearing bunraku puppet theatre. Writing the piece meant learning notation systems that do not measure time the way Western scores do.","Listen closer: Takemitsu said he was not trying to fuse the traditions but to let them sound their difference. The orchestral writing imitates the soloists rather than accompanying them, with clusters of strings sliding in pitch to shadow the shakuhachi's breathy bending, and harp and percussion answering the biwa's percussive scrape. The soloists' parts are only partly notated; much is left to players trained in an oral tradition.","The New York premiere baffled much of the audience, and Takemitsu himself was uncertain about it for years, calling it a work he could not repeat. Critics debated whether it was a genuine encounter or an exotic display for a Western commissioner. Its reputation has risen steadily: it is now heard as the piece that made a non-Western tradition audible inside the orchestra without domesticating it. Performances remain rare because the soloists are.","Wait for the cadenza; everything before it is preparation. Listen for the biwa's noise, since the scrape and slap are the point rather than an incidental. Then hear Debussy, whose orchestral colour Takemitsu adored, and notice how differently the two composers use an orchestra that mostly hums. Ozawa's recording with the original soloists is still the reference."],"takemitsu-toward-sea":["Eleven minutes for alto flute and guitar in three short movements: The Night, Moby Dick, Cape Cod. The alto flute's low register does most of the work, dark, breathy and hovering. The guitar supplies chords that ring and fade rather than accompaniment in any regular sense. Nothing is fast; the movements are built from short phrases separated by silence, so the piece feels like a series of glimpses rather than a continuous argument.","Greenpeace commissioned it in 1981 for its Save the Whales campaign, and Takemitsu, who loved Melville, made Moby-Dick the connecting image. He was by then internationally established and writing constantly for concert hall and cinema, nearly a hundred film scores among them, including Kurosawa's Ran. He also made versions for alto flute with harp and strings, and for alto flute and harp, which are heard almost as often.","Listen closer: three notes, E flat, E and A, open the piece and keep returning. In the German note-names Takemitsu was using, they spell S-E-A. The motif is transposed, inverted and buried, but it is the seed of nearly every phrase. He also asks the flute for pitch-bends and airy, half-voiced notes taken directly from shakuhachi technique and applied to a Western instrument.","Toward the Sea has become one of the most played duos in both the flute and guitar repertoires, helped by its brevity and by the scarcity of good music for the pairing. Critics sometimes file the sea pieces as atmosphere; players counter that the motivic construction is tight and the silences exactly measured. The multiple versions raise the usual question of which is authoritative, and Takemitsu approved them all.","Listen for the guitar's decay, since Takemitsu builds phrases around the moment a chord dies away. Take the movements separately if you like; they do not depend on each other. Compare the flute-and-guitar version with the one for flute, harp and strings to hear how added strings change the silence. Then try Debussy's Syrinx for the ancestor."],"takemitsu-rain-tree-sketch-ii":["Five minutes for solo piano, quiet almost throughout. Short figures fall in irregular droplets across the keyboard, sustained by the pedal so that resonances overlap into blurred chords. A recurring gesture, a rising phrase that hangs unfinished, returns several times, and long silences separate the phrases while the piano is left to ring. It does not build to a climax; it simply stops raining. The dynamics sit mostly between soft and very soft.","Takemitsu wrote it in 1992 in memory of Olivier Messiaen, who had died that April and whose harmony and birdsong had shaped him since the 1950s. It followed an earlier Rain Tree Sketch of 1982 and a percussion trio called Rain Tree, all taking their image from Kenzaburō Ōe's writing about a tree whose leaves store rainwater and release it in drops through the night. It was his last piano piece.","Listen closer: the harmony is Messiaen's, used as a memorial rather than a borrowing. The chords are built from his favourite symmetrical scales and spaced the way he spaced them, but they move at Takemitsu's much slower rate. The pedal markings are unusually precise, because the piece is composed as much of decaying resonance as of struck notes. What sounds improvised is written out to the last rest.","Pianists have taken it up as a modern encore, and its accessibility has led some critics to file Takemitsu's late music as gentle and undemanding, a reading his advocates resist by pointing to the strictness of the rhythmic notation. Performance debate centres on the pedal: too little and the drops stay separate, too much and the piece becomes a wash. Recordings vary widely in length for a five-minute score.","Play it quietly and late. Listen for what happens after each phrase rather than during it, because the resonance is the music. Then hear a piece by Messiaen to catch the harmonic accent Takemitsu is speaking in. Roger Woodward's survey of the piano music is a good starting point; compare any two readings for how differently the pedal is handled."],"takemitsu-litany":["Two short movements for solo piano, about seven minutes together, marked Adagio and Lento misterioso. The music is spare and mostly quiet, with a clear, almost song-like line in the right hand supported by widely spaced chords. Compared with Takemitsu's later piano writing it is direct and openly lyrical, closer to French music of the 1920s than to anything avant-garde, and it ends on a chord left hanging in the pedal.","This is a reconstruction. In 1950 Takemitsu, aged twenty, wrote a two-movement piece called Lento in due movimenti, his first published work, and later lost the score. In 1989 he rebuilt it from memory and renamed it Litany, dedicating it to Michael Vyner, the London Sinfonietta's artistic director, who had died that year. Forty years separate the young self-taught composer from the man reconstructing him.","Listen closer: what you are hearing is memory twice over, a 1950 piece recalled by a 1989 composer, so nobody can now say which turns of phrase belong to which. The harmonic language is Messiaen and Debussy filtered through wartime radio, but the pacing, with its long pauses and unhurried arrivals, is already the mature Takemitsu. The join, if there is one, is invisible.","Litany is played more often than its slight scale suggests, partly because it offers an approachable door into a composer with a reputation for difficulty. Scholars treat it cautiously as evidence of the lost original, since it may tell us more about 1989 than about 1950. The 1950 manuscript has never been found.","Start here if Takemitsu's later music has defeated you; this is the most immediately beautiful thing he wrote. Listen for the silences between phrases, which are already doing the work they do in the late pieces. Then go to Rain Tree Sketch II to hear the same hand forty years on, with the melody dissolved into resonance."],"gubaidulina-offertorium":["A violin concerto in one continuous span of about thirty-five minutes. It opens with a theme announced by the orchestra in pointillist fragments, roughly one note per instrument. Each time the theme returns it has lost its outer notes, shrinking from both ends until almost nothing survives. The soloist's music grows more strained and more physical as the theme dies, all scraping, high harmonics and microtones. At the end a long, slow chorale rises in the solo violin.","Written in 1980 for Gidon Kremer, who premiered it in Vienna in 1981 and carried it round the world. Gubaidulina was then nearly fifty and unofficially blacklisted in the Soviet Union: in 1979 she had been named at a composers' congress among a group of seven whose music was to be discouraged. She earned a living writing film scores. Shostakovich had earlier told her to continue on her 'mistaken path'.","Listen closer: the theme is the royal theme that Frederick the Great gave Bach to improvise on in 1747, the subject of the Musical Offering, and Gubaidulina takes it apart note by note as a sacrifice. When the chorale arrives at the end, it is the same theme played backwards, restored to life reversed. The pointillist opening deliberately recalls Webern's orchestration of Bach's own Ricercar.","Offertorium made Gubaidulina internationally famous while she was still officially discouraged at home, and it remains her most performed large work. She revised it twice, in 1982 and 1986, tightening the middle, and the later version is standard. Critics divide over the explicit religious programme, some hearing a genuine sacramental structure and others a literary scheme laid over the notes, but almost nobody disputes the writing for violin.","Learn the royal theme first: find any recording of Bach's Musical Offering and hear the subject once or twice. After that the concerto becomes legible, because you can hear what is being taken away. Wait for the chorale, since the moment it turns up is the argument of the whole piece. Kremer's recording is the place to start."],"gubaidulina-tempus-praesens":["A second violin concerto, thirty-five unbroken minutes, for an orchestra with no violins at all: the soloist is the only one in the hall. Around her Gubaidulina puts low strings, heavy brass, harpsichord, celesta and a large percussion section, so the solo line floats over a dark, clanging landscape it can never merge into. The music alternates ritual slowness with sudden violent tuttis, and ends with the soloist alone and very high.","Written in 2007 for Anne-Sophie Mutter, who gave the premiere at the Lucerne Festival that August under Simon Rattle. Gubaidulina was seventy-six and long settled outside Hamburg, having left Russia in 1992. The title, meaning in the present time, comes from her preoccupation with sacred time, and the score's proportions are built on the Fibonacci sequence, a habit that runs through her whole output.","Listen closer: removing the orchestral violins is not a gimmick but a theology. The soloist stands for the individual soul and can have no doubles, so every other string voice lies below her. Listen also for the harpsichord, an instrument out of its own time in a modern orchestra, and for the way climaxes fall at points determined by number rather than by rhetoric.","The premiere was a festival event and the piece entered the repertoire quickly for a new concerto, helped by Mutter's advocacy. Reviewers split along the usual line with Gubaidulina: admiration for the sound, scepticism about whether the numerical and religious schemes are audible or merely stated. Later performances by other violinists have made the case that the piece survives without its dedicatee.","Listen for what is missing; an orchestra without violins sounds bottom-heavy in a way you feel before you can identify. Track the soloist's register, which climbs across the whole piece. Hear Offertorium first if you can, then this: twenty-seven years apart, they show the same mind with more force and less explanation."],"gubaidulina-chaconne":["Ten minutes of hard, driving piano writing over a repeating harmonic pattern, in the old variation form the title names. It begins with a blunt statement of the chords in massive block harmony, then works through variations of increasing density and speed, toccata figuration, hammered octaves, passages covering the whole keyboard, before the opening chords return, slower and heavier. Almost everything is loud, angular and physically demanding.","An early work, written in 1962 while Gubaidulina was completing postgraduate study in Moscow. She was thirty-one, a Tatar-Russian from Chistopol who had arrived at the conservatory determined to write religious music in a state that forbade it. None of her mature sound-world is here yet; what is here is the Soviet piano tradition, Prokofiev's percussive attack and Shostakovich's grim insistence, handled with complete confidence.","Listen closer: the form is Baroque and Gubaidulina keeps to it strictly, which is the first appearance of a lifelong habit, using old and tightly ruled forms to carry ideas that could not be stated openly. The chord pattern is short and its repetitions are audible throughout, so you can count them. Almost nothing else she wrote is this easy to follow structurally.","For years the Chaconne was her only widely available score in the West, played by Soviet pianists as a competition piece, and it is now sometimes dismissed as apprentice work. The counter-argument is that it establishes the variation-over-a-ground principle she returned to for decades, most famously in Offertorium. Recordings tend to be either brutally fast or monumentally slow, with little in between.","Listen for the bass pattern returning; once you can hear where each variation begins, the piece organises itself. Play it next to Bach's organ passacaglia to hear how little the form has changed in two hundred and fifty years. Then jump to Offertorium and hear the same principle applied to a theme being destroyed rather than decorated."],"gubaidulina-profundis":["Twelve minutes for solo bayan, the Russian button accordion, alone. It begins at the very bottom of the instrument with a sound so low it is felt more than heard, and climbs across the whole piece towards the top. Gubaidulina exploits everything the instrument can do that a piano cannot: notes that swell after they are struck, clusters that breathe, the noise of the bellows themselves, and tones held past the point of comfort.","Written in 1978 for Friedrich Lips, the bayan virtuoso who has premiered much of her music for the instrument. The title comes from Psalm 130, out of the depths have I cried unto thee, a text no Soviet concert programme would have printed, which is one reason the piece carries only a Latin tag and no words at all. The bayan was then a folk instrument with almost no concert repertoire.","Listen closer: the ascent from bottom to top is the psalm's argument made literal, a cry rising out of the depths, and the piece contains no melody in the usual sense to distract from it. Gubaidulina asks for the bellows to be shaken so the sound trembles, and for air to be pushed through the instrument without any key pressed, breathing without words.","De Profundis effectively created the bayan's modern concert repertoire and remains the piece every serious player learns. Western audiences, who associate the accordion with dance bands, are routinely startled by it. Scholars have used it to argue that Gubaidulina's religious programmes are structural rather than decorative: here the shape of the piece and the shape of the psalm are the same thing.","Listen on good speakers or headphones, because the opening notes are below what small speakers reproduce and losing them loses the piece. Follow the slow climb rather than waiting for events. Then hear Pärt's Fratres, written a year earlier in another corner of the Soviet empire, for a very different way of writing sacred music under the same constraints."],"john-williams-star-wars-main-title":["A fanfare in B flat, three notes hammered by the brass, then a soaring melody that leaps upward and keeps climbing, followed by a second, gentler theme for strings and a return to the opening. Five minutes, scored for full symphony orchestra with a heavy brass section and no electronics whatever. The whole thing rides on a driving repeated figure in the low strings. It ends with the first theme blazing.","George Lucas temp-tracked his 1977 film with Korngold, Holst and Walton and asked Williams for something in that vein; Williams, then forty-five and known for disaster movies, proposed original themes instead. It was recorded in London in March 1977 by the London Symphony Orchestra. Hollywood had spent a decade replacing orchestral scores with pop songs and jazz, and Star Wars reversed that within a year.","Listen closer: the fanfare is not the film's general theme but Luke Skywalker's, and it returns throughout the saga attached to him, transformed and often in disguise. Williams built the score on Wagner's leitmotif method, recurring labelled tunes for people, objects and ideas, worked out over decades of opera and then abandoned by most film composers. Dozens of such motifs run across the films.","The soundtrack album sold in the millions, won an Academy Award, and made a symphony orchestra commercially viable in cinema again. Critics at the time were divided over how much was borrowed: the resemblance of the main theme to Korngold's Kings Row score is real and Williams has never denied the debt. Concert programmers still argue about whether film music belongs in subscription series.","Listen to the low strings rather than the tune, because the whole thing rides on their repeated figure. Then find Holst's Mars and hear the machinery Williams inherited. A live orchestral recording will show you how much of the effect is simply loud brass played precisely; the London Symphony's original sessions still sound better than most remakes."],"john-williams-schindler-s-list-theme":["A solo violin, a slow melody in D minor, and a quiet orchestra of strings with occasional woodwind. The tune rises, hesitates, falls back, and repeats with small variations; it never resolves into anything triumphant. Around four minutes. The violin part is written to invite a klezmer player's inflection, sliding between notes and leaning on the top of a phrase, but the setting is that of a nineteenth-century orchestral song without words.","Williams saw a rough cut of Spielberg's 1993 film and reportedly told the director he needed a better composer; Spielberg replied that they were all dead. Williams asked for Itzhak Perlman, whose parents were Polish Jews and who plays the solo on the soundtrack. The film follows Oskar Schindler and the Kraków ghetto; Spielberg shot it in black and white and used almost no music in its most violent sequences.","Listen closer: the theme is rarely allowed a full ending. In the film its appearances break off or dissolve, and even the concert version withholds the cadence you expect until the last possible moment. Williams also keeps the orchestra deliberately thin, so that for long stretches the violin has only sustained strings beneath it and the soloist sounds like one voice rather than a leader.","It won Williams an Academy Award and became, alongside Barber's Adagio, a piece conductors reach for at memorial occasions. Some critics have questioned whether so lyrical and consoling a theme suits the subject; others argue that its refusal of climax is precisely the point. It is now a fixture of violin recitals, which raises the separate question of what happens to film music detached from its images.","Hear Perlman's recording first, since the sliding between notes is not decoration but the whole character. Then listen to the same theme played straight by another violinist and notice how much is lost. If it moves you, go to Górecki's Third Symphony, written seventeen years earlier out of the same history and offering far less consolation."],"john-williams-jurassic-park-theme":["A broad, hymn-like melody in slow triple time, given first to horns over a rocking accompaniment and then to the full orchestra with choir-like weight. It moves mostly by step, with one big upward leap that is the tune's signature. Around three minutes in its concert form. The film also carries a second, faster theme full of brass fanfares, but the famous one is this slower, almost reverent piece.","Written for Spielberg's 1993 film about a dinosaur park, in the same year as Schindler's List, which Williams scored immediately afterwards. Spielberg wanted the first sight of the animals to feel like awe rather than terror, and Williams supplied music with the character of a hymn. The film's computer-animated dinosaurs were a technical landmark; the score is deliberately old-fashioned against that novelty.","Listen closer: the theme's shape, a stepwise rise, one leap and a gentle fall, is the shape of a great many hymn tunes, and Williams doubles the horns in a way that imitates an organ. He is not writing dinosaur music but music for people looking at something they believed impossible. The faster fanfare theme, by contrast, belongs to the island itself and to the helicopter arriving.","The score was overshadowed at the time by Schindler's List, released weeks later, and critics treated it as the lighter of the two commissions. It has aged into one of Williams's most recognised themes, played at graduations and sporting events far from any dinosaur. The recurring complaint, that Williams writes the same noble horn tune repeatedly, is easiest to test here against E.T. and Superman.","Listen for the horns' first entry and then for the moment the strings take the tune over an octave higher; the whole design is that single lift. Compare it with the Flying Theme from E.T.: same composer, same instinct, different weight. A concert recording with a large orchestra suits it better than the compressed film mix."],"john-williams-e-t-flying-theme":["Fast, airborne music: strings surging upward in long phrases, horns underneath, a melody that keeps climbing in steps and never quite lands. The orchestration is bright and busy, with harp and high woodwind glinting through. About three minutes in its concert form. It is one of the few film cues that seems to accelerate without changing tempo, because the accompaniment subdivides more and more finely as the tune rises.","Written for the bicycle-flight sequence in Spielberg's 1982 film. The music and picture would not fit, and Spielberg took the unusual step of letting Williams record the cue freely and then re-editing the film's final minutes to match it, an inversion of normal practice that both men have described. Williams was by then Spielberg's regular collaborator and had just finished Raiders of the Lost Ark.","Listen closer: the theme is built to rise indefinitely, using a sequence that starts each phrase a step higher than the last so the ear never gets a landing. Williams reserves the full brass for the very last statement, and everything before is strings and woodwind, which is why the ending lifts rather than crashes. The same tune appears earlier in the film, slower and sadder, before you know what it is for.","The score won Williams an Academy Award and is frequently cited as the closest fit of music to picture in his catalogue, precisely because the picture was cut to it. Critics unsympathetic to Williams point to its unembarrassed sentimentality; his defenders answer that the film is about a child, and that the writing is technically fastidious beneath the sugar.","Watch the sequence once, then listen to the concert version with your eyes shut and see how much of the film your memory supplies. Listen for the accompaniment getting busier as the tune climbs. Then hear Tchaikovsky's Waltz of the Flowers for the tradition Williams is working in: big tune, glittering orchestra, no irony."],"john-williams-raiders-march":["A brisk march built from two contrasting tunes: a swaggering brass theme with a jaunty dotted rhythm, and a broad, warmer melody for strings in the middle. Williams alternates them and then stacks them, ending with the brass theme at full volume. Around five minutes in the concert arrangement. The scoring is deliberately period, with snare drum, trumpets in unison and a big horn countermelody: 1930s adventure serials played by a modern orchestra.","Written for Spielberg's Raiders of the Lost Ark in 1981, a film consciously modelled on the adventure serials of the 1930s. Williams wrote two candidate themes for the hero and, when Spielberg could not choose between them, joined them into a single march. The second, more lyrical theme belongs to Marion Ravenwood; in the film the two are usually kept apart and only combined at the end.","Listen closer: the march is not heard complete until well into the film. Williams withholds it, giving fragments during the early escapes and releasing the whole tune only once the hero is established, a technique borrowed from opera, where a character's music is assembled across an act. The middle theme's return at the close is how the film tells you the two characters have ended up together, before anything on screen does.","The march has become one of the most played concert pieces by any film composer, and one of the most parodied. Musicologists point to its debt to the studio marches of Max Steiner and Alfred Newman and to Elgar's ceremonial style. The recurring criticism that Williams's heroic themes are interchangeable meets its hardest test here, where the tune is unmistakably one character's and nobody else's.","Listen for the switch from the brass theme to the string theme and back, since the whole piece is that alternation. Then hear Elgar's first Pomp and Circumstance march for the ancestor, and Williams's own Superman march for the sibling. Any good live recording works; this is music that wants an audience in the room."],"john-williams-hedwig-s-theme":["A celesta, the small keyboard instrument with metal bars that sounds like a music box, plays a curling melody in a lilting triple time, alone. Strings and low woodwind creep in beneath it, then the orchestra takes the tune and turns it darker, with brass and a scurrying string figure. About five minutes in the film version. The celesta returns at the end, and the piece closes as quietly and strangely as it began.","Written in 2001 for the first Harry Potter film. Williams scored the first three, and the theme was retained by every composer who followed him across the series, becoming the franchise's signature. He was sixty-nine, and reached for the celesta deliberately: it is the instrument of Tchaikovsky's Sugar Plum Fairy and of a century of stage magic, and it announced a world of children's enchantment before a word was spoken.","Listen closer: the theme sits in an unstable minor with one note kept deliberately ambiguous, so it can be turned menacing without changing a note, which is exactly what the later films do with it. The lurching triple metre is a waltz that keeps tripping. The title is also a red herring: the tune is not really the owl's, it is the whole world's, and Williams uses it as such from the first scene.","It is now among the most recognised melodies written this century, and in popular use it has largely detached from Williams's name. Critics have noted how much of its character comes from orchestration rather than melody, since the same tune on a piano loses most of its strangeness. Composers who inherited the series kept it, making this a rare case of a film theme surviving several different hands.","Listen to the first thirty seconds alone, then to the full orchestral treatment, and notice how far the mood travels on the same notes. Compare Tchaikovsky's Dance of the Sugar Plum Fairy for the instrument's earlier life. Then try a later film's version of the same theme to hear it turned sinister."],"john-williams-jaws-main-theme":["Two notes a semitone apart, alternating in the lowest strings: that is the whole idea. They start slow and quiet and accelerate, gathering instruments, until the orchestra is at full cry with brass and percussion. Around two minutes. There is no melody in any ordinary sense and almost no harmony; what the piece has instead is a rate of acceleration, and that is what makes it frightening.","Spielberg's 1975 film had a mechanical shark that would not work, so the creature is barely seen for the first hour and the music had to stand in for it. Williams played the two-note idea to Spielberg on a piano and Spielberg thought at first that he was joking. Williams was forty-three and had spent fifteen years in television and disaster films; the score won him an Academy Award and reset his career.","Listen closer: the two notes are not always played at the same speed, and the film uses tempo as information, so you know how close the shark is by how fast the figure moves. Once, crucially, the music lies. Williams also scores it for the very bottom of the orchestra, where sound is hard for the ear to locate, so the threat has no direction.","The theme has been discussed to exhaustion, usually alongside its debt to Stravinsky and to the ostinato that opens Dvořák's New World finale. Some critics treat it as a novelty; composers generally do not, pointing out how precisely the acceleration is controlled. It has become the standard teaching example of music doing narrative work the camera cannot do.","Listen without the film and time the accelerations, because the piece is a study in one parameter. Then hear the Augurs of Spring from The Rite of Spring for the ancestor of that low, hammering menace. A recording with real bass extension matters more here than the choice of orchestra."],"john-williams-superman-main-theme":["A ceremonial march in B flat: a rising brass fanfare, then a long striding melody with a three-note rhythmic tag that recurs constantly, then a broad lyrical middle section for strings before the march returns. Around four minutes. The orchestration is heavy on horns and trumpets and light on strings in the main statements, which gives a hard, bright sound. It ends with the fanfare and a broad, church-like closing chord.","Written for Richard Donner's 1978 film, the first big-budget superhero picture, and recorded in London a year after Star Wars had proved that a full orchestra could sell records. Williams supplied a main march, a love theme and a hymn-like theme for the hero's destroyed home planet. The producers wanted grandeur without camp, and the score is the main reason the film reads as sincere.","Listen closer: the tag that opens the march sets the syllables of the hero's name, three notes for Su-per-man, and once you have heard it that way you will find it everywhere in the score, including inside the love theme. Williams uses the three-note cell exactly as an opera composer uses a name-motif, so that the whole film is effectively signed.","It won a Grammy and appears at every orchestra's film-music concert, but it has also become the exhibit for the case against Williams: that his heroic marches share a rhythmic and harmonic fingerprint. His defenders reply that this is what a leitmotif composer does, and that Wagner repeated himself too. Later Superman films have alternately revived and abandoned it, which measures how firmly it fixed the character.","Count the three-note tag; once you hear it as the hero's name, the piece changes. Listen for the middle section, the best tune in the score and the one nobody quotes. Compare Elgar's ceremonial marches and Williams's own Raiders March, since the family resemblance is the point of the exercise."],"penderecki-threnody-victims-hiroshima":["Fifty-two string players, nine minutes, and almost nothing a listener would recognise as notes. It opens with every instrument screaming at the top of its range at once, then breaks into a catalogue of noises: bows scraped behind the bridge, wood struck against strings, instruments tapped, whole bands of pitch sliding against each other. In the middle a dense cluster builds and holds. It ends on a single vast chord that simply stops.","Penderecki wrote it in 1960, aged twenty-six, in Kraków, and titled it 8'37\", a duration taken from the stopwatch. He gave it the Hiroshima dedication afterwards, on hearing a broadcast of it, and later said the sound had suggested the subject rather than the reverse. Poland's radio studios had become an unlikely refuge for experiment, and the Warsaw Autumn festival gave Eastern European composers access to Western avant-garde music.","Listen closer: the score cannot be written in ordinary notation and is not. Penderecki drew it, using black wedges for bands of pitch, symbols for each way of attacking a string, and durations measured in seconds along the page. The players are being told how to make noises, not which notes to play. That graphic score is now as famous as the sound, and it changed how a generation wrote music down.","It won a UNESCO prize in 1961 and made Penderecki internationally famous almost overnight. The retitling has drawn steady criticism, that a piece composed abstractly was given a moral subject for effect, and Penderecki's own accounts varied. Meanwhile it became cinema's default sound for horror, raided by Kubrick, Friedkin and many others, and Jonny Greenwood has both recorded and answered it. Penderecki later disowned the whole avant-garde project.","Listen loud, once, without stopping. Do not try to follow anything; register the mass and the way it thins and thickens. Then hear Barber's Adagio for Strings, the same instruments and the same public grief by the exact opposite means, and the two pieces will argue with each other in your head for a long time."],"penderecki-polymorphia":["Forty-eight strings, ten minutes, and a companion to Threnody in almost every respect: sliding bands of sound, scraping and tapping, clusters that thicken and dissolve, the same drawn notation. Then, after ten minutes of noise, the whole ensemble lands together on a plain, radiant C major chord and holds it. Nothing prepares it and nothing follows it. That final chord is one of the more shocking gestures in twentieth-century music.","Commissioned by North German Radio in Hamburg and premiered there in 1962. The title means many forms. Penderecki was riding the success of Threnody and consolidating the technique the Polish school called sonorism, composing by sound-quality rather than by pitch and rhythm. He was working in a country where the Church and the state were in open competition for the population's loyalty, and his own commitments were with the Church.","Listen closer: some of the score's contours were traced from encephalograph readings taken from listeners while they heard Threnody, so Penderecki turned graphs of brain activity into shapes for the strings to follow. Whether that produces audible results is arguable, but the procedure is documented and the drawn score shows the lines. The final C major chord is the only conventionally notated bar in the piece.","Opinion has always divided over the ending: a profound gesture of consolation, or a stunt. Penderecki gave no explanation. Radiohead's Jonny Greenwood, who became a friend and collaborator, wrote a piece explicitly in response to Polymorphia, which has brought it a rock audience. Musicologists now treat the sonorist works as a coherent body rather than a phase Penderecki grew out of.","Play it straight after Threnody, which it answers; the pairing takes twenty minutes and is one of the great double bills. Wait for the last chord and note what your body does. Then compare Pärt's Cantus in Memoriam Benjamin Britten, another string threnody that reaches the opposite conclusion by consonant means."],"penderecki-symphony-no-3-passacaglia":["The fourth of five movements, about six minutes, and the symphony's engine. A short bass pattern is stated by the low strings, quiet and stalking, and repeated without interruption while everything above it accumulates, woodwind, then brass, then percussion, until the orchestra is at full weight. The pattern never changes. It is the plainest music in a dark and difficult symphony, and the movement most often extracted for concert programmes.","Penderecki began the Third Symphony in 1988 for the Munich Philharmonic, abandoned it, and published two movements separately before finishing the whole work in 1995. By then he had spent two decades writing openly Romantic music, to the fury of the avant-garde that had made him famous. He was also conducting internationally and living at Lusławice, where he planted a celebrated arboretum.","Listen closer: the passacaglia is the oldest trick in the book, a repeating bass with everything else built over it, and Penderecki uses it as Shostakovich did, to make an accumulation feel inevitable rather than argued. Underneath the neo-Romantic surface the cluster harmonies of his sonorist years are still present; they have simply been given a tonal centre to lean on.","The Third Symphony divided critics exactly along the line Penderecki's later career always did: to some a serious late-Romantic symphonist in the line of Shostakovich and Bruckner, to others a defector who traded a genuine discovery for respectability. The Passacaglia is the movement both camps concede. It is now among his most performed orchestral music after the sonorist pieces.","Follow the bass line from the first bar and keep counting its repetitions; the movement is a demonstration of how much can be piled on one unchanging idea. Then hear Britten's Passacaglia from Peter Grimes and Bach's organ passacaglia for the tradition. A big, dark-toned orchestra suits it; period brightness does not."],"penderecki-agnus-dei":["Six minutes for eight unaccompanied voices on three sentences of the Latin Mass. It begins in low, close harmony, almost a drone, and climbs by degrees to a huge, spread chord on the words asking for rest, before falling back. The writing is dense, with eight real parts often moving in tight dissonant clusters that resolve into consonance, and the choir must sustain long lines at extremes of range without a break.","Cardinal Stefan Wyszyński, primate of Poland and the central figure in the Church's long confrontation with the communist state, died in May 1981. Penderecki wrote this in a few days for the funeral. Poland was in the middle of the Solidarity crisis and months away from martial law; the funeral was a national event. He later folded the piece into his much larger Polish Requiem.","Listen closer: the harmony is Renaissance in principle, clusters of voices leaning on one another and resolving, but the clusters are the ones Penderecki learned to build with fifty-two strings twenty years earlier. He is using sonorist technique to write church music. Listen for the basses, asked to go lower than most choirs can manage, and for the silence he leaves before the final chord.","It has become one of the most sung modern choral pieces, partly because it is short and needs no orchestra, and it has been used at funerals well beyond Poland, including commemorations of Penderecki himself. Critics who dismissed his neo-Romantic turn generally exempt the choral music, where the older technique and the new language fit together without strain.","Listen for the top of the arch, roughly two thirds through, where the eight voices open out as wide as they will go. Then hear Górecki's Totus Tuus, written six years later for the same country and the same church, and notice how differently the two Poles handled the same job. A chamber choir of about twenty-four suits it best."],"gorecki-symphony-no-3-second-movement":["Nine minutes, and the simplest music in the symphony. Strings hold a slow, warm chord in a bright major; the soprano enters on a rising phrase and sings a short text twice, once quietly and once with the orchestra opened out beneath her. There is no development and almost no counterpoint, just the voice, sustained strings and a harmonic sway that keeps returning to the same place. It ends with the strings alone.","The text is not literary. In September 1944 an eighteen-year-old girl, Helena Wanda Błażusiakówna, scratched a few words on the wall of a Gestapo cell in the mountain town of Zakopane: a prayer to the Virgin, and the line asking her mother not to weep. Górecki found it in a published collection of such inscriptions and set it exactly, including the girl's name and age, which the score records.","Listen closer: the prisoner comforts her mother, not the other way round, which inverts the first movement, where a mother mourns her son. Górecki chose a text in which the victim consoles the survivor, and set it in a major key, which is why the movement disturbs people who expect a lament. The soprano's rising phrase has the shape of Polish folk song rather than of concert melody.","This is the movement that sold a million records. It has been used in films, adverts and memorials, often stripped of its text and its history, and Górecki was uneasy about that. Critics divide sharply: some hear a work of great moral seriousness, others consolation offered too cheaply for the subject. Musicologists have shown the harmonic scheme to be far more controlled than its apparent simplicity suggests.","Read the text before you listen, since nine minutes of unfamiliar Polish will otherwise pass as pure sound and the words are the point. Listen for the moment the orchestra opens beneath the second statement. Then hear Totus Tuus for the same instinct in shorter form, and Shostakovich's Leningrad Symphony for the opposite response to the same war."],"gorecki-totus-tuus":["Ten minutes for unaccompanied choir, mostly homophonic, with all voices moving together on the same words, and built on a handful of chords repeated with small changes. A short Latin refrain returns again and again, each time scored a little differently, and the piece ends with the name Maria whispered by the whole choir. There is no counterpoint to follow and no climax in the usual sense; the effect is of a crowd praying.","Written in 1987 for John Paul II's third pilgrimage to Poland as pope. The title was the pope's own motto, meaning wholly yours and addressed to the Virgin, and the text is a short poem by Maria Bogusławska. Poland was still under communist rule, and a papal visit was one of the few occasions on which enormous crowds could legally gather; the music was designed for such an occasion.","Listen closer: nearly the whole piece uses one small group of chords, and the variety comes from spacing and dynamics alone, from how high the sopranos sit, how many voices are singing, and how loud. Górecki repeats rather than develops, which is a decision about liturgy as much as about form: the piece behaves like a litany, where repetition is the point rather than a shortage of ideas.","Totus Tuus is now among the most performed a cappella works of its generation and a staple of church and competition programmes. It is also the piece most often used to file Górecki with Pärt and Tavener as a holy minimalist, a label all three disliked. Critics unsympathetic to that music call it undemanding; choirs who have sung it point out that sustaining it in tune is not.","Listen for the ending, where the last Maria fades almost to nothing; how a choir handles it tells you what kind of performance you are hearing. Compare Penderecki's Agnus Dei, written six years earlier for the same country and church. A large choir with a warm, blended sound serves this better than a small, bright one."],"gorecki-symphony-no-3-first-movement":["Twenty-seven minutes, most of them very quiet, for orchestra and one soprano. The double basses alone begin a slow melody; a second group enters with the same tune before the first has finished, then a third, then more, until eight string parts are stacked in canon across the whole range of the orchestra. When the pile is complete it recedes the way it came. Only then, more than fifteen minutes in, does the soprano sing.","Górecki composed the Symphony of Sorrowful Songs in 1976 in Katowice, where he taught. He had been one of Poland's fiercest modernists in the early 1960s; by the mid-1970s he had abandoned that language entirely for slow, consonant, plainly religious music, and lost most of his avant-garde supporters. The premiere took place at the Royan festival in France in April 1977, sung by Stefania Woytowicz. The first reviews were dismissive.","Listen closer: the canon theme is not Górecki's. It is a fifteenth-century Polish lament preserved in a Holy Cross Monastery collection, and the soprano's text, when she finally sings, is a Lamentation in which the Virgin Mary speaks to her dying son. Górecki structures the movement as a single arch built entirely from one melody chasing itself; the canon rises for exactly as long as it falls.","For fifteen years the symphony was a minor curiosity. Then a 1992 recording with Dawn Upshaw and David Zinman sold over a million copies, reached the pop album charts in Britain, and made Górecki, to his visible bewilderment, a commercial phenomenon. Critics have argued ever since about whether that success was a genuine hearing or a misreading of the piece as ambient consolation. Górecki refused to write anything like it again.","Give this movement its full length in one sitting, because the slowness is the argument and skipping ahead destroys it. Count the entries at the start; you can hear each new voice take up the same tune. Then compare the second movement, which does in nine minutes what this does in twenty-seven. Zinman's recording remains the reference, though darker and slower ones exist."],"gorecki-three-pieces-old-style":["Ten minutes for string orchestra in three short movements: a slow, modal opening; a faster central piece with a dancing, folk-like tune; and a quiet close. The harmony is deliberately archaic, all bare intervals and no chromatic colouring, and the string writing is plain, with the melody usually in one line over simple sustained chords. It sounds like a Renaissance dance collection rescored, which is very nearly what it is.","Written in 1963, when Górecki was one of the most aggressive young modernists in Poland and producing dense, dissonant orchestral scores at the same time. The Three Pieces were a side project drawing on old Polish sources, and for years he treated them as light work. They now look like the first appearance of nearly everything he became after 1970.","Listen closer: the melodies are borrowed. Górecki took them from sixteenth-century Polish sources and set them with almost no alteration, so his contribution is the spacing, the silences and the pacing rather than the tunes. Listen for how often he leaves a bare interval where a fuller chord is expected; that hollowness is a modern choice, not an archaism copied from the originals.","For thirty years these were a footnote. After the Third Symphony's success, recordings multiplied and critics began reading them backwards as a manifesto, the young modernist quietly rehearsing the language he would adopt a decade later. Others see nothing so deliberate, just a busy composer arranging some old tunes. Either way they are now his most played short work.","Start with the middle movement for the tune, then go back to the first for the atmosphere. Listen for the bare intervals, which are what make it sound old. Compare Vaughan Williams's Tallis Fantasia and Schnittke's Suite in the Old Style, two other twentieth-century arguments with the past, and notice how differently each composer treats borrowed material."],"schnittke-concerto-grosso-no-1":["Six movements, thirty minutes, for two violins, harpsichord, prepared piano and string orchestra. It opens with the prepared piano tinkling a simple, childlike tune, then plunges into a Baroque toccata that could almost be Vivaldi, until the harmony curdles. Across the piece a tango, a chorale, a cadenza and a wall of sliding clusters collide, and the childlike tune keeps returning between them. It ends with the prepared piano alone, unresolved.","Written in 1976 and 1977 for Gidon Kremer and Tatiana Grindenko, who premiered it in Leningrad in March 1977. Schnittke was forty-two, unable to travel, and supporting himself by scoring Soviet films and cartoons, some sixty of them. Officially he was a difficult figure; unofficially his music circulated widely. The Concerto Grosso is where he first put his working method on public display and gave it a name.","Listen closer: several themes come from his film scores, reused without disguise. Schnittke wanted to stop pretending that the music he wrote for money and the music he wrote for himself were different things. The tango in the fifth movement is deliberately vulgar, and it is placed so that it interrupts the most serious writing in the piece. The prepared piano's opening tune is the seed of everything.","The premiere was a sensation and the piece travelled fast, largely because Kremer played it everywhere. It became the standard example of what Schnittke called polystylism, not collage for its own sake but a claim that a modern European hears all of the past at once, in fragments. Critics unsympathetic to it call the juxtapositions cheap; his defenders argue the Baroque material is structurally load-bearing rather than quoted for irony.","Listen straight through without pausing, since the shocks depend on sequence. Wait for the tango; you will know it when it arrives. Then hear a real concerto grosso by Corelli and come back, and the effect doubles. Kremer's recordings are the reference, and the prepared piano should sound like a toy rather than an effect."],"schnittke-piano-quintet":["Five movements, about thirty minutes, for piano and string quartet, and among the bleakest chamber music of its century. The strings mostly play quietly, often in narrow sliding intervals that sound like weeping, while the piano answers with bare, widely spaced chords. A distorted waltz appears in the second movement and returns, wrecked, later on. The finale is a slow repeating figure in the piano over which the strings gradually fall silent.","Schnittke's mother, Maria Vogel, died suddenly in 1972; he began the quintet almost immediately and could not finish it for four years. He was of German-Jewish descent, born in Engels on the Volga in the German autonomous republic, and had grown up speaking German at home, an outsider in the Soviet Union twice over. He later orchestrated the finale separately and called it In memoriam.","Listen closer: a short figure runs through the whole work, said to spell his mother's name in the German system that lets notes stand for letters, the device Bach used for his own. The waltz is not decoration either. Schnittke connected it to a memory of his mother, and it degrades a little more on each appearance until only its outline is left.","The quintet is now central to the twentieth-century chamber repertoire, and performers describe it as physically hard to play for reasons that are not technical. Critics who find Schnittke's orchestral polystylism theatrical often single this out as the work where the method turns inward and earns itself. The orchestral version of the finale divides opinion: some find it explains the ending, others that it explains too much.","Listen late and alone; this is not concert-hall music in temperament. Follow the waltz through its returns, since tracking how much of it survives each time is the piece's plot. Then hear Shostakovich's Eighth Quartet, written sixteen years earlier out of a comparable despair, and notice how much more Schnittke is willing to let fall apart."],"schnittke-viola-concerto-second-movement":["The central movement, around sixteen minutes, and the longest and wildest of three. It bursts out of the slow opening movement into a manic sequence of dances, tangos, waltzes and marches, which the viola plays against an orchestra that keeps trying to swallow it. The soloist is exposed almost throughout, often in the instrument's hardest register, and the movement drives to a shattering climax before collapsing into the finale's slow music.","Written in 1985 for Yuri Bashmet, who premiered it in Amsterdam in January 1986. Days after finishing the score Schnittke suffered the first of a series of severe strokes; he was declared clinically dead and revived. He always spoke of the concerto as having predicted that, and the fact has coloured every account of it since. He was fifty-one, and would compose for another twelve years with increasing difficulty.","Listen closer: the concerto's basic material spells the soloist's name. Schnittke drew a motif from the letters of Bashmet's name in the German note-name system, a private dedication built into the harmony rather than written above it, and a deliberate echo of Bach's own signature motif. The dances are all in the wrong dialect: each is recognisable and each is slightly, systematically deformed.","Bashmet's advocacy made this the viola concerto of the late twentieth century, and it did more than any other single work to establish the instrument as a solo voice. Critics argue about the stroke, and whether hearing the piece as prophecy is illuminating or a sentimental distortion of a score finished before anything happened. What is not disputed is the writing for viola, which nobody had pushed that far.","Come to the movement inside the whole concerto if you can, because its violence depends on the stillness before it. Listen for the dances and try to name them; the recognition is part of the design. Bashmet's recording is the obvious first choice. Then hear the Concerto Grosso for the same technique used with more humour and less blood."],"schnittke-suite-old-style":["Five short movements, Pastorale, Ballet, Minuet, Fugue and Pantomime, for violin with piano or harpsichord, about sixteen minutes. It sounds on first hearing like a competent eighteenth-century suite: clean melodies, tidy cadences, a fugue that behaves. The violin writing is grateful and the harmony almost never steps outside what a Baroque composer would allow. Only the pacing and a few odd turns of phrase suggest the twentieth century at all.","Assembled in 1972 from music Schnittke had written for two Soviet films, a comedy about a dentist and a sports documentary, and dedicated to the violinist Mark Lubotsky. Film work paid the rent for a composer whose concert music the state distrusted, and Schnittke wrote hundreds of hours of it. Recycling it into a concert suite was both practical and, given his views on the divide between the two, a point of principle.","Listen closer: there is no irony here, which is the surprise. Schnittke wrote the pastiche straight and left it straight, and the strangeness comes only from context, since the same composer in the same years was writing music that tore Baroque style to pieces. Heard beside the Concerto Grosso, this suite is the undamaged original that the later works vandalise, and that is arguably its function.","Some listeners meet Schnittke here and are misled entirely; others treat the suite as slight. The serious reading is that it is one half of a deliberate pair, and that his whole method depends on the pastiche being convincing. It is much played by students and by violinists wanting an encore, which has kept it in circulation while the harder works come and go.","Play it to someone without telling them the date and see what they guess. Then play a movement of the Concerto Grosso and watch the same material come apart. Listen for the Fugue, the most tightly written movement and the one where the pastiche is hardest to fault. Harpsichord and piano give noticeably different pieces."],"arvo-part-spiegel-im-spiegel":["One unbroken span of about nine minutes for a single melody instrument and piano. The piano climbs and falls through a rocking broken triad in even quarter notes, never louder, never faster. The violin plays one slow phrase, returns to the centre note, then plays a phrase one note longer, and so on outwards. There is no theme, no development, no climax and no cadence to prepare; the music simply stops when the pattern has reached its edge.","Written in Tallinn in 1978 for the violinist Vladimir Spivakov, and the last piece Pärt finished in Estonia before emigrating in 1980. He had spent most of 1968 to 1976 not composing at all, copying out plainchant and Franco-Flemish polyphony after Soviet officials attacked his collage works for religious content. Publication and performance in the USSR were uncertain; the piece travelled west in manuscript and reached record buyers only in the 1980s.","The two players are two halves of one voice, and both are generated by rule. The piano touches only the three notes of the F major chord; the violin moves only by step; wherever they meet, the piano takes the chord note nearest the violin's. Nothing is chosen by taste. The high and low bell notes the piano adds at the edges of each phrase are also produced by the system, not by feeling.","The ECM recordings of the 1980s and the 1999 album Alina turned it into one of the most reproduced pieces of the late century, borrowed by dozens of films, dance companies and funerals. That success brought the standard charge: that it is devotional wallpaper, a mood rather than a work. Defenders point to the strictness of the process and to the fact that no two instruments' versions sound alike.","Follow the violin and count: each phrase adds one note, so you can hear the shape widening. Then ignore the violin and listen only to the piano's steady climb. Compare a violin recording with a cello one, an octave lower, and notice how much the character changes although not a note has. Für Alina is the same idea for one player."],"arvo-part-fratres":["A set of variations on a short chord sequence, each separated by the same brief refrain, over a low drone that never moves for the whole eleven minutes. The sequence descends a step each time it returns, so the music sinks by slow degrees while the drone stays put. The best-known version, for violin and piano, opens with a furious rush of arpeggios before the violin drops to bare double stops and the pattern begins.","Composed in Tallinn in 1977 for Hortus Musicus, the Estonian early-music ensemble whose players were among the few colleagues who understood what Pärt was doing. He wrote it without fixed instrumentation, as a piece of medieval-style music that any suitable group could take up, and has since authorised more than twenty scorings, from solo guitar to full string orchestra with percussion.","The drone is two notes, a fifth apart, and they are held from the first bar to the last; every harmony you hear is measured against them. Between variations, a soft percussion tap or a pair of muted chords marks the joint, like the bar that separates verses of a psalm. The rushing violin opening was added for that version alone and belongs to no other.","The 1984 ECM album Tabula Rasa, with Gidon Kremer and Keith Jarrett playing the violin-and-piano version, made Fratres famous outside Estonia and helped invent the market label holy minimalism. Some critics argue the proliferation of versions has diluted it into a house style. Performers counter that the piece was designed as a template, and that the drone and the process are the work, not the instruments.","Pick two contrasting versions, the violin-and-piano one and the eight cellos one, and play them back to back; the first is a display piece, the second a slow procession. Keep your ear on the drone and notice how it turns every repeat into a different colour. Count the variations until the descent becomes obvious."],"arvo-part-cantus-memoriam-benjamin-britten":["Six minutes for strings and a single bell. After the bell strikes alone, the violins begin a slow descending scale; then the second violins enter with the same scale at half the speed, then the violas at half of that, and so on down to the basses. Everyone is playing the same falling line at four different speeds at once. The texture thickens into a roar, the bell keeps tolling, and the whole mass settles onto one held minor chord.","Britten died on 4 December 1976. Pärt, then still in Tallinn and only recently able to hear Western scores, had come to Britten's music late and had hoped to meet him; he wrote afterwards that the chance was gone before he took it. The tribute came at the moment his own new manner was barely a year old, so the piece is both a memorial and a demonstration.","The device is a mensuration canon, a medieval trick in which one melody runs simultaneously at different speeds, and Pärt uses it with no adjustment for taste: the pile-up of dissonance in the middle is arithmetic, not expression. The bell rings on the same note throughout. The final chord is not a resolution the music worked towards; it is simply where all four speeds happen to arrive.","It became one of the most played string works of the late twentieth century and a fixture at memorial concerts, which has occasionally counted against it: critics who admire the construction resent how easily it functions as generic mourning. Conductors argue about the bell, which some strike softly as a distant church, others hard enough to blur the strings, and about how loud the central climax should be allowed to grow.","Listen once for the bell alone, then again following the double basses, who are so slow you can hear the scale as a separate line. The moment to wait for is the collapse into the last chord. Set it beside Britten's own Peter Grimes Passacaglia, which builds by repetition too, and beside Górecki's Third Symphony."],"arvo-part-fur-alina":["Two pages of solo piano lasting two or three minutes, depending who plays it. A single low note is struck and held down with the sustaining pedal, and a high note rings above it like a small bell. Between them, two lines move together in slow single notes: one wanders up and down by step, the other touches only the three notes of the B minor chord. There are no bar lines and no tempo marking, so the player decides how long every silence lasts.","Written in Tallinn in 1976, after eight years in which Pärt published almost nothing, having withdrawn to study plainchant, Notre-Dame organum and Renaissance polyphony. The title refers to the teenage daughter of a family friend who had left Estonia for London; the piece was a small consolation for her mother. It was among the first works played in public in the new manner Pärt christened tintinnabuli, from the Latin for little bells.","The whole method is visible here in miniature. One voice is the melody, the other is the chord, and the chord voice is not composed at all: it always takes whichever note of the B minor triad lies nearest. Each phrase is one note longer than the last. A few notes are marked to be played with a silvery tone, the only expressive instruction in the score.","For years it circulated as a curiosity rather than a work; ECM's 1999 album Alina, which alternates it with Spiegel im Spiegel in several different readings, made it standard repertoire and started the argument about whether so little material can carry a performance. Pianists now differ enormously over pacing, some taking ninety seconds and some five minutes over identical notes.","Play it loud enough to hear the low note still ringing under everything else. Follow the upper of the two moving lines and notice that it never leaps. Then hear the same rule at greater length in Spiegel im Spiegel. Two very different recordings, side by side, will tell you how much the performer is actually deciding."],"arvo-part-tabula-rasa-ludus":["The first of two movements in a double concerto. It opens with the two soloists hammering a single note in octaves at full force, followed by complete silence, and that alternation of attack and emptiness sets the terms. What follows is a set of variations in which each one runs a few notes further out from the centre than the last, driven by a hard pulse, with a prepared piano ringing metallically at the joins. The movement ends abruptly at its widest point.","Premiered in Tallinn on 30 September 1977 by Gidon Kremer and Tatjana Grindenko with Eri Klas conducting, at a concert mixing new Soviet music with older repertoire. Pärt was still officially out of favour, and the score's austerity was read by some in the hall as a religious statement in a state that forbade one. Kremer carried the piece west, and it left the USSR with him.","The piano has screws and bolts wedged between its strings, a technique borrowed from John Cage but used for an opposite purpose: not to make a percussion orchestra out of a piano, but to give the ensemble a bell. The silences are notated exactly, in bars of rest, so the pauses are part of the counting rather than gaps between events. Nothing accelerates; the impression of speed comes from the notes getting shorter.","The 1984 ECM album named after the work was the label's first New Series release and is often credited with creating the audience for so-called holy minimalism, selling in quantities no contemporary score expected. Sceptics have called the piece a system in search of a listener. The second movement, Silentium, tends to get the devotion; Ludus, harder and more violent, is the one that shows the machinery.","Brace for the opening: the shock is deliberate, and the silence after it is the point. Track how each variation reaches one note further before turning back. Then listen to Silentium, which uses the same material at a crawl, and compare Cage's prepared piano in the Sonatas and Interludes to hear the same modified instrument used for something else entirely."],"arvo-part-beatitudes":["Seven minutes for four-part choir with organ. The choir intones the blessings from the Sermon on the Mount in English, one clause at a time, in even chords with almost no melodic movement, and each new blessing starts a semitone higher than the last. By the final Amen the singers are at the top of their range and the pressure is audible. Then the choir stops and the organ alone unwinds the whole climb in a long descending solo.","Written in 1990 in Berlin, where Pärt had settled after leaving the Soviet Union in 1980 by way of Vienna. It is one of his first settings of an English text, made for the Western choral societies and cathedral choirs that had become his principal performers, and it dates from the period when his music was moving from specialist new-music concerts into ordinary church and choral repertoire.","The rising semitones are not word-painting; they are a mechanism, and they mean the choir has no comfortable place to stand by the end. The organ, silent for most of the piece, then descends through exactly the ground the voices covered, so the postlude is the whole work played backwards in compressed form. Set the two halves against each other and the shape is a single arch.","Choirs took it up quickly because it lies well for amateurs and sounds far harder than it is, which has also made it a target: critics who admire the Estonian instrumental works of the 1970s hear the English-language choral pieces as a more comfortable, more marketable Pärt. Conductors argue mainly about the organ, whether it should be barely present under the voices or a full second voice.","Listen for the key change at each new blessing; once you notice it, the accumulating strain is unmistakable. Save your attention for the organ solo at the end, which most listeners treat as an outro and which is actually the argument. Pair it with Shaw's and the swallow for another way of setting scripture without preaching."],"arvo-part-summa":["A setting of the Latin Credo for four voices, six minutes long, in which the entire text is delivered once, syllable by syllable, without a single word repeated or emphasised. Two voices carry stepwise melody, two supply the chord notes beneath, and the four rotate through their roles by rule. There are no climaxes and no illustration: the crucifixion and the resurrection get exactly the same music as the list of attributes around them.","Composed in Tallinn in 1977. Pärt's 1968 Credo, which quoted Bach and set a sacred text openly, had brought official disapproval and helped drive him into silence, so a piece with that word on the cover was not a safe object in Soviet Estonia. The colourless title, Latin for the sum or the whole, kept the text out of the programme. The string arrangements, which lose the words entirely, came later.","The whole thing is generated by a rotation: the four parts pass the melody and the chord notes between them on a fixed cycle, so the voice you hear on top keeps changing without the music changing character. That is why the piece can lose its text and survive as a string quartet, and it is also why the Credo, the most argued-over text in Western music, arrives here with no rhetoric attached at all.","For years it was better known in the string version, which several ensembles programme as abstract music without knowing what the vocal original says. Scholars have made the disguised title a small emblem of how sacred music survived late Soviet cultural policy. Others note that the neutrality was not only tactical: Pärt has consistently refused to dramatise doctrine, and the disguise suited the method.","Hear the choral version first, with a text in front of you, and notice that the music takes no interest in what the words mean. Then hear the string quartet version and check whether anything is actually lost. Compare it with a Renaissance Credo, Josquin or Palestrina, where every phrase gets its own rhetorical treatment."],"reich-music-18-musicians-opening":["The hour-long piece begins with a cycle of eleven chords, each one pulsed by the whole ensemble for the length of two long breaths, then released as the next arrives. Pianos and mallet instruments keep a bright, ringing patter underneath; clarinets and wordless voices supply the sustained tone on top. The eleven chords take about five minutes to pass, and the rest of the work is a small piece built on each one in turn, before the cycle returns at the end.","Written in New York between 1974 and 1976 for Reich's own ensemble and premiered at The Kitchen on 24 April 1976. He had spent the previous decade making pieces for a handful of players out of a single process; this was the first to use a large group, real harmony and a full evening's architecture. The ECM recording of 1978 sold in numbers no downtown score had managed, in record shops that filed it next to rock.","There is no conductor and there are no bar lines in the usual sense. The clarinettists and singers hold each pulsed chord for as long as one comfortable breath, twice, so the tempo of the whole opening is set by human lungs. Changes are cued by the metallophone, a vibraphone with its motor switched off, whose short pattern is an instruction to the players rather than a melody.","The 1978 ECM album is one of the few contemporary records to have created its own audience, and the work is now taught as the point where minimalism acquired harmonic depth. Reich's later Nonesuch remake of 1996 opened a long argument about whether the piece belongs to his own ensemble's oral tradition or can be handed to any competent group; the published score arrived only decades after the premiere.","Listen to the first five minutes on headphones and count the chords as they turn over, then feel for the breath that governs them. After that, notice how the mallet patterns never stop even when the harmony moves. The Ensemble Modern and Reich's own recordings differ audibly in tempo and warmth; hear both before deciding which you prefer."],"reich-clapping-music":["Five minutes for two pairs of hands and nothing else. Both players clap the same twelve-beat pattern of claps and gaps in unison. After a dozen repeats the second player jumps forward by one beat and locks there; a dozen repeats later, forward again. Thirteen positions later the two are back in unison and the piece is over. What you actually hear is not either pattern but the composite they make, which changes character completely with every shift.","Written in 1972, partly out of practical frustration: touring with an ensemble that needed pianos, marimbas and tape machines, Reich wanted a piece that could be performed anywhere by two people carrying nothing. It followed his 1970 study of drumming in Ghana with a master of Ewe percussion, and the twelve-beat pattern is close in shape to a West African bell timeline.","Despite the reputation, this is not a phase piece. In the earlier tape and piano works one part slides continuously ahead; here the shift is abrupt, a clean jump on a downbeat, because two clapping humans cannot drift smoothly and stay together. Reich also specifies that players clap with cupped or flat hands to find a dry, pitchless sound, so no accidental melody creeps in.","It has become the most performed minimalist work in the world, largely because school groups, festivals and street performers can do it, and there is now a phone app that lets anyone play the second part. That ubiquity draws the charge that it is a demonstration rather than a piece. Performers who have tried it point out how brutally exposed the twelve shifts are.","Try clapping the first part along with a recording; you will find out within thirty seconds how hard steadiness is. Listen for the composite rhythm rather than either player, and notice which positions sound dense and which sound almost empty. Music for Pieces of Wood is the same thinking with tuned claves, and worth hearing straight afterwards."]});